    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:prepare", title: "Give every system a purpose", minutes: 10, summary: "Record the starting configuration, place both controllers on their own segment, and explain what they will do.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "Start by understanding the services you are about to build and where they will live. Then record the names, addresses, and dependencies for two controllers in the same domain."), LessonSection("What is Active Directory?", "The shared directory behind domain identities."), LessonSection("A directory for people and computers", "Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) is the Windows Server directory service. It stores objects such as user accounts, computer accounts, and groups, together with their attributes. Administrators use this shared directory to organize identities and manage access consistently."), LessonSection("The domain and the controller", "A domain is a logical collection of directory objects, named here sbd.test. A domain controller (DC) is a server running AD DS that stores and serves that directory. DC01 and DC02 are two servers serving the same domain. A forest contains one or more domains; this build starts with one."), LessonSection("Identity, permissions, and policy", "Authentication checks who is signing in. Authorization determines what that identity may access. Signing in successfully does not grant access to every resource: the destination service still applies its permissions. Groups make it practical to assign access to roles instead of managing every person separately."), LessonSection("Organization and consistent settings", "Organizational units (OUs) organize objects and support delegated administration and Group Policy. Group Policy applies centrally managed settings to users and computers. A global catalog helps find directory objects across a forest; both controllers in this course provide that role."), LessonSection("Remember", "AD DS stores the directory, and every domain controller serves it. Authentication proves who someone is; authorization decides what they may reach."), LessonSection("Where do the controllers live?", "On a segment of their own, behind a core that denies by default."), LessonSection("A segment reserved for controllers", "SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 sit on VLAN 10 (10.0.10.0/24), a segment that holds domain controllers and nothing else. Workstations, member servers, security tools, and administrators sit on segments of their own. The core joins the segments, and its VLAN 10 address, 10.0.10.1, is the controllers' default gateway."), LessonSection("Deny by default, allow by exception", "The core denies every session between segments unless a documented rule permits it, and logs what it denies. Members reach the controllers only for name resolution (course 1.2) and the services in \"What crosses the network?\". Traffic between the two controllers stays inside VLAN 10, so the core never routes it."), LessonSection("Only Tier 0 manages a controller", "From other segments, Remote Desktop, PowerShell remoting, and AD Web Services reach a controller only from AdminJumpBox01, the Tier 0 host on VLAN 60 that course 1.8 builds; until then, use the VM console. Members still reach SMB and RPC, which can carry administration too, so Tier 0 accounts never sign in to them."), LessonSection("Two layers, and no internet", "Windows Defender Firewall stays on in every profile and blocks what no rule allows, so an unopened port stays closed even if a core rule is wrong. Keep Remote Desktop off. The directory rules accept any address, and the remoting and AD Web Services rules do too until course 1.8 limits them to the Tier 0 host, so the core decides who reaches those ports. No segment routes to the internet; approved media is the only way in."), LessonSection("Remember", "A controller's segment is part of its security: VLAN 10 for controllers only, default deny at the core, management only from Tier 0, and the host firewall on in every profile."), LessonSection("Prepare two separate VMs", "Use new, patched Windows Server installations (Desktop Experience) with console access and the required administrative credentials. Connect both to VLAN 10, the segment reserved for domain controllers; nothing else joins it. Obtain approved media and updates through your established offline process."), LessonSection("Record names and addresses", "Rename the servers SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 and restart as needed. Use 10.0.10.10/24 and 10.0.10.11/24 with 10.0.10.1, the core's VLAN 10 address, as the default gateway. Every other segment reaches the controllers only through the core, which denies anything a rule doesn't permit."), LessonSection("Point DC02 at DC01 and check the clocks", "Set SBD-DC02's preferred Domain Name System (DNS) server to 10.0.10.10 so it can find the domain when you promote it, and leave it that way; course 1.2 explains the DNS design. Confirm both clocks agree and document the intended internal time source: SBD-DC01, which course 1.3 makes the domain's time source."), LessonSection("Inspect each server before promotion", "hostname\nGet-NetIPConfiguration\nw32tm /query /status"), LessonSection("What to look for", "The two names and static addresses match your plan, the default gateway is 10.0.10.1, and SBD-DC02 lists 10.0.10.10 as its DNS server. Record actual time-source output; a configured address alone does not prove time synchronization."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "Renaming a domain controller later is a separate procedure with its own steps and checks. Set each server's final name and static address before you promote it."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record your VM names, OS builds, addresses, segment and gateway, time source, and any differences from the sample.")], question: "Why set each server's final name and static address before promotion?", answers: ["Changing them on a domain controller later is a separate procedure", "Promotion assigns a name and address automatically", "The domain takes its name from the first server's hostname"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Promotion records the server as a domain controller under its current name. Settle the name and address first; renaming a controller later has its own procedure and checks.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#prepare", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:dc01", title: "Create the first domain controller", minutes: 10, summary: "Create the sample forest and bring AD DS online on DC01.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "A new forest establishes the directory's initial boundary. Here, sbd.test is a sample domain, not an existing company domain. Follow this path only for a new forest."), LessonSection("Add the server role", "On DC01, open Server Manager → Manage → Add Roles and Features. Choose the local server and Active Directory Domain Services, including its management tools."), LessonSection("Create the forest", "Select Promote this server to a domain controller, then Add a new forest. Enter sbd.test. Because both controllers run the same Windows Server release, select the highest forest and domain functional levels the wizard offers. Leave the DNS server option selected; the wizard's DNS delegation warning is expected, and course 1.2 explains both. The first controller in a forest is always a global catalog. Enter and confirm a unique Directory Services Restore Mode password on the server."), LessonSection("Review and promote", "Review the SBD NetBIOS name, paths, and prerequisite results. Read any warnings, resolve blocking errors, install, and allow the restart. Then inspect the services and domain."), LessonSection("Verify DC01 after the restart", "Get-ADDomain | Select-Object DNSRoot, NetBIOSName\nGet-Service NTDS,Netlogon\nGet-SmbShare -Name SYSVOL,NETLOGON"), LessonSection("What to look for", "The domain is sbd.test with NetBIOS name SBD. NTDS and Netlogon are running, and SYSVOL and NETLOGON shares exist. Investigate missing services or shares before moving on."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "Installing the AD DS role is only the first part. The promotion wizard creates the domain controller; a completed role installation alone is not a completed forest."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the domain, promotion outcome, service checks, and the file names of your screenshots or saved output. Do not enter passwords.")], question: "What turns an AD DS role installation into a domain controller?", answers: ["Renaming the server", "Completing domain-controller promotion", "Adding a second network adapter"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Promotion configures the directory role for a new forest or an existing domain. Merely adding the role is insufficient.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#dc01", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:dc02", title: "Give the domain a second service point", minutes: 10, summary: "Promote DC02 into the existing domain and inspect the replication relationship.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "A second domain controller gives the domain another live service point. Read why this is a standard availability practice, then add DC02 and verify the result."), LessonSection("Why use two domain controllers?", "Keep the directory available when one server is unavailable."), LessonSection("Remove the single-server dependency", "With one DC, its outage removes the only live directory service point. With two healthy, reachable controllers, clients can use a surviving DC for supported operations. This also lets you patch or restart one server while the other continues serving requests. Reconnection may take time; uninterrupted service is not guaranteed."), LessonSection("Both controllers do useful work", "Both writable DCs can process normal directory changes and authentication requests. Replication shares directory changes between them. “Primary” and “secondary” describe the order you promote them, not a primary server and an idle spare. Certain operations still depend on a single FSMO role holder, so role placement and recovery need their own plan."), LessonSection("Make the redundancy real", "Microsoft recommends at least two virtualized DCs per domain on different virtualization hosts. Two VMs on one host share that host as a failure point. Consider shared storage, power, and network dependencies too."), LessonSection("Availability still needs recovery", "Replication can spread an accidental deletion or harmful change to both servers. Keep protected backups and test recovery separately. Two is a starting point for this design, not a universal capacity target: additional sites, users, and recovery needs may require more controllers."), LessonSection("Remember", "Before relying on DC02, verify replication, review shared dependencies, and confirm both controllers pass their health checks."), LessonSection("Reach the existing directory", "On DC02, check that DC01 answers at 10.0.10.10 and that both clocks agree before promotion."), LessonSection("Add a controller to this domain", "Install the AD DS role and management tools. In the promotion wizard choose Add a domain controller to an existing domain, enter sbd.test, and authenticate with an authorized Domain Admin account. Leave the DNS server and global catalog options selected, leave Read only domain controller unselected, and set the DSRM password securely."), LessonSection("Finish and inspect", "Review site placement, replication source, and prerequisite results; install and restart."), LessonSection("Inspect both controllers and replication", "Get-ADDomainController -Filter * |\n  Select-Object HostName, IPv4Address, IsGlobalCatalog\nrepadmin /replsummary\nrepadmin /showrepl SBD-DC02"), LessonSection("What to look for", "Both hostnames appear with the intended addresses and global catalog roles. After initial convergence, replication checks show successful recent attempts and no unresolved replication failures. Inspect errors rather than assuming the restart completed replication."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "Choosing Add a new forest creates a different directory. For this chapter, DC02 joins the domain established by DC01."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record both controllers, replication status and collection time, plus any failures or follow-up actions.")], question: "A damaging directory change reaches both controllers. What does that illustrate?", answers: ["DC02 should never replicate", "Two controllers guarantee recovery", "Replication and historical recovery are different capabilities"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Both controllers can hold the same unwanted change. Recovery requires protected backups and an appropriate, tested recovery procedure.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#dc02", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:verify", title: "Ask the domain to prove it works", minutes: 10, summary: "Check each controller's health, replication in both directions, the roles each holds, and its host firewall.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "A service can be running while the domain is unhealthy. Test each controller by name so every result tells you which controller you checked. Capture actual results and failures in your evidence record."), LessonSection("What crosses the network?", "The ports a domain member needs to reach its domain controllers."), LessonSection("Every conversation crosses the core", "Domain members sit on other segments, so each conversation in this table crosses the core, which allows it only because a rule says so. Inside the Network shows each one crossing the core switch."), LessonSection("Sign-in is only the start", "Kerberos proves who is signing in. After that, the member reads the directory over LDAP, pulls Group Policy and scripts from SYSVOL over SMB, and uses RPC for NetLogon. Time matters too: Kerberos rejects clocks that drift too far apart."), LessonSection("Remember", "Allowing Kerberos isn't enough. Applying Group Policy and reading SYSVOL also need LDAP, SMB, and RPC. In the Inside the Network design, rules F02, F03, and F09 permit this profile for domain members, with LDAP signing and channel binding required, and rule A01 limits administration to the Tier 0 host. Name resolution has its own rule (F01), and so does credentialed scanning (F15), which arrives with course 1.17, coming soon; everything else is denied."), LessonSection("Test each controller", "In an elevated PowerShell window on either controller, run DCDiag against SBD-DC01 and then SBD-DC02. Its default tests cover connectivity, advertising, replication, SYSVOL, services, and role holders. Read each test's result, not just the last line."), LessonSection("Check replication both ways", "Run the replication summary, then list each controller's inbound partners. Expect recent successful attempts from DC01 to DC02 and from DC02 to DC01, with no failures. If an error appears right after promotion, wait a few minutes and run the checks again; record any error that persists."), LessonSection("Confirm both controllers' roles", "List both controllers with their global catalog status, then the operations master (FSMO) role holders; in this build SBD-DC01 holds all five. Confirm SYSVOL and NETLOGON are shared on SBD-DC02 as well. If they are not, its first SYSVOL replication has not finished."), LessonSection("Confirm the second layer", "On each controller, confirm the network profile is DomainAuthenticated and that the effective firewall policy is on for the Domain, Private, and Public profiles and blocks unsolicited inbound connections. A controller that reports the Public or Private profile is applying a different rule set than the domain expects; find out why before you rely on it."), LessonSection("Read-only domain health checks", "dcdiag /s:SBD-DC01\ndcdiag /s:SBD-DC02\nrepadmin /replsummary\nrepadmin /showrepl *\nGet-ADDomainController -Filter * | Select-Object HostName, IsGlobalCatalog\nnetdom query fsmo\nGet-SmbShare -Name SYSVOL,NETLOGON -CimSession SBD-DC02\nGet-NetConnectionProfile | Select-Object InterfaceAlias, NetworkCategory\nGet-NetFirewallProfile -PolicyStore ActiveStore |\n  Select-Object Name, Enabled, DefaultInboundAction"), LessonSection("What to look for", "Each controller passes its DCDiag tests, or every failure has a recorded cause. Replication shows recent successful attempts in both directions and no failures. Both controllers are global catalogs, SBD-DC01 holds the five operations master roles, SYSVOL and NETLOGON are shared on both, and each controller reports the DomainAuthenticated profile with every firewall profile on and inbound blocked by default. Save output with host, time, and interpretation; a command that ran is not a pass."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "A successful ping proves only that an address answers. It doesn't show that the directory replicates, that SYSVOL is shared, or that a controller can authenticate anyone."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "For each controller, record the DCDiag results, replication status in both directions, the roles it holds, its network profile and firewall state, the test time, and what the results establish.")], question: "repadmin /replsummary reports no failures. What does that establish?", answers: ["The most recent replication attempts between the controllers succeeded", "Every authentication control is satisfied", "The system is authorized to operate"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The summary describes the latest attempts at that moment. Authentication requirements, recovery, and authorization need their own evidence and decisions.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#verify", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:evidence", title: "Leave a build someone else can understand", minutes: 10, summary: "Assemble your configuration record, test evidence, and remaining work.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "The useful deliverable is a build you can explain and check again. Your evidence record connects each action with an observed result and identifies what remains unresolved."), LessonSection("Describe the final state", "Record the domain, both controllers, their segment and gateway, site placement, and actual role holders. Preserve the distinction between the intended design and observed configuration."), LessonSection("Keep a small evidence set", "Store your environment outputs locally with descriptive filenames and collection times. In this companion, write a short summary and the file references; the form does not upload or collect your screenshots, server output, or credentials."), LessonSection("Plan the next check", "List remaining hardening, auditing, backup, recovery, and client-validation work. Assign an owner or next action. Revisit checks after relevant configuration changes. Export the evidence record before moving devices."), LessonSection("Record the current role holders", "Get-ADDomain |\n  Select-Object DNSRoot, PDCEmulator, RIDMaster, InfrastructureMaster\nGet-ADForest |\n  Select-Object RootDomain, SchemaMaster, DomainNamingMaster"), LessonSection("What to look for", "The record identifies the actual role holders and links configuration decisions to dated test results. Open issues remain visible. Lesson completion records learning activity; it is not a system authorization decision."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "A diagram becoming complete does not establish that your VM passed a check. The model tells the lesson story; your observations establish what happened in your environment."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Summarize the final state, reference your evidence files, and list remaining work with a next action or review trigger.")], question: "Which event is a useful reason to revisit the evidence?", answers: ["Only the anniversary of the video", "A relevant configuration change or new finding", "Finishing the animation"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Changes and findings can affect prior conclusions. A monitoring plan defines when and how to reassess the relevant security state.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#evidence", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "dns-configuration", number: "1.2", title: "Configure DNS", subtitle: "Service discovery, zones, records & troubleshooting", category: "Network build", description: "Learn how DNS lets every domain member find its domain controllers, then build dependable internal name resolution on DC01 and DC02: set each controller's DNS order, configure forward and reverse zones, manage service records, and prove resolution, discovery and failover in the air-gapped training network.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
