    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:plan", title: "Map names to the network", minutes: 25, summary: "Domain Name System (DNS) turns names into addresses, and for Active Directory it does one more job: it tells every domain member where its domain controllers are. Start from the working directory build, learn what DNS answers and what it doesn't, set each controller's DNS client order, and record which server owns each name and how a client finds it.", objectives: ["Explain how DNS lets a domain member find a domain controller, and what a DNS answer does not prove.", "Set each domain controller's DNS client order and point domain members only at internal DNS.", "Inventory the existing sbd.test and _msdcs zones without creating duplicates.", "Document the forwarding and recursion decision for an air-gapped network."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.1, the DC01 + DC02 identity build, with both domain controllers healthy. Both were promoted with the DNS server option selected, and SBD-DC02 still uses SBD-DC01 for DNS.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server practice environment, and work at a domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and nothing is forwarded outside."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Start with what DNS answers. An A record maps a host name to an IPv4 address (AAAA maps to IPv6), so SBD-DC01.sbd.test resolves to 10.0.10.10. Service (SRV) records advertise which servers offer a service: a domain member asks for _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test, resolves a listed controller's name, contacts it, and only then authenticates. DNS never checks a password, and it works on an isolated network with no internet access."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Record the domain, each subnet, both DNS addresses, and the owner of each service name. Keep host records and service aliases distinguishable."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On each domain controller, confirm the DNS Server service is installed and running; an address typed into a network adapter installs nothing. Then open DNS Manager and review the sbd.test and _msdcs.sbd.test zones that promotion created, without creating duplicates. The DNS delegation warning from course 1.1's promotion is expected: no parent zone exists above the isolated .test namespace, so no action is needed."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Set each controller's DNS client order. Promotion pointed SBD-DC01 at itself (the loopback address, 127.0.0.1) while it was the only controller, and SBD-DC02 pointed at SBD-DC01 so it could find the domain. Run the replication summary first; once replication succeeds in both directions, list the partner controller first and the loopback address second on each, and never an outside resolver. Domain members list both controllers; a public resolver is not an alternate for this environment."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Confirm the network's service-path register permits DNS: every networked system to both domain controllers on TCP and UDP 53 (rule F01 in Inside the Network). A member has to find a controller before any Kerberos, LDAP, or Group Policy traffic can start."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Decide whether any separate internal namespace needs a conditional forwarder. With no recursive dependencies, document a no-recursion design; do not add internet forwarders or delete every root hint as a shortcut."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Invoke-Command SBD-DC01,SBD-DC02 { Get-WindowsFeature DNS; Get-Service DNS }\nrepadmin /replsummary\nGet-DnsClientServerAddress -AddressFamily IPv4 -CimSession SBD-DC01,SBD-DC02\n# After replication succeeds both ways (adapter name varies):\nSet-DnsClientServerAddress -CimSession SBD-DC01 -InterfaceAlias Ethernet `\n  -ServerAddresses 10.0.10.11,127.0.0.1\nSet-DnsClientServerAddress -CimSession SBD-DC02 -InterfaceAlias Ethernet `\n  -ServerAddresses 10.0.10.10,127.0.0.1\nGet-DnsClientServerAddress -AddressFamily IPv4 -CimSession SBD-DC01,SBD-DC02\nGet-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC01"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both controllers run the DNS Server service and, once replication succeeds both ways, list their partner first and the loopback address second. Members list both controllers only; the AD zones, the recursion decision, and the register rows that permit DNS are recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Save the address/name worksheet, each controller's DNS client order and the reason for it, and the initial zone inventory before changing anything else."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Install and configure DNS Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/quickstart-install-configure-dns-server\n\nMicrosoft · What is DNS?\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/dns-overview\n\nMicrosoft · Best practices for DNS client settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/best-practices-for-dns-client-settings\n\nMicrosoft · Locating domain controllers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/dc-locator\n\nMicrosoft · Service overview and network port requirements\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements\n\nMicrosoft · AD DS configuration wizard page descriptions\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/deploy/ad-ds-installation-and-removal-wizard-page-descriptions\n\nMicrosoft · Install-ADDSForest\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/addsdeployment/install-addsforest")], question: "Which DNS servers should a domain member use in this lab?", answers: ["The internal DNS servers that resolve sbd.test", "A public resolver whenever the first DC is unavailable", "The offline Root CA"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "AD clients need the internal namespace and its service records. Internet reachability would not replace that requirement.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Promotion already created the sbd.test and _msdcs zones. What should you do?", ["Delete them and start from a clean zone list", "Review and record them without creating duplicate zones", "Create a second sbd.test zone for DC02"], 1, "Promotion builds the zones Active Directory needs. Duplicating or recreating them breaks the record you are trying to verify."), QuizQuestion("Why does this design document a no-recursion decision instead of adding internet forwarders?", ["Forwarders are always a security violation", "Root hints make zones replicate faster", "The network has no recursive dependencies, so the decision is recorded deliberately"], 2, "In an air-gapped lab there is nothing to forward to. Writing the decision down keeps the design intentional rather than accidental."), QuizQuestion("Why did SBD-DC02 point to SBD-DC01 for DNS while it was promoted?", ["Its own DNS service didn't exist yet, and SBD-DC01 held the records that locate the domain", "A domain controller can never use its own DNS service", "Promotion needs an internet resolver"], 0, "A new controller finds the existing domain through DNS. Until replication has given its own DNS service a current copy of the zones, it relies on a controller that already has one."), QuizQuestion("You can reach 10.0.10.10, but a member still can't find a domain controller for sbd.test. What do you inspect first?", ["The Kerberos ticket lifetime", "The member's DNS server list and the SRV records on the servers it uses", "The forest functional level"], 1, "Reaching an address isn't discovery. Check that the member asks the internal DNS servers and that they hold the domain controller locator records.")], recap: ["DNS finds domain controllers through SRV records; it never checks a password.", "Each controller lists its partner, then itself; members list both controllers only.", "The AD zones are inventoried, not recreated, and the delegation warning is expected.", "The forwarding and recursion decision is written down first."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 turns this plan into AD-integrated forward and reverse zones.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#plan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:zones", title: "Configure forward and reverse zones", minutes: 20, summary: "A zone holds the records for one part of the namespace. Forward lookup answers where a name lives. Reverse lookup answers which name is assigned to an address. AD integration lets the directory replicate zone data between the selected DNS servers.", objectives: ["Confirm sbd.test is AD-integrated with the intended replication scope.", "Create a secure, AD-integrated reverse zone for 10.0.10.0/24.", "Explain how zone data moves between the two DNS hosts."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.1, the DC01 + DC02 identity build, with both domain controllers healthy. Both were promoted with the DNS server option selected, and SBD-DC02 still uses SBD-DC01 for DNS.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server practice environment, and work at a domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and nothing is forwarded outside."), LessonSection("Step 1", "In DNS Manager, inspect sbd.test Properties. Confirm that it is AD-integrated and choose the domain or forest replication scope that matches the DNS servers intended to host it. Domain-wide zones live in the DomainDnsZones partition and forest-wide zones in ForestDnsZones, the partitions you saw in course 1.1's replication and DCDiag output."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Use secure dynamic updates for the AD-integrated zone. Review delegation and permissions before broadening who can change records."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Under Reverse Lookup Zones, create an IPv4 primary AD-integrated zone for network ID 10.0.10 if an equivalent zone does not already exist. Select the approved replication scope and secure updates."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Review zone-transfer settings. AD replication between these DCs does not require enabling transfers to arbitrary DNS servers; restrict any separately required secondary-server transfers explicitly."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC01 |\n  Select-Object ZoneName, ZoneType, IsDsIntegrated, DynamicUpdate, ReplicationScope, SecureSecondaries\nGet-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC02"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The intended forward and reverse zones appear on both DNS hosts after replication. The reverse zone for this /24 is 10.0.10.in-addr.arpa."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Capture the zone type, update policy, replication scope, and transfer policy for each zone."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage DNS zones\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/manage-dns-zones\n\nMicrosoft · Active Directory-integrated DNS zones\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/active-directory-integrated-dns-zones")], question: "How does an AD-integrated zone reach the other DNS-enabled domain controller in its replication scope?", answers: ["An unrestricted zone transfer to every client", "Active Directory replication", "A manual copy of the DNS cache"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "AD-integrated zone data follows directory replication. A conventional secondary zone uses a different transfer mechanism.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What is the reverse lookup zone for the 10.0.10.0/24 network?", ["10.0.10.in-addr.arpa", "sbd.test.reverse", "arpa.10.0.10"], 0, "IPv4 reverse zones place the reversed network octets under in-addr.arpa, so this /24 becomes 10.0.10.in-addr.arpa."), QuizQuestion("Why choose secure dynamic updates for the AD-integrated zone?", ["It makes lookups return faster", "It removes the need for a reverse zone", "Only authenticated computers can register or change their records"], 2, "Secure updates tie record changes to directory authentication, which keeps the zone’s contents accountable.")], recap: ["Forward and reverse zones are AD-integrated with a deliberate replication scope.", "Dynamic updates are secure-only.", "Zone transfers stay restricted; directory replication moves the data."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the record set your services will actually use.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#zones", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:records", title: "Create and maintain service records", minutes: 20, summary: "Build a small, intentional record set. A host record identifies a machine; an alias provides a stable service name without making that alias a separate server.", objectives: ["Distinguish host records (A and PTR) from service aliases (CNAME).", "Create a host record with its PTR and a service alias that match the plan.", "Maintain records with recorded ownership, lifetimes, and reasons."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.1, the DC01 + DC02 identity build, with both domain controllers healthy. Both were promoted with the DNS server option selected, and SBD-DC02 still uses SBD-DC01 for DNS.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server practice environment, and work at a domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and nothing is forwarded outside."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Review the DC A and PTR records first. Correct mismatches through the owning host and DNS administration process rather than blindly adding another record with the same name."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Choose one approved service host from your address plan. In sbd.test, use New Host (A or AAAA), enter its actual name and address, and select the associated PTR option when the reverse zone is ready."), LessonSection("Step 3", "For a service alias, use New Alias (CNAME) and enter the intended fully qualified host. For the course 1.6 PKI, create pki.sbd.test as an alias for SBD-PKI-MGMT01.sbd.test, the internal publication host that course builds at 10.0.30.30; the alias answers with an address once that host exists, and it never points at the offline root."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Set the record lifetime to the operational plan, record static ownership, and remove only records confirmed obsolete. An alias cannot coexist with other record types at the same name."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DnsServerResourceRecord -ComputerName SBD-DC01 -ZoneName sbd.test\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Type A -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName 10.0.10.10 -Type PTR -Server 10.0.10.10\n# The alias course 1.6 relies on; its target is built in course 1.6\nAdd-DnsServerResourceRecordCName -ComputerName SBD-DC01 -ZoneName sbd.test -Name pki -HostNameAlias SBD-PKI-MGMT01.sbd.test\nResolve-DnsName pki.sbd.test -Type CNAME -Server 10.0.10.11   # after replication: SBD-PKI-MGMT01.sbd.test"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Forward and reverse responses agree with the address plan. Each alias names the intended host (pki.sbd.test returns an address only once course 1.6 builds SBD-PKI-MGMT01), and creating a DNS name alone does not configure its service."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep a record table with owner, type, target, time to live, and reason for the change."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage DNS resource records\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/manage-resource-records")], question: "What does creating pki.sbd.test as an alias accomplish?", answers: ["It publishes every certificate automatically", "It connects the offline root to the network", "It points a service name at the selected host"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "DNS supplies name resolution. The publication service, files, access, and certificate URLs still need configuration.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A CNAME already exists at a name. What else can live at that same name?", ["Any A record", "No other record types", "A second CNAME"], 1, "An alias cannot coexist with other record types at the same name — one reason service aliases are planned deliberately."), QuizQuestion("Forward and reverse answers disagree for a domain controller. What is the right fix?", ["Correct it through the owning host and the DNS administration process", "Add another record with the same name", "Delete the reverse zone"], 0, "Mismatches get fixed at the owner, not papered over with duplicates that make troubleshooting harder.")], recap: ["Host records identify machines; aliases provide stable service names.", "pki.sbd.test is reserved for the internal publication host, never the offline root.", "Every record carries an owner, a lifetime, and a reason."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 proves that both resolvers and directory discovery actually work.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#records", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:verify", title: "Prove resolution and directory discovery", minutes: 30, summary: "Test each server explicitly before testing client fallback. A successful cached answer does not prove that both DNS servers hold the right records, and a correct DNS answer proves neither a successful sign-in nor access to a protected resource.", objectives: ["Query each DNS server explicitly instead of trusting cached answers.", "Verify the locator (SRV) records that let clients discover domain controllers.", "Run a controlled failover test and record the result.", "Optionally capture a locator query and compare it with the model's packet card."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.1, the DC01 + DC02 identity build, with both domain controllers healthy. Both were promoted with the DNS server option selected, and SBD-DC02 still uses SBD-DC01 for DNS.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server practice environment, and work at a domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and nothing is forwarded outside."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From a domain client or either controller, query each DNS server by address for both controllers' host records, so each result tells you which server answered. Expect SBD-DC01.sbd.test at 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02.sbd.test at 10.0.10.11 from both servers."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Query the domain controller locator service (SRV) records against both servers. These records let a client discover domain controllers; an ordinary host lookup does not prove discovery works, and a locator answer does not by itself prove that every listed controller is reachable."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Run the DNS diagnostics against every domain controller, then the replication summary. Classify root-hint, forwarder, and delegation findings against the documented isolated design instead of treating them as faults, and investigate everything else."), LessonSection("Step 4", "During an approved lab test, make one DNS server unavailable to the test client and verify resolution through the other. Restore connectivity and repeat for the opposite server. Do not interrupt shared production services for this exercise."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Optional: see the locator query on the wire. On SBD-DC02, in an elevated PowerShell window, capture port 53 with Packet Monitor (pktmon), which is built into Windows Server, while you query SBD-DC01, so the request crosses the network. Expect a query from 10.0.10.11 to 10.0.10.10 on UDP port 53 and an answer naming both controllers on port 389. Read the text file, or open the .pcapng file in a packet analyzer such as Wireshark, and keep the capture with your evidence; it holds only the traffic the filter allowed."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "foreach (\$server in '10.0.10.10','10.0.10.11') {\n  Write-Output \"DNS server: \$server\"\n  Resolve-DnsName 'SBD-DC01.sbd.test' -Type A -Server \$server\n  Resolve-DnsName 'SBD-DC02.sbd.test' -Type A -Server \$server\n  Resolve-DnsName '_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test' -Type SRV -Server \$server\n}\ndcdiag /test:DNS /e /v\nrepadmin /replsummary\n# Optional, on SBD-DC02: capture the locator query\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence -Force\npktmon filter remove\npktmon filter add DNS -p 53\npktmon start --capture --comp nics --pkt-size 0 --file-name C:\\Evidence\\dc-locator.etl\nResolve-DnsName '_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test' -Type SRV -Server 10.0.10.10\npktmon stop\npktmon etl2txt C:\\Evidence\\dc-locator.etl --out C:\\Evidence\\dc-locator.txt\npktmon etl2pcap C:\\Evidence\\dc-locator.etl --out C:\\Evidence\\dc-locator.pcapng\npktmon filter remove"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both DNS servers return both controllers' host records and the domain controller locator records, the DCDiag DNS findings are explained, replication is healthy, and the client still resolves internal names when either resolver is unavailable. If you ran the capture, it shows the query from 10.0.10.11 and the answer from 10.0.10.10."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep paired lookup results with the server queried, the DCDiag and replication output, the failover test record with start, restore, and final verification times, any capture file, and a short statement of what the results do and don't establish: DNS helps users find the identity service, but a correct answer proves nothing about authentication."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Locating domain controllers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/dc-locator\n\nMicrosoft · Resolve-DnsName\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dnsclient/resolve-dnsname\n\nMicrosoft · Dcdiag\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag\n\nMicrosoft · Packet Monitor (pktmon)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/pktmon")], question: "Why query each DNS server directly?", answers: ["To distinguish a healthy pair from a single working resolver or cached answer", "To permanently bypass the client's DNS configuration", "To generate a certificate request"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Explicit server queries expose differences between resolvers before client caching and fallback obscure the result.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which record type lets a client discover domain controllers?", ["PTR records in the reverse zone", "CNAME aliases such as pki.sbd.test", "SRV records such as _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test"], 2, "Service (SRV) records advertise the directory service. An ordinary host lookup does not prove discovery works."), QuizQuestion("During the failover exercise, what belongs in the evidence record?", ["Only a screenshot of one successful lookup", "Start, restore, and final verification times with the paired lookup results", "The DNS service account password"], 1, "A dated failover record shows redundancy was proven, not assumed — and secrets never belong in evidence."), QuizQuestion("What does a successful domain controller locator query establish?", ["Every authentication control is satisfied", "The queried server returned the requested service records", "The system is authorized to operate"], 1, "The query demonstrates one DNS response. Reachability, authentication, control effectiveness, and authorization need their own evidence and decisions."), QuizQuestion("A ping to 10.0.10.10 succeeds. What has it proved?", ["That DNS service discovery works", "That sign-in will succeed", "Only that the address answered"], 2, "Ping confirms limited IP reachability. It says nothing about service discovery, directory replication, or authentication.")], recap: ["Both resolvers answer the host and SRV queries correctly.", "dcdiag and repadmin confirm DNS and replication health, with isolated-design findings explained.", "The failover test is documented with times, not just a passing screenshot."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 practices finding and recording a DNS fault, one layer at a time.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#verify", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dns-configuration:troubleshoot", title: "Find the fault and record the fix", minutes: 15, summary: "Troubleshoot one layer at a time: client settings, server reachability, authoritative data, replication, and finally the application that uses the name.", objectives: ["Isolate a DNS failure one layer at a time.", "Distinguish a missing name (NXDOMAIN) from timeouts and service failures.", "Record the cause, the fix, and the rollback — not just a passing result."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.1, the DC01 + DC02 identity build, with both domain controllers healthy. Both were promoted with the DNS server option selected, and SBD-DC02 still uses SBD-DC01 for DNS.\n\nUse an isolated Windows Server practice environment, and work at a domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nContinue the existing sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 is 10.0.10.10 and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11 on 10.0.10.0/24.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The addresses and names below continue the training example. Match every change to your own address plan. This network has no internet DNS dependency: names resolve on the domain controllers, and nothing is forwarded outside."), LessonSection("Step 1", "For a failing lookup, record the exact name, record type, client, resolver, timestamp, and error. Compare the fully qualified name with any short-name search behavior."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Query both servers directly. If they disagree, inspect zone scope and replication. If both are correct but the client is not, inspect client DNS settings and cached data."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Distinguish a missing name (NXDOMAIN) from a timeout or service failure. Fix the owning configuration first, then use Clear-DnsClientCache on the test client if stale cache remains relevant."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Repeat the original test and the redundancy checks. Record what changed and the rollback method. Review aging/scavenging separately against DHCP, static records, and backup practices before enabling automatic deletion."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DnsClientServerAddress -AddressFamily IPv4\nGet-DnsClientCache\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.11"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The original failure is reproducibly resolved, both resolvers agree, and the change record explains the cause rather than only showing a passing screenshot."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Complete the DNS checklist and retain the before/after lookup results with the configuration owner."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage DNS resource records\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/manage-resource-records\n\nMicrosoft · Resolve-DnsName\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dnsclient/resolve-dnsname")], question: "Both DNS servers return the correct address, but one client keeps using the old address. What should you inspect next?", answers: ["The Root CA private key", "The client resolver configuration and cache", "Every zone's transfer policy at once"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Narrow the investigation to the failing client before changing otherwise consistent authoritative data.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A lookup returns NXDOMAIN from both servers. What does that tell you?", ["The name does not exist in the authoritative data", "The DNS service is stopped", "The client cache is corrupted"], 0, "NXDOMAIN is an authoritative answer that the name is missing — a different problem from a timeout or an unreachable service."), QuizQuestion("When is Clear-DnsClientCache the right step?", ["As the first step for every DNS problem", "After the owning configuration is fixed, when stale cached data remains relevant", "Instead of checking the authoritative servers"], 1, "Clearing the cache hides symptoms if the authoritative data is still wrong. Fix the owner first, then clear what is stale.")], recap: ["Troubleshoot client settings, reachability, authoritative data, replication, then the application.", "NXDOMAIN, timeouts, and service failures point at different owners.", "The change record explains the cause and the rollback."], nextStep: "That completes Configure DNS. Course 1.3, Configure domain time, makes SBD-DC01 the domain's time source and proves every system follows it.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dns-configuration#troubleshoot", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "domain-time", number: "1.3", title: "Configure domain time", subtitle: "The Windows Time hierarchy, the PDC emulator & chrony", category: "Network build", description: "Give the air-gapped domain one clock it can trust: make SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, the reliable source serving its own clock and check it against an approved reference, keep SBD-DC02, Windows members and Linux servers on the hierarchy, and prove the chain with measured offsets.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "domain-time-guide", title: "Configure domain time · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "The Windows Time hierarchy, the PDC emulator & chrony", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "domain-time-package", title: "Configure domain time · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time/SecureByDesign-domain-time-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:domain-time:time-plan", title: "Plan the domain's time", minutes: 25, summary: "Every sign-in, log entry and certificate in this network depends on clocks that agree, and an air-gapped network has no internet time to lean on. Learn why time matters here and how the Windows Time hierarchy carries one clock from SBD-DC01 to every system, then write down the reference, the fallback and the service path you will hold the domain to.", objectives: ["Explain why Kerberos, log correlation, certificates and replication need clocks that agree.", "Trace the Windows Time hierarchy from the forest root's PDC emulator to every member.", "Record the reference, the fallback and the service path the domain's time is held to, and what verification must show."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1 and 1.2: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, and DNS verified on both, so every member can find a domain controller by name.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account — until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens — and have access to the hypervisor settings for both controller VMs.\n\nContinue the sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 (10.0.10.10) holds the PDC emulator role and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11, both on VLAN 10.\n\nHave an approved time reference that never connects to this network, such as a GPS receiver's display, and a log to record each check.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This network has no internet time. SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, serves time from its own clock, an administrator checks that clock against an approved reference, and everything else follows the domain hierarchy. For the member checks, use a domain-joined Windows test client or member server if you have one (SBD-DC02 shows the same chain if not); the Linux settings in lesson 3 are filed now and applied by the courses that build each Linux server."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Start with why the clocks must agree. Kerberos refuses a request whose timestamp differs from the domain controller's clock by more than the domain's tolerance — five minutes by default — so drift turns into failed sign-ins and failed replication. The logging courses (1.10–1.12) line events up across systems by their timestamps, certificates from the course 1.6 PKI are valid only between two fixed times, and domain controllers need agreeing clocks to replicate accurately."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Learn the hierarchy that carries one clock everywhere. The PDC emulator of the forest root domain is at the top; every other domain controller takes its time from the PDC emulator, and every member server and workstation from the domain controller that authenticated it — the NT5DS client type every domain member uses by default. sbd.test is the forest's only domain, so SBD-DC01 is the top and SBD-DC02 follows it, and each Windows member's time arrives signed with its computer's Net Logon session key."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Face the air gap. With no internet or outside NTP server, SBD-DC01 serves time from its own clock and is the reference by declaration, so name an approved reference that never connects to this network, such as a GPS receiver's display, and write down who compares SBD-DC01 with it, when — after any restart, restore or revert of SBD-DC01 included — and how a correction is made and recorded. Record the fallback too: if SBD-DC01 is lost for long, SBD-DC02 eventually stops advertising as a time source, so the PDC emulator role moves to it and it becomes the source (lesson 5)."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Decide what verification will show. Every system should name the source the hierarchy gives it, and its measured offset from that source should be small and stay stable from one reading to the next. Five minutes is where Kerberos fails, not a target: an offset that grows or jumps is the warning to act on long before that."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Confirm the service path. Rule F09 in Inside the Network permits every domain member to both controllers on UDP 123, and each controller's inbound firewall rule for the Windows Time service accepts it; SBD-DC02's own requests to SBD-DC01 stay inside VLAN 10, where only the host firewalls see them. SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9) is not a domain member, so F09 does not list it yet; course 1.9 adds it."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADForest | Select-Object RootDomain, Domains       # sbd.test: the forest root and its only domain\nGet-ADDomain | Select-Object PDCEmulator               # expected: SBD-DC01.sbd.test\n# The domain's Kerberos tolerance, from the Default Domain Policy (expected: MaxClockSkew = 5, in minutes)\nSelect-String -Pattern MaxClockSkew -Path \"\\\\sbd.test\\SYSVOL\\sbd.test\\Policies\\{31B2F340-016D-11D2-945F-00C04FB984F9}\\MACHINE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\SecEdit\\GptTmpl.inf\"\n# Each controller's inbound UDP 123 rule, and where each takes its time today\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01,SBD-DC02 {\n  Get-NetFirewallPortFilter -PolicyStore ActiveStore | Where-Object { \$_.Protocol -eq 'UDP' -and \$_.LocalPort -contains '123' } |\n    Get-NetFirewallRule | Select-Object DisplayName, Direction, Enabled, Action\n  w32tm /query /source }"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The plan names the forest root and its PDC emulator, the hierarchy each system follows, the approved reference with who checks it and when, the verification each system must show, the fallback, the Kerberos tolerance read from the Default Domain Policy, and rule F09 with each controller's inbound UDP 123 rule."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the time plan with the output behind it: the forest root and PDC emulator, the reference and checking procedure, the fallback, MaxClockSkew, rule F09, both controllers' UDP 123 firewall rules, and each controller's time source before you change anything."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Time service\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-top\n\nMicrosoft · How the Windows Time service works\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/how-the-windows-time-service-works\n\nMicrosoft · How to configure an authoritative time server in Windows Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/configure-authoritative-time-server\n\nMicrosoft · Kerberos authentication troubleshooting guidance\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/kerberos-authentication-troubleshooting-guidance")], question: "The domain tolerates five minutes of clock difference. Why not treat five minutes as the target?", answers: ["Kerberos fails well before five minutes", "Five minutes is where sign-ins fail; small, stable offsets show every clock following its source, and keep events from different systems in order", "w32tm can't measure a larger offset"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Kerberos's tolerance is the failure point, not a target. Watching for offsets that stay small and stable gives warning while there is time to act, and timelines you can trust in the logging courses.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("In the default hierarchy, where does SBD-DC02 get its time?", ["From the PDC emulator, SBD-DC01", "From its hypervisor host", "From whichever member authenticated last"], 0, "Domain controllers take time from the PDC emulator and members from a domain controller. The forest root's PDC emulator takes it from its own source — here, its own clock."), QuizQuestion("Why can't a Linux server get the signed time that Windows members get?", ["The controllers refuse Linux clients", "Linux can't use UDP 123", "Windows signs domain time with each computer's Net Logon session key, a Microsoft extension chrony doesn't use as a client"], 2, "Linux servers take unsigned NTP from the same controllers. That is one reason the time path stays narrow: two controllers, one rule, UDP 123.")], recap: ["Kerberos fails past five minutes; small, stable offsets on every link show drift long before that.", "One hierarchy: SBD-DC01, then SBD-DC02, then every member, with Linux servers pointed at both controllers.", "The reference, its checks, the fallback and rule F09 are written down before anything changes."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 makes SBD-DC01 the reliable source and takes the hypervisor out of both controllers' time.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time#time-plan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:domain-time:time", title: "Make SBD-DC01 the domain's time source", minutes: 30, summary: "With no outside time to lean on, the domain's clock is whatever SBD-DC01 says, so make that a deliberate, checked configuration. Take the hypervisor out of both controllers' time, mark SBD-DC01 — the PDC emulator — as the reliable source serving its own clock with no external peer, and run the first check against the approved reference.", objectives: ["Configure SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the domain's reliable time source.", "Turn off hypervisor time synchronization for both domain controller VMs.", "Run and record the first check of SBD-DC01's clock against the approved reference."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1 and 1.2: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, and DNS verified on both, so every member can find a domain controller by name.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account — until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens — and have access to the hypervisor settings for both controller VMs.\n\nContinue the sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 (10.0.10.10) holds the PDC emulator role and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11, both on VLAN 10.\n\nHave an approved time reference that never connects to this network, such as a GPS receiver's display, and a log to record each check.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This network has no internet time. SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, serves time from its own clock, an administrator checks that clock against an approved reference, and everything else follows the domain hierarchy. For the member checks, use a domain-joined Windows test client or member server if you have one (SBD-DC02 shows the same chain if not); the Linux settings in lesson 3 are filed now and applied by the courses that build each Linux server."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Confirm which domain controller holds the PDC emulator role — SBD-DC01 in this sample. The PDC emulator of the forest root domain sits at the top of the Windows Time hierarchy, so it is the one system you configure; everything else follows it."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization for both controller VMs — SBD-DC01 on SBD-HV01 and SBD-DC02 on SBD-HV02 — so no host clock can silently override the PDC emulator. On Hyper-V that is the Time synchronization integration service; other hypervisors have an equivalent guest-tools setting, and some also set the guest clock after a resume or restore, so check those settings too. Inside each guest, w32tm /query /source must no longer name a hypervisor provider such as VM IC Time Synchronization Provider."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On SBD-DC01, mark Windows Time as a reliable time source and restart the service. w32tm /config /reliable:yes writes AnnounceFlags 5 — always a time server (0x1) and always a reliable one (0x4) — in place of the automatic default, 10 (0x2 and 0x8). Leave the client type at NT5DS and add no manual peer: the forest root's PDC emulator has nothing above it, so it serves its own clock, and clients accept time from a server that synchronizes with nothing only because it is marked reliable."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Read what SBD-DC01 now advertises: source Local CMOS Clock, stratum 1, reference ID LOCL, and a root dispersion of 10 seconds — the default LocalClockDispersion for a local clock, which you leave alone (lesson 3 sets the Linux servers to accept it). Expect Time-Service warning 12, which says the forest root's PDC emulator has nothing above it and will act as the authoritative source: that is the designed state, so record it rather than fix it. nltest /dsgetdc:sbd.test /gtimeserv should return SBD-DC01 with the GTIMESERV flag, the reliable time server Net Logon now advertises."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Run the first reference check with lesson 1's procedure: read the approved reference and SBD-DC01's clock at the same moment and record both. If they differ, correct SBD-DC01 by the measured difference (Set-Date -Adjust), never by typing a time from memory; Security event 4616 records the change, and your log records who made it and why."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADDomain | Select-Object PDCEmulator   # expected: SBD-DC01.sbd.test\n# Hyper-V example (other hypervisors have an equivalent setting). On SBD-HV01, and the same for SBD-DC02 on SBD-HV02:\nDisable-VMIntegrationService -VMName SBD-DC01 -Name 'Time Synchronization'\nGet-VMIntegrationService -VMName SBD-DC01 -Name 'Time Synchronization'   # Enabled: False\n# On SBD-DC01 (the PDC emulator): the reliable source, serving its own clock\nw32tm /config /reliable:yes /update\nRestart-Service w32time\nw32tm /query /configuration   # AnnounceFlags: 5 (Local); NtpClient Type: NT5DS (Local)\nw32tm /query /source          # Local CMOS Clock\nw32tm /query /status          # Stratum: 1, ReferenceId: LOCL, Root Dispersion: 10s\nnltest /dsgetdc:sbd.test /gtimeserv   # DC: \\\\SBD-DC01.sbd.test; Flags include GTIMESERV\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; ProviderName='Microsoft-Windows-Time-Service'; Id=12} -MaxEvents 1 |\n  Format-List TimeCreated, Message\n# Reference check: run this as you read the approved reference, and log both readings\nGet-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fff K'\n# A correction is made only by the measured difference (example: SBD-DC01 two seconds slow):\n# Set-Date -Adjust ([TimeSpan]::FromSeconds(2))"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Hypervisor time synchronization is off for both controller VMs; SBD-DC01 reports AnnounceFlags 5 and NT5DS, source Local CMOS Clock, stratum 1 and reference LOCL; nltest returns it with the GTIMESERV flag; event 12 is recorded as expected; and the first reference check is logged with both readings and any correction."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the hypervisor setting for both controller VMs; SBD-DC01's configuration, source and status output; the nltest result; the event 12 text; and the first reference check — both readings, the difference, any correction with its 4616 event, who and when."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Time service\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-top\n\nMicrosoft · How the Windows Time service works\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/how-the-windows-time-service-works\n\nMicrosoft · Windows Time service tools and settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings\n\nMicrosoft · How to configure an authoritative time server in Windows Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/configure-authoritative-time-server\n\nMicrosoft · Virtualizing domain controllers with Hyper-V\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/virtualized-domain-controllers-hyper-v\n\nMicrosoft · Manage Hyper-V Integration Services\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/manage/manage-hyper-v-integration-services\n\nMicrosoft · Configure the root PDC with an authoritative time source\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/services-hub/microsoft-engage-center/health/remediation-steps-ad/configure-the-root-pdc-with-an-authoritative-time-source-and-avoid-widespread-time-skew")], question: "Which system is the domain's authoritative time source in this design?", answers: ["Every domain member individually", "SBD-DC01, the domain controller holding the PDC emulator role", "The offline root CA"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The PDC emulator of the forest root domain is the top of the Windows Time hierarchy. Marking it reliable makes it the source everything else follows.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does the air gap change about time design?", ["Nothing — pool.ntp.org still works", "Kerberos stops needing accurate time", "There is no internet source, so DC01's clock is the reference by declaration and how it is checked is a documented decision"], 2, "With no external source, accuracy is whatever the lab declares and monitors — which is why the time-stamp controls (AU-8, SC-45) land on the PDC emulator."), QuizQuestion("Why does time matter to authentication at all?", ["Kerberos rejects tickets outside its allowed clock skew, so drifting clocks become sign-in failures", "DNS records expire hourly", "Passwords change with the clock"], 0, "Kerberos builds replay protection on timestamps. Skew beyond the tolerance breaks authentication before anything looks wrong."), QuizQuestion("Why turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization for the domain controller virtual machines?", ["It saves processor time", "Windows Time will not start otherwise", "The host clock would silently override the PDC emulator and fight the domain hierarchy"], 2, "A virtual machine that takes time from its host has two masters. Turning host sync off leaves the domain hierarchy, rooted at the PDC emulator, as the only authority."), QuizQuestion("Where does a domain workstation get its time in this design?", ["From SBD-DC01 only, set by hand on each machine", "From a domain controller over UDP 123, following the domain hierarchy", "From the core router"], 1, "Windows Time follows the domain hierarchy: members sync from a domain controller, the other domain controller from the PDC emulator, and the PDC emulator from its own clock. That is why rule F09 permits UDP 123 from every domain member to both domain controllers.")], recap: ["SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, is the declared source: AnnounceFlags 5, its own clock, no external peer.", "No hypervisor sets either controller's clock.", "The first reference check is logged, and any correction was made by the measured difference."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 brings SBD-DC02, the Windows members and the Linux servers onto this source, and keeps Group Policy out of the way.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time#time", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:domain-time:time-members", title: "Bring every system onto the hierarchy", minutes: 35, summary: "One source is only useful if every other clock follows it and nothing quietly overrides that. Leave SBD-DC02 and the Windows members on the domain hierarchy's defaults, keep Group Policy and hypervisors out of their time, and write the chrony configuration every Linux server in later courses uses to follow both controllers.", objectives: ["Keep SBD-DC02 and every Windows member on the domain hierarchy, with nothing from Group Policy or a hypervisor overriding it.", "Write the chrony configuration every Linux server uses to follow both controllers, and explain maxdistance 16.", "Account for the systems outside the domain hierarchy: SBD-REPO01 and the offline root CA."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1 and 1.2: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, and DNS verified on both, so every member can find a domain controller by name.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account — until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens — and have access to the hypervisor settings for both controller VMs.\n\nContinue the sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 (10.0.10.10) holds the PDC emulator role and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11, both on VLAN 10.\n\nHave an approved time reference that never connects to this network, such as a GPS receiver's display, and a log to record each check.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This network has no internet time. SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, serves time from its own clock, an administrator checks that clock against an approved reference, and everything else follows the domain hierarchy. For the member checks, use a domain-joined Windows test client or member server if you have one (SBD-DC02 shows the same chain if not); the Linux settings in lesson 3 are filed now and applied by the courses that build each Linux server."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Leave SBD-DC02 and every Windows member on the defaults: client type NT5DS, no manual peer list, and on SBD-DC02 the automatic AnnounceFlags 10, so it serves time without claiming to be the reliable source. w32tm /query /configuration shows each value with where it came from, (Local) or (Policy), and a system someone once set by hand returns with w32tm /config /syncfromflags:domhier /update and a restart of the service."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Keep time out of Group Policy. The Windows Time Service policies (Computer Configuration > Administrative Templates > System > Windows Time Service) overwrite the local W32Time settings, so one that names an NTP server or sets the type to NTP pulls its targets off the hierarchy, and one that reaches SBD-DC01 overrides what makes it the source. Leave them Not Configured in every GPO, the STIG GPOs of courses 1.4–1.5 included: the STIGs expect members on the domain hierarchy, and their rule that the PDC emulator take time from an authorized external source becomes a recorded deviation in course 1.5's exception record."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Keep hypervisors out of every virtual machine's time, as you did for the controllers. When a later course builds a virtual machine, server or client — SBD-ADMIN-PC01, the Tier 2 seat on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, included — turn off its hypervisor time synchronization, so the domain stays the only time authority: a Windows guest whose source reads VM IC Time Synchronization Provider, or any other hypervisor provider, is not following the domain."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the chrony configuration every Linux server uses. In /etc/chrony.conf (Red Hat Enterprise Linux family), remove every pool, server and sourcedir line, add server SBD-DC01.sbd.test iburst, server SBD-DC02.sbd.test iburst and maxdistance 16, and add no allow line, because these servers serve time to no one; then enable and restart chronyd. Domain-joined Linux servers authenticate with Kerberos through SSSD, so the same five-minute limit applies to them."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Record why maxdistance 16 is this path's choice. Serving its own clock, SBD-DC01 advertises 10 seconds of root dispersion and SBD-DC02 a little more, while chrony refuses any source whose root distance is above 3 seconds by default, so without the line chronyc sources marks both controllers ^? (unusable). Sixteen seconds is the largest dispersion NTP defines, so the setting admits both controllers without admitting an unsynchronized source; the time itself is unsigned, which is why F09 limits it to the two controllers."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Apply the same file to every Linux server before its first domain join or package update: SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9), which is not domain-joined, so F09's member list leaves it out until course 1.9 adds it; SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) and SBD-WEB01 (course 1.18) when they are built; and SBD-NESSUS01 when course 1.17, coming soon, arrives. The offline root CA (course 1.6) is the one clock no network reaches: set it against the approved reference before each signing ceremony."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC02, and on a Windows test client or member server if you have one:\nw32tm /query /configuration | Select-String 'AnnounceFlags|Type:'   # SBD-DC02: AnnounceFlags: 10 (Local); Type: NT5DS (Local)\nw32tm /query /configuration | Select-String '\\(Policy\\)'           # expected: no output\nw32tm /query /source                                                # a controller, never a hypervisor provider\n# A system once set by hand returns to the hierarchy:\n#   w32tm /config /syncfromflags:domhier /update; Restart-Service w32time; w32tm /resync /rediscover\n# The Linux chrony file, filed now (/etc/chrony.conf on the Red Hat Enterprise Linux family):\n#   server SBD-DC01.sbd.test iburst\n#   server SBD-DC02.sbd.test iburst\n#   maxdistance 16\n# ...with no pool, other server, sourcedir or allow lines; then:\n#   sudo systemctl enable chronyd; sudo systemctl restart chronyd\n# Each Linux build later checks:\n#   chronyc sources -v     # ^* one controller, ^+ or ^- the other (^? = unusable)\n#   chronyc ntpdata | grep -E '^(Remote address|Stratum|Root dispersion|Authenticated)'\n#   chronyc tracking       # Reference ID: a controller; System time small and stable"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-DC02 shows AnnounceFlags 10 and NT5DS and takes its time from SBD-DC01, a Windows member names a controller, no W32Time value shows (Policy), and the chrony file — both controllers, maxdistance 16 and the reason for it — is filed for courses 1.9, 1.12 and 1.18 to apply."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep SBD-DC02's and the member's configuration and source output, the empty (Policy) search, the rule that no VM takes its time from its host, and the filed chrony configuration with the reason for maxdistance 16 and the systems that will use it."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · How the Windows Time service works\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/how-the-windows-time-service-works\n\nMicrosoft · Windows Time service tools and settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings\n\nMicrosoft · Virtualizing domain controllers with Hyper-V\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/virtualized-domain-controllers-hyper-v\n\nRed Hat · Configuring time synchronization (chrony)\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/configuring_basic_system_settings/configuring-time-synchronization_configuring-basic-system-settings\n\nchrony · chrony.conf(5)\nhttps://chrony-project.org/doc/latest/chrony.conf.html\n\nRFC 5905 · Network Time Protocol Version 4: Protocol and Algorithms Specification\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5905\n\nDISA · STIGs document library\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads")], question: "A member's w32tm /query /configuration shows Type: NTP (Policy). What does that tell you?", answers: ["The member follows the domain hierarchy normally", "Its hypervisor set the value", "A Group Policy object has moved the member off the domain hierarchy, and a local change won't override it"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "(Policy) means the value comes from Group Policy, which overwrites the local W32Time settings. Find the GPO and set its Windows Time policies back to Not Configured.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Without maxdistance 16, chronyc sources marks both controllers ^?. Why?", ["Serving SBD-DC01's own clock, the controllers advertise about 10 seconds of root dispersion, above chrony's 3-second default limit", "The core blocks UDP 123 from Linux servers", "chrony can't talk to a Windows time server"], 0, "The packets arrive; chrony rejects the sources for the error they advertise. Raising the limit to 16 seconds, the largest dispersion NTP defines, admits both controllers and nothing unsynchronized."), QuizQuestion("Why must course 1.9 add SBD-REPO01 to rule F09?", ["It uses a different time protocol", "Rule F09 covers domain members, and SBD-REPO01 is not joined to the domain", "It takes its time from SBD-MGMT01"], 1, "F09 was written for domain members. Course 1.9 adds SBD-REPO01 to it with an owner and a reason, and gives it the same chrony file as every other Linux server.")], recap: ["SBD-DC02 and the Windows members run on defaults, and nothing shows (Policy).", "Every VM takes its time from the domain, never from its host.", "One chrony file — both controllers, maxdistance 16 — serves every Linux server, SBD-REPO01 included."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 proves the chain: each link's source, offsets that are small and stable, and the events worth keeping.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time#time-members", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:domain-time:time-verify", title: "Prove the time chain", minutes: 30, summary: "A source you configured is a claim until every link answers to it. Read each system's source and status, measure its offsets and confirm they are small and stable, and keep the events and readings that let an assessor see the time chain for themselves.", objectives: ["Confirm each link's source and stratum, from SBD-DC01 down to a member.", "Measure controller and member offsets and confirm they are small and stable.", "Keep the Time-Service events and readings that make up the time evidence record."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1 and 1.2: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, and DNS verified on both, so every member can find a domain controller by name.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account — until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens — and have access to the hypervisor settings for both controller VMs.\n\nContinue the sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 (10.0.10.10) holds the PDC emulator role and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11, both on VLAN 10.\n\nHave an approved time reference that never connects to this network, such as a GPS receiver's display, and a log to record each check.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This network has no internet time. SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, serves time from its own clock, an administrator checks that clock against an approved reference, and everything else follows the domain hierarchy. For the member checks, use a domain-joined Windows test client or member server if you have one (SBD-DC02 shows the same chain if not); the Linux settings in lesson 3 are filed now and applied by the courses that build each Linux server."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Read each link's source and status, from the top down. SBD-DC01 reports Local CMOS Clock at stratum 1, SBD-DC02 reports SBD-DC01.sbd.test at stratum 2, and a member reports a controller at stratum 2 or 3; a member showing Local CMOS Clock, Free-running System Clock or a hypervisor provider is not following the domain (lesson 5)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Measure the controllers against each other: w32tm /monitor queries each controller in the domain, marks the PDC emulator, and reports every other controller's NTP offset from it. SBD-DC02's offset should be small and stay small from one reading to the next; the ICMP line is only a ping, and the NTP offset is the measurement."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Measure a member from the member itself: w32tm /stripchart against SBD-DC01 takes timed samples of its offset, and five that are small and close to one another show a member following its source. On a Linux server later, chronyc tracking reports the same measure as System time, beside a root dispersion of about 10 seconds that is the controllers' advertised error, not an offset."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Keep the Time-Service events that tell the chain's story, from the System log: 12 on SBD-DC01 (it serves as the authoritative source), 35 and 37 on the followers (a source chosen, valid time arriving), and 36, 129 and 142 as warnings worth an alert (no synchronization for a long period, no domain source found, a controller no longer advertising as a time source). Security event 4616 records every change to a clock, your reference corrections included; list these for the event set course 1.10 collects."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the dated evidence record: for each system, its source, stratum and measured offsets, plus its time zone, since audit time stamps must be in UTC or carry their offset; then the event extracts, the reference check log, and who ran each check. Repeat the offset checks after any change to a controller."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "New-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\time -Force | Out-Null\n\$f = \"C:\\Evidence\\time\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME-\$(Get-Date -Format yyyyMMdd)\"\n# On SBD-DC01, SBD-DC02 and a member:\nw32tm /query /source | Out-File \"\$f-source.txt\"\nw32tm /query /status | Out-File \"\$f-status.txt\"\nw32tm /tz | Out-File \"\$f-tz.txt\"\n# On a controller: each controller's NTP offset from the PDC emulator\nw32tm /monitor /domain:sbd.test | Out-File \"\$f-monitor.txt\"\n# On the member: five timed samples against SBD-DC01\nw32tm /stripchart /computer:SBD-DC01.sbd.test /samples:5 /dataonly | Out-File \"\$f-stripchart.txt\"\n# The Time-Service events to keep, and every clock change (4616)\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; ProviderName='Microsoft-Windows-Time-Service'; Id=12,35,36,37,129,142} -MaxEvents 50 |\n  Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, Message | Export-Csv \"\$f-time-events.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4616} -MaxEvents 20 |\n  Select-Object TimeCreated, Message | Export-Csv \"\$f-4616.csv\" -NoTypeInformation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every link reports the expected source and stratum, w32tm /monitor shows a small offset between SBD-DC02 and the PDC emulator, the member's five samples against SBD-DC01 are small and stable, the event extract holds the designed events with every warning explained, and the reference check log is kept with them."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the dated source, status, time zone, monitor and stripchart files from C:\\Evidence\\time, the Time-Service and 4616 extracts, the reference check log, and a one-page summary of each system's source beside the offsets measured for it."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · How the Windows Time service works\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/how-the-windows-time-service-works\n\nMicrosoft · Windows Time service tools and settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings\n\nMicrosoft · w32tm /resync fails because no time data was available\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/error-message-run-w32tm-resync-no-time-data-available\n\nMicrosoft · Event 142: The time service has stopped advertising as a time source\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/w32time-event-142-time-service-stopped-advertising\n\nMicrosoft · Configure the root PDC with an authoritative time source\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/services-hub/microsoft-engage-center/health/remediation-steps-ad/configure-the-root-pdc-with-an-authoritative-time-source-and-avoid-widespread-time-skew\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "w32tm /query /source on a member returns Free-running System Clock. What does that mean?", answers: ["The member has become a reliable source", "The member isn't synchronizing from the domain; it is running on its own clock", "The member is synchronized with SBD-DC01"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A member should name a domain controller. Its own clock as the source means the hierarchy failed for this system, and lesson 5 finds out why.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("w32tm /monitor shows SBD-DC02 at +0.0021s. What has that proved?", ["That every member is synchronized", "That SBD-DC01 agrees with the approved reference", "That SBD-DC02 agreed with the PDC emulator to about two milliseconds at that moment"], 2, "Each measurement proves one link at one time. Members need their own samples, and SBD-DC01's accuracy comes only from the reference check."), QuizQuestion("Which record shows that someone changed SBD-DC01's clock by hand?", ["Security event 4616, alongside the reference check log", "Time-Service event 37", "The w32tm /monitor output"], 0, "Event 4616 records each change to the system time and the account that made it. The log adds why: the readings, the difference and who decided to correct it.")], recap: ["Each link names the right source: Local CMOS Clock at the top, a controller everywhere else.", "Offsets are measured and shown small and stable, not assumed.", "Sources, offsets, events and the reference log form one dated record."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 finds and fixes time faults — skew, a member off its source, a moved role, a restored VM — and records the fix.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time#time-verify", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:domain-time:time-troubleshoot", title: "Find the time fault and record the fix", minutes: 30, summary: "Time faults rarely look like time: a refused sign-in, a replication error, a certificate that is suddenly not yet valid. Measure the offset first, find where the hierarchy broke — a member off its source, a moved PDC emulator role, a restored or reverted VM — then fix the cause, resync safely and record what happened.", objectives: ["Recognize clock skew behind Kerberos, replication and certificate errors, and measure it before changing anything.", "Return a member to the hierarchy, and move the time source with the PDC emulator role.", "Resync safely after a restore or revert, and record the fix with its rollback."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1 and 1.2: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, and DNS verified on both, so every member can find a domain controller by name.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account — until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens — and have access to the hypervisor settings for both controller VMs.\n\nContinue the sbd.test example: SBD-DC01 (10.0.10.10) holds the PDC emulator role and SBD-DC02 is 10.0.10.11, both on VLAN 10.\n\nHave an approved time reference that never connects to this network, such as a GPS receiver's display, and a log to record each check.\n\nThe network's routing and VLAN segmentation are already in place, with a documented service-path register."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This network has no internet time. SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, serves time from its own clock, an administrator checks that clock against an approved reference, and everything else follows the domain hierarchy. For the member checks, use a domain-joined Windows test client or member server if you have one (SBD-DC02 shows the same chain if not); the Linux settings in lesson 3 are filed now and applied by the courses that build each Linux server."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Recognize skew by its symptoms. Kerberos errors that name time — KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW, or 'There is a time and/or date difference between the client and server' at sign-in — access-denied errors in replication between the controllers, and certificates reported as not yet valid all point at a clock; if the controllers audit Kerberos failures, Security event 4771 with failure code 0x25 names the client. Measure before you change anything: w32tm /stripchart from the failing system to SBD-DC01 shows the offset."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Find out why a member left its source. Local CMOS Clock or Free-running System Clock means no usable source, a hypervisor provider means its host's time sync is on, and a peer name means someone set it by hand; a (Policy) value in w32tm /query /configuration points at a GPO, and events 129 and 36 show how long it has been adrift. Practice on the test client or SBD-DC02: set a manual peer with w32tm /config /manualpeerlist:SBD-DC01.sbd.test,0x8 /syncfromflags:manual /update, note that its time is still right but unauthenticated and off the hierarchy, then put it back with /syncfromflags:domhier."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Move the source with the PDC emulator role. When the role moves — a planned transfer, or a seizure after SBD-DC01 is lost — run w32tm /config /reliable:yes /update on the new holder and w32tm /config /syncfromflags:domhier /reliable:no /update on the old one, restart Windows Time on both, move the reference check to the new holder, and confirm that nltest /gtimeserv names it. Otherwise the old holder still advertises itself as the reliable source and the domain's time can keep flowing from a controller that is no longer the PDC emulator; practice only if you can move the role back the same day, because later courses expect SBD-DC01 to hold it."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Treat a restored or reverted VM as a clock problem as well as a directory one. It can come back with the clock it had when it was saved: members correct themselves, a domain controller corrects at most 48 hours each way (MaxPosPhaseCorrection and MaxNegPhaseCorrection) and logs an event instead, and SBD-DC01 has nothing to correct it at all, so every member follows its old time. After any restore or revert of SBD-DC01, compare it with the reference before you trust it."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Resync safely. On Windows, w32tm /resync /rediscover finds the source again and resynchronizes; on Linux, sudo chronyc makestep steps the clock once a controller is selectable. When an offset is beyond what the service will correct, set the clock close by hand first, correct SBD-DC01 only by the measured difference, and never point a system at an outside or manual source to make an error go away."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Record the fix like any other change: the symptom and when it started, the first offset measured, the cause, what you changed, the evidence that the chain is whole again — source, offset and events — and the rollback. A moved role also updates the time plan: who now runs the reference check, and the reverse procedure for when the role returns."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Measure before changing anything (on the failing system)\nw32tm /stripchart /computer:SBD-DC01.sbd.test /samples:3 /dataonly\nw32tm /query /source\nw32tm /query /configuration | Select-String 'Type:|NtpServer:|\\(Policy\\)'\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; ProviderName='Microsoft-Windows-Time-Service'; Id=36,129} -MaxEvents 10 |\n  Format-Table TimeCreated, Id -AutoSize\n# Practice fault, then the fix (test client or SBD-DC02)\nw32tm /config /manualpeerlist:SBD-DC01.sbd.test,0x8 /syncfromflags:manual /update\nw32tm /query /source            # SBD-DC01.sbd.test,0x8: a manual peer, off the hierarchy\nw32tm /config /syncfromflags:domhier /update\nRestart-Service w32time\nw32tm /resync /rediscover\nw32tm /query /source            # a controller again\n# If the PDC emulator role moves to SBD-DC02:\n#   SBD-DC02: w32tm /config /reliable:yes /update; Restart-Service w32time\n#   SBD-DC01: w32tm /config /syncfromflags:domhier /reliable:no /update; Restart-Service w32time\nnltest /dsgetdc:sbd.test /gtimeserv   # must name the current PDC emulator\n# Linux: sudo chronyc makestep, then chronyc tracking"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The practice fault was found from the system's configuration before anything changed, the system names a controller again with Type NT5DS and a small, stable offset, nltest names the PDC emulator as the reliable source, and the record explains the cause rather than only showing a passing result."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the fault record: the symptom and start time, the first offset, the source and configuration output before and after, the events, the change and its rollback, and the final offsets; after a role move, the updated time plan."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Time service tools and settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings\n\nMicrosoft · How to configure an authoritative time server in Windows Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/configure-authoritative-time-server\n\nMicrosoft · Virtualizing domain controllers with Hyper-V\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/virtualized-domain-controllers-hyper-v\n\nMicrosoft · Kerberos authentication troubleshooting guidance\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/windows-security/kerberos-authentication-troubleshooting-guidance\n\nMicrosoft · w32tm /resync fails because no time data was available\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/error-message-run-w32tm-resync-no-time-data-available\n\nRed Hat · Configuring time synchronization (chrony)\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/configuring_basic_system_settings/configuring-time-synchronization_configuring-basic-system-settings")], question: "A member's source reads VM IC Time Synchronization Provider. What is wrong?", answers: ["Nothing: that is the domain hierarchy", "SBD-DC01 has failed", "The member takes its time from its hypervisor host instead of the domain; turn off that VM's host time sync"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "A hypervisor provider means the host's clock, which the domain doesn't control, is setting the member's. Turn off the VM's host time sync and resync from the hierarchy.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The PDC emulator role moved to SBD-DC02. What else must change?", ["SBD-DC02 becomes the reliable source, SBD-DC01 returns to the domain hierarchy, and the reference check moves to SBD-DC02", "Nothing: Windows Time follows the role on its own", "Every member gets SBD-DC02 as a manual peer"], 0, "SBD-DC01 still carries AnnounceFlags 5 until you change it, so it would go on advertising itself as the reliable source. The configuration and the reference check must follow the role."), QuizQuestion("SBD-DC01 was reverted to last week's checkpoint. Why compare it with the reference before anything else?", ["Reverting removes its PDC emulator role", "Nothing corrects the source itself: it serves whatever its clock says, and every member follows it", "Kerberos tickets are stored in the checkpoint"], 1, "Members and SBD-DC02 correct toward SBD-DC01, so its error becomes everyone's. Only the reference check can catch it.")], recap: ["Measure the offset first; a symptom only tells you which clock to look at.", "A member off its source, a moved role and a restored VM each have their own fix.", "Every fix is recorded with cause, evidence and rollback, and the time plan follows the role."], nextStep: "That completes Configure domain time. Course 1.4, Measure against the STIG, scans both controllers — and meets the one rule this design answers with a declared, checked clock.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/domain-time#time-troubleshoot", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "stig-scap", number: "1.4", title: "Measure against the STIG", subtitle: "DISA STIGs, SCAP scans & STIG Viewer checklists", category: "Network build", description: "Stage the DISA SCAP tools and content across the air gap, scan SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 against the Windows Server STIG with SCC, answer the manual checks in STIG Viewer checklists, and hand course 1.5 a classified findings record traced to NIST SP 800-53 controls.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "stig-scap-guide", title: "Measure against the STIG · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "DISA STIGs, SCAP scans & STIG Viewer checklists", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "stig-scap-package", title: "Measure against the STIG · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap/SecureByDesign-stig-scap-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:stig-scap:stig-basics", title: "Understand STIGs, SCAP and checklists", minutes: 25, summary: "A Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) is DISA's configuration standard for one product, and every scan and checklist in this course measures against one. Learn how a rule is identified, rated and traced to NIST SP 800-53, which rules a scanner can read and which a person must answer, then record which STIGs apply to each controller.", objectives: ["Explain how SRGs, STIGs, SCAP benchmarks and CCIs relate.", "Read a STIG rule's identifiers and severity category.", "Record which STIGs apply to each controller, and what a scan can and cannot prove."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS verified, and the domain time design in place with SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the time source.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nHave an approved connected system and the removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded DISA content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course measures before anything changes. It stages the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) tools and content, scans SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 against the Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) with the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), answers the rest in STIG Viewer checklists, and writes the findings record course 1.5 hardens against. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, SCC runs on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01; course 1.8 moves both tools to AdminJumpBox01 and removes them from the controllers. Versions, paths and session folders below are examples — record yours, because course 1.5's rescan must reuse them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Start with the two layers the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) publishes. A Security Requirements Guide (SRG) states requirements for a class of technology, such as general-purpose operating systems; a STIG turns them into checks and fixes for one product, so every STIG rule traces back to an SRG requirement."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Read a rule's identity and weight. Each rule has a Group ID (V-…), a Rule ID (SV-…r…_rule, where the r number is its revision), a STIG ID naming the product and check, and a severity category: CAT I (high) when exploiting the weakness directly and immediately causes loss of confidentiality, integrity or availability, CAT II (medium) when it has the potential to, and CAT III (low) when it degrades the protections against that loss."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Separate what a scanner can read from what a person must answer. A Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) benchmark is the automated part of a STIG — rules written in XCCDF with OVAL checks — which the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC) evaluates on a host. Every other rule is a manual check, and STIG Viewer is where you build a checklist for each assessed asset, import SCC's results and record the human answers."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Trace rules to controls and keep a scan's limits honest. Each rule cites Control Correlation Identifiers (CCIs), DISA's single, actionable requirement statements, each mapped to a NIST SP 800-53 control, so a result can serve as control evidence. A scan shows settings on one host, at one moment, against one release: it can err or miss, it says nothing about procedures, and a pass is not an authorization."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Record what applies. These controllers take the Windows Server STIG (its domain controller rules included) plus the Active Directory Domain and Active Directory Forest STIGs. They also run DNS and Windows Defender Firewall, which have STIGs of their own — list those with an owner and a review date instead of dropping them silently."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem | Select-Object CSName, Caption, Version\n(Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem).DomainRole   # 5 = primary domain controller (holds the PDC emulator role), 4 = backup domain controller\nGet-WindowsFeature AD-Domain-Services, DNS | Select-Object Name, InstallState\nGet-ADForest | Select-Object Name, ForestMode, Domains"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The applicability record lists each controller's operating system and roles, the STIGs that apply with the release you will measure against, and the later-scope STIGs with an owner and a review date."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the applicability record: host, operating system, roles, each applicable STIG with its release, the later-scope STIGs with owner and date, and the command output behind each line."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nDISA · STIGs document library (STIGs, SRGs and the CCI list)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What does a CAT I severity mean?", answers: ["The scanner checks the rule first", "Exploiting the weakness directly and immediately results in loss of confidentiality, integrity or availability", "The rule applies only to domain controllers"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "DISA rates rules by consequence: CAT I is direct and immediate loss, CAT II the potential for loss, and CAT III degraded protection against it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which part of a STIG can SCC evaluate on its own?", ["The rules in the SCAP benchmark — XCCDF rules with OVAL checks that read the host's configuration", "Every rule in every STIG", "Only the CAT I rules"], 0, "The benchmark is the automatable part of the STIG. Manual rules, and whole STIGs such as the Active Directory ones, need a person in STIG Viewer."), QuizQuestion("What does a CCI on a STIG rule give you?", ["The rule's revision number", "The scanner's confidence in its result", "A traceable link from the rule to a NIST SP 800-53 control, so the result can serve as control evidence"], 2, "Each Control Correlation Identifier is one testable statement mapped to a NIST control. It is how a technical result reaches the authorization record.")], recap: ["SRGs set requirements for a technology class; STIGs implement them for one product.", "Every rule has its identifiers, a CAT severity, and CCIs that reach a NIST control.", "SCC measures the benchmark, STIG Viewer holds the human answers, and neither is an authorization."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 carries the DISA content across the air gap: the tools, the benchmarks, the STIGs and the GPO package course 1.5 will import.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap#stig-basics", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:stig-scap:staging", title: "Stage the DISA content across the air gap", minutes: 20, summary: "Everything this course and the next one use is downloaded DISA content: the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), STIG Viewer, the SCAP benchmarks, the STIGs and the Group Policy object (GPO) package. It crosses the boundary the way everything does here — reviewed, hashed, and carried on approved removable media.", objectives: ["Identify the DISA artifacts this course and the courses after it need.", "Move them across the air gap with hashes compared on both sides.", "Record versions so scans stay comparable over time."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS verified, and the domain time design in place with SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the time source.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nHave an approved connected system and the removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded DISA content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course measures before anything changes. It stages the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) tools and content, scans SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 against the Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) with the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), answers the rest in STIG Viewer checklists, and writes the findings record course 1.5 hardens against. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, SCC runs on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01; course 1.8 moves both tools to AdminJumpBox01 and removes them from the controllers. Versions, paths and session folders below are examples — record yours, because course 1.5's rescan must reuse them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On an approved connected system, download from DISA's Cyber Exchange: SCC for Windows and STIG Viewer 3 with their published checksums; the SCAP benchmarks for Windows Server and for the Windows client; the Windows Server, Windows client, Active Directory Domain and Active Directory Forest STIGs and the CCI list from the document library; and the current GPO package. The Windows client content waits for the Tier 2 seat in course 1.8 and the fleet in course 1.13, and the GPO package travels now so course 1.5 imports policies from the same quarterly release as the STIGs you measure; note each file's version and release."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Check SCC and STIG Viewer against DISA's published checksums, then hash every file with Get-FileHash into connected-side-hashes.csv, which travels with the media, and transfer the set with the approved removable-media procedure. No live link to the lab is created for convenience."), LessonSection("Step 3", "In the lab, at SBD-DC01's console, copy the files into C:\\Staging\\DISA and the hash list into C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer, and compare every hash before opening anything; repeat on SBD-DC02 with the same full set, so its comparison is one-to-one as well, although it needs only SCC and the Windows Server benchmark. A mismatch stops the work, exactly as it will in the course 1.6 signing ceremony."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Install SCC on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01, then install the Windows Server benchmark into SCC on each controller. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, administration happens at the controllers' consoles; course 1.8 then moves both tools there and removes them from the controllers. Record SCC's, STIG Viewer's, each benchmark's and each STIG's version, and leave the GPO package (for course 1.5) and the Windows client content hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ChildItem C:\\Staging\\DISA -File | Select-Object Name, Length, LastWriteTime\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer -Force | Out-Null\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Staging\\DISA -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer\\lab-side-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation\n# connected-side-hashes.csv came with the media; no output means every hash matches\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer\\lab-side-hashes.csv).Hash"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every transferred file's hash matches the value recorded on the connected side on both controllers, SCC and STIG Viewer match DISA's published checksums, SCC runs on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01, and every version is written down."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the transfer record in C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer on SBD-DC01: file names, versions and releases, both hash lists, the comparison result on each controller, media custody, and the date."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nDISA · STIGs document library (STIGs, SRGs and the CCI list)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nDISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "Why hash the files on both sides of the transfer?", answers: ["To prove the same files arrived unaltered", "To compress them for the media", "Because SCC refuses unhashed input"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "An independently recorded hash is how a human-controlled transfer proves integrity across the boundary.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which release cadence do the DISA GPO package and STIGs follow?", ["Weekly", "Quarterly", "Only at new Windows releases"], 1, "DISA refreshes STIG content on a quarterly cycle, and course 1.5 sets its standing rescan cadence to follow it."), QuizQuestion("A hash mismatch appears on the lab side. What now?", ["Open the file and inspect it manually", "Stop, set the media aside, and repeat the approved transfer", "Regenerate hashes until they match"], 1, "A mismatch means the file is not the one that was reviewed. Unverified content never gets opened inside the boundary.")], recap: ["SCC, STIG Viewer, the benchmarks, the STIGs and the GPO package come from DISA's Cyber Exchange.", "Hashes are recorded on both sides of the boundary and compared on both controllers.", "Versions are evidence, and the GPO package and client content wait, verified, for later courses."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 measures both controllers before anything is hardened.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap#staging", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:stig-scap:baseline", title: "Capture the pre-hardening baseline", minutes: 25, summary: "Measure before you change. A scan of the freshly built controllers shows the honest starting point, teaches what the operating system already secures by default, and becomes the before picture every later scan is compared against.", objectives: ["Run a SCAP baseline scan against both domain controllers.", "Distinguish automated results from manual-review items.", "Preserve raw results as evidence with hashes and versions."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS verified, and the domain time design in place with SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the time source.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nHave an approved connected system and the removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded DISA content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course measures before anything changes. It stages the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) tools and content, scans SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 against the Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) with the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), answers the rest in STIG Viewer checklists, and writes the findings record course 1.5 hardens against. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, SCC runs on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01; course 1.8 moves both tools to AdminJumpBox01 and removes them from the controllers. Versions, paths and session folders below are examples — record yours, because course 1.5's rescan must reuse them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On each controller's console, run SCC locally against the Windows Server STIG benchmark staged in lesson 2. DISA content offers several mission assurance category (MAC) and confidentiality profiles: use the one your direction calls for, the same on both controllers, and record it. Each scan is a dated session; note SCC's version and the benchmark's version and release with it."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Open the results and read, don't just count. Note which checks already pass: the newest Windows Server release ships with defaults such as required signing on outbound SMB connections and LDAP sealing on new deployments, so part of the STIG is green before you touch anything."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Separate the automated results from the manual checks. SCAP answers what a scanner can read; the manual rules, and any check that returned an error rather than a pass or a fail, go to STIG Viewer for a human answer in lesson 4."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Store the raw results unmodified, with recorded hashes: copy each session into C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\<host name> and hash it, then carry SBD-DC02's set to the evidence folder on SBD-DC01, where its hashes must match again. The baseline is evidence; a cleaned-up copy is a summary, not the record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On each controller, after its SCC scan. Adapt the path to SCC's results directory.\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\baseline -Force | Out-Null\n\$session = Get-ChildItem \"\$env:USERPROFILE\\SCC\\Sessions\" -Directory | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 1\nCopy-Item \$session.FullName -Destination \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME\" -Recurse\nGet-ChildItem \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME\" -Recurse -Filter '*XCCDF-Results*.xml' | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-ChildItem \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME\" -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME-hashes.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\n# On SBD-DC02: carry its set to SBD-DC01. Then, on SBD-DC01, no output means the copy is identical.\nCopy-Item C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\SBD-DC02, C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\SBD-DC02-hashes.csv -Destination '\\\\SBD-DC01\\C\$\\Evidence\\baseline\\' -Recurse\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\SBD-DC02-hashes.csv).Hash (Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\SBD-DC02 -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both controllers have a dated baseline session from the same SCC version, benchmark release and profile; the already-compliant operating system defaults are noted; and both raw result sets sit hashed in C:\\Evidence\\baseline on SBD-DC01, with SBD-DC02's hashes matching after the copy."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the per-host result sets and their hash lists in C:\\Evidence\\baseline, the SCC version, benchmark release and profile, and a short note of what the operating system defaults already covered."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nNIST · National Checklist Program (SP 800-70)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/national-checklist-program\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server (newest release)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "Why scan before applying any GPO?", answers: ["The scanner requires an unhardened system", "A dated before record makes every later change measurable and attributable", "Scanning after GPOs is not possible"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The baseline is the comparison point. Without it, course 1.5's rescan proves only a state, not what your hardening changed.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Several checks pass before any hardening. What is the likely reason on the newest Windows Server release?", ["The scanner is misconfigured", "Earlier lessons secretly applied GPOs", "The OS ships secure defaults, such as required SMB signing, that satisfy those checks"], 2, "The newest Windows Server release raised several security defaults, so a fresh build already meets part of the benchmark."), QuizQuestion("What happens with checks the scanner marks for manual review?", ["They are ignored as not applicable", "They count as failures forever", "A person answers them in STIG Viewer with recorded rationale"], 2, "SCAP automates what a scanner can read; manual checks need a reviewer's observed evidence to be defensible.")], recap: ["The baseline is measured, dated, and stored unmodified.", "Secure-by-default items are recognized, not claimed as your work.", "Manual checks are queued for human review."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 builds the STIG Viewer checklists and answers what the scanner could not.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap#baseline", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:stig-scap:checklists", title: "Build the checklists and answer the manual checks", minutes: 40, summary: "A scan answers only what it can read. In STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01, build a checklist for each controller and one for the directory, let the SCC results fill the automated rules, and answer every remaining rule with what you observed.", objectives: ["Create checklists for both controllers and the directory from the staged STIG releases.", "Import the SCC results and resolve any rules that do not match.", "Answer every remaining rule with a status, finding details and comments."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS verified, and the domain time design in place with SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the time source.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nHave an approved connected system and the removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded DISA content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course measures before anything changes. It stages the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) tools and content, scans SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 against the Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) with the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), answers the rest in STIG Viewer checklists, and writes the findings record course 1.5 hardens against. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, SCC runs on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01; course 1.8 moves both tools to AdminJumpBox01 and removes them from the controllers. Versions, paths and session folders below are examples — record yours, because course 1.5's rescan must reuse them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-DC01, open STIG Viewer, add the staged STIGs to its library, and create three checklists: SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02, each with the Windows Server STIG, and sbd.test with the Active Directory Domain and Active Directory Forest STIGs, whose rules assess the domain and the forest rather than one host. Fill in each controller's target data — host name, fully qualified domain name, address, and the domain controller role."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Import each controller's XCCDF results from C:\\Evidence\\baseline into its own checklist with Import XCCDF or CMRS Results, and record the import summary: rules matched, rules matched with a different revision, and rules not matched. A revision mismatch means the rule changed between the benchmark and the STIG release in the checklist, so align the releases or review those rules by hand — don't include loosely matched results blindly."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Work every rule still Not Reviewed — the manual checks, the whole sbd.test checklist, and anything the scan could not evaluate. Read the check text, look at the system, and set Open, Not A Finding, or Not Applicable. Write what you observed in Finding Details (the command or screen and what it showed) and the context in Comments (who reviewed, when, and any related decision)."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Change an automated result only with evidence in Finding Details, and never lower a severity to improve the numbers: STIG Viewer requires an override reason because an assessor will read it. Save the checklists in STIG Viewer's CKLB format in C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists as SBD-DC01.cklb, SBD-DC02.cklb and sbd.test.cklb, then hash them."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "foreach (\$t in 'SBD-DC01', 'SBD-DC02', 'sbd.test') {\n  \$ckl = Get-Content \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists\\\$t.cklb\" -Raw -Encoding UTF8 | ConvertFrom-Json\n  \$ckl.stigs | Select-Object @{n='Checklist';e={\$t}}, stig_name, version, release_info | Format-Table -AutoSize\n  \$ckl.stigs.rules | Group-Object status | Select-Object @{n='Checklist';e={\$t}}, Name, Count | Format-Table -AutoSize   # expect no not_reviewed\n}\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists -Filter *.cklb | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "All three checklists hold the intended STIG releases and target data, each import matched by exact revision or had its mismatches reviewed, no rule is left Not Reviewed, and every manual answer carries its observation in Finding Details."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the three .cklb files with their hashes, each import's summary counts, the status counts per checklist, and any severity override with its reason."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · SRG/STIG tools (STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nDISA · STIGs document library (STIGs, SRGs and the CCI list)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads")], question: "STIG Viewer's import reports rules that matched with a different revision. What does that tell you?", answers: ["The rule changed between the benchmark and the checklist's STIG release, so align the releases or review those rules by hand", "The scanner found new vulnerabilities", "Those rules passed"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Rule IDs carry a revision. A mismatch means the check text may have changed, so a loosely matched result could describe a different requirement.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("You checked a manual rule and found the controller compliant. What makes Not A Finding defensible?", ["The green status icon", "Finding details recording what you examined and what it showed, such as the command and its output", "A comment that says 'checked'"], 1, "A status without an observation is an assertion. The recorded observation is what another reviewer or an assessor can repeat."), QuizQuestion("Why do the Active Directory Domain and Forest STIGs get their own checklist, sbd.test, instead of one copy per controller?", ["STIG Viewer allows each STIG in only one checklist", "SBD-DC02 is a read-only domain controller", "Their rules assess the domain and the forest the two controllers share, so one set of answers covers both"], 2, "Answering domain-wide rules twice invites two different answers to one question. The directory gets one checklist, and each controller's checklist holds its own host rules.")], recap: ["Two controller checklists and one for the directory, built from the same release as the benchmark.", "Automated results are imported by exact revision; a person answers the rest.", "Every status carries its observation, and no rule is left Not Reviewed."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 turns the three checklists into the findings record course 1.5 works from.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap#checklists", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:stig-scap:findings", title: "Write the findings record for hardening", minutes: 30, summary: "A checklist full of statuses is not yet a decision. Turn the open items from all three checklists into one findings record — counted by severity, classified, owned, and traced from rule to CCI to NIST control — that course 1.5 can harden against and prove.", objectives: ["Count open findings per checklist by severity category.", "Classify every open item as fix by GPO, deviation candidate, or POA&M candidate.", "Trace findings to NIST SP 800-53 controls through their CCIs and hand the record to course 1.5."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.3: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS verified, and the domain time design in place with SBD-DC01, the PDC emulator, as the time source.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens.\n\nHave an approved connected system and the removable-media procedure for bringing downloaded DISA content into the lab."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course measures before anything changes. It stages the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) tools and content, scans SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 against the Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) with the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC), answers the rest in STIG Viewer checklists, and writes the findings record course 1.5 hardens against. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, SCC runs on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01; course 1.8 moves both tools to AdminJumpBox01 and removes them from the controllers. Versions, paths and session folders below are examples — record yours, because course 1.5's rescan must reuse them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Export the open rules from all three checklists into one findings record, C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings\\findings-record.csv, and count them per checklist by severity. Read the CAT I items first: their exploitation directly and immediately causes loss."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Classify every open item with one of three values. Fix by GPO: the DISA package sets it, so course 1.5's import should close it. Deviation candidate: the setting conflicts with a design decision — for example a rule expecting the PDC emulator to synchronize with an authorized external time source, where the course 1.3 design declares and checks its own clock — and course 1.5 decides and records it. POA&M candidate: the fix lies outside Group Policy, such as the PKI server certificate the STIG expects on every domain controller, which the course 1.6 PKI issues, so it needs an owner and a planned date — the start of a Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) entry."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Trace each finding to its controls. A rule's CCIs map to NIST SP 800-53 controls in DISA's CCI list, and STIG Viewer shows them with each rule — CCI-000366, for example, is implementing the security configuration settings under CM-6 — so the record tells an assessor which control each open item weakens."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff course 1.5 works from as C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings\\handoff.txt: SCC's version, the benchmark release and profile, the STIG releases, the GPO package release waiting hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA, and where the checklists and this record live. Then hash the whole folder into C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv. Course 1.5 checks that manifest, rescans with exactly those versions into C:\\Evidence\\hardened, updates copies of the checklists there, and closes or records every item."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "New-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings -Force | Out-Null\n\$rows = foreach (\$t in 'SBD-DC01', 'SBD-DC02', 'sbd.test') {\n  \$ckl = Get-Content \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists\\\$t.cklb\" -Raw -Encoding UTF8 | ConvertFrom-Json\n  \$ckl.stigs.rules | Where-Object status -eq 'open' | Select-Object @{n='Target';e={\$t}}, @{n='GroupId';e={\$_.group_id}}, @{n='StigId';e={\$_.rule_version}}, @{n='Severity';e={\$_.severity}}, @{n='CCIs';e={\$_.ccis -join ' '}}, @{n='Title';e={\$_.rule_title}}, Classification, Owner, TargetDate\n}\n\$rows | Group-Object Target, Severity | Select-Object Name, Count\n\$rows | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings\\findings-record.csv -NoTypeInformation\n# After you fill in Classification, Owner and TargetDate and write handoff.txt, close the baseline:\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\baseline -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The findings record lists every open rule from the three checklists with target, severity and CCIs; each carries one of the three classifications and an owner, POA&M candidates also a planned date; the counts by severity are recorded; and handoff.txt and the manifest give course 1.5 every version, path and hash it reuses."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep findings-record.csv, the counts by severity per checklist, the CCI-to-control notes for the open items, handoff.txt, and C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv with its own hash."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIGs document library (STIGs, SRGs and the CCI list)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "An open rule's setting is in the DISA GPO package. How is it classified?", answers: ["POA&M candidate", "Fix by GPO — course 1.5's import should close it, and its rescan will prove it", "Not Applicable"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "If the package sets it, the fix is policy. The rescan with the same versions shows whether the import actually closed it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why carry each finding's CCIs into the record?", ["They trace the rule to the NIST SP 800-53 control it supports, so findings read as control evidence", "STIG Viewer won't save a checklist without them", "They set the finding's severity"], 0, "The CCI is the bridge between a configuration rule and a control. Without it, an assessor has to rebuild the trail from scratch."), QuizQuestion("What must course 1.5 keep the same when it rescans?", ["Only the host names", "Nothing — newer content is always better", "SCC's version, the benchmark release and the profile, so the comparison measures the hardening rather than a change of content"], 2, "A different measuring stick changes which checks exist and how they evaluate. Matched versions make every change attributable to a policy.")], recap: ["Open items are counted by severity, CAT I first.", "Every open item is fix by GPO, deviation candidate, or POA&M candidate, with an owner.", "The record, the checklists, the versions and the manifest hand straight to course 1.5."], nextStep: "That completes Measure against the STIG. Course 1.5, Harden with Group Policy, builds the organizational units, imports the DISA GPOs you staged, and rescans with the same versions to prove what changed.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/stig-scap#findings", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "harden-baseline", number: "1.5", title: "Harden with Group Policy", subtitle: "OU design, Group Policy processing & DISA GPOs proven by rescan", category: "Network build", description: "Build the organizational units the hardening hangs on, learn exactly how Group Policy decides what applies, import the DISA STIG Group Policy objects course 1.4 staged and link them to SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 by OU, and prove the change with a rescan matched to course 1.4's baseline, updated checklists and an owned exception record.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "harden-guide", title: "Harden with Group Policy · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "OU design, Group Policy processing & DISA GPOs proven by rescan", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "harden-baseline-package", title: "Harden with Group Policy · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline/SecureByDesign-harden-baseline-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:ou-design", title: "Design and build the OU structure", minutes: 20, summary: "Organizational units exist for delegation and Group Policy targeting, not to mirror an org chart. Build the structure the hardening policies will hang on before importing any of them.", objectives: ["Design an OU structure around delegation and Group Policy targeting.", "Create protected OUs and redirect the default Computers and Users containers.", "Explain why the built-in Domain Controllers OU stays untouched."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.4: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time design (course 1.3) in place, and course 1.4's sealed baseline — the DISA GPO package hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA, the raw results, three checklists and findings record under C:\\Evidence\\baseline, and handoff.txt with the versions to reuse.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens, with SCC on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01.\n\nLeave SCC, STIG Viewer and the benchmark at the versions course 1.4 recorded until this course's rescan is filed."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the organizational unit (OU) structure, learns how Group Policy decides what applies, imports the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) that course 1.4 staged, and proves the result with a rescan against course 1.4's baseline. Names, paths and versions below are examples — adapt them, but rescan with exactly the SCC version, benchmark release and profile in handoff.txt. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Design the structure by administration: a top-level SBD OU containing Servers, Workstations, Users, Groups, and an Admin OU for privileged accounts. The built-in Domain Controllers OU stays where it is and keeps its name."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create the planned OUs with accidental-deletion protection enabled. Record each OU's purpose and who will administer its contents; an OU with no delegation or policy purpose is clutter."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Redirect the default containers with redircmp and redirusr: new computer accounts to the Workstations OU and new user accounts to the Users OU. Group Policy cannot be linked to the default containers, so anything landing there would get only domain-level policy, never the OU-linked hardening. Join servers straight into the Servers OU with Add-Computer -OUPath, so they never pass through the workstation policies."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Keep privileged accounts and systems separate: the Admin OU holds the identities that administer the domain controllers. The domain-joined PKI servers from course 1.6 and SBD-DHCP01 from course 1.7 join the Servers OU, and course 1.8 adds a Tier 0 Servers OU and the admin seats' sub-OUs. Do not nest deeper than the delegation plan requires."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "New-ADOrganizationalUnit -Name SBD -Path 'DC=sbd,DC=test' -ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion \$true\n'Servers', 'Workstations', 'Users', 'Groups', 'Admin' | ForEach-Object { New-ADOrganizationalUnit -Name \$_ -Path 'OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion \$true }\nredircmp 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nredirusr 'OU=Users,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\hardened -Force | Out-Null\nGet-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter * -Properties ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName, ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\ou-design.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-ADDomain | Select-Object ComputersContainer, UsersContainer\nGet-ADDomainController -Filter * | Select-Object Name, ComputerObjectDN"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The planned OUs exist with deletion protection, the computer and user redirects point at the Workstations and Users OUs, both controllers are still in the Domain Controllers OU, and each OU has a recorded purpose and administrator."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the OU design sheet: each OU's distinguished name, purpose and delegated administrator, the ou-design.csv export in C:\\Evidence\\hardened, and the redirect commands with their output."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · New-ADOrganizationalUnit\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/activedirectory/new-adorganizationalunit\n\nMicrosoft · Redirect the Users and Computers containers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/redirect-users-computers-containers")], question: "What should drive the shape of the OU structure?", answers: ["Group Policy targeting and delegated administration", "The company org chart", "Alphabetical order of departments"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "OUs are an administrative tool. Policy and delegation boundaries define them; reporting lines belong in HR systems.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why redirect the default Computers container?", ["It makes domain join faster", "Group Policy cannot be linked to the default containers, so new machines would miss the OU-linked hardening", "It is required before DNS works"], 1, "Redirection sends newly joined machines into an OU where policy applies from their first start."), QuizQuestion("Where do the two domain controllers live after this lesson?", ["In the new Servers OU", "In the built-in Domain Controllers OU, unchanged", "In the Users container"], 1, "Domain controllers stay in their built-in OU — it is where the DC hardening policies link in lesson 3.")], recap: ["OUs follow administration, not the org chart.", "The default containers are redirected so nothing joins unmanaged.", "The Domain Controllers OU remains the link point for DC policies."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 explains how Group Policy decides what applies, before you link anything to these OUs.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#ou-design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:gpo-basics", title: "Learn how Group Policy applies", minutes: 30, summary: "Before the DISA settings arrive, learn how a computer decides which Group Policy objects (GPOs) apply and which setting wins. Map the order, build the central store the import will use, and record what both controllers apply today.", objectives: ["Predict which GPO wins from processing order, link order, inheritance, Enforced and Block Inheritance.", "Explain how security filtering, WMI filters and loopback processing change a GPO's reach.", "Build the central store and record each controller's starting policy with gpresult."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.4: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time design (course 1.3) in place, and course 1.4's sealed baseline — the DISA GPO package hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA, the raw results, three checklists and findings record under C:\\Evidence\\baseline, and handoff.txt with the versions to reuse.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens, with SCC on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01.\n\nLeave SCC, STIG Viewer and the benchmark at the versions course 1.4 recorded until this course's rescan is filed."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the organizational unit (OU) structure, learns how Group Policy decides what applies, imports the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) that course 1.4 staged, and proves the result with a rescan against course 1.4's baseline. Names, paths and versions below are examples — adapt them, but rescan with exactly the SCC version, benchmark release and profile in handoff.txt. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Know where a GPO lives. Its settings sit in two halves — a container object in Active Directory and a template folder in SYSVOL — and they replicate separately, by directory replication and by DFS Replication. A change made on SBD-DC01 therefore reaches SBD-DC02 in two steps: before you judge a result there, check that the GPO's AD and SYSVOL version numbers agree."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Learn the order. Policy applies local first, then site, domain and OU, parent OU before child, so the GPO linked closest to the computer is applied last and wins a conflict; at one OU, link order 1 is processed last and wins. Different settings add up across GPOs, but a single setting's values generally don't merge: the highest-precedence GPO that configures it wins, so a user-rights list in one GPO replaces another's instead of adding to it."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Treat the two overrides as exceptions. Block Inheritance on an OU stops GPOs linked above it; Enforced on a link makes that GPO win over everything below it and pierces Block Inheritance. Enforced is not a shortcut — it hides a precedence problem instead of solving it — so this network uses neither unless the exception record says why."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Read what narrows a GPO's reach. Security filtering applies it only to accounts with both Read and Apply group policy (Authenticated Users by default), and computers still need Read on GPOs that apply only to users, because user policy is read in the computer's security context; a WMI filter applies it only where its query is true. Loopback processing gives anyone who signs in to a computer the user settings from that computer's GPOs — merge adds them after the user's own, replace uses only the computer's — so use it only where the computer should decide; lesson 3 meets the one place this course needs it."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Know when policy arrives, and build the central store. Members refresh in the background every 90 minutes with a random offset of up to 30, controllers every five minutes, and gpupdate /force reapplies everything at once, though some extensions run only at startup or sign-in. On SBD-DC01, copy C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions with its en-US folder to \\\\sbd.test\\SYSVOL\\sbd.test\\Policies\\PolicyDefinitions: from then on, every Group Policy editor in the domain uses these templates instead of its local ones."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Record the starting state before anything is imported. On both controllers, save a gpresult report of the Resultant Set of Policy (RSoP), what actually applied: the Default Domain Policy and the Default Domain Controllers Policy should be the only domain GPOs listed. Read the applied and filtered-out lists — the reasons gpresult gives, such as a security denial or a false WMI filter, are how you will troubleshoot the import."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Once, on SBD-DC01: create the central store from the controller's own templates\nCopy-Item C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions -Destination \\\\sbd.test\\SYSVOL\\sbd.test\\Policies\\PolicyDefinitions -Recurse\n# On SBD-DC01: links in precedence order, and each GPO's AD and SYSVOL versions\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'DC=sbd,DC=test'\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Domain Controllers,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nforeach (\$dc in 'SBD-DC01', 'SBD-DC02') { Get-GPO -All -Server \$dc | Select-Object @{n='DC';e={\$dc}}, DisplayName, GpoStatus, @{n='AD';e={\$_.Computer.DSVersion}}, @{n='SYSVOL';e={\$_.Computer.SysvolVersion}} }\n# On each controller's console; carry SBD-DC02's report to the same folder on SBD-DC01\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\policy-before -Force | Out-Null\ngpresult /r /scope:computer\ngpresult /h \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\policy-before\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME-gpresult.html\" /f"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "You can list both controllers' GPOs in precedence order and say why each applies, nothing is Enforced or blocked, every GPO's AD and SYSVOL versions agree, the central store holds SBD-DC01's templates, and each controller's starting gpresult report is saved."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep both starting gpresult reports in C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\policy-before, the inheritance output for the domain and the Domain Controllers OU, the GPO version listing, and a note of the central store's source and date."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group Policy processing\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-processing\n\nMicrosoft · Group Policy scope\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-scope\n\nMicrosoft · Get-GPInheritance\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/grouppolicy/get-gpinheritance\n\nMicrosoft · User Group Policy needs Read permission for computers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/group-policy/cannot-apply-user-gpo-when-computer-objects-dont-have-read-permissions\n\nMicrosoft · Create and manage the Group Policy Central Store\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-client/group-policy/create-and-manage-central-store\n\nMicrosoft · Gpupdate\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/gpupdate\n\nMicrosoft · Gpresult\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/gpresult\n\nMicrosoft · Group Policy Modeling and Group Policy Results\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-modeling-results")], question: "A GPO linked at the domain and a GPO linked to the Domain Controllers OU configure the same administrative template setting differently, and neither link is Enforced. Which value does a controller end up with?", answers: ["The domain GPO's, because the domain is the higher level", "Whichever GPO was edited last", "The Domain Controllers OU GPO's, because it is applied after the domain's"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Local, site, domain, then OU: the GPO linked closest to the computer is applied last and wins, unless a link above it is Enforced.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A setting doesn't take effect on a controller. Why is setting the link to Enforced a poor fix?", ["It hides the precedence problem instead of explaining it, and makes the next conflict harder to trace", "Domain controllers ignore Enforced links", "Enforced removes the GPO's security filtering"], 0, "Enforced makes a GPO win over everything below it and pierces Block Inheritance. Find out why the setting lost — link order, filtering, or a closer GPO — and fix that instead."), QuizQuestion("Someone removes Authenticated Users from a user-settings GPO's security filtering and adds a group of users. Now its settings reach none of them. What is missing?", ["A WMI filter", "Read permission for the computers, because user policy is read in the computer's security context", "Loopback processing in replace mode"], 1, "Since security update MS16-072, Windows reads user policy in the computer's context. Give computers Read — through Authenticated Users or Domain Computers — while only the user group keeps Apply group policy.")], recap: ["The closest link applies last and wins; at one OU, link order 1 wins.", "Enforced and Block Inheritance are exceptions to record, not tools to reach for.", "gpresult shows what actually applied, and why the rest was filtered out."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 imports the DISA policies course 1.4 staged and links them where they belong.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#gpo-basics", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:apply", title: "Import and link the DISA GPOs", minutes: 40, summary: "Group Policy is how the STIG becomes configuration. Import the package course 1.4 staged, link each policy where its systems live so the findings marked fix by GPO can close, and keep the lab's deliberate deviations written down rather than silently skipped.", objectives: ["Verify the staged package and add its templates to the central store.", "Import DISA's policies and link them by OU, with account policies at the domain root.", "Handle lab-specific deviations with documentation instead of silent edits."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.4: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time design (course 1.3) in place, and course 1.4's sealed baseline — the DISA GPO package hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA, the raw results, three checklists and findings record under C:\\Evidence\\baseline, and handoff.txt with the versions to reuse.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens, with SCC on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01.\n\nLeave SCC, STIG Viewer and the benchmark at the versions course 1.4 recorded until this course's rescan is filed."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the organizational unit (OU) structure, learns how Group Policy decides what applies, imports the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) that course 1.4 staged, and proves the result with a rescan against course 1.4's baseline. Names, paths and versions below are examples — adapt them, but rescan with exactly the SCC version, benchmark release and profile in handoff.txt. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Start from course 1.4's handoff. Read the package release in C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings\\handoff.txt, confirm the package in C:\\Staging\\DISA still matches its connected-side hash, and only then extract it. Copy its ADMX files and their en-US ADML files into the central store from lesson 2, without replacing a newer Microsoft template with an older copy of the same file."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Import each policy this network uses into a new GPO with the GPMC Import Settings wizard or Import-GPO: the Windows Server domain controller, member server and user policies, and the Windows client policies for your fleet's release, which stay unlinked until courses 1.8 and 1.13 link them. Keep DISA's names behind an SBD prefix so the source release stays identifiable. An import replaces every setting in the target GPO and brings no links, security filtering or WMI filter. Where the package's user-rights settings name placeholder groups such as ADD YOUR DOMAIN ADMINS, map them to sbd.test's real groups with a migration table — check the package's support files for one before building your own in GPMC's Migration Table Editor."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Link by OU. Link the domain controller policy to the Domain Controllers OU at link order 1, above the Default Domain Controllers Policy, and the member-server policy to the Servers OU, so the systems later courses build there are hardened from first boot. Domain controllers read password, lockout and Kerberos policy only from GPOs linked at the domain root: if the package carries account policies, set those values in a GPO linked there above the Default Domain Policy, because at an OU they do nothing for domain accounts. Use neither Enforced nor Block Inheritance."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Apply the user policy where people sign in. Its settings belong to every session on a hardened server, whoever signs in — an administrator from the Admin OU, or the built-in Administrator in the default Users container, where no GPO can link. So link the DISA user policy to the Domain Controllers and Servers OUs and, in a small GPO linked to both, enable Configure user Group Policy loopback processing mode set to Merge. Record that decision — it is the only loopback this course uses — and compare it with the package's own instructions for its user policy."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Walk the settings that need a decision in this network — anything that assumes infrastructure the lab does not have or conflicts with an earlier design — and record each deviation with its rationale instead of deleting the setting. Check each fix-by-GPO item in course 1.4's findings record against the imported settings, so you know which ones the rescan should show closed. Then, once SBD-DC02 shows the same GPO versions, refresh policy on both controllers and confirm with gpresult that the SBD DISA GPOs apply and that replication still succeeds both ways. Finally, confirm the course 1.3 time design is intact with no time setting from Group Policy: w32tm /query /configuration must show no (Policy) value, and any Windows Time Service setting an imported GPO carries goes back to Not Configured, with the change recorded."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC01. True means the package matches the hash recorded on the connected side.\n\$package = Get-ChildItem C:\\Staging\\DISA -Filter '*GPO*.zip' | Select-Object -First 1\n(Get-FileHash \$package.FullName -Algorithm SHA256).Hash -in (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\transfer\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash\nExpand-Archive \$package.FullName -DestinationPath C:\\Staging\\GPO\n# Per policy; take the exact names and folders from the package:\nImport-GPO -BackupGpoName '<DISA GPO name>' -TargetName 'SBD <DISA GPO name>' -Path '<package GPOs folder>' -MigrationTable '<migration table>' -CreateIfNeeded\nNew-GPLink -Name 'SBD <DISA domain controller GPO name>' -Target 'OU=Domain Controllers,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Order 1\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Domain Controllers,DC=sbd,DC=test'\n# On each controller's console; carry SBD-DC02's report to SBD-DC01:\ngpupdate /force\ngpresult /r /scope:computer\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\import -Force | Out-Null\ngpresult /h \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\import\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME-gpresult.html\" /f\nw32tm /query /source   # SBD-DC01: Local CMOS Clock; SBD-DC02: a domain controller\nw32tm /query /configuration | Select-String '\\(Policy\\)'   # expected: no output, so no time setting comes from Group Policy\nrepadmin /replsummary"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The package matched its connected-side hash, the central store serves its templates, placeholder groups map to real ones, the links follow the OU design with the domain controller policy at link order 1 and nothing Enforced or blocked, gpresult on both controllers lists the SBD DISA GPOs as applied, time and replication are unchanged with no time setting from Group Policy, and every deviation has a written reason and owner."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the import record — package release and hash check, each GPO's name and source, the migration table, the links with their order, the loopback decision, and both gpresult reports in C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\import — and the deviation list with rationale and owner."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group Policy processing\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-processing\n\nMicrosoft · Create and manage the Group Policy Central Store\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-client/group-policy/create-and-manage-central-store\n\nMicrosoft · Gpresult\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/gpresult\n\nMicrosoft · Import-GPO\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/grouppolicy/import-gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Back up, restore, migrate, and copy Group Policy Objects\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-backup-restore\n\nMicrosoft · New-GPLink\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/grouppolicy/new-gplink\n\nMicrosoft · Group Policy application rules for domain controllers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/group-policy-application-rules-for-domain-controller\n\nDISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo")], question: "Why does the OU work from lesson 1 matter here?", answers: ["GPOs link to OUs, so the structure decides which systems receive which hardening", "OUs make the GPO files smaller", "DISA requires an OU named DISA"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Policy targeting is the OU structure's whole job: domain-controller policies to the Domain Controllers OU, server policies to the Servers OU.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where do the ADMX templates go so every domain controller's editor agrees?", ["Each administrator's desktop", "The Group Policy central store in SYSVOL", "The offline root CA"], 1, "The central store replicates with SYSVOL, so every Group Policy editor in the domain reads the same definitions."), QuizQuestion("A STIG setting assumes infrastructure the lab doesn't have. What is the right move?", ["Delete the setting from the GPO quietly", "Mark the whole package not applicable", "Record a deviation with rationale and keep it visible for the assessment"], 2, "Documented deviations keep the hardening honest. A silent edit hides a decision an assessor will later need to see.")], recap: ["The central store keeps policy definitions consistent across controllers.", "Links follow the OU design; account policies live at the domain root.", "Deviations are documented decisions, not deletions."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 rescans with course 1.4's versions to prove what the policies changed.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#apply", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:rescan", title: "Rescan, reconcile, and record", minutes: 35, summary: "The second scan is the verdict. Rescan with exactly the versions course 1.4 used, compare each controller with its own baseline, update copies of the course 1.4 checklists, and give every remaining open item an answer.", objectives: ["Rescan with course 1.4's SCC version, benchmark release and profile, and compare against the baseline.", "Update copies of the course 1.4 checklists and re-answer what the policies changed.", "Close or classify every item in the findings record."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.4: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time design (course 1.3) in place, and course 1.4's sealed baseline — the DISA GPO package hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA, the raw results, three checklists and findings record under C:\\Evidence\\baseline, and handoff.txt with the versions to reuse.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens, with SCC on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01.\n\nLeave SCC, STIG Viewer and the benchmark at the versions course 1.4 recorded until this course's rescan is filed."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the organizational unit (OU) structure, learns how Group Policy decides what applies, imports the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) that course 1.4 staged, and proves the result with a rescan against course 1.4's baseline. Names, paths and versions below are examples — adapt them, but rescan with exactly the SCC version, benchmark release and profile in handoff.txt. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Check that the baseline is untouched: the hash of C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv must match the one course 1.4 recorded, and every file in C:\\Evidence\\baseline must still match the manifest. Read handoff.txt, confirm SCC and the benchmark on each controller are still those versions, and rescan both controllers locally with the same profile. Changing anything between scans makes the comparison meaningless."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Copy each new session into C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\<host name> and hash it, and carry SBD-DC02's set to SBD-DC01 as in course 1.4. Compare each controller with its own baseline: which rules moved from open to pass, which stayed open, and whether any regressed. Attribute each change to the GPO that caused it — the gpresult report names the winning GPO for every setting."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Copy the three checklists from C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists into C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\checklists and open only the copies in STIG Viewer, so nothing it saves can touch the baseline. Import each controller's new XCCDF results into its copy — with the benchmark and the checklist's STIG release unchanged, the import summary should match course 1.4's — then revisit every manual answer the policies may have changed, sbd.test's included, updating Finding Details and Comments with what you observed and when. Save and hash the copies; the baseline checklists stay exactly as hashed."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Work through the findings record. Every fix-by-GPO item should now be closed; if one isn't, find out why — the GPO didn't apply, a closer GPO won, or the setting needs the domain root or a restart. Work in a copy, C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\findings\\findings-record.csv: give each item a Resolution — closed by GPO, deviation (for lesson 5's exception record) or POA&M candidate (Plan of Action and Milestones), with its owner and date — and the Evidence that shows it. Treat any new open item as a finding to fix or classify."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC01: the manifest's hash must match course 1.4's record; no Compare-Object output means no baseline file changed\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline-manifest.csv).Hash (Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\baseline -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash\nGet-Content C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings\\handoff.txt   # the SCC version, benchmark release and profile to reuse\n# On each controller, after its SCC rescan (adapt the path to SCC's results directory); SBD-DC02 then copies its set to SBD-DC01\n\$session = Get-ChildItem \"\$env:USERPROFILE\\SCC\\Sessions\" -Directory | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 1\nCopy-Item \$session.FullName -Destination \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME\" -Recurse\nGet-ChildItem \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME\" -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\\$env:COMPUTERNAME-hashes.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nCopy-Item C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\SBD-DC02, C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\SBD-DC02-hashes.csv -Destination '\\\\SBD-DC01\\C\$\\Evidence\\hardened\\' -Recurse   # on SBD-DC02\n# On SBD-DC01: verify the copy, then list each changed rule twice, <= its baseline result and => its result now\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\SBD-DC02-hashes.csv).Hash (Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\SBD-DC02 -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash\nfunction Read-Xccdf(\$dir) { Get-ChildItem \$dir -Recurse -Filter '*XCCDF-Results*.xml' | Select-Xml -XPath \"//*[local-name()='rule-result']\" | ForEach-Object { [pscustomobject]@{ Rule = \$_.Node.idref; Result = \$_.Node.result } } }\nforeach (\$h in 'SBD-DC01', 'SBD-DC02') { Compare-Object (Read-Xccdf \"C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\\$h\") (Read-Xccdf \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\\$h\") -Property Rule, Result | Sort-Object Rule | Export-Csv \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\\$h-changes.csv\" -NoTypeInformation }\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\checklists, C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\findings -Force | Out-Null\nCopy-Item C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\checklists\\*.cklb C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\checklists\nImport-Csv C:\\Evidence\\baseline\\findings\\findings-record.csv | Select-Object *, Resolution, Evidence | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\findings\\findings-record.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\checklists -Filter *.cklb | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\checklists-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation   # after the STIG Viewer updates"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The manifest hash matched and no baseline file changed, both rescans used the SCC version, benchmark release and profile in handoff.txt, each controller's changes are listed and attributed, the checklist copies carry the new results and re-checked answers while the baseline copies keep their hashes, and every findings-record item has a resolution."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep both rescan sessions with their hash lists, the two change lists, the updated checklist copies with their hashes, and the findings record with a resolution and evidence for every item, all in C:\\Evidence\\hardened."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Gpresult\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/gpresult\n\nDISA · SCAP content and the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · National Checklist Program (SP 800-70)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/national-checklist-program")], question: "Why keep the same benchmark and SCC versions for the rescan?", answers: ["Newer versions cost more", "Results are only comparable when the measuring stick didn't change", "DISA forbids upgrading mid-quarter"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A version change alters which checks exist and how they evaluate, so the before/after comparison would mix causes.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A check is still open and the lab intends to leave it that way, with a reason. What is that called?", ["A false positive", "A passing result", "A documented deviation — and possibly, later, a POA&M entry"], 2, "An intentional, justified open item is a deviation; one that will be fixed on a plan becomes a POA&M entry in the authorization path."), QuizQuestion("What makes a checklist entry for a manual check defensible?", ["The reviewer's observed evidence and rationale are recorded with it", "The box is checked", "It was green in the automated scan"], 0, "A manual answer without recorded evidence is an assertion. The observation is what an assessor can verify.")], recap: ["Same versions, honest comparison.", "Copies of the checklists carry the new results; the baseline stays sealed.", "Open items end the lesson classified, never mysterious."], nextStep: "The final lesson turns this into a standing practice with an exception record, owners and triggers.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#rescan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:harden-baseline:maintain", title: "Set the rescan cadence and exception record", minutes: 25, summary: "Hardening is a state you maintain, not a step you finish. Close the course with an owned exception record and a recorded policy state — reports and links, while course 1.19 backs the GPOs up — and decide who rescans, when, and which event forces an early look.", objectives: ["Consolidate deviations into an owned, dated exception record.", "Define the quarterly and event-driven rescan cadence.", "Export the applied policy state — reports and links — as evidence, leaving the GPO backup to course 1.19."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.4: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time design (course 1.3) in place, and course 1.4's sealed baseline — the DISA GPO package hashed in C:\\Staging\\DISA, the raw results, three checklists and findings record under C:\\Evidence\\baseline, and handoff.txt with the versions to reuse.\n\nWork at each domain controller's VM console with an authorized Domain Admin account: until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, that is where administration happens, with SCC on both controllers and STIG Viewer on SBD-DC01.\n\nLeave SCC, STIG Viewer and the benchmark at the versions course 1.4 recorded until this course's rescan is filed."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the organizational unit (OU) structure, learns how Group Policy decides what applies, imports the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) that course 1.4 staged, and proves the result with a rescan against course 1.4's baseline. Names, paths and versions below are examples — adapt them, but rescan with exactly the SCC version, benchmark release and profile in handoff.txt. Expect STIG settings to change behavior: that is the point, and the reason everything is scanned and recorded."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Consolidate the deviations from lesson 3 and the items lesson 4 resolved as deviations into one exception record, C:\\Evidence\\exception-record.csv — rule, setting, reason, compensating measure, risk note, owner and review date — and file a dated copy in C:\\Evidence\\hardened. Course 1.4's example belongs here — the rule expecting the PDC emulator to synchronize with an authorized external time source, which the course 1.3 design answers with a declared, checked clock. It stays a living record outside the sealed folder: later courses add their entries to it, and it travels into the authorization path as part of the Body of Evidence."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Set the standing cadence: review each quarterly DISA release for applicable updates, and rescan after any role addition or significant configuration change — course 1.6's closing rescan of both controllers, once its certificates and autoenrollment policy reach them, is the first change-driven run. When you adopt a new release, scan once with the new content before changing any policy, so the record separates what the content changed from what your policies changed."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Export the Group Policy reports and each GPO link into C:\\Evidence\\hardened: the reports make the applied policy state reviewable without a live console, and the link export records where each policy applies — the part a GPO backup never restores, since it brings back settings and permissions, not links. The backup itself belongs to course 1.19, which defines and schedules the backup of every GPO with the rest of the recovery set, so this course sets up no backup job."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff a future administrator or assessor would need — where the evidence lives, which versions produced it, and what is intentionally open — and hash C:\\Evidence\\hardened into C:\\Evidence\\hardened-manifest.csv. Course 1.8 later moves SCC and STIG Viewer to AdminJumpBox01 and links these policies to the admin seats' OUs; until then the cadence runs at the controllers' consoles."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Seed the exception record from the findings resolved as deviations; add lesson 3's deviations and fill in every column\nImport-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\findings\\findings-record.csv | Where-Object Resolution -eq 'deviation' | Select-Object Target, GroupId, StigId, Title, Setting, Reason, CompensatingMeasure, RiskNote, Owner, ReviewDate | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\exception-record.csv -NoTypeInformation -NoClobber\nCopy-Item C:\\Evidence\\exception-record.csv \"C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\exception-record-\$(Get-Date -Format yyyy-MM-dd).csv\"   # once it is complete\n# The applied state as records; course 1.19 schedules the GPO backup itself\nGet-GPOReport -All -ReportType Html -Path C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\gpo-report.html\n'DC=sbd,DC=test', 'OU=Domain Controllers,DC=sbd,DC=test', 'OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' | ForEach-Object { (Get-GPInheritance -Target \$_).GpoLinks } | Select-Object Target, Order, DisplayName, Enabled, Enforced | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened\\gpo-links.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\hardened -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\hardened-manifest.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\hardened-manifest.csv"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The exception record has owners, compensating measures and review dates, with a dated copy filed, the GPO reports and link export are filed, the evidence folder is sealed in the manifest with its own hash recorded, and the rescan triggers are written where course 1.6 will meet them."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the hardening checklist: the living exception record in C:\\Evidence and its dated copy, the GPO report and link export, C:\\Evidence\\hardened-manifest.csv with its own hash, the cadence, and the named owner of the next review."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Get-GPInheritance\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/grouppolicy/get-gpinheritance\n\nMicrosoft · Back up, restore, migrate, and copy Group Policy Objects\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-backup-restore\n\nDISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nNIST · National Checklist Program (SP 800-70)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/national-checklist-program")], question: "What triggers a rescan before the next quarterly cycle?", answers: ["Only a calendar reminder", "A role addition or significant configuration change", "Nothing — quarterly is the only trigger"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Change is the other trigger. Course 1.6 ends with exactly such a rescan of the domain controllers it changed, and the build courses after it rescan what they change.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why export the GPO reports and links into the evidence?", ["It makes gpupdate run faster", "DISA requires reports inside SYSVOL", "The applied policy state becomes reviewable without a live console, and the links are recorded — a GPO backup doesn't restore them"], 2, "Reports and the link export let anyone — including a future you — verify the applied state without trusting memory. With the GPO backup course 1.19 schedules, they also make it restorable, links included."), QuizQuestion("Where does the exception list end up in the bigger arc?", ["It is deleted after the course", "In the Body of Evidence the authorization path organizes and assesses", "Inside the offline root CA"], 1, "Deviations and POA&M candidates are authorization-package material; course 2.2 shows how artifacts like this list join the Body of Evidence.")], recap: ["Exceptions have owners and review dates.", "Rescans follow the DISA quarter and every meaningful change.", "The applied GPO state is reported, its links exported, and both hashed; course 1.19 backs the GPOs up."], nextStep: "That completes Harden with Group Policy. Course 1.6 builds the two-tier PKI on this hardened base: its domain-joined servers join the Servers OU, where the member-server policies already apply, and its closing rescan of the controllers is this cadence's first change-driven run.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/harden-baseline#maintain", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "two-tier-pki", number: "1.6", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI", subtitle: "Offline root, issuing CA, pki.sbd.test publication & templates", category: "Network build", description: "Build a complete two-tier public key infrastructure: an offline standalone Root CA and an enterprise issuing CA, signed through a controlled ceremony, with trust data served from pki.sbd.test at the exact embedded URLs, template administration on its own seat, certificates for both domain controllers, and enrollment and revocation proven end to end.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "10 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 10,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-guide", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Offline root, issuing CA, pki.sbd.test publication & templates", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "two-tier-pki-package", title: "Configure the two-tier PKI · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki/SecureByDesign-two-tier-pki-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:design", title: "Design the two-tier trust hierarchy", minutes: 25, summary: "The root establishes trust. The subordinate, also called the issuing CA in this design, handles approved end-entity certificates. Keeping these roles separate protects the root from daily network exposure.", objectives: ["Explain why the root stays offline while the issuing CA handles enrollment.", "Record the CA identities, cryptography, lifetimes, and CRL periods before installation, starting from this lab's example values.", "Plan AIA and CDP publication locations that clients can reach inside the air gap."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Draw the hierarchy: offline standalone root → enterprise issuing CA → approved server/client certificates. There is no network connection to the root."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Record final host and CA names, key provider, algorithm, key sizes, hashes, validity periods, renewal owners, and recovery arrangements. Start from this lab's example values in the next step and change any that your own policy requires: they are examples, not universal requirements."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Example values for this lab: the root CA uses an RSA 4096 key with SHA-256 and a 20-year certificate, and publishes a base CRL every 26 weeks with an overlap period and no delta CRL, refreshed at each offline ceremony. The issuing CA uses RSA 4096 (3072 at minimum) with SHA-256 and a 10-year certificate, a weekly base CRL, and a daily delta CRL. End-entity certificates last one to two years. NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 rates RSA 2048 at 112 bits of security, which it accepts for new signatures only through 2030, so a CA that will still be signing after 2030 uses at least 3072 bits (128 bits of security); the newest Windows Server release requires at least 2,048-bit RSA for TLS server authentication."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Know where each value is set. You choose the key, the hash, and the root's 20-year validity in the AD CS Configuration wizard. Each CA's CAPolicy.inf sets its CRL periods (CRLPeriod, CRLPeriodUnits, CRLDeltaPeriod, CRLDeltaPeriodUnits), which certutil -setreg can change later. The issuing CA's 10-year lifetime is set on the root (CA\\ValidityPeriod and CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits) before the signing ceremony, because a subordinate CA's lifetime comes from the CA that signs it."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Plan a stable internal publication name such as pki.sbd.test. The Authority Information Access (AIA) location supplies issuer certificates; the CRL Distribution Point (CDP) supplies certificate revocation lists."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Choose the publication host, paths, access rules, and refresh schedule before issuing certificates. Clients inside this air-gapped network must reach those locations without relying on the public internet."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Run on the future issuing CA, not the offline root.\nGet-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object Name, Domain, PartOfDomain\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The written plan separates trust, issuance, publication, and administration. It identifies who renews CRLs before they expire, and every key size, hash, lifetime, and CRL period has a recorded value and the setting that applies it."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the approved hierarchy, the cryptography and lifetime table with the setting that applies each value, and a worksheet mapping every certificate/CRL URL to its actual publisher and file."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · CAPolicy.inf syntax\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/prepare-the-capolicy-inf-file\n\nNIST · SP 800-57 Part 1 Rev. 5, Recommendation for key management\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/57/pt1/r5/final")], question: "Which role issues routine server certificates in this design?", answers: ["The offline root for every request", "The enterprise issuing CA", "The DNS server"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The issuing CA handles end-entity enrollment while the offline root signs the approved subordinate CA certificate.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What do the AIA and CDP locations supply?", ["Private keys for backup", "DNS zone transfers", "Issuer certificates and certificate revocation lists"], 2, "Authority Information Access serves issuer certificates; CRL Distribution Points serve revocation lists. Clients must be able to reach both."), QuizQuestion("Why is pki.sbd.test planned before any CA is installed?", ["Issued certificates embed these URLs, so the publication name must be stable first", "The root cannot be installed without it", "DNS requires a CNAME before zones replicate"], 0, "Certificates keep the extensions they were issued with. A stable publication name avoids reissuing everything later."), QuizQuestion("In this lab, where is the issuing CA's 10-year certificate lifetime set?", ["In the issuing CA's own CAPolicy.inf", "On the offline root, with CA\\ValidityPeriod and CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits, before the signing ceremony", "In the certificate template for web servers"], 1, "A subordinate CA's lifetime is set by the CA that signs it. The root's issued-certificate validity settings decide how long the issuing CA's certificate lasts, so they are set and verified before the ceremony.")], recap: ["Trust, issuance, publication, and administration are separated in writing.", "Cryptography, names, lifetimes, and CRL periods are approved before installation, starting from this lab's example values.", "CRL renewal has a named owner and a date."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 performs the controlled offline root installation.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:root", title: "Configure the offline Root CA", minutes: 30, summary: "Treat root installation as a controlled event. Its private key is the highest-value secret in the hierarchy, and its configuration shapes the subordinate certificate it will sign.", objectives: ["Verify the disconnected workgroup host before installing the role.", "Install a standalone root CA with the approved CAPolicy.inf and settings.", "Configure publication URLs and the CRL schedule before signing anything."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Verify SBD-ROOTCA01 is in a workgroup with no active network path. Prepare approved installation media, protected administration, and backup storage, and set its clock from the approved reference that course 1.3 names, recording both readings: no network will ever correct this clock."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Place the reviewed CAPolicy.inf in %SystemRoot% before configuration. Tailor it to the root role: in this lab it sets CRLPeriod=Weeks and CRLPeriodUnits=26, and CRLDeltaPeriodUnits=0 so the root publishes no delta CRL. A self-signed root's own CDP/AIA choices differ from the extensions the root places into issued subordinate certificates."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Install the Certification Authority role with management tools. In the AD CS Configuration wizard choose Standalone CA, Root CA, and a new private key, then apply the approved provider, cryptography (RSA 4096 with SHA-256 in this lab), CA name, validity (20 years), database, and log settings."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Before signing anything, configure the root's issued-certificate AIA/CDP settings, the subordinate validity policy (CA\\ValidityPeriod Years and CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits 10 in this lab), and the base-CRL schedule (26 weeks with an overlap period, and no delta CRL). Hold requests for explicit approval. Produce and inspect a current root CRL, then capture a protected CA backup — a build-time step that stays inside this ceremony, and which course 1.19 accounts for in its recovery set without ever bringing it onto the network."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Inspect locally on SBD-ROOTCA01 during the approved root session.\nGet-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object Name, Domain, PartOfDomain\nGet-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fff K'   # read against the approved reference; log both readings\nGet-Service CertSvc\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CRLPublicationURLs\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CACertPublicationURLs\ncertutil -getreg CA\\ValidityPeriodUnits\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CRLPeriodUnits"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The host remains disconnected, its clock matches the approved reference, the CA is standalone/root, requests require approval, and the planned subordinate URLs and 10-year subordinate validity are configured before signing."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the root certificate identity, policy file, publication settings, CRL dates, backup location, the clock check, and operator sign-off. Protect backups containing private keys."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · CAPolicy.inf syntax\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/prepare-the-capolicy-inf-file\n\nMicrosoft · Install the offline root\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/2-install-offline-root\n\nMicrosoft · Perform the offline root signing ceremony\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/4-perform-offline-root-signing-ceremony")], question: "When must the root's issued-certificate publication URLs be configured?", answers: ["After clients have enrolled", "Only after the first CRL expires", "Before signing the subordinate CA certificate"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Issued certificates retain their extensions. Changing the CA configuration later does not rewrite existing certificates.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why is the root a standalone CA rather than an enterprise CA?", ["Standalone CAs use stronger cryptography", "It is not domain-joined and must work disconnected from the directory", "Enterprise CAs cannot sign subordinate certificates"], 1, "An enterprise CA depends on Active Directory. The offline root lives in a workgroup with no network path, so it must be standalone."), QuizQuestion("Where does CAPolicy.inf belong before configuration?", ["The domain SYSVOL share", "The removable media only", "%SystemRoot% on the CA host"], 2, "Setup reads CAPolicy.inf from %SystemRoot% during configuration. Placing it afterward does not change the installed CA.")], recap: ["The root host stays disconnected, with controlled media, administration, and time.", "CAPolicy.inf is in place before the role is configured.", "Publication URLs, validity policy, the CRL schedule, and a protected backup exist before any signing."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 prepares the online issuing CA and its certificate request.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#root", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:request", title: "Create the issuing CA request", minutes: 25, summary: "Prepare the online issuing server, but keep issuance paused until its CA certificate has been signed and installed. The request carries the public key; the private key stays protected on the issuing system or its approved key provider.", objectives: ["Prepare the domain-joined issuing host with its final identity.", "Generate the subordinate CA request without exposing the private key.", "Verify and hash the request before it travels to the root."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Confirm SBD-SUBCA01 has its final hostname, domain membership, static address (10.0.30.20), internal DNS, and its time from the domain hierarchy, with the hypervisor's time synchronization off. Use a dedicated host and the privileges approved for enterprise CA configuration."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Prepare its role-specific CAPolicy.inf (in this lab: CRLPeriod=Weeks, CRLPeriodUnits=1, CRLDeltaPeriod=Days, CRLDeltaPeriodUnits=1, and LoadDefaultTemplates=0) and protected request directory. Decide which templates may later be published instead of enabling a broad default enrollment set."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Install the CA role and management tools. In the AD CS Configuration wizard choose Enterprise CA, Subordinate CA, and the approved new-key/provider settings (RSA 4096 with SHA-256 in this lab). Enter the planned CA identity and storage locations."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Choose Save a certificate request to file. Inspect the resulting request's subject, public key, and CA constraints; record its SHA-256 hash before handing it to the media custodian."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "w32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, never Local CMOS Clock or a hypervisor provider\n# Use the actual request path chosen in the wizard.\ncertutil -dump C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.req\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.req"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The request matches the approved issuing identity and key profile. The CA is waiting for its signed certificate; do not troubleshoot that expected state by installing another CA."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Retain the request, its reviewed fields and hash, and the request-transfer record. Do not place private keys in the transfer package."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · CAPolicy.inf syntax\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/prepare-the-capolicy-inf-file\n\nMicrosoft · Deploy the issuing CA role\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/3-deploy-issuing-certification-authority-role")], question: "What moves to the offline root for signing?", answers: ["The issuing CA's certificate request", "The issuing CA's private key", "A network cable connected to the domain"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The signed request contains the public key. Root signing does not require exporting the issuing CA private key.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why record the request’s SHA-256 hash before the media transfer?", ["So the root operator can confirm the file was not altered in transit", "It encrypts the request", "The configuration wizard requires it to continue"], 0, "An independently recorded hash lets the ceremony verify integrity. It does not encrypt anything — it proves the same file arrived."), QuizQuestion("The new issuing CA is waiting with its service stopped. What is correct?", ["Reinstall the role until the service starts", "That is the expected state until the signed certificate is installed", "Join the root to the domain to finish the setup"], 1, "A subordinate CA cannot run before its CA certificate exists. The waiting state is normal, not a failure to fix.")], recap: ["The issuing host has its final name, addressing, DNS, and time before the role install.", "The request file carries the public key only.", "The request’s fields and hash are reviewed and recorded before transfer."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 walks the request through the offline signing ceremony.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#request", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:sign", title: "Sign through the offline ceremony", minutes: 30, summary: "A signing ceremony connects an approved request to an approved CA identity. A matching filename alone is not proof that the root should trust the request.", objectives: ["Verify the root's clock and the request's integrity before submission.", "Issue the subordinate certificate under the approved validity policy.", "Export and verify the public artifacts, then return the root to a secured state."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Use approved removable media and custody procedures to bring the request to the root. Before signing, read the root's clock against the approved reference and correct it by the measured difference if it has drifted: the certificate and CRL dates it signs come from that clock. Recalculate the request's hash, compare it with the independently recorded value, and inspect the request before submission."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Verify the root's issued-certificate validity policy and publication extensions. In Certification Authority, submit the request, review its pending entry, and issue it only after the required approval."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Export the subordinate certificate, root certificate, and current root CRL. Check identities, constraints, validity, signatures, URLs, and revocation data against the approved hierarchy. Record file hashes and the request ID."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Transfer only the approved public artifacts back to the connected environment. Match published filenames to the URLs embedded in certificates, retain the ceremony record and protected backup, then shut down and secure the root."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-ROOTCA01, first: read this against the approved reference and log both readings\nGet-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fff K'\n# Inspect public artifacts; use your actual approved paths.\ncertutil -dump C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.cer\ncertutil -dump C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.crl\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\CAConfig\\IssuingCA.cer, C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.cer, C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.crl"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The root's clock matched the approved reference, and artifact identities, signatures, validity, and custody checks pass. The root remains offline; a field dump alone is not end-to-end trust validation."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the clock check, approval, request ID, artifact hashes, verified certificate/CRL properties, media custody, and root shutdown evidence together."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Perform the offline root signing ceremony\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/4-perform-offline-root-signing-ceremony")], question: "Which transfer preserves the root boundary?", answers: ["An automated shared folder on the root", "Approved removable media carrying the reviewed request and public artifacts", "Temporary direct internet access"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "This training architecture uses manual, human-controlled transfer. It never adds a live link to the root.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What leaves the root environment after the ceremony?", ["The root’s private-key backup", "A live network share for future requests", "Only approved public artifacts: the subordinate certificate, root certificate, and current CRL"], 2, "The boundary stays intact: public artifacts travel on approved media; keys and live connections never do."), QuizQuestion("The request’s recalculated hash does not match the recorded value. What now?", ["Stop and investigate; do not submit the request", "Submit it anyway and compare afterward", "Regenerate the hash until it matches"], 0, "A hash mismatch means the file is not the one that was approved. Submitting it would sign an unverified request.")], recap: ["Integrity is verified before submission, and issuance follows explicit approval.", "Artifact identities, validity, and URLs are checked against the plan.", "The ceremony record, hashes, and root shutdown are all evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 brings the issuing CA online and publishes the trust data.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#sign", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:publish", title: "Activate the issuing CA and distribute trust", minutes: 25, summary: "A signed certificate is not yet a working CA. Install it, start Certificate Services, set the AIA and CDP locations every issued certificate will carry, and distribute the approved root through managed domain policy.", objectives: ["Install the signed certificate and start Certificate Services.", "Configure the AIA and CDP URLs before any certificate is issued.", "Distribute root trust through managed domain policy."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Verify the returned artifacts and add the approved root certificate and the current root CRL to the issuing server's local stores: the CA checks its own chain when it starts, before pki.sbd.test serves those files. Complete CA certificate installation through Certification Authority and start Certificate Services."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Configure the issuing CA's AIA and CDP settings before issuing any certificate, pointing them at the planned pki.sbd.test locations. Every certificate the CA signs carries these URLs, so they are fixed now and served exactly as written in lessons 6 and 7."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Distribute the approved root trust through the managed domain policy and arrange the required intermediate/directory publication with delegated administrators."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Hold off on certificate templates: lessons 8 and 9 give template administration its own seat and publish only the templates the plan needs."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Before installing the signed certificate: the ceremony's root certificate and CRL, into the local stores\ncertutil -addstore -f Root C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.cer\ncertutil -addstore -f Root C:\\CAConfig\\RootCA.crl\n# Inspect the issuing CA after installation and publication.\nGet-Service CertSvc\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CRLPublicationURLs\ncertutil -getreg CA\\CACertPublicationURLs\ncertutil -store Root\ncertutil -store CA"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Certificate Services is running, the AIA and CDP settings carry the planned pki.sbd.test URLs, and the approved root reaches domain clients through policy."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the CA installation record, the AIA and CDP settings, and the trust-distribution policy."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Publish trust data and validate clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/6-publish-trust-data-validate-clients\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why are the AIA and CDP URLs set before the issuing CA signs its first certificate?", answers: ["They can be edited on each certificate afterward", "Every issued certificate carries them for its whole life, so a later change strands the certificates already in use", "Certificate Services will not start until they are published"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Extensions are fixed at issuance. Setting the locations first means every certificate points at the pki.sbd.test paths lessons 6 and 7 will serve.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why must published filenames match the URLs embedded in certificates?", ["The CA service will not start otherwise", "Clients fetch exactly those URLs; a renamed file breaks chain building and revocation checks", "Filenames control template permissions"], 1, "Chain building and revocation checking follow the embedded AIA and CDP URLs literally. Publication must serve those names."), QuizQuestion("How does the approved root certificate reach domain clients?", ["Each user imports it manually from email", "It replicates automatically through DNS", "Through the managed domain policy trust distribution"], 2, "Group Policy distributes the trusted root deliberately and reversibly, which keeps trust decisions managed.")], recap: ["The issuing CA is running with its signed certificate.", "AIA and CDP URLs are set before the first certificate is issued.", "Root trust distributes through managed domain policy."], nextStep: "Lesson 6 gives those embedded URLs a real home: the pki.sbd.test publication site.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#publish", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:publication", title: "Build the pki.sbd.test publication site", minutes: 30, summary: "Every certificate the issuing CA has signed carries the pki.sbd.test URLs inside it. This lesson gives those URLs a real home: a small web site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01 serving certificates and revocation lists to the whole network.", objectives: ["Stand up the HTTP publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01.", "Confirm the DNS alias from course 1.2 points at the publication host.", "Explain why CDP and AIA publication uses HTTP, not HTTPS."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-PKI-MGMT01, install the Web Server role and create a site (or virtual directory) whose folder will hold the published certificates and revocation lists. Serve .cer, .crt, and .crl files, and allow double escaping in that site's request filtering only, because delta CRL file names carry a + that IIS otherwise refuses; nothing else belongs in this directory."), LessonSection("Step 2", "From SBD-DC01 in VLAN 10, outside VLAN 30, confirm the pki.sbd.test alias from course 1.2 resolves to 10.0.30.30 and that rule F18 in the service-path register permits HTTP to the site."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Leave the site on plain HTTP. Revocation checking has to work before any certificate can be trusted — putting the CRL behind HTTPS creates a loop where validating the site needs the very file it serves."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Restrict who can write to the publication folder: the CA's publishing process and the PKI administrators, nobody else. Clients read; they never write."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC01 (VLAN 10): the name and the path from outside VLAN 30\nResolve-DnsName pki.sbd.test\nTest-NetConnection pki.sbd.test -Port 80\n# On SBD-PKI-MGMT01: the site, and double escaping allowed there for delta CRL names\nGet-Website | Select-Object Name, State, PhysicalPath\nGet-WebConfigurationProperty -PSPath 'IIS:\\Sites\\<PKI site>' -Filter system.webServer/security/requestFiltering -Name allowDoubleEscaping"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "pki.sbd.test resolves to the publication host from outside VLAN 30, the site answers on port 80 with double escaping allowed only there, and the publication folder's permissions separate writers from readers."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the site configuration, folder permissions, the DNS resolution output from SBD-DC01, and rule F18, the register row that permits the path."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · Publish trust data and validate clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/6-publish-trust-data-validate-clients\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why does the publication site stay on plain HTTP?", answers: ["HTTPS licenses cost money", "Clients need the CRL to validate certificates — serving it over HTTPS creates a dependency loop", "HTTP is faster than HTTPS in every case"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Revocation data must be reachable before trust can be evaluated. The files are public and signed; integrity comes from the signatures, not the transport.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What belongs in the publication directory?", ["Public CA certificates and CRLs only", "The issuing CA's private key backup", "Administrator scripts and passwords"], 0, "The same rule that runs through the whole PKI: clients need the public trust artifacts; secrets never enter a client-readable path."), QuizQuestion("Which earlier work made this lesson a one-page job?", ["Nothing — it stands alone", "Course 1.1's domain controller promotion", "The DNS alias reserved in 1.2 and the HTTP row in the service-path register"], 2, "pki.sbd.test was reserved in course 1.2 and the path through the boundary is already in the register — the sequence pays off here.")], recap: ["pki.sbd.test now answers from SBD-PKI-MGMT01 over HTTP.", "Only public artifacts live in the folder; only PKI processes write to it.", "The transport is HTTP on purpose — revocation precedes trust."], nextStep: "Lesson 7 fills the site with the real trust data and proves the embedded URLs work.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#publication", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:publish-trust", title: "Publish the trust data at its advertised URLs", minutes: 25, summary: "A publication site is only correct when the filenames it serves match the URLs the certificates already carry. Copy the current certificates and CRLs into place, wire the issuing CA to keep them fresh, and prove retrieval from a client that has never cached anything.", objectives: ["Publish the root and issuing certificates and current CRLs at the exact embedded URLs.", "Configure the issuing CA to refresh its published files automatically.", "Prove live retrieval and chain building from outside VLAN 30."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Compare the issuing CA's configured AIA and CDP URLs against the publication folder's layout. Every embedded URL must map to a real file path on the site — the filenames are not yours to improve."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Copy the current root certificate, root CRL, issuing certificate, and issuing CRL into the folder. The root's files still arrive by approved media; the issuing CA's can be written directly by its publishing process."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Configure the issuing CA's CRL publication to write into the site's folder on its schedule, so each new CRL replaces the old file at the same name before it expires."), LessonSection("Step 4", "From SBD-DC01, outside VLAN 30, clear the URL cache and verify the issuing CA's certificate with live retrieval: the chain must build to the root and the root's CRL must come from the live site, not a cache. Fetch the issuing CA's own base and delta CRLs at their URLs too; the certificates it issues from lesson 9 on point clients at them."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC01 (VLAN 10), with nothing cached; IssuingCA.cer is the ceremony's public certificate\ncertutil -urlcache * delete\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\pki\\IssuingCA.cer\n'<issuing CA CRL name>.crl', '<issuing CA CRL name>+.crl' | ForEach-Object { (Invoke-WebRequest \"http://pki.sbd.test/\$_\" -UseBasicParsing).StatusCode }   # 200 for the base and the delta CRL\n# On SBD-PKI-MGMT01: what the site serves, with timestamps\nGet-ChildItem C:\\inetpub\\pkiroot | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every AIA and CDP URL the issuing CA's certificate carries returns the current file, urlfetch verification passes from SBD-DC01 outside VLAN 30, the issuing CA's base and delta CRLs answer at their URLs, and the CRL refresh lands at the same filename automatically."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the URL-to-file mapping table, the folder listing with timestamps, and the urlfetch and CRL retrieval output from SBD-DC01."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · Publish trust data and validate clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/6-publish-trust-data-validate-clients\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil")], question: "Why can't the published filenames be renamed to something tidier?", answers: ["The web server forbids renames", "Issued certificates already embed the exact URLs; clients fetch those literally", "The hardening baseline assigns the filenames"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The same lesson this course taught at signing time: extensions are fixed at issuance, so publication conforms to them, never the reverse.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How do new root CRLs reach the site in this lab?", ["By approved removable media from the offline ceremony", "Over a direct network share from the root", "They never need refreshing"], 0, "The root has no network path. Its refreshed CRL travels like everything else that crosses that boundary — reviewed, hashed, carried."), QuizQuestion("What does certutil -verify -urlfetch add over opening the certificate?", ["It prints the certificate in color", "Nothing", "It follows the embedded URLs live, proving publication and reachability rather than cached trust"], 2, "The -urlfetch option is the end-to-end test: chain building plus live AIA and CDP retrieval through the routed network.")], recap: ["Published filenames mirror the embedded URLs exactly.", "The issuing CA refreshes its files on schedule; the root's arrive by media.", "A cache-cleared check from outside VLAN 30 proves the chain and live revocation."], nextStep: "Lesson 8 moves template administration off the CA and onto its own seat.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#publish-trust", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:template-admin", title: "Separate template administration", minutes: 25, summary: "Certificate templates are directory objects, and whoever edits them decides what the PKI will sign. Give that power its own seat — SBD-PKI-MGMT01 — with named administrators, instead of leaving it wherever a CA console happens to be open.", objectives: ["Establish SBD-PKI-MGMT01 as the only administration seat for templates.", "Delegate template permissions to a named PKI administrators group.", "Record the template change process before any template changes."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the certificate management tools on SBD-PKI-MGMT01 and confirm the Certificate Templates console can read the directory's template store from there."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create the SBD PKI Admins group in the directory, place it in the Admin OU from course 1.5, and delegate the template permissions to that group — membership is the record of who may change what the CA signs."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Remove day-to-day template editing from everywhere else: the issuing CA's console stays for CA operations, not template design, and no Domain Admins or Enterprise Admins account edits templates as routine work."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the change process: a template change names its requester, its reviewer, the versioning action (edit, duplicate, or supersede), and lands in the evidence record like any other configuration change."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADGroup 'SBD PKI Admins' -Properties Members | Select-Object Name, Members\ncertutil -template | Select-String 'TemplatePropCommonName' | Select-Object -First 10"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The templates console works from SBD-PKI-MGMT01, the PKI administrators group holds the delegated permissions, and the written change process names requester, reviewer, and versioning action."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the delegation record, the group membership listing, and the signed-off template change process."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · PKI design considerations\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/pki-design-considerations\n\nMicrosoft · Manage certificate templates\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/manage-certificate-templates")], question: "Why do certificate templates deserve their own administration seat?", answers: ["Templates are directory objects that define what the CA will sign — editing them is a trust decision", "The CA console cannot display templates", "Templates take too much disk space on the CA"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "A template edit changes what enrollment can produce. Separating the seat and naming the editors makes that power visible and auditable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where does the PKI administrators group live?", ["The default Users container", "Under the Admin OU from course 1.5, with the other privileged groups", "On the offline root"], 1, "The OU design already has a home for privileged identity — this group joins it so delegation and policy follow the same structure."), QuizQuestion("A template needs a change. What is the first artifact?", ["The edited template itself", "A restart of Certificate Services", "The change record: requester, reviewer, and the versioning action"], 2, "The record precedes the change, the same discipline as the network's service-path register — configuration follows documentation, not the other way around.")], recap: ["SBD-PKI-MGMT01 is the template administration seat.", "A named group in the Admin OU holds the delegated power.", "Template changes have a written process before they have edits."], nextStep: "Lesson 9 uses that process to publish the lab's first working templates.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#template-admin", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:first-templates", title: "Publish the first working templates", minutes: 30, summary: "The default template set is a menu, not a deployment. Duplicate the three templates this network actually needs — domain controller, computer and server authentication — set their cryptography deliberately, and publish only those.", objectives: ["Duplicate and configure the domain controller, computer and web server templates against the approved plan.", "Set key sizes and validity that meet the Windows Server minimums.", "Publish only the needed templates, with autoenrollment limited to the domain controllers and a pilot group."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From the administration seat, duplicate Kerberos Authentication, the template Microsoft designates for domain controllers, as SBD Domain Controller Authentication: subject name format None with the DNS name in the alternative name, a key storage provider with RSA keys of at least 2,048 bits and SHA-256, a validity within lesson 1's one-to-two-year example, and the Domain Controller, Domain Controller Authentication and Kerberos Authentication templates on its Superseded Templates tab. Confirm Domain Controllers and Enterprise Domain Controllers hold Read, Enroll and Autoenroll: this is the PKI server certificate the STIG expects on every domain controller, course 1.4's POA&M candidate."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Duplicate the Computer template as SBD Computer Authentication. Set the approved key provider, a key size of at least 2,048 bits, a validity period within the same example, subject built from directory information, and autoenrollment permission for a pilot computer group only — SBD-PKI-MGMT01 for now, until the fleet arrives in course 1.13."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Duplicate the Web Server template as SBD Server Authentication for the TLS services later courses build — the collector (course 1.10), SBD-SPLUNK01 (1.12), SBD-WSUS01 (1.14) and the business services (1.18): the subject supplied in the request, Enroll for the server administrators (a Linux host's request is made on the host and submitted from their seat) and for an SBD TLS Servers computer group (a Windows server requesting into its own computer store is checked against its computer account, so each later course adds its server to that group first), no autoenrollment, and exportable keys only if the course 1.19 recovery plan explicitly says so."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Record each template's version, settings, and justification through the lesson 8 change process before publishing."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Publish exactly these three templates on the issuing CA and remove any default template the plan does not call for — an unpublished template cannot be mis-enrolled."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-PKI-MGMT01: the templates the CA publishes (use your CA's host\\name configuration string)\ncertutil -CATemplates -config 'SBD-SUBCA01\\SBD Issuing CA'\n# Each SBD template's minimum key size and subject-name flags, read from the directory\nGet-ADObject -SearchBase 'CN=Certificate Templates,CN=Public Key Services,CN=Services,CN=Configuration,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter \"Name -like 'SBD*'\" -Properties displayName, msPKI-Minimal-Key-Size, msPKI-Certificate-Name-Flag | Select-Object Name, displayName, msPKI-Minimal-Key-Size, msPKI-Certificate-Name-Flag"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The CA publishes exactly the three planned templates, each shows the approved key size and permissions, and each carries a completed change record."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep each template's configuration export, its change record, and the CA's published-template listing."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Manage certificate templates\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/manage-certificate-templates\n\nMicrosoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server (newest release)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025\n\nMicrosoft · On-premises key trust deployment guide (domain controller certificates)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/identity-protection/hello-for-business/deploy/on-premises-key-trust")], question: "Why duplicate the built-in templates instead of editing them?", answers: ["Built-ins cannot be published at all", "Duplication preserves the originals and gives your version its own name, version, and record", "Duplicates issue faster"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Working on a named copy keeps the baseline intact and makes your settings — and their history — unmistakably yours.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What floor applies to the template key sizes on this network?", ["512 bits for speed", "1,024 bits", "2,048-bit RSA at minimum — the newest Windows Server release enforces it for TLS server authentication"], 2, "The newest Windows Server release requires at least 2,048-bit RSA keys for TLS server authentication, so a plan below that produces certificates the platform itself will reject."), QuizQuestion("Why publish to a pilot group before the whole domain?", ["A small blast radius proves the template before autoenrollment touches fifty machines in course 1.13", "Pilots are a licensing requirement", "Templates expire if unused"], 0, "The fleet course will turn autoenrollment loose at scale. Proving the template on a pilot first is the same test-then-widen habit as the Group Policy work."), QuizQuestion("Why do the domain controllers get a duplicate of Kerberos Authentication rather than of the Computer template?", ["Computer certificates cannot be autoenrolled", "Domain controllers cannot use RSA keys", "It is the template Microsoft designates for domain controllers: it adds KDC Authentication to client and server authentication"], 2, "Kerberos Authentication is the current domain controller template, with the KDC Authentication usage that certificate-based Kerberos expects. A named duplicate with its own permissions gives the controllers their PKI server certificate through autoenrollment.")], recap: ["Three named templates exist, configured on purpose, nothing else published.", "Key sizes meet the platform's 2,048-bit floor; autoenrollment reaches only the domain controllers and the pilot group.", "Every template carries its change record."], nextStep: "The final lesson proves enrollment and revocation end to end, closes the domain controller certificate finding, and runs the closing scans.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#first-templates", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:two-tier-pki:validate", title: "Validate enrollment, revocation, and renewal", minutes: 35, summary: "Finish the way relying systems see it: both domain controllers and a pilot machine enroll on their own, validate against the live publication site, and reject a certificate you revoke — then the closing scans record what this course changed and close course 1.4's domain controller certificate finding.", objectives: ["Prove hands-free autoenrollment of the domain controller and pilot certificates through the published templates.", "Verify chain building and live revocation retrieval from pki.sbd.test, and prove revocation works.", "Schedule the renewals that keep the PKI alive, run the closing scans, close the domain controller certificate finding, and file the evidence."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.5: both domain controllers healthy and replicating, DNS (course 1.2) with the pki.sbd.test alias reserved, the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, and course 1.5's OU structure, member-server policies and exception record; verify domain health and internal name resolution.\n\nPrepare separate Windows Server hosts: SBD-ROOTCA01, a disconnected workgroup machine in the offline vault, and two virtual machines with the hypervisor's time synchronization off, taking their time from the domain: SBD-SUBCA01 (10.0.30.20) joined to sbd.test, and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.30) as a hardened member server, both joined straight into the Servers OU (course 1.8 moves them, with their administration, to a Tier 0 Servers OU).\n\nApprove the CA names, cryptography, validity periods, publication URLs, administration, backups, and removable-media procedure before installation; confirm the network's service-path register permits HTTP to pki.sbd.test (rule F18) and carries a row for certificate enrollment from the domain controllers to SBD-SUBCA01, which lesson 10 relies on."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the whole PKI: a standalone offline Root CA, an enterprise subordinate/issuing CA, the pki.sbd.test publication site on SBD-PKI-MGMT01, template administration on its own seat, and certificates for both domain controllers. The root stays disconnected; approved removable media carries requests and public artifacts, and its clock is set from the approved reference at every session. Until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01, work at each server's own console. Publication stays plain HTTP by design — clients must be able to fetch revocation data before they can validate any certificate."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Turn on autoenrollment: create SBD Certificate Autoenrollment, a GPO that enables Certificate Services Client - Auto-Enrollment with renewal and template updates, and link it at the domain root, so each member enrolls only for the templates whose permissions name it. The controllers' requests reach SBD-SUBCA01 over RPC across the core from VLAN 10, so confirm the register's enrollment row first. Refresh policy: both controllers receive SBD Domain Controller Authentication certificates and the pilot, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, an SBD Computer Authentication certificate, without a human touching a request."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On SBD-DC01, outside VLAN 30, export its new certificate and the pilot's, clear the URL cache, and run the full verification on each: the chain must reach the approved root and every AIA and CDP URL must answer from pki.sbd.test with current CRL dates — cached success is not evidence of publication."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Enroll a disposable test certificate, revoke only that one, publish an updated issuing CRL, and verify on SBD-DC01, with the cache cleared again, that a fresh evaluation detects the revocation. Diagnose retrieval or stale-publication problems rather than turning off revocation checks."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Record owners and reminders for root and issuing CRL publication and certificate renewal. Schedule offline root ceremonies early enough to transfer and publish replacement CRLs before expiry, and start each one by setting the root's clock against the approved reference. Backups are not set up here: course 1.19 defines and schedules the issuing CA's backup — database, key and configuration — with the rest of the network's, accounts for the root's ceremony backup from lesson 2, and rehearses recovery, so list those items for it in the record."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Take the first SCAP baselines of SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 the course 1.4 way: SCC and the Windows Server benchmark from the course 1.4 media set, checked against its hashes, at the same versions and profile, run locally, with each session hashed and carried to C:\\Evidence\\pki\\<host name> on SBD-DC01, where its hashes must match again, as SBD-DC02's did in course 1.4. List the IIS STIGs the publication site now falls under with an owner and a review date, then rescan both controllers the same way, because their certificates, root trust and autoenrollment policy are this course's change."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Close what this course fixed: in copies of the course 1.5 checklists in C:\\Evidence\\pki\\checklists on SBD-DC01, answer the rule that every domain controller has a PKI server certificate Not A Finding, with each controller's certificate as the observation, and record course 1.4's POA&M candidate as closed. The companion rule, that those certificates come from the DoD PKI or an approved External Certificate Authority, stays open: record it in C:\\Evidence\\exception-record.csv if it is not there yet, because this lab's own hierarchy stands in for DoD's. Export the new GPOs' reports and links for the change record (course 1.19 backs the GPOs up), file the enrollment, urlfetch, revocation, template and owner records with the scans, and seal C:\\Evidence\\pki."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC01, SBD-DC02 and the pilot, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, once SBD Certificate Autoenrollment is linked at the domain root\ngpupdate /force\ncertutil -pulse\nGet-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Select-Object @{n='Template';e={ \$x = \$_.Extensions | Where-Object { \$_.Oid.Value -eq '1.3.6.1.4.1.311.21.7' }; if (\$x) { \$x.Format(\$false) } }}, DnsNameList, NotAfter, Thumbprint\n# On SBD-DC01 (outside VLAN 30): export its certificate, then verify it and the pilot's with nothing cached\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\pki -Force | Out-Null\n\$dc = Get-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Where-Object { \$_.EnhancedKeyUsageList.ObjectId -contains '1.3.6.1.5.2.3.5' } | Select-Object -First 1   # KDC Authentication\nExport-Certificate -Cert \$dc -FilePath C:\\Evidence\\pki\\SBD-DC01-dc.cer\ncertutil -urlcache * delete\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\pki\\SBD-DC01-dc.cer\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\pki\\pilot.cer   # the pilot's certificate, exported on SBD-PKI-MGMT01 with Export-Certificate\n# On SBD-SUBCA01: revoke only the disposable test certificate, then publish a new CRL\ncertutil -revoke '<test certificate serial number>' 5   # 5 = cessation of operation\ncertutil -crl\n# Back on SBD-DC01: the test certificate must now fail as revoked\ncertutil -urlcache * delete\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\pki\\test.cer\n# After the scans (each carried to C:\\Evidence\\pki\\<host name> on SBD-DC01) and the checklist updates: record the new GPOs (course 1.19 backs them up), then seal the evidence\nGet-GPOReport -Name 'SBD Certificate Autoenrollment' -ReportType Html -Path C:\\Evidence\\pki\\gpo-report-autoenrollment.html   # and any root-trust GPO from lesson 5\n(Get-GPInheritance -Target 'DC=sbd,DC=test').GpoLinks | Select-Object Target, Order, DisplayName, Enabled, Enforced | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\pki\\gpo-links.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\pki -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\pki-manifest.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\pki-manifest.csv"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both controllers and the pilot enroll hands-free and verify with live retrieval from pki.sbd.test; after revocation and publication the test certificate is rejected for the expected reason; SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 have first baselines and both controllers a rescan; and the domain controller certificate rule is answered with its evidence, its DoD companion recorded as an exception."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the PKI checklist with each controller's and the pilot's certificate details, urlfetch and revocation results, renewal dates and owners, the items handed to course 1.19 for backup, the first baselines of SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01, the controllers' rescans, the updated checklist copies, the exception-record row, the GPO report and link export, and C:\\Evidence\\pki-manifest.csv with its own hash."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Publish trust data and validate clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/certificate-services-root-issuing-authority/6-publish-trust-data-validate-clients\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil\n\nMicrosoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nMicrosoft · On-premises key trust deployment guide (domain controller certificates)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/identity-protection/hello-for-business/deploy/on-premises-key-trust\n\nDISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · STIGs document library (STIGs, SRGs and the CCI list)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads")], question: "What does a hands-free pilot enrollment prove?", answers: ["Template permissions, autoenrollment policy, the CA, DNS, and the network path all agree — the whole chain works", "Only that Group Policy refreshed", "That the root CA is online"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Autoenrollment succeeds only when every layer this path has built cooperates — which is exactly why it is the closing test.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A valid certificate fails because its CRL cannot be retrieved. What is the next step?", ["Fix and retest the publication or reachability problem", "Disable revocation checking for all clients", "Copy the root private key to the client"], 0, "Reliable revocation publication is part of the service. Bypassing the check conceals the failed dependency."), QuizQuestion("Why schedule offline root ceremonies ahead of CRL expiry?", ["A replacement CRL must be transferred and published before the old one expires", "The root certificate expires monthly", "Clients delete their trust store on expiry"], 0, "If the root CRL lapses, revocation checking fails across the hierarchy. Manual transfer takes lead time, so it is scheduled early."), QuizQuestion("Which hosts get scanned at the end of this course?", ["Only the pilot machine", "SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 for their first baselines, and both domain controllers, which this course changed", "The offline root over the network"], 1, "The cadence is change-triggered: two new servers joined the domain and the controllers took new certificates, trust and policy, so two first baselines and two rescans.")], recap: ["Both controllers and the pilot enroll hands-free, with live revocation retrieval from pki.sbd.test.", "A revoked test certificate is rejected for the expected reason.", "CRL publication and renewal each have an owner and a date, backup and recovery are handed to course 1.19, and the closing scans and the closed controller finding finish the PKI chapter."], nextStep: "That completes Configure the two-tier PKI. Course 1.7, Run DHCP on SBD-DHCP01, builds the member server that leases VLAN 80 its addresses before any fleet exists.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/two-tier-pki#validate", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "dhcp", number: "1.7", title: "Run DHCP on SBD-DHCP01", subtitle: "Workstation leases, secure name registration & an audited lease trail", category: "Network build", description: "Design and build the workstation VLAN's addressing service on SBD-DHCP01: a full-subnet scope with exclusions and the three options clients trust, directory authorization, the core's relay verified rather than built, client names registered under one dedicated low-privilege account with name protection, a lease history kept beyond Windows' weekly overwrite, the restore note course 1.19's backup relies on, and a proof that a name takeover fails.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "dhcp-guide", title: "Run DHCP on SBD-DHCP01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workstation leases, secure name registration & an audited lease trail", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "dhcp-package", title: "Run DHCP on SBD-DHCP01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp/SecureByDesign-dhcp-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:dhcp:dhcp-design", title: "Design the addressing service", minutes: 25, summary: "DHCP decides the first things every workstation believes: its address, its gateway, and which servers answer its names. Design that service before building it — one scope for VLAN 80, the options clients will trust, and the identities that authorize, run, and register for it — and confirm the core already relays VLAN 80's requests.", objectives: ["Decide which segment gets leases and design its scope: range, exclusions, reservations, and lease length.", "Choose the options clients will trust and the identities that authorize, run, and register for the server.", "Confirm the register rows and the core's relay for VLAN 80 without changing the router."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.6: both domain controllers with DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, course 1.5's OU structure with the member-server STIG policies on the Servers OU, and course 1.6's PKI. The network's routing, default-deny segmentation and service-path register are already in place, including the core's DHCP relay for VLAN 80.\n\nProvision a Windows Server virtual machine for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10, VLAN 20) with a second volume for its audit-log copies (D: in this sample), and a disposable Windows client virtual machine on VLAN 80 for testing, named SBD-TEST80 in this sample, with sbd.test as its primary DNS suffix and left out of the domain. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization on both, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine.\n\nUntil course 1.8 builds the administration seats, Tier 0 steps (the directory, DNS, and authorizing the server, which takes an Enterprise Admins member) happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is built and run at its own console with the server-administration accounts lesson 2 creates."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-DHCP01 hands the workstation VLAN its addresses, and with them the gateway, the DNS servers, and the domain name every client will trust. This course designs that service, builds it on a member server, registers client names under one dedicated low-privilege account, keeps its lease trail, writes the restore note that course 1.19's backup of the server relies on, and proves it with a test client. It runs before the tiered administration plane exists: Tier 0 steps happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is run at its own console by a server-administration group that course 1.8 adopts as Tier 1. The core's DHCP relay is part of the network's build, so this course verifies it and never changes the router. Evidence goes in C:\\Evidence\\dhcp — on SBD-DHCP01 for the server's own records, and on SBD-DC01 for the Tier 0 steps — and course 1.8 carries each to its tier's seat. Scope values are sample values from Inside the Network."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Decide where DHCP runs and for whom. Only VLAN 80 gets leases — the 50 PCs, and in this course a temporary test client; every server, every admin seat, and the core keep static addresses, because the register names them by address. SBD-DHCP01 is a member server at 10.0.20.10 on VLAN 20, never a domain controller: a DHCP server on a controller inherits the controller's right to change any record in a secure DNS zone."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Design the scope the way Microsoft recommends: the whole subnet, 10.0.80.1–254 with mask 255.255.255.0, then exclusion ranges for what must never be leased — 10.0.80.1–99, which holds the core's gateway at .1, and 10.0.80.200–254 — leaving the pool at 10.0.80.100–199, as Inside the Network records it. One hundred addresses cover the fifty PCs, test clients, and replacement machines whose old leases haven't expired. Plan no reservations: a reservation is keyed to a MAC address that any device can claim, so it is an addressing convenience, never an identity check; if a device ever needs one, record it with an owner and a reason."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Set the lease length as the outage budget: with one DHCP server, a lease is how long clients keep working while SBD-DHCP01 is down. Keep the Windows default of 8 days as the sample value — clients try to renew halfway through (day 4) and rebind at seven-eighths (day 7) — and record your own value with that reason. Then choose the options, because every option is something every client trusts: router (option 3) 10.0.80.1, DNS servers (option 6) 10.0.10.10 and 10.0.10.11, both domain controllers and never an outside resolver, and DNS domain name (option 15) sbd.test. Add nothing else: no WINS, no boot server, no proxy auto-discovery."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Assign three identities. Authorizing a DHCP server writes to the forest's configuration partition, so an Enterprise Admins member does it — Tier 0 work, done at SBD-DC01's console until course 1.8 builds AdminJumpBox01. The server is built and run by named server-administration accounts in a new group, SBD Tier 1 Admins in this sample, because course 1.8 makes member-server administration Tier 1; and client names are registered by a third, unprivileged account in lesson 3."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Confirm the paths. Rule F04 in Inside the Network lets VLAN 80 reach SBD-DHCP01 on UDP 67/68 through the core's DHCP relay, owned by the network owner; the relay is part of the network's build, so verify it from the network's record with the network owner — it must forward to 10.0.20.10 and nowhere else, which lesson 2's first lease then proves — and take any gap to the network owner rather than changing the router. As a domain member, SBD-DHCP01 also uses F01 (DNS), F02 (Kerberos), F03 (directory), and F09 (time); its registrations ride F01, and A02 already lists it for course 1.8's Tier 1 seat."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# At SBD-DC01's console (Tier 0): the values and identities the design names\nResolve-DnsName SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.11; Resolve-DnsName SBD-DC02.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.10\nGet-ADOrganizationalUnit -Identity 'OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName\nGet-ADGroupMember 'Enterprise Admins' | Select-Object Name   # who can authorize a DHCP server\nGet-ADGroupMember DnsUpdateProxy   # expected: no members\nGet-DnsServerZone -ComputerName SBD-DC01 | Select-Object ZoneName, IsDsIntegrated, DynamicUpdate, ReplicationScope"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The design records VLAN 80 as the only leased segment, the full-subnet scope with its two exclusion ranges and no reservations, the lease length with its reason, the three options, who holds each of the three identities, and the network's confirmation that the core relays VLAN 80 to 10.0.20.10 under F04."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the DHCP design (scope, exclusions, lease length, options, identities), the relay confirmation with where it came from, and the register rows that name SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-top\n\nMicrosoft · DHCP scopes\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-scopes\n\nMicrosoft · Troubleshooting guide: DHCP authorization failures\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshooting-guide-dhcp-authorization-failures\n\nMicrosoft · DNS dynamic update\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/dynamic-update\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does SBD-DHCP01 run on a member server instead of a domain controller?", answers: ["DHCP can't be installed on a domain controller", "A DHCP server on a controller inherits the controller's right to change any record in a secure DNS zone", "Member servers hand out leases faster"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Microsoft's guidance is direct: on a domain controller, the DHCP service carries the controller's permissions in DNS. A member server that registers through a dedicated, unprivileged account keeps that power out of the address service.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The scope covers 10.0.80.1–254, but only .100–.199 are ever leased. What keeps the rest out of the pool?", ["Exclusion ranges inside the scope", "A reservation for every unused address", "The core drops requests for those addresses"], 0, "Microsoft recommends defining the whole subnet as the scope and excluding what must never be leased. The exclusions keep the core's gateway and the held-back ranges out of every offer."), QuizQuestion("Why does this course verify the core's DHCP relay instead of configuring it?", ["The relay is optional for VLAN 80", "SBD-DHCP01 configures the relay automatically", "The router belongs to the network's own build: later courses read and test it but never change it"], 2, "Routing, segmentation, and the relay already exist. This course confirms that F04 does what the register says and takes any gap to the network owner.")], recap: ["One leased segment, VLAN 80, with a full-subnet scope, two exclusions, and no reservations.", "The lease length is the outage budget, and three options are all clients get.", "Authorization, administration, and registration each have their own identity; the relay is confirmed, not built."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds SBD-DHCP01, authorizes it, and creates the scope.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp#dhcp-design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dhcp:dhcp", title: "Build SBD-DHCP01 and its scope", minutes: 30, summary: "Addressing is the first service fifty machines will ask for. Build the DHCP server on a member server run by its own administration accounts, authorize it in the directory — the check that keeps unauthorized Windows DHCP servers from serving — and create the VLAN 80 scope, then pull a first lease through the core's relay.", objectives: ["Build SBD-DHCP01 in the Servers OU, run by SBD Tier 1 Admins.", "Install and authorize the DHCP role, then create the VLAN 80 scope with the planned exclusions and options.", "Prove a first lease through the core's relay before any name is registered."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.6: both domain controllers with DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, course 1.5's OU structure with the member-server STIG policies on the Servers OU, and course 1.6's PKI. The network's routing, default-deny segmentation and service-path register are already in place, including the core's DHCP relay for VLAN 80.\n\nProvision a Windows Server virtual machine for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10, VLAN 20) with a second volume for its audit-log copies (D: in this sample), and a disposable Windows client virtual machine on VLAN 80 for testing, named SBD-TEST80 in this sample, with sbd.test as its primary DNS suffix and left out of the domain. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization on both, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine.\n\nUntil course 1.8 builds the administration seats, Tier 0 steps (the directory, DNS, and authorizing the server, which takes an Enterprise Admins member) happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is built and run at its own console with the server-administration accounts lesson 2 creates."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-DHCP01 hands the workstation VLAN its addresses, and with them the gateway, the DNS servers, and the domain name every client will trust. This course designs that service, builds it on a member server, registers client names under one dedicated low-privilege account, keeps its lease trail, writes the restore note that course 1.19's backup of the server relies on, and proves it with a test client. It runs before the tiered administration plane exists: Tier 0 steps happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is run at its own console by a server-administration group that course 1.8 adopts as Tier 1. The core's DHCP relay is part of the network's build, so this course verifies it and never changes the router. Evidence goes in C:\\Evidence\\dhcp — on SBD-DHCP01 for the server's own records, and on SBD-DC01 for the Tier 0 steps — and course 1.8 carries each to its tier's seat. Scope values are sample values from Inside the Network."), LessonSection("Step 1", "At SBD-DC01's console, create SBD Tier 1 Admins and a named server-administration account for each person who will run SBD-DHCP01, all in the Admin OU, and let the group create computer objects in the Servers OU. The delegation covers that OU and computer objects only, so the group can add servers but gains no rights over the ones already there, including SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01, which course 1.8 moves to their own OU."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Install Windows Server on SBD-DHCP01 with its static address (10.0.20.10/24, the core's 10.0.20.1 as gateway, both domain controllers as DNS servers) and its hypervisor time synchronization off, then, at its console, join it straight into the Servers OU with Add-Computer -OUPath and a server-administration account, so course 1.5's member-server policies apply from first boot. After the restart, still as the local Administrator (the STIG policy may have renamed it), make SBD Tier 1 Admins the only domain group in its local Administrators group, in place of Domain Admins, per the current member-server STIG; from then on, work as your server-administration account."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Install the DHCP role with its tools, create the DHCP Administrators and DHCP Users groups, and restart the service. An Enterprise Admins member then authorizes the server at SBD-DC01's console, after adding the DHCP management tools there (course 1.8 moves this work to AdminJumpBox01). Restart the DHCP Server service on SBD-DHCP01 so it checks its authorization now, and find event 1044: authorized, and servicing clients."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Create the scope inactive, exactly as designed: 10.0.80.1–254, mask 255.255.255.0, the two exclusion ranges, an 8-day lease, and the router, DNS server, and domain name options. Turn dynamic DNS updates off for now (DynamicUpdates Never): a record registered under the server's own computer account stays owned by that account, so lesson 3's dedicated account could never update it. Then activate the scope."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Verify the core's relay with the test client: on SBD-TEST80 in VLAN 80, release and renew, and confirm the lease came from 10.0.20.10 with the planned gateway, DNS servers, and suffix. The relay wrote its own VLAN 80 address, 10.0.80.1, into each request's gateway address field; that is how SBD-DHCP01 picked the 10.0.80.0 scope. On SBD-DHCP01, find the lease in the scope — and no DNS record yet, by design."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# At SBD-DC01's console (Tier 0): the group and its one delegation; then create each person's named\n# server-administration account in the Admin OU and add it to the group (New-ADUser, Add-ADGroupMember)\nNew-ADGroup 'SBD Tier 1 Admins' -GroupScope Global -Path 'OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\ndsacls 'OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' /G 'SBD\\SBD Tier 1 Admins:CC;computer'\n# On SBD-DHCP01 as its local Administrator: join with a server-administration account, then, after the restart:\nAdd-Computer -DomainName sbd.test -OUPath 'OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Credential (Get-Credential) -Restart\nAdd-LocalGroupMember Administrators -Member 'SBD\\SBD Tier 1 Admins'; Remove-LocalGroupMember Administrators -Member 'SBD\\Domain Admins'\n# As a server-administration account: the role and its local groups\nInstall-WindowsFeature DHCP -IncludeManagementTools; Add-DhcpServerSecurityGroup; Restart-Service DHCPServer\n# At SBD-DC01's console, as an Enterprise Admins member: authorize\nInstall-WindowsFeature RSAT-DHCP; Add-DhcpServerInDC -DnsName SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test -IPAddress 10.0.20.10\n# Back on SBD-DHCP01: the authorization check, then the scope, inactive until it is complete\nRestart-Service DHCPServer; Get-DhcpServerInDC\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{ProviderName='Microsoft-Windows-DHCP-Server'; Id=1044} -MaxEvents 1\nAdd-DhcpServerv4Scope -Name 'VLAN 80 workstations' -StartRange 10.0.80.1 -EndRange 10.0.80.254 -SubnetMask 255.255.255.0 -LeaseDuration 8.00:00:00 -State InActive\nAdd-DhcpServerv4ExclusionRange -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 -StartRange 10.0.80.1 -EndRange 10.0.80.99\nAdd-DhcpServerv4ExclusionRange -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 -StartRange 10.0.80.200 -EndRange 10.0.80.254\nSet-DhcpServerv4OptionValue -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 -Router 10.0.80.1 -DnsServer 10.0.10.10, 10.0.10.11 -DnsDomain sbd.test\nSet-DhcpServerv4DnsSetting -DynamicUpdates Never   # lesson 3 turns registration on\nSet-DhcpServerv4Scope -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 -State Active\nGet-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 10.0.80.0   # after SBD-TEST80 runs ipconfig /release and ipconfig /renew\nw32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, as course 1.3 designed"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Get-DhcpServerInDC lists SBD-DHCP01 at 10.0.20.10 and event 1044 shows it servicing clients; the scope covers 10.0.80.1–254 with exclusions .1–.99 and .200–.254, an 8-day lease, and the three options; DNS updates are off; SBD Tier 1 Admins is the only domain group in its local Administrators; its time comes from a domain controller; and the test client's first lease came from 10.0.20.10 through the relay."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the build sheet (OU placement, local Administrators, time source), the authorization output and event 1044, the scope, exclusion, and option configuration, and the test client's ipconfig /all with the matching lease."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-top\n\nMicrosoft · Deploy DHCP using Windows PowerShell\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-deploy-wps\n\nMicrosoft · Troubleshooting guide: DHCP authorization failures\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshooting-guide-dhcp-authorization-failures\n\nMicrosoft · Active Directory domain join permissions\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/active-directory-domain-join-permissions\n\nMicrosoft · DHCP troubleshooting guidance\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/troubleshoot-dhcp-guidance\n\nMicrosoft · DHCP server events\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-server-events")], question: "What does directory authorization of a DHCP server give you?", answers: ["Faster leases", "A built-in check that makes unauthorized Windows DHCP servers refuse to serve the domain", "Larger scopes"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Authorization is the directory's rogue-DHCP control — a one-click setting with a real security story behind it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does the relay exist at all?", ["DHCP broadcasts do not cross the router, so the core forwards VLAN 80's requests to the server in VLAN 20", "It encrypts DHCP traffic", "Relays make addresses permanent"], 0, "Broadcasts stop at the VLAN boundary. The relay is the deliberate, registered exception that carries them to SBD-DHCP01."), QuizQuestion("Why are dynamic DNS updates off until lesson 3?", ["The zone refuses updates from member servers", "DHCP can't register names until the scope has served for a day", "Whoever registers a record first owns it, so records created under the server's computer account would lock out the dedicated update account"], 2, "Ownership follows the first secure update. Turning registration on only after the dedicated account is in place means every VLAN 80 record starts with the right owner.")], recap: ["SBD-DHCP01 is built in the Servers OU, run by SBD Tier 1 Admins, and authorized.", "The VLAN 80 scope hands out the planned pool and options; DNS updates wait for lesson 3.", "A first lease proved the core's relay end to end."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 turns on secure name registration under a dedicated update account.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp#dhcp", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dhcp:dhcp-dns", title: "Register client names securely", minutes: 30, summary: "Course 1.2's zone accepts only secure updates, so the real question is who writes VLAN 80's records. Give that job to one dedicated, unprivileged account, turn on name protection so no device can take over another's name, and keep the DnsUpdateProxy group empty — it strips the protection off every record its members create.", objectives: ["Create the reverse zone and the dedicated update account, and give SBD-DHCP01 its credential.", "Register A and PTR records for every client, with name protection and cleanup when leases end.", "Explain why DnsUpdateProxy is a trap, and prove the records belong to the update account."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.6: both domain controllers with DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, course 1.5's OU structure with the member-server STIG policies on the Servers OU, and course 1.6's PKI. The network's routing, default-deny segmentation and service-path register are already in place, including the core's DHCP relay for VLAN 80.\n\nProvision a Windows Server virtual machine for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10, VLAN 20) with a second volume for its audit-log copies (D: in this sample), and a disposable Windows client virtual machine on VLAN 80 for testing, named SBD-TEST80 in this sample, with sbd.test as its primary DNS suffix and left out of the domain. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization on both, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine.\n\nUntil course 1.8 builds the administration seats, Tier 0 steps (the directory, DNS, and authorizing the server, which takes an Enterprise Admins member) happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is built and run at its own console with the server-administration accounts lesson 2 creates."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-DHCP01 hands the workstation VLAN its addresses, and with them the gateway, the DNS servers, and the domain name every client will trust. This course designs that service, builds it on a member server, registers client names under one dedicated low-privilege account, keeps its lease trail, writes the restore note that course 1.19's backup of the server relies on, and proves it with a test client. It runs before the tiered administration plane exists: Tier 0 steps happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is run at its own console by a server-administration group that course 1.8 adopts as Tier 1. The core's DHCP relay is part of the network's build, so this course verifies it and never changes the router. Evidence goes in C:\\Evidence\\dhcp — on SBD-DHCP01 for the server's own records, and on SBD-DC01 for the Tier 0 steps — and course 1.8 carries each to its tier's seat. Scope values are sample values from Inside the Network."), LessonSection("Step 1", "At SBD-DC01's console, create the reverse lookup zone for 10.0.80.0/24 — Active Directory-integrated, with the replication scope you chose in course 1.2, and secure updates only — so client PTR records have somewhere to go; course 1.2 created only 10.0.10's."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create the update account there too: a standard user (svc-dhcp-dns in this sample) in the Admin OU and in Domain Users only, with a long random password — at least the length the current STIG sets for manually managed application accounts — set not to expire, which the current STIG allows for an application account whose owner changes the password on the schedule the STIG sets and whenever someone who knew it leaves. It needs no DNS permission of its own: any authenticated account may create a record in a secure zone and becomes the owner of what it creates. Keep DnsUpdateProxy empty: records its members create are left unsecured, so the next device to update the name takes it over."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On SBD-DHCP01, store the credential with Set-DhcpServerDnsCredential, then set the server to register both A and PTR records for every client (DynamicUpdates Always), including clients that don't ask (UpdateDnsRRForOlderClients), to remove them when a lease expires or is deleted (DeleteDnsRROnLeaseExpiry), and to protect names (NameProtection). Name protection adds a DHCID record that ties each name to the client that registered it, and an update for that name from a different client fails instead of overwriting it."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On SBD-TEST80, renew the lease and watch registration happen: SBD-DHCP01's audit log shows the update requested (30) and done (32), both controllers answer for the name and the address, and a DHCID record sits beside the A record. At SBD-DC01's console, read the record's owner: it must be SBD\\svc-dhcp-dns, not SBD-DHCP01's computer account."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Record the decision: one update identity for VLAN 80, its owner, where the sealed password lives, and that after any password change you set the credential again on SBD-DHCP01, because registrations fail until you do. With every VLAN 80 record owned by that one account, the server cleans up names when leases end, so VLAN 80 doesn't depend on scavenging, which course 1.2 left as a separate decision. Course 1.13 finishes the picture for the fleet: it turns off the workstations' own DNS self-registration by GPO on the Workstations OU, with the reason recorded, so SBD-DHCP01, as svc-dhcp-dns, stays the only writer of their records."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# At SBD-DC01's console (Tier 0): the reverse zone (course 1.2's replication scope), the update account, an empty proxy group\nAdd-DnsServerPrimaryZone -NetworkId '10.0.80.0/24' -ReplicationScope Domain -DynamicUpdate Secure\nNew-ADUser svc-dhcp-dns -Path 'OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -AccountPassword (Read-Host -AsSecureString) -PasswordNeverExpires \$true -Enabled \$true\nGet-ADGroupMember DnsUpdateProxy   # expected: no members\n# On SBD-DHCP01: the credential, then registration for every client, with name protection\nSet-DhcpServerDnsCredential -Credential (Get-Credential 'SBD\\svc-dhcp-dns')\nSet-DhcpServerv4DnsSetting -DynamicUpdates Always -UpdateDnsRRForOlderClients \$true -DeleteDnsRROnLeaseExpiry \$true -NameProtection \$true\nGet-DhcpServerDnsCredential; Get-DhcpServerv4DnsSetting\n# On SBD-TEST80: renew, then ask both controllers for the name, the address, and the DHCID record\nipconfig /renew\nResolve-DnsName SBD-TEST80.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.11\nResolve-DnsName (Resolve-DnsName SBD-TEST80.sbd.test -Type A -Server 10.0.10.10).IPAddress -Type PTR -Server 10.0.10.10\nResolve-DnsName SBD-TEST80.sbd.test -Type DHCID -Server 10.0.10.10\n# At SBD-DC01's console: the record's owner (sbd.test lives in DomainDnsZones in this sample)\nImport-Module ActiveDirectory\n(Get-Acl 'AD:DC=SBD-TEST80,DC=sbd.test,CN=MicrosoftDNS,DC=DomainDnsZones,DC=sbd,DC=test').Owner   # SBD\\svc-dhcp-dns"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The 10.0.80 reverse zone exists with secure updates; svc-dhcp-dns is in Domain Users only and DnsUpdateProxy is empty; Get-DhcpServerDnsCredential names svc-dhcp-dns; the server registers A and PTR records for every client with name protection and cleanup on; and the test client's A, PTR, and DHCID records answer from both controllers, owned by SBD\\svc-dhcp-dns."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the zone and account settings, the empty DnsUpdateProxy result, the server's DNS settings and the credential's account name (never the password), the audit-log lines for the update, both controllers' answers, and the record's owner."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Deploy DHCP using Windows PowerShell\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-deploy-wps\n\nMicrosoft · DNS dynamic update\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/dynamic-update\n\nMicrosoft · Set-DhcpServerv4DnsSetting\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/set-dhcpserverv4dnssetting\n\nMicrosoft · Set-DhcpServerDnsCredential\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/set-dhcpserverdnscredential\n\nMicrosoft · Add-DnsServerPrimaryZone\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dnsserver/add-dnsserverprimaryzone")], question: "Why use a dedicated credential for dynamic DNS updates?", answers: ["The server cannot update DNS otherwise", "It speeds up record creation", "Record ownership and updates trace to one low-privilege identity instead of a powerful computer account"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Secure updates from course 1.2 are only as accountable as the identity writing them — a scoped credential keeps that clean.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What happens to records created by members of the DnsUpdateProxy group?", ["They're left unsecured, so the next client to update the name becomes its owner", "They're encrypted in the zone", "Only Domain Admins can change them"], 0, "The group exists for DHCP servers that must share records, at the cost of securing them. With one server and one dedicated update account, it stays empty."), QuizQuestion("With name protection on, a new device asks the server to register a name another client already holds. What happens?", ["The new device's address replaces the old record", "The update fails, because the name's DHCID record belongs to a different client", "Both addresses are kept under the name"], 1, "The DHCID record ties each name to the client that registered it. Without name protection, the server would overwrite the record, and any device could take a name just by asking for it.")], recap: ["VLAN 80's names are written by one dedicated, unprivileged account, not a computer account.", "Name protection ties each name to its client, and DnsUpdateProxy stays empty.", "The test client's records answer from both controllers with the right owner."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 keeps the server's lease trail and backups.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp#dhcp-dns", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dhcp:dhcp-audit", title: "Audit leases and write the restore note", minutes: 30, summary: "A router's deny log names addresses, not machines; the DHCP audit log is how you turn an address at a moment in time into a workstation. Keep that log longer than the week Windows keeps it, review who holds leases, know what an unauthorized DHCP server can and can't do here, and write the restore note that course 1.19's backup of the server relies on.", objectives: ["Read the DHCP audit log and keep each day's file beyond its weekly reuse.", "Review leases and the authorized-server list, and explain what an unauthorized DHCP server does in a domain.", "Explain why the automatic database copy isn't a backup, and write the restore note course 1.19's DHCP backup relies on."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.6: both domain controllers with DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, course 1.5's OU structure with the member-server STIG policies on the Servers OU, and course 1.6's PKI. The network's routing, default-deny segmentation and service-path register are already in place, including the core's DHCP relay for VLAN 80.\n\nProvision a Windows Server virtual machine for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10, VLAN 20) with a second volume for its audit-log copies (D: in this sample), and a disposable Windows client virtual machine on VLAN 80 for testing, named SBD-TEST80 in this sample, with sbd.test as its primary DNS suffix and left out of the domain. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization on both, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine.\n\nUntil course 1.8 builds the administration seats, Tier 0 steps (the directory, DNS, and authorizing the server, which takes an Enterprise Admins member) happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is built and run at its own console with the server-administration accounts lesson 2 creates."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-DHCP01 hands the workstation VLAN its addresses, and with them the gateway, the DNS servers, and the domain name every client will trust. This course designs that service, builds it on a member server, registers client names under one dedicated low-privilege account, keeps its lease trail, writes the restore note that course 1.19's backup of the server relies on, and proves it with a test client. It runs before the tiered administration plane exists: Tier 0 steps happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is run at its own console by a server-administration group that course 1.8 adopts as Tier 1. The core's DHCP relay is part of the network's build, so this course verifies it and never changes the router. Evidence goes in C:\\Evidence\\dhcp — on SBD-DHCP01 for the server's own records, and on SBD-DC01 for the Tier 0 steps — and course 1.8 carries each to its tier's seat. Scope values are sample values from Inside the Network."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Read the audit log. Windows writes one file per weekday (DhcpSrvLog-Mon.log, and so on) in %windir%\\System32\\dhcp, each line carrying an event code, the time, the address, the host name, and the MAC address: 10 for a new lease, 11 a renewal, 12 a release, 30 and 32 a DNS update requested and done, 31 a failed one. Each file is reused a week later, and logging pauses when free disk space drops below the configured minimum, so check Get-DhcpServerAuditLog and keep that volume's free space well above it."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Keep every day: a daily task, running as SYSTEM shortly after midnight, copies the previous day's file to D:\\DHCP\\AuditArchive under its date and appends its SHA-256 hash to a catalog. Keep the copies as long as your records requirement says — the period depends on your environment and policy, so record the value you chose and why — because lease history is what ties an address in any log to a machine."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Review the leases each week: scope statistics show how full the pool is, and the lease list should hold only machines you know — today, just the test client. An unknown host name or MAC address is a finding to trace, not something to delete. The server's own event logs record every scope, option, exclusion, and reservation change with the account that made it, and warn when the scope is nearly full."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Know what an unauthorized DHCP server can do here. A Windows DHCP server in the domain that isn't authorized refuses to lease and logs event 1046, and a workgroup Windows server stops when it meets an authorized one on its network; a server of any other kind ignores authorization entirely and can hand clients its own gateway and DNS servers, a ready-made interception point. So check that Get-DhcpServerInDC lists SBD-DHCP01 alone and that clients' DHCP server is 10.0.20.10, and record that network controls, such as 802.1X admission in course 1.13, carry the rest."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the restore note. Windows backs the DHCP database up every 60 minutes, but to %windir%\\System32\\dhcp\\backup on the same disk, so that copy fails with the server and isn't a backup of it. Course 1.19 defines and schedules SBD-DHCP01's backup — the database and an export of the configuration and leases — with every other system's, so this course sets up no backup job. What belongs here is what a restore will need: the DNS update credential isn't in a DHCP backup, so after any restore it is set again with Set-DhcpServerDnsCredential from the sealed record, before names register."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-DhcpServerAuditLog   # Enable: True; Path: C:\\Windows\\system32\\dhcp\n\$dir = Join-Path \$env:windir 'System32\\dhcp'\nGet-Content (Join-Path \$dir ('DhcpSrvLog-{0}.log' -f (Get-Date).ToString('ddd', [cultureinfo]'en-US'))) -Tail 5\n# The daily task's work (SYSTEM, 00:15): yesterday's file kept under its date, with its hash\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory D:\\DHCP\\AuditArchive -Force | Out-Null\n\$y = (Get-Date).AddDays(-1); \$copy = 'D:\\DHCP\\AuditArchive\\DhcpSrvLog-{0:yyyy-MM-dd}.log' -f \$y\nCopy-Item (Join-Path \$dir ('DhcpSrvLog-{0}.log' -f \$y.ToString('ddd', [cultureinfo]'en-US'))) \$copy\nGet-FileHash \$copy -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv D:\\DHCP\\AuditArchive\\catalog.csv -Append -NoTypeInformation\n# Weekly review: pool use, who holds leases, who may serve\nGet-DhcpServerv4ScopeStatistics -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Select-Object ScopeId, InUse, Free, PercentageInUse\nGet-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Select-Object IPAddress, HostName, ClientId, AddressState, LeaseExpiryTime\nGet-DhcpServerInDC   # SBD-DHCP01 alone\n# For the restore note: the automatic copy sits on the same disk (BackupPath), so course 1.19 schedules the real backup\nGet-DhcpServerDatabase\nGet-DhcpServerDnsCredential   # the account the note says to set again after any restore (never the password)"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Audit logging is on with free space to spare; yesterday's file sits in the archive under its date, with its hash in the catalog; the lease list holds only known machines; Get-DhcpServerInDC lists SBD-DHCP01 alone; and the restore note names the credential step for course 1.19's backup."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the audit log settings, the archive catalog, the dated lease review, the authorized-server list, and the restore note for course 1.19."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · DNS dynamic update\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/dynamic-update\n\nMicrosoft · DHCP server events\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-server-events\n\nMicrosoft · Set-DhcpServerAuditLog\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/set-dhcpserverauditlog\n\nMicrosoft · Backup-DhcpServer\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/backup-dhcpserver\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "Why copy the DHCP audit log every day?", answers: ["Windows reuses each weekday's file a week later, so without copies the lease history is gone after seven days", "The audit log is encrypted until it's copied", "Copied logs make leases renew faster"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The audit log is the bridge between an address in a router or firewall log and a machine. A week of history is rarely enough for an investigation, so the dated, hashed copies are the record.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A server that isn't running Windows starts answering DHCP requests on VLAN 80. What does directory authorization do about it?", ["It shuts the rogue server down", "Nothing: authorization is a check Windows DHCP servers run on themselves, so other servers ignore it", "It deletes the rogue server's leases"], 1, "Authorization stops unauthorized Windows servers in the domain. Anything else is caught by network controls such as 802.1X admission, and by checking that clients got their lease from 10.0.20.10."), QuizQuestion("SBD-DHCP01 is restored from a backup. What must happen before names register again?", ["Nothing: everything is in the backup", "The domain controllers must be authorized again", "The DNS update credential must be set again, because the backup doesn't include it"], 2, "Microsoft notes that DHCP's DNS credentials aren't backed up. Keeping that step in the restore note that course 1.19 files with the backup means the rebuilt server registers names as the dedicated account again.")], recap: ["Each day's lease history is archived with a hash before Windows reuses the file.", "Leases and the authorized-server list are reviewed; authorization stops only Windows servers.", "The automatic database copy isn't a backup: course 1.19 backs the server up, and the credential step is written for its restore."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 proves the whole service with the test client and takes SBD-DHCP01's first baseline.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp#dhcp-audit", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dhcp:dhcp-prove", title: "Prove the service and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "Prove DHCP the way clients will meet it: the test client gets the right lease and its name registers securely, a device that tries to take that name is refused, and the evidence lands in the logs. Then take SBD-DHCP01's first SCAP baseline and hand the server to course 1.8, which moves its administration to the Tier 1 seat.", objectives: ["Prove the lease, the options, and secure registration from the test client.", "Prove name protection with a deliberate takeover attempt.", "Take SBD-DHCP01's first SCAP baseline, seal the record, and write the handoff to course 1.8."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.6: both domain controllers with DNS (course 1.2) and the domain time hierarchy (course 1.3), course 1.4's DISA media set and baseline method, course 1.5's OU structure with the member-server STIG policies on the Servers OU, and course 1.6's PKI. The network's routing, default-deny segmentation and service-path register are already in place, including the core's DHCP relay for VLAN 80.\n\nProvision a Windows Server virtual machine for SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10, VLAN 20) with a second volume for its audit-log copies (D: in this sample), and a disposable Windows client virtual machine on VLAN 80 for testing, named SBD-TEST80 in this sample, with sbd.test as its primary DNS suffix and left out of the domain. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization on both, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine.\n\nUntil course 1.8 builds the administration seats, Tier 0 steps (the directory, DNS, and authorizing the server, which takes an Enterprise Admins member) happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is built and run at its own console with the server-administration accounts lesson 2 creates."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-DHCP01 hands the workstation VLAN its addresses, and with them the gateway, the DNS servers, and the domain name every client will trust. This course designs that service, builds it on a member server, registers client names under one dedicated low-privilege account, keeps its lease trail, writes the restore note that course 1.19's backup of the server relies on, and proves it with a test client. It runs before the tiered administration plane exists: Tier 0 steps happen at SBD-DC01's console, and SBD-DHCP01 is run at its own console by a server-administration group that course 1.8 adopts as Tier 1. The core's DHCP relay is part of the network's build, so this course verifies it and never changes the router. Evidence goes in C:\\Evidence\\dhcp — on SBD-DHCP01 for the server's own records, and on SBD-DC01 for the Tier 0 steps — and course 1.8 carries each to its tier's seat. Scope values are sample values from Inside the Network."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-TEST80, release and renew, and check everything the client now believes: an address from 10.0.80.100–199, DHCP server 10.0.20.10, gateway 10.0.80.1, DNS servers 10.0.10.10 and 10.0.10.11, suffix sbd.test, and an 8-day lease. On SBD-DHCP01, find the same lease and its audit-log lines, and confirm both controllers answer for the name and the address."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Try to take the name: shut the test client down, give it a new MAC address in the hypervisor — to the server, a new client with a new DHCID — and start it again. It gets a lease, but its registration is refused: event 1340 on SBD-DHCP01, a failed update (31) in the audit log, and the name still resolving to the original address. Restore the original MAC address, renew, delete the stray lease, and confirm the name still points at the test client."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Take SBD-DHCP01's first SCAP baseline the course 1.4 way: copy SCC and the Windows Server STIG benchmark from the course 1.4 media set into C:\\Staging\\DISA, check them against the connected-side-hashes.csv that travels with the media, install the same SCC version, and scan locally with the same profile. Store the session unmodified in C:\\Evidence\\dhcp\\baseline, classify each open finding as fix-by-GPO, deviation candidate, or POA&M candidate, and leave the manual checks for a STIG Viewer checklist on the Tier 1 seat in course 1.8."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Seal both halves of the record, each into its own manifest: C:\\Evidence\\dhcp on SBD-DHCP01 (design, build sheet, scope export, registration and takeover results, audit catalog, restore note, baseline) and C:\\Evidence\\dhcp on SBD-DC01 (authorization, reverse zone, update account, record owner). Course 1.8 carries each to its tier's seat."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the handoff to course 1.8: SBD-DHCP01 serves VLAN 80 and registers names as svc-dhcp-dns; SBD Tier 1 Admins runs it, still at its console; the test client stays for course 1.8's proof, where it joins the domain as the Tier 2 test machine; course 1.13 adds the Network Policy Server role for 802.1X to this server; and course 1.19 schedules its backup."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-TEST80: release, renew, and read what the client believes\nipconfig /release; ipconfig /renew; ipconfig /all\n# On SBD-DHCP01: the lease, then, after the MAC change, the refused takeover\nGet-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Select-Object IPAddress, HostName, ClientId, LeaseExpiryTime\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{ProviderName='Microsoft-Windows-DHCP-Server'; Id=1340} -MaxEvents 1 | Format-List TimeCreated, Message\nResolve-DnsName SBD-TEST80.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.10   # still the original address\nRemove-DhcpServerv4Lease -IPAddress '<stray lease address>'   # after the original MAC is back\n# Before installing SCC: no output means every copied file is on the connected-side list\n(Get-ChildItem C:\\Staging\\DISA\\* -File -Exclude *.csv | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash | Where-Object { \$_ -notin (Import-Csv C:\\Staging\\DISA\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash }\n# After the SCC scan: keep the session unmodified (adapt the path to SCC's results directory), then seal the folder\n\$session = Get-ChildItem (Join-Path \$env:USERPROFILE 'SCC\\Sessions') -Directory | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 1\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory C:\\Evidence\\dhcp\\baseline -Force | Out-Null\nCopy-Item \$session.FullName -Destination C:\\Evidence\\dhcp\\baseline\\SBD-DHCP01 -Recurse\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\dhcp -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\dhcp-manifest.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\dhcp-manifest.csv\n# Run the same two lines at SBD-DC01's console for the Tier 0 half, its own C:\\Evidence\\dhcp"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The test client holds a lease from the pool with every planned option; its A, PTR, and DHCID records answer from both controllers and belong to SBD\\svc-dhcp-dns; the takeover attempt was refused with event 1340 and left the record unchanged; SBD-DHCP01 has a first baseline with every open item classified; and both manifests and the handoff are filed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the client's ipconfig /all, the lease and audit-log lines, the takeover attempt with event 1340, the SCC session with its hashes and classified findings, both manifests with their own hashes, and the handoff note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Set-DhcpServerv4DnsSetting\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/set-dhcpserverv4dnssetting\n\nMicrosoft · DHCP server events\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-server-events\n\nDISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why change the test client's MAC address during the proof?", answers: ["To test the relay again", "To the server, a new MAC address is a new client, so its claim on the same name tests name protection", "To get a longer lease"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The DHCID is derived from the client's identifier. A different client asking for a name that's already registered is exactly the takeover name protection must refuse.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where does SBD-DHCP01's first baseline get its tools and content?", ["From the course 1.4 media set, checked against its connected-side hashes, at the same SCC and benchmark versions", "From the newest versions on DISA's website, downloaded on the server", "From the domain controllers' staging folder over the network"], 0, "A baseline is comparable only when the tool, the benchmark, and the profile match course 1.4's record, and nothing from outside is opened before its hash is checked."), QuizQuestion("What does course 1.8 change for SBD-DHCP01?", ["It moves the server to VLAN 60", "It replaces the update account", "Its administration moves to the Tier 1 seat, SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and the tier rules start applying to it"], 2, "The server keeps its job and its identities. What changes is where it's administered from, and which sign-ins the directory refuses on it.")], recap: ["The test client's lease, options, and secure records are proven from both sides.", "A name takeover was refused and logged.", "SBD-DHCP01 is baselined, its record sealed, and course 1.8 takes over its administration."], nextStep: "That completes Run DHCP on SBD-DHCP01. Course 1.8 builds the tiered administration seats and moves SBD-DHCP01's administration to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dhcp#dhcp-prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "tiered-admin", number: "1.8", title: "Tiered administration", subtitle: "Admin seats, the tier rules & where privilege may sign in", category: "Network build", description: "Build the administration plane before the fleet: three administration seats on two machines in VLAN 60 (the endpoint seat runs as a virtual machine on the server seat), directory-enforced tier restrictions proven by both successful in-tier sessions and logged out-of-tier denials, and the move of earlier console work — SBD-DHCP01's administration and the assessment tools — onto the seats.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-guide", title: "Tiered administration · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Admin seats, the tier rules & where privilege may sign in", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "tiered-admin-package", title: "Tiered administration · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin/SecureByDesign-tiered-admin-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:design", title: "Design the tiers and the admin seats", minutes: 20, summary: "Tiering is one sentence with teeth: credentials that control a tier never touch a less-trusted one. Write that policy against the OU structure and give each tier its seat and its group — all before any host is built.", objectives: ["Define the tiers and map each to systems, OUs, and admin accounts.", "Assign each tier its admin seat under the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) privileged access workstation (PAW) rules.", "Write the credential rule each tier enforces."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: SBD-DHCP01 serves VLAN 80 and is run at its console by course 1.7's SBD Tier 1 Admins, with the VLAN 80 test client still in place. Course 1.6's two-tier PKI and course 1.5's OU structure, with the Admin OU, are already built, and the network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server hosts for the two physical administration seats in VLAN 60, AdminJumpBox01 (.10) and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), and plan SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30) as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 running the Enterprise or Education edition of the Windows client. SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 needs hardware virtualization and an input–output memory management unit (IOMMU); where it is itself a virtual machine, turn on nested virtualization and media access control (MAC) address spoofing for it; where either seat is a virtual machine in your lab, turn off its hypervisor time synchronization, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine; and record what your lab can't provide, such as Credential Guard, as a lab limitation.\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights.\n\nHave course 1.4's media set at hand — SCC, STIG Viewer, and the benchmarks, with connected-side-hashes.csv — because lesson 5 moves the assessment tools from the domain controllers onto AdminJumpBox01, gives SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 its own Tier 1 copy, and installs SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, to baseline the Tier 2 seat."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the administration plane before the fleet exists: a tiered model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space. To save a machine, the three seats run on two: the endpoint seat is a virtual machine on the server seat, the arrangement the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows Privileged Access Workstation (PAW) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) allows where workstations are scarce. The Tier 0 seat runs no virtual machines. Work that earlier courses did at consoles moves onto the seats: SBD-DHCP01's administration (course 1.7) to the Tier 1 seat, and the assessment tools and evidence (course 1.4) from the domain controllers to AdminJumpBox01, with a Tier 1 copy of the tools on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy for Tier 2."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Define three tiers against what exists, using Microsoft's rule: anything that can control a Tier 0 system is Tier 0. Tier 0 is the identity and trust fabric (DCs, both CAs, PKI-MGMT01) plus what controls it — the hypervisor hosts and their console, the core router's management, and the DC-facing parts of the tools that patch, protect, scan, monitor, and back up the DCs. Tier 1 is the member servers; Tier 2 the user endpoints. Map each tier to its OUs: Tier 0 to the Domain Controllers OU plus a new Tier 0 Servers OU for SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (lesson 3 moves them out of the Servers OU), Tier 1 to the Servers OU, where course 1.7's SBD-DHCP01 already sits, and Tier 2 to the Workstations OU."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Assign the admin seats under DISA's Windows PAW Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG): each privileged access workstation serves one tier (V-243445), and the one that manages the domain controllers is reserved for that work (V-243454, high severity). Where workstations are scarce, V-243445 allows one exception: lower-tier admin accounts in a virtual machine on a higher-tier seat, with the higher-tier accounts on the host. This network uses it to run three seats on two machines: AdminJumpBox01 for Tier 0, running no virtual machines, and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 for Tier 1, which also runs SBD-ADMIN-PC01, the Tier 2 seat, as a virtual machine with its own name and address. Record the STIG differences in the exception record (AdminJumpBox01 manages all of Tier 0, not only the domain controllers; both physical seats run Windows Server where the STIG expects a Windows client, V-243447), and the PAW rules left open, such as smart-card sign-in (V-243457) and application allowlisting (V-243450, V-243451)."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Write the credential rule per tier: a Tier 0 account signs in only on Tier 0 systems and AdminJumpBox01; Tier 1 admins work on the SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 host; Tier 2 admins only inside the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine, never on its host. The audit custodian uses separate accounts and sessions on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 that ordinary server operators cannot use, and is refused on Tier 2 machines like the higher tiers. Each administrator gets separate per-tier accounts — the daily account holds no admin rights anywhere — gathered in one group per role in the Admin OU: SBD Tier 0 Admins, SBD Tier 1 Admins (course 1.7 created it for SBD-DHCP01), SBD Tier 2 Admins, and SBD Audit Custodians in this sample."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Add the register rows this course needs: VLAN 60 to each tier's management ports (rules A01–A04 in Inside the Network), and explicit denies from VLAN 80 into VLAN 60 — user space never initiates toward the admin plane. A02 already names SBD-DHCP01, whose administration moves to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 in lesson 2."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# At SBD-DC01's console until lesson 2 builds AdminJumpBox01: OU targets, current account separation, and the role groups.\nGet-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName\nGet-ADGroupMember 'Domain Admins' | Select-Object Name, SamAccountName\n'SBD Tier 0 Admins', 'SBD Tier 2 Admins', 'SBD Audit Custodians' | ForEach-Object { New-ADGroup \$_ -GroupScope Global -Path 'OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' }\nGet-ADGroup -SearchBase 'OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name   # SBD Tier 1 Admins came from course 1.7"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The tier map names systems, OUs, admin seats, accounts, and groups per tier, with SBD-ADMIN-PC01 a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01; the exception record holds the seat plan's STIG differences and open PAW items; the role groups exist in the Admin OU; and the register has the new permit and deny rows with owners."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the tier access matrix, the seat plan with its STIG rationale and recorded differences, the per-administrator account plan with its groups, and the updated register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nMicrosoft · Tier model for Active Directory Domain Services\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/tier-model\n\nDISA · STIGs Document Library (Windows PAW STIG)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What is the one-sentence tiering rule?", answers: ["Admins should use strong passwords", "Credentials that control a tier never sign in to a less-trusted tier", "Every admin needs Domain Admin rights"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Everything else in the model — hosts, OUs, logon policies — exists to enforce that single sentence.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does each administrator get separate per-tier accounts?", ["So a credential captured on a lower tier cannot open a higher one", "Licensing counts accounts per tier", "Password policies require it"], 0, "Credential theft travels with sign-ins. Separate accounts bound what any one capture is worth."), QuizQuestion("Which direction does the register explicitly deny?", ["Admin VLAN to the tiers it manages", "DCs to anything", "User VLAN 80 initiating into the admin VLAN 60"], 2, "The admin plane reaches down to manage; user space never reaches up. The deny row makes that a logged fact, not an intention."), QuizQuestion("SBD-WSUS01 approves the updates the domain controllers install. Which tier administers the domain controllers' update ring?", ["Tier 1, because WSUS is a member server", "Tier 0, because anything that can push code to a DC can control it", "Tier 2, because updates start on workstations"], 1, "Microsoft's tier model treats systems that patch, scan, monitor, protect, back up, or host Tier 0 as Tier 0. The DC-facing scope of a tool belongs to Tier 0 even when the server itself sits in a member-server VLAN."), QuizQuestion("To save a machine, one admin seat will run another as a virtual machine. Which arrangement does DISA's Windows PAW STIG accept?", ["The Tier 2 seat as a virtual machine on AdminJumpBox01", "Tier 1 and Tier 2 accounts both signed in on the SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 host", "The Tier 2 seat as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, with Tier 1 accounts on the host and Tier 2 accounts only inside it"], 2, "The exception puts lower-tier admin accounts in a virtual machine on a higher-tier seat, with the higher-tier accounts on the host. The seat that manages the domain controllers is reserved for that work, so it never hosts a lower tier.")], recap: ["Three tiers, each with its systems, OU, admin seat, accounts, and group.", "The credential rule is written before any host enforces it.", "Each tier's management path and the VLAN 80 → VLAN 60 deny are register rows."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds the seats the administrators will work from: two physical systems and one virtual machine.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:admin-hosts", title: "Build the administration systems", minutes: 45, summary: "Privileged work gets dedicated, hardened seats: a Tier 0 jump host that runs no virtual machines, and a server-administration seat that also runs the endpoint seat as a virtual machine. Each seat sits in the Admin OU, does nothing but administer, and is maintained by its own group.", objectives: ["Link the hardening policies to the Admin OU's computer sub-OUs, then build the two seats and the seat virtual machine into them, each with its own Group Policy object (GPO) and maintenance group.", "Run SBD-ADMIN-PC01 as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 with its own name and address, and close the channels that share data between host and guest.", "Keep the seats single-purpose, with each tier's tools on its own seat, and move earlier courses' console work onto them.", "Register and restrict their management paths."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: SBD-DHCP01 serves VLAN 80 and is run at its console by course 1.7's SBD Tier 1 Admins, with the VLAN 80 test client still in place. Course 1.6's two-tier PKI and course 1.5's OU structure, with the Admin OU, are already built, and the network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server hosts for the two physical administration seats in VLAN 60, AdminJumpBox01 (.10) and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), and plan SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30) as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 running the Enterprise or Education edition of the Windows client. SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 needs hardware virtualization and an input–output memory management unit (IOMMU); where it is itself a virtual machine, turn on nested virtualization and media access control (MAC) address spoofing for it; where either seat is a virtual machine in your lab, turn off its hypervisor time synchronization, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine; and record what your lab can't provide, such as Credential Guard, as a lab limitation.\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights.\n\nHave course 1.4's media set at hand — SCC, STIG Viewer, and the benchmarks, with connected-side-hashes.csv — because lesson 5 moves the assessment tools from the domain controllers onto AdminJumpBox01, gives SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 its own Tier 1 copy, and installs SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, to baseline the Tier 2 seat."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the administration plane before the fleet exists: a tiered model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space. To save a machine, the three seats run on two: the endpoint seat is a virtual machine on the server seat, the arrangement the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows Privileged Access Workstation (PAW) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) allows where workstations are scarce. The Tier 0 seat runs no virtual machines. Work that earlier courses did at consoles moves onto the seats: SBD-DHCP01's administration (course 1.7) to the Tier 1 seat, and the assessment tools and evidence (course 1.4) from the domain controllers to AdminJumpBox01, with a Tier 1 copy of the tools on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy for Tier 2."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Create two computer sub-OUs under the Admin OU — Admin Servers for AdminJumpBox01 and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Admin Workstations for the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine — and link course 1.5's STIG GPOs to them before any seat joins: the member-server computer policy, the user policy, and the loopback (Merge) GPO to Admin Servers, and the Windows client computer and user policies, which course 1.5 imported but left unlinked, with the same loopback GPO to Admin Workstations. Course 1.5 linked them only to the Domain Controllers and Servers OUs, so without this step the seats would boot unhardened."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Build AdminJumpBox01 and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as clean Windows Server installs and join both into Admin Servers, so the hardening applies from first boot; AdminJumpBox01 never gets the Hyper-V role or a virtual machine, because the seat that manages the domain controllers never hosts a lower tier. Give every seat its own seat GPO, linked above the STIG GPO — one on Admin Workstations, and two on Admin Servers, each filtered to its seat (deny Apply group policy to the other) — that makes the seat's own maintenance group (SBD PAW Maintenance T0, T1, or T2 in this sample), used for nothing else, the only domain group in its local Administrators: the PAW STIG gives tier accounts user rights only on their seat (V-243456, V-243462), and no group spans tiers. SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's GPO also adds NT VIRTUAL MACHINE\\Virtual Machines (*S-1-5-83-0) to Create symbolic links beside Administrators: Group Policy replaces the right's local value, and without that entry, which the STIG accepts on Hyper-V hosts, the virtual machine won't start. Lesson 3 adds each seat's sign-in rights."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Add the Hyper-V role to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and create SBD-ADMIN-PC01 as a generation 2 virtual machine with Secure Boot and a virtual Trusted Platform Module (TPM), set to start whenever the host starts. The Windows client needs the TPM, and Credential Guard, which the Windows client STIG requires, runs in a virtual machine only on generation 2 and on a host with an input–output memory management unit (IOMMU). Connect it through an external virtual switch on the seat's VLAN 60 adapter with its own address, 10.0.60.30, install the Enterprise or Education edition of the Windows client, and join it into Admin Workstations. Because it keeps its own name and address, the register's rule for SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (A03) still names it alone, never its host."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Close the channels that share data between host and guest, because the virtual machine separates the tiers only while they share nothing they don't need: turn off enhanced session mode on the host, so the console carries only screen, keyboard, and mouse, with no shared clipboard, drives, or devices; keep the Guest Service Interface off, so the host can't copy files in; turn off Key-Value Pair Exchange, which passes data both ways; turn off Time Synchronization, because the guest takes its time from the domain like every virtual machine (course 1.3); and add no shared folders. What remains, the basic integration services and PowerShell Direct (which can run commands and copy files either way), needs Hyper-V administrator rights on the host, and PowerShell Direct also needs an account in the guest. Administrators who hold both a Tier 1 and a Tier 2 account open the console from the host with the Tier 1 account, which SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's seat GPO puts in the local Hyper-V Administrators group — the one privileged local group the PAW STIG lets a Hyper-V seat fill (V-243463), so they need no host administrator rights — and sign in inside with the Tier 2 one. One person uses the console at a time and signs out of the virtual machine before closing it, because without enhanced session mode the next person to connect takes over an open desktop; a Tier 2-only administrator needs a seat of their own."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Strip them to purpose: no browsing to anything but internal admin interfaces, no mail, no file shares hosted, and the management tools for exactly their tier — Remote Server Administration Tools (RSAT) on the two server seats, endpoint tooling inside the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine — with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 running that one virtual machine and no other. Then move the console work earlier courses did onto the seats: Tier 0 directory, DNS, and DHCP authorization work moves from SBD-DC01's console to AdminJumpBox01, so remove the DHCP management tools course 1.7 added to SBD-DC01; and SBD-DHCP01 is administered from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 with Tier 1 accounts, over the remote desktop and remote management paths rule A02 registers, no longer at its console."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Register the management paths: VLAN 60 to Tier 0 and Tier 1 ports (remote desktop 3389, remote management 5985–5986, AD Web Services 9389 to the domain controllers, SSH 22 for the domain-joined Linux servers) from the specific admin host each tier allows, and the router's management on VLAN 100 from AdminJumpBox01 only; keep the VLAN 80 → VLAN 60 deny from lesson 1 in force. Require the admin seats' own local firewalls, the virtual machine's included, to accept management inbound from nowhere — sessions originate here, they never terminate here, so neither the host nor its guest can manage the other over the network. On both domain controllers, scope the existing Remote Desktop, Windows Remote Management, and Active Directory Web Services firewall rules to AdminJumpBox01 and VLAN 10: allow rules add up, so a new, narrower rule would not restrict the broad ones already enabled."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Admin Servers,OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nGet-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Admin Workstations,OU=Admin,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\nGet-LocalGroupMember Administrators   # each seat: its maintenance group, no tier group\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: its one virtual machine, and what host and guest share.\nGet-VM | Select-Object Name, Generation, State, AutomaticStartAction   # Start\n(Get-VMFirmware SBD-ADMIN-PC01).SecureBoot; (Get-VMSecurity SBD-ADMIN-PC01).TpmEnabled\nGet-VMHost | Select-Object EnableEnhancedSessionMode   # False\nGet-VMIntegrationService SBD-ADMIN-PC01 | Select-Object Name, Enabled   # Guest Service Interface, Key-Value Pair Exchange, Time Synchronization: False\nGet-LocalGroupMember 'Hyper-V Administrators'   # the console users' Tier 1 accounts\nResolve-DnsName SBD-ADMIN-PC01.sbd.test   # 10.0.60.30\nInvoke-Command SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test { Get-DhcpServerv4Scope }   # Tier 1 account, over remote management (A02)\n# Inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01: Credential Guard is running when the list includes 1.\n\$dg = Get-CimInstance Win32_DeviceGuard -Namespace root\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\n\$dg.SecurityServicesRunning\nw32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, never the VM IC Time Synchronization Provider\n# On AdminJumpBox01: Hyper-V stays Available, never Installed.\nGet-WindowsFeature Hyper-V | Select-Object Name, InstallState\nUninstall-WindowsFeature RSAT-DHCP -ComputerName SBD-DC01   # course 1.7's DHCP tools leave the controller\nTest-NetConnection SBD-DC01.sbd.test -Port 5985\ngpresult /r /scope:computer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "AdminJumpBox01 and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 sit in Admin Servers and the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine in Admin Workstations, with the STIG and seat GPOs applied; each seat's local Administrators holds its maintenance group, no tier group; SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 runs only SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (10.0.60.30, generation 2, Secure Boot on, TPM, Credential Guard, starts with the host) with enhanced session mode, the Guest Service Interface, Key-Value Pair Exchange, and Time Synchronization off, and the guest takes its time from a domain controller; AdminJumpBox01 has no Hyper-V role; SBD-DHCP01 is managed from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and the DHCP tools are gone from SBD-DC01; management ports answer from the correct seat per tier; and user space can open no session toward VLAN 60."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record each seat's build sheet (for the virtual machine: host, generation, address), OU placement, GPO links, gpresult output, local group members, host and guest settings with the guest's time source, the administration moves, and register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nDISA · STIGs Document Library (Windows PAW STIG)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nMicrosoft · Configure a firewall for Active Directory domains\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts\n\nMicrosoft · Manage Hyper-V Integration Services\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/manage/manage-hyper-v-integration-services\n\nMicrosoft · Hyper-V Virtual Machine Connection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/virtual-machine-connection\n\nMicrosoft · Plan for Hyper-V security in Windows Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/plan/plan-hyper-v-security-in-windows-server\n\nMicrosoft · Credential Guard overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/identity-protection/credential-guard/\n\nMicrosoft · Hyper-V virtual machine creation troubleshooting guidance\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/virtualization/troubleshoot-hyper-v-virtual-machine-creation")], question: "Why do the admin hosts run nothing but administration?", answers: ["Extra software costs licenses", "Every extra capability — mail, browsing, documents — is a way to phish or poison the most valuable sessions on the network", "Admin tools conflict with browsers"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "These hosts carry privileged credentials. Minimizing what runs on them minimizes what can steal from them.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which way do management sessions flow?", ["From the admin hosts outward to their tier — never inbound to the admin hosts", "In both directions equally", "From workstations to the jump box"], 0, "The admin plane originates; it never serves. Inbound-nothing is what keeps a compromised endpoint from riding into VLAN 60."), QuizQuestion("Why link the STIG GPOs to the Admin sub-OUs before the seats join?", ["The seats cannot join the domain otherwise", "The OU name requires it", "Course 1.5 linked them only to the Domain Controllers and Servers OUs, so the seats would otherwise boot unhardened"], 2, "Policy follows links, not names. Linking first means each seat is STIG-configured from its first boot, before it ever does privileged work."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 run with enhanced session mode off?", ["It makes the virtual machine start faster", "Enhanced session mode carries the clipboard, drives, and devices between host and guest — exactly what the tier boundary must stop", "The Windows client cannot run in enhanced session mode"], 1, "The virtual machine separates the tiers only while host and guest share nothing they don't need. Enhanced session mode shares local resources such as the clipboard, drives, and devices with the guest; without it, the console carries only the screen, keyboard, and mouse."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-ADMIN-PC01 keep its own name and address as a virtual machine?", ["So the register and the host firewalls still tell the Tier 2 seat apart from its host", "Hyper-V cannot start a virtual machine without its own address", "It lets the virtual machine skip the Admin Workstations policies"], 0, "Rule A03 names SBD-ADMIN-PC01 alone, and later courses let the workstations accept management only from 10.0.60.30. With its own address, the Tier 2 seat stays a separate source on the network, and its host gets nothing those rules allow."), QuizQuestion("Who holds administrator rights on the admin seats?", ["The tier accounts that work on each seat", "Each seat's own maintenance group, used for nothing else", "Domain Admins, added when each seat joins the domain"], 1, "The PAW STIG keeps the accounts that manage high-value systems to user rights on their seat, so they can't weaken it. A maintenance group per seat builds and patches it, so no group spans tiers, and the seat GPO makes it the only domain group in that seat's local Administrators.")], recap: ["Two seats and a seat virtual machine, hardened from first boot; AdminJumpBox01 runs no virtual machines.", "Each seat has its own maintenance group; host and guest share no clipboard, drives, or folders.", "Management flows outward only; VLAN 60 accepts nothing inbound.", "Every management path is a register row."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 makes the credential rule something the directory enforces.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#admin-hosts", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:logon-restrictions", title: "Restrict where privilege can sign in", minutes: 45, summary: "The tier rule becomes real when the directory refuses the wrong sign-in. Give Tier 0 servers their own OU first, deny privileged logons on lower tiers by policy, protect the highest accounts, and make the refusal something you can show in a log.", objectives: ["Move the Tier 0 member servers into their own OU before any deny policy lands.", "Enforce per-tier sign-in restrictions and administrators with Group Policy — on the tier OUs, every admin seat, and both sides of the seat virtual machine — and record the one network-logon exception.", "Protect Tier 0 accounts and prove a cross-tier sign-in is denied and logged."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: SBD-DHCP01 serves VLAN 80 and is run at its console by course 1.7's SBD Tier 1 Admins, with the VLAN 80 test client still in place. Course 1.6's two-tier PKI and course 1.5's OU structure, with the Admin OU, are already built, and the network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server hosts for the two physical administration seats in VLAN 60, AdminJumpBox01 (.10) and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), and plan SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30) as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 running the Enterprise or Education edition of the Windows client. SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 needs hardware virtualization and an input–output memory management unit (IOMMU); where it is itself a virtual machine, turn on nested virtualization and media access control (MAC) address spoofing for it; where either seat is a virtual machine in your lab, turn off its hypervisor time synchronization, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine; and record what your lab can't provide, such as Credential Guard, as a lab limitation.\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights.\n\nHave course 1.4's media set at hand — SCC, STIG Viewer, and the benchmarks, with connected-side-hashes.csv — because lesson 5 moves the assessment tools from the domain controllers onto AdminJumpBox01, gives SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 its own Tier 1 copy, and installs SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, to baseline the Tier 2 seat."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the administration plane before the fleet exists: a tiered model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space. To save a machine, the three seats run on two: the endpoint seat is a virtual machine on the server seat, the arrangement the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows Privileged Access Workstation (PAW) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) allows where workstations are scarce. The Tier 0 seat runs no virtual machines. Work that earlier courses did at consoles moves onto the seats: SBD-DHCP01's administration (course 1.7) to the Tier 1 seat, and the assessment tools and evidence (course 1.4) from the domain controllers to AdminJumpBox01, with a Tier 1 copy of the tools on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy for Tier 2."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Create the Tier 0 Servers OU (OU=Tier 0 Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test) with deletion protection, link course 1.5's member-server STIG GPOs to it — the computer policy, the user policy, and the loopback GPO — so nothing loses its hardening, and move SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 there. Both are Tier 0, and the Servers OU is about to deny Tier 0 sign-ins — left there, they would lock Tier 0 out of its own systems."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create one tier logon GPO for the Servers OU — now Tier 1 only — and one for the Workstations OU, and never link either to the Domain Controllers or Tier 0 Servers OUs. User rights don't merge across GPOs: for each right, the highest-precedence GPO that sets it wins, so each list repeats the entries course 1.5's STIG GPO sets for that right (such as Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins), and the tier GPO links above the STIG GPO. On the Servers OU, give SBD Tier 0 Admins Deny log on locally, Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services, Deny log on as a batch job, and Deny log on as a service. On the Workstations OU, give SBD Tier 0 Admins, SBD Tier 1 Admins, and SBD Audit Custodians those four denies plus Deny access to this computer from the network: the custodian's accounts administer the audit record, so they never sign in where Tier 2 risks apply."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Give the tier OUs their administrators. Now that the Tier 0 servers are out, a GPO linked only to the Servers OU adds SBD Tier 1 Admins to each server's local Administrators group and removes Domain Admins (Group Policy Preferences, Local Users and Groups), as the member-server STIG expects, so every server joined there, starting with SBD-DHCP01, is run by Tier 1 from first boot; kept apart from the tier logon GPO, it lets an OU with its own administrators, such as course 1.11's Audit Servers OU, take the denies without this grant. Then join course 1.7's VLAN 80 test client to the domain with a Tier 2 account, delegated Create Computer objects on the Workstations OU as course 1.7 did for Tier 1 on the Servers OU; the course 1.5 redirect lands it in Workstations. Add SBD Tier 2 Admins to its local Administrators and turn on remote management there, accepted only from SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (10.0.60.30), so it can serve as this course's Tier 2 test machine."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On the Servers OU, set Deny access to this computer from the network — SBD Tier 0 Admins plus the STIG's entries — in a separate GPO that excludes the SBD Tier 0 Console Hosts group through security filtering (deny Apply group policy). Course 1.14 adds SBD-WSUS01 to that group, so Tier 0 can manage the domain controllers' update ring there from AdminJumpBox01 with Tier 0 task accounts outside Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins (which the STIG denies network logon on member servers), and SBD-EPO01's console will join it when course 1.16, coming soon, arrives; a Kerberos network logon leaves no reusable credentials on the server. Record the exception — the group, the servers as they join it, the accounts, the reason, and its owner — in the course 1.5 exception record. Link both deny GPOs, this one and the tier logon GPO, to any OU created later for member servers, such as course 1.11's Audit Servers OU, before a server joins it; step 3's administrators GPO never goes there."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Finish the seat GPOs from lesson 2 so each seat admits only its own tier, on both sides of the seat virtual machine. Allow log on locally replaces the STIG's list with Administrators and the seat's own groups: SBD Tier 0 Admins on AdminJumpBox01 (every Tier 0 account, Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins members included), SBD Tier 1 Admins and SBD Audit Custodians on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and SBD Tier 2 Admins inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (V-243452; V-243455 for the seat that manages Active Directory). The four denies plus Deny access to this computer from the network go to the other tiers: Tier 0, Tier 1, and SBD Audit Custodians inside the virtual machine, Tier 0 and Tier 2 on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1 and Tier 2 on AdminJumpBox01. The PAW STIG requires the higher-tier denies (V-243453, high severity if a Tier 0 account can sign in). Each deny list repeats the STIG GPO's entries for that right, except that AdminJumpBox01 leaves Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins out of Deny log on locally, because V-243455 admits them there. Record every STIG value these settings change in the exception record."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Add Tier 0 administrators to the Protected Users group so the domain refuses their weaker authentication paths, and note the behavior of the newest Windows Server release that supports this design: RC4 ticket-granting tickets are gone and legacy remote password changes are blocked for protected accounts. Protected Users accounts also cannot be delegated, which keeps the console exception free of reusable credentials. Keep the built-in safety valve: one documented break-glass account, excluded from the deny policies and used at a domain controller's console (the admin seats admit only their own tiers), with its password sealed and its every use alarmed once course 1.12 builds the alerts. Record the multifactor decision the model calls for on VLAN 60 — smart card or certificate-based sign-in for privileged accounts is the natural follow-on to course 1.6's templates — with its target date, even if implementation waits."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Tier 0 Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name\nGet-ADGroupMember 'Protected Users' | Select-Object Name\nInvoke-Command SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test { Get-LocalGroupMember Administrators }   # from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: SBD Tier 1 Admins, no Domain Admins\ndsacls 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' /G 'SBD\\SBD Tier 2 Admins:CC;computer'   # from AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0)\n# On the test client, as its local administrator: join with a Tier 2 account, then, after the restart, its managers\nAdd-Computer -DomainName sbd.test -Credential (Get-Credential) -Restart\nAdd-LocalGroupMember Administrators -Member 'SBD\\SBD Tier 2 Admins'; Enable-PSRemoting -Force\nSet-NetFirewallRule -DisplayGroup 'Windows Remote Management' -RemoteAddress 10.0.60.30\n# On the Tier 2 test machine: export the effective logon rights and read the deny lines.\nsecedit /export /mergedpolicy /cfg C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\user-rights.inf /areas user_rights\nSelect-String -Path C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\user-rights.inf -Pattern 'SeDeny'\n'SBD Tier 0 Admins', 'SBD Tier 1 Admins', 'SBD Audit Custodians' | ForEach-Object { (Get-ADGroup \$_).SID.Value }   # from an admin seat: these SIDs must appear on those lines\n# Repeat on each seat as its maintenance account, and read who may sign in there.\nSelect-String -Path C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\user-rights.inf -Pattern 'SeInteractive'\n# After the refused Tier 0 sign-in: event 4625 with status 0xC000015B (logon type not granted).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Where-Object Message -match '0xC000015B' | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 sit in the Tier 0 Servers OU and still accept Tier 0 sign-ins; the Servers OU's administrators GPO puts SBD Tier 1 Admins, not Domain Admins, in SBD-DHCP01's local Administrators; on the Tier 2 test machine, course 1.7's test client now in the Workstations OU, the deny rights list the SIDs of the Tier 0, Tier 1, and audit custodian groups, and a Tier 0 sign-in there is refused with Security event 4625 (status 0xC000015B); each admin seat lets only its own groups sign in and denies the other tiers — Tier 0, Tier 1, and the audit custodians inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01, Tier 0 and Tier 2 on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1 and Tier 2 on AdminJumpBox01; the exception record holds the console network-logon exception and the seat GPOs' STIG differences; Protected Users contains the Tier 0 admins; and the break-glass and MFA decisions are written."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the Tier 0 Servers OU move, the logon-rights and administrators GPO reports with their links and security filtering, the user-rights exports from the test machine and each seat, the refused sign-in's 4625 event, the exception record entries, the Protected Users membership, and the break-glass and MFA decision records."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nDISA · STIGs Document Library (Windows PAW STIG)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nMicrosoft · User Rights Assignment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/user-rights-assignment\n\nMicrosoft · Administrative tools and logon types\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/securing-privileged-access/reference-tools-logon-types\n\nMicrosoft · Protected Users security group\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/credentials-protection-and-management/protected-users-security-group\n\nMicrosoft · secedit /export\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/secedit-export\n\nMicrosoft · 4625(F): An account failed to log on\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4625\n\nMicrosoft · Active Directory domain join permissions\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/active-directory-domain-join-permissions\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server (newest release)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025")], question: "What turns the tier rule from policy into enforcement?", answers: ["An email to administrators", "Deny-logon rights applied by GPO at each lower tier's OU", "Renaming the admin accounts"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The OU-linked deny rights are the mechanism: the directory itself refuses the out-of-tier sign-in.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does Protected Users membership buy a Tier 0 account?", ["Faster sign-ins", "Exemption from password changes", "The domain refuses its weaker authentication paths, shrinking what a credential thief can replay"], 2, "Protected Users trades convenience for resistance — appropriate exactly for the accounts whose theft ends the network."), QuizQuestion("Why does a break-glass account exist outside the deny policies?", ["So a policy mistake cannot lock every administrator out — and its sealed, alarmed use keeps the exception honest", "Because every admin wants one", "Deny policies cannot apply to more than ten accounts"], 0, "Strict tiering needs one documented escape hatch. Sealing and alarming it is what keeps the hatch from becoming a habit."), QuizQuestion("Why do SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 move out of the Servers OU before the deny policy is linked?", ["The Servers OU has reached its size limit", "They are Tier 0: the Servers OU now denies Tier 0 sign-ins, so they would lock Tier 0 out of its own systems", "Certificate Services cannot run in an OU with linked policies"], 1, "Tier follows control, not hostname. Systems that control Tier 0 need an OU where Tier 0 can sign in and the lower-tier denies never apply."), QuizQuestion("Why may Tier 0 still use a network logon on SBD-WSUS01 once course 1.14 builds it?", ["Tier 0 manages the domain controllers' update ring there, and a Kerberos network logon leaves no reusable credentials on the server", "Network logons are never logged", "SBD-WSUS01 sits in the Tier 0 Servers OU"], 0, "Interactive, Remote Desktop, batch, and service logons leave reusable credentials behind; a Kerberos network logon does not, and Protected Users accounts cannot be delegated. The exception is narrow, named, and recorded."), QuizQuestion("Why deny Tier 1 accounts inside the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine when its host is the Tier 1 seat?", ["Tier 1 accounts cannot use Hyper-V", "Virtual machines refuse every domain account by default", "The virtual machine is a Tier 2 system: a Tier 1 credential used inside it could be stolen there"], 2, "The host's tier doesn't travel into the guest. Inside the virtual machine, Tier 2 accounts work and Tier 2 risks apply, so a higher-tier sign-in there — or an audit custodian's — is exactly the exposure the tier rule forbids.")], recap: ["Tier 0 servers have their own OU, so the deny policies never lock Tier 0 out.", "Lower tiers refuse higher-tier credentials by policy, with events to prove it, and each admin seat admits only its own tier; the console network-logon exception is recorded.", "Tier 0 accounts sit in Protected Users, backed by the stricter defaults of the newest Windows Server release.", "Break-glass and MFA are written decisions, not loose ends."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 tests the whole plane — the paths that must work and the sign-ins that must fail.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#logon-restrictions", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:prove", title: "Prove the tiers end to end", minutes: 30, summary: "Run the matrix: every tier's administration works from its seat, every cross-tier attempt fails with an event, and course 1.7's addressing service still behaves exactly as registered.", objectives: ["Verify each tier's management path from its designated seat.", "Capture the denial evidence for out-of-tier attempts, across the seat virtual machine's boundary too.", "Re-prove course 1.7's DHCP service and secure registration under the finished rules."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: SBD-DHCP01 serves VLAN 80 and is run at its console by course 1.7's SBD Tier 1 Admins, with the VLAN 80 test client still in place. Course 1.6's two-tier PKI and course 1.5's OU structure, with the Admin OU, are already built, and the network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server hosts for the two physical administration seats in VLAN 60, AdminJumpBox01 (.10) and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), and plan SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30) as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 running the Enterprise or Education edition of the Windows client. SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 needs hardware virtualization and an input–output memory management unit (IOMMU); where it is itself a virtual machine, turn on nested virtualization and media access control (MAC) address spoofing for it; where either seat is a virtual machine in your lab, turn off its hypervisor time synchronization, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine; and record what your lab can't provide, such as Credential Guard, as a lab limitation.\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights.\n\nHave course 1.4's media set at hand — SCC, STIG Viewer, and the benchmarks, with connected-side-hashes.csv — because lesson 5 moves the assessment tools from the domain controllers onto AdminJumpBox01, gives SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 its own Tier 1 copy, and installs SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, to baseline the Tier 2 seat."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the administration plane before the fleet exists: a tiered model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space. To save a machine, the three seats run on two: the endpoint seat is a virtual machine on the server seat, the arrangement the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows Privileged Access Workstation (PAW) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) allows where workstations are scarce. The Tier 0 seat runs no virtual machines. Work that earlier courses did at consoles moves onto the seats: SBD-DHCP01's administration (course 1.7) to the Tier 1 seat, and the assessment tools and evidence (course 1.4) from the domain controllers to AdminJumpBox01, with a Tier 1 copy of the tools on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy for Tier 2."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From AdminJumpBox01, manage a DC and SBD-SUBCA01 with a Tier 0 account — the Tier 0 Servers OU kept that access; from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, manage SBD-DHCP01 with a Tier 1 account; from the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine, opened from the host by a Tier 1 account without administrator rights there, manage the VLAN 80 test client with a Tier 2 account. Record each success with its session details, including the target's Security event 4624 (and 4672, which marks a logon that received special privileges)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Attempt the forbidden combinations — Tier 0 credentials at the test client, a Tier 1 account and an audit custodian account inside the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine, a Tier 2 account on its host, a session from VLAN 80 toward VLAN 60 — and capture both the refusal the user sees and the record the infrastructure writes: Security event 4625 with status 0xC000015B on the system that refused each sign-in, and the router's deny-log entry for the VLAN 80 session."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Prove the virtual machine and its host stay apart: remote management attempts between SBD-ADMIN-PC01 and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 fail in both directions at the target's firewall (they share VLAN 60, so no router sees them), and the console opens as a basic session, with no clipboard or drive sharing."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Renew the test client's lease and confirm course 1.7's results still hold with every restriction active — the options, the relay, and the records owned by the DHCP update account; the control plane must not have broken the service plane."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Reconcile the tier access matrix against what you just proved and file it with the register — the two documents together are the admin plane's assessable record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Enter-PSSession SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test   # from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1 account\nvmconnect.exe localhost SBD-ADMIN-PC01   # same account, not elevated: the console opens\nTest-NetConnection SBD-ADMIN-SRV01.sbd.test -Port 5985   # from the guest: must fail\nTest-NetConnection SBD-ADMIN-PC01.sbd.test -Port 5985   # from the host: must fail\nipconfig /renew   # on the VLAN 80 test client\n# Where a sign-in was refused: last hour's 0xC000015B failures (logon type not granted).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Where-Object Message -match '0xC000015B' | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every in-tier path succeeds, every tested out-of-tier path fails with captured evidence — in both directions across the seat virtual machine's boundary too — and course 1.7's DHCP service and secure registration still work end to end under the full rule set."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the completed tier matrix with timestamps, the denial captures, the host and guest separation results, and the post-restriction lease and registration proof."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Privileged access workstations and the access model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/privileged-access-workstations/overview\n\nDISA · STIGs Document Library (Windows PAW STIG)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nMicrosoft · 4625(F): An account failed to log on\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4625\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WinEvent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent\n\nMicrosoft · Configure a firewall for Active Directory domains\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/active-directory/config-firewall-for-ad-domains-and-trusts\n\nMicrosoft · Use local resources on Hyper-V virtual machine with VMConnect\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/use-local-resources-virtual-machine-connection")], question: "What pairs make the denial evidence complete?", answers: ["Two screenshots of the same error", "The user-visible refusal plus the infrastructure's event or deny-log entry", "A verbal report"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Same standard as every boundary check: the client's view shows the attempt, the log shows the boundary deciding — together they prove enforcement.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why re-test DHCP after the restrictions landed?", ["Hardening the control plane can accidentally break the service plane — proving both still work is the point", "Leases expire during lessons", "The relay resets daily"], 0, "Every tightening risks collateral damage. The matrix ends with services proven healthy, not presumed."), QuizQuestion("What is the admin plane's assessable record?", ["The jump box's wallpaper", "A list of administrator names", "The tier access matrix reconciled with the ACL register"], 2, "Who may manage what, from where, over which path — matrix plus register answers it in a form an assessor can test."), QuizQuestion("A Tier 1 account is refused inside the SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine. Where is the 4625 event written?", ["On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, because it hosts the virtual machine", "Inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01, the system that refused the logon type", "Nowhere: virtual machines do not write Security events"], 1, "The system that refuses a sign-in records it. The virtual machine has its own Security log, so that is where the evidence for its boundary lives.")], recap: ["In-tier works, out-of-tier fails — across the seat virtual machine's boundary too — and both are on record.", "Addressing and registration survived the restrictions.", "Matrix and register reconcile as one assessable story."], nextStep: "The final lesson moves the assessment tools onto the seats, runs the scans, and hands a managed network to course 1.9.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:tiered-admin:scan", title: "Move the scan tools, rescan, and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "Three new seats joined, SBD-DHCP01 changed hands, and the directory's logon policy changed — the cadence fires again. Move the assessment tools off the domain controllers onto the seats, baseline the three seats, rescan what changed, and hand the next course a network administered only from somewhere safe.", objectives: ["Move SCC, STIG Viewer, and the evidence from the domain controllers onto the seats, each tier on its own.", "Baseline the three seats, SBD-ADMIN-PC01 from its own SCC copy, and rescan the changed systems.", "Fold the course's decisions into the standing records and write the handoff to course 1.9."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.7: SBD-DHCP01 serves VLAN 80 and is run at its console by course 1.7's SBD Tier 1 Admins, with the VLAN 80 test client still in place. Course 1.6's two-tier PKI and course 1.5's OU structure, with the Admin OU, are already built, and the network's routing and default-deny segmentation, with its access control list (ACL) register, are in place.\n\nProvision Windows Server hosts for the two physical administration seats in VLAN 60, AdminJumpBox01 (.10) and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (.20), and plan SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (.30) as a virtual machine on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 running the Enterprise or Education edition of the Windows client. SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 needs hardware virtualization and an input–output memory management unit (IOMMU); where it is itself a virtual machine, turn on nested virtualization and media access control (MAC) address spoofing for it; where either seat is a virtual machine in your lab, turn off its hypervisor time synchronization, as course 1.3 requires of every virtual machine; and record what your lab can't provide, such as Credential Guard, as a lab limitation.\n\nDecide the privileged accounts: separate admin identities per tier, never daily-driver accounts with admin rights.\n\nHave course 1.4's media set at hand — SCC, STIG Viewer, and the benchmarks, with connected-side-hashes.csv — because lesson 5 moves the assessment tools from the domain controllers onto AdminJumpBox01, gives SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 its own Tier 1 copy, and installs SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, to baseline the Tier 2 seat."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course builds the administration plane before the fleet exists: a tiered model where privileged sessions only ever start from dedicated hosts. The model's rule is strict — the jump systems administer; they browse nothing, read no mail, and accept no inbound sessions from user space. To save a machine, the three seats run on two: the endpoint seat is a virtual machine on the server seat, the arrangement the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Windows Privileged Access Workstation (PAW) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) allows where workstations are scarce. The Tier 0 seat runs no virtual machines. Work that earlier courses did at consoles moves onto the seats: SBD-DHCP01's administration (course 1.7) to the Tier 1 seat, and the assessment tools and evidence (course 1.4) from the domain controllers to AdminJumpBox01, with a Tier 1 copy of the tools on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy for Tier 2."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Move the assessment tools onto the seats, the last step of course 1.4's convention. Install SCC and STIG Viewer on AdminJumpBox01 from the course 1.4 media set, checked against connected-side-hashes.csv and at the same versions, and copy C:\\Evidence from SBD-DC01 — the baseline and hardening folders, course 1.6's pki folder, and the course 1.7 Tier 0 folders, their manifests, and the exception record; once every set matches its manifest, remove SCC from both controllers and STIG Viewer from SBD-DC01, and retire the controllers' copy of the evidence. Each tier then assesses its own systems from its own seat: SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 gets its own copy of the tools and course 1.7's C:\\Evidence\\dhcp, and scans member servers locally in Tier 1 sessions, because A02 carries remote desktop and remote management, not the file-sharing and RPC ports SCC's remote scans use; and SBD-ADMIN-PC01 gets its own SCC copy, carried into the virtual machine on read-only media — an ISO image of the course 1.4 media set, attached to its virtual DVD drive from the host, because the Guest Service Interface stays off — checked against connected-side-hashes.csv, at the same version as the other seats' copies, with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged. Keep the checked installer and the hash list in C:\\Staging\\DISA inside the virtual machine, where course 1.13 reuses them for the fleet's scans, and detach the ISO."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Run first baselines on the three seats with the standard tool versions — AdminJumpBox01 and SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 with the Windows Server benchmark, SBD-ADMIN-PC01 with the Windows client benchmark, scanned locally in a Tier 2 session from its own copy — and rescan what this course changed: SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01, whose move to the Tier 0 Servers OU should leave their hardening intact; SBD-DHCP01, whose administrators and logon rights now come from the tier GPOs; and both domain controllers from AdminJumpBox01, whose firewall rules lesson 2 scoped and whose results also show the moved tools reproduce course 1.5's. Complete SBD-DHCP01's STIG Viewer checklist on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 with the manual answers course 1.7 left open."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Add this course's rows to the living records: the deny-logon, local Administrators, and Protected Users decisions into the exception record where they interact with STIG values, beside lesson 3's console network-logon exception, and the new permit and deny rows into the register with owners. The exception record stays one record, kept on AdminJumpBox01 by its owner, who adds the rows that Tier 1 and Tier 2 work sends in."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Confirm the admin hosts appear in course 1.5's quarterly cadence; privileged seats are rescanned first from now on, not last."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the handoff: administration now has a home, so course 1.9 can build SBD-REPO01 administered over SSH from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 alone, and the logging courses (1.10–1.12) can require that collectors, the archive, and SIEM consoles are managed only from VLAN 60 — with the DC-facing scopes from the Tier 0 seat and audit retention under the custodian's separate accounts. Say so explicitly for course 1.9 and the courses after it."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): carry the evidence over; no output means a set matches its manifest\nCopy-Item \\\\SBD-DC01\\C\$\\Evidence -Destination C:\\ -Recurse\n'baseline', 'hardened', 'pki', 'dhcp' | ForEach-Object { Compare-Object (Import-Csv \"C:\\Evidence\\\$_-manifest.csv\").Hash (Get-ChildItem \"C:\\Evidence\\\$_\" -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash }\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01, SBD-DC02 { Test-Path 'C:\\Program Files*\\SCAP Compliance Checker*' }   # False once SCC is removed\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (Tier 1): course 1.7's server record, checked the same way\n\$s = New-PSSession SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test\nCopy-Item C:\\Evidence\\dhcp, C:\\Evidence\\dhcp-manifest.csv -FromSession \$s -Destination C:\\Evidence\\ -Recurse\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv C:\\Evidence\\dhcp-manifest.csv).Hash (Get-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\dhcp -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash\n# Still on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, as a Hyper-V administrator: the media set's ISO into the Tier 2 seat, read-only\nSet-VMDvdDrive -VMName SBD-ADMIN-PC01 -Path '<course 1.4 media set ISO>'\n# Inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (Tier 2), with the ISO as D: the installer matches the record (expected: True); keep both for course 1.13\n\$scc = Get-Item 'D:\\<SCC installer>'\n(Get-FileHash \$scc.FullName -Algorithm SHA256).Hash -in (Import-Csv D:\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\\Staging\\DISA | Out-Null\nCopy-Item \$scc.FullName, D:\\connected-side-hashes.csv -Destination C:\\Staging\\DISA\\\nSet-VMDvdDrive -VMName SBD-ADMIN-PC01 -Path \$null   # back on the host, after the install: detach the ISO\n# On each seat, after its scans: this course's evidence, hashed\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin -Recurse | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\tiered-admin\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SCC and STIG Viewer run from AdminJumpBox01 and are gone from the controllers, every moved evidence set matches its manifest, and each seat has its tier's tools; first baselines for the three seats, SBD-ADMIN-PC01's from its own SCC copy, and rescans of SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and both domain controllers are filed with attributed deltas; SBD-DHCP01's checklist is complete; the exception record and register carry the new rows; and the handoff names the management constraint for course 1.9 and the logging courses."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: the tool and evidence moves with their hash comparisons, SBD-ADMIN-PC01's installer check, the scan set, the updated records, the tier matrix, and the handoff note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Which systems are rescanned, not baselined, this time?", answers: ["SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and both domain controllers — this course changed each of them", "The brand-new admin hosts", "The offline root"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "New host, new baseline; changed host, rescan. The Tier 0 servers changed OU, SBD-DHCP01 took its rights from the tier GPOs, and the controllers' firewall rules were scoped, so each rescan shows its change kept the hardening.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why do the privileged seats move to the front of the quarterly cadence?", ["They have the fastest disks", "They carry the credentials that control everything else — drift there costs the most", "Scanners work alphabetically"], 1, "Scan priority follows consequence. The hosts that hold Tier 0 sessions earn the first look every quarter."), QuizQuestion("What does this course hand to course 1.9?", ["Fifty workstations", "The business services", "A safe management plane — SBD-REPO01, the collectors, and the SIEM consoles can be administered only from VLAN 60"], 2, "Infrastructure that holds packages and logs is a target; building it after the admin plane means it is managed safely from day one."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 get its own copy of SCC instead of member servers being scanned from AdminJumpBox01?", ["SCC can run only one scan at a time", "AdminJumpBox01 is the Tier 0 seat: Tier 0 accounts are denied on member servers, and Tier 1 accounts can't sign in there", "Member servers need an older SCC version"], 1, "Assessment follows the tier rule like any other administration. Each tier scans its own systems from its own seat, with the same verified tool and benchmark versions.")], recap: ["The assessment tools and the evidence moved onto the seats, one tier per seat.", "Baselines, rescans, and attributed deltas are on file, and the records absorbed the course's decisions.", "Course 1.9 inherits a network administered only from VLAN 60."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.8. Course 1.9 builds SBD-REPO01, the Linux repository, administered from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 before any Linux server exists.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/tiered-admin#scan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "linux-repository", number: "1.9", title: "Build the Linux repository on SBD-REPO01", subtitle: "Mirrored, signature-verified package snapshots for the Linux servers", category: "Network build", description: "Build the one approved source of operating-system packages before any Linux server exists: verify the vendor's signing keys, build and harden SBD-REPO01 on VLAN 70 as a minimal web server, mirror and publish dated snapshots carried across the air gap and checked by hash and signature, write the client configuration every Linux server will use, and prove promotion, rollback and the first STIG baseline with the SCC for Linux set every Linux server is scanned with.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "linux-repository-guide", title: "Build the Linux repository on SBD-REPO01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Mirrored, signature-verified package snapshots for the Linux servers", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "linux-repository-package", title: "Build the Linux repository on SBD-REPO01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository/SecureByDesign-linux-repository-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:linux-repository:repo-design", title: "Design the repository and its trust", minutes: 25, summary: "Before anything is mirrored, decide what SBD-REPO01 is for: the one approved source of operating-system packages for the domain-joined Linux servers, fed by dated snapshots whose upstream signing keys you verified first. Then place it on VLAN 70 and write its rows into the register.", objectives: ["Define the repository's job, its consumers, and the one-source rule for Linux packages.", "Choose what is mirrored and verify the upstream signing keys that make it trustworthy.", "Place SBD-REPO01 and register every path it uses."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.8: the domain with DNS and course 1.3's time design with its chrony file, the course 1.4 media procedure and DISA tooling, course 1.6's root certificate and the hash it recorded, and the tiered administration plane, with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as the Tier 1 seat.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-REPO01 on VLAN 70 (10.0.70.20) with a separate data disk for /srv (it holds /srv/repo, /srv/incoming and /srv/repo-records), and install media for the one Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release your Linux servers will use, with the vendor's published checksum.\n\nHave an approved connected system that can reach the distribution's repositories (for Red Hat Enterprise Linux, a registered system of the same release and architecture) and the removable-media procedure for carrying the mirror across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-REPO01 is the Linux side's one road for operating-system packages, built before any Linux server exists so that SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) patches from it from its first day. It also stages the Linux assessment set, SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark, that every Linux server is scanned with. Every Linux server in the lab runs a Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family distribution, so the commands use dnf, rpm, firewalld and update-ca-trust; choose one release for every Linux server and follow it throughout."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the repository's job: the only source of operating-system packages for the domain-joined Linux servers (SBD-SPLUNK01 in course 1.12, SBD-WEB01 in course 1.18, and SBD-NESSUS01 when course 1.17, coming soon, arrives) and for SBD-REPO01 itself. Application software, such as Splunk in course 1.12 and SCC for Linux in lesson 5, still arrives as hashed vendor media; anything else is a documented exception, never a second source."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Choose one Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family distribution and major release for every Linux server, one with a published DISA STIG and a SCAP benchmark that SCC for Linux supports, so there is one mirror to feed and one benchmark to scan against. Mirror only its operating-system repositories for one architecture: BaseOS and AppStream. Third-party repositories stay out."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Decide the trust anchor: the vendor's signing keys. Get each key file, read its fingerprint, and compare it with the vendor's published value through a second channel before anything is published; the fingerprints go into the snapshot catalog. Each published mirror is a dated snapshot, and that date is the repository's version."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Place it as Inside the Network does: SBD-REPO01 at 10.0.70.20 on VLAN 70, not domain-joined, administered over SSH 22 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 only (rule A02, Tier 1). Confirm the register rows: the Linux servers to REPO01 on HTTP 80 (F19), REPO01 to both domain controllers for DNS (F01), and REPO01's syslog over TLS to the collector (F07, enabled when course 1.10 builds it). Rule F09 lists only domain members for time, so add SBD-REPO01's own time row, to both controllers on UDP 123, with an owner and a reason, as course 1.3 planned."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: start the evidence folder and confirm no record exists yet\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository | Out-Null\nResolve-DnsName SBD-REPO01.sbd.test -Server 10.0.10.10 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue   # expected: no answer yet\n# On the approved connected system: read each upstream signing key's fingerprint\ngpg --show-keys --with-fingerprint /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release   # or the distribution's own key file"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The design names the consumers, the one distribution and the repositories mirrored; each upstream key's fingerprint matches the vendor's published value; and the register carries F19, F01, F07 and SBD-REPO01's added time row, each with an owner."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the repository design (job, consumers, distribution, repositories, snapshot naming), the verified key fingerprints with where you checked them, and the register rows that name SBD-REPO01."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Red Hat · Managing software with the DNF tool\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_software_with_the_dnf_tool/index\n\nRed Hat · Product signing keys\nhttps://access.redhat.com/security/team/key\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does this course come before any Linux server is built?", answers: ["Linux servers cannot be installed without a repository", "So every Linux server takes its packages from the approved source from its first day, with no earlier source to explain", "Because the collector must be built from it"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "SBD-SPLUNK01, the first Linux server, arrives in course 1.12. With REPO01 already running, its first update and every one after come from the reviewed snapshot.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What makes a mirrored package trustworthy in this design?", ["SBD-REPO01 served it", "It came from a well-known mirror", "Its upstream signature verifies against a signing key whose fingerprint you checked against the vendor's published value"], 2, "The repository is only a carrier. Trust comes from the vendor's signature, checked against a key you verified once, deliberately, and recorded."), QuizQuestion("SBD-REPO01 is not domain-joined. What follows from that?", ["It uses local named accounts and a DNS record the DNS owner creates, and patching does not depend on the directory being up", "It needs no register rows", "It needs no time source"], 0, "Keeping the package source outside the domain removes a dependency, but nothing registers it automatically: its accounts, DNS record and time row are deliberate decisions.")], recap: ["One distribution, one mirror, one source of Linux packages.", "Verified upstream keys are the trust anchor; the dated snapshot is the version.", "REPO01's place and every path it uses are in the register, including its time row."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds and hardens SBD-REPO01 in its place.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository#repo-design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:linux-repository:repo-build", title: "Build and harden SBD-REPO01", minutes: 40, summary: "Build SBD-REPO01 as a minimal Linux server that does one job: answer package requests on TCP 80 and accept administration on SSH from one seat. Every other service, listener and account is left out.", objectives: ["Install the server with its static address and DNS record, course 1.3's chrony file, and the course 1.6 root in its trust store.", "Restrict administration to named local accounts over SSH from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01.", "Serve only the repository directory from a minimal web server behind a host firewall that mirrors the register."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.8: the domain with DNS and course 1.3's time design with its chrony file, the course 1.4 media procedure and DISA tooling, course 1.6's root certificate and the hash it recorded, and the tiered administration plane, with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as the Tier 1 seat.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-REPO01 on VLAN 70 (10.0.70.20) with a separate data disk for /srv (it holds /srv/repo, /srv/incoming and /srv/repo-records), and install media for the one Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release your Linux servers will use, with the vendor's published checksum.\n\nHave an approved connected system that can reach the distribution's repositories (for Red Hat Enterprise Linux, a registered system of the same release and architecture) and the removable-media procedure for carrying the mirror across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-REPO01 is the Linux side's one road for operating-system packages, built before any Linux server exists so that SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) patches from it from its first day. It also stages the Linux assessment set, SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark, that every Linux server is scanned with. Every Linux server in the lab runs a Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family distribution, so the commands use dnf, rpm, firewalld and update-ca-trust; choose one release for every Linux server and follow it throughout."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Install the chosen distribution's minimal server profile from install media whose hash matches the vendor's published checksum, with a separate file system mounted at /srv, sized for at least two snapshots plus growth: /srv/repo is the web root, /srv/incoming receives each crossing, and /srv/repo-records holds the catalog. Set the name SBD-REPO01.sbd.test and the static address 10.0.70.20/24, with the core's VLAN 70 interface (10.0.70.1 in this sample) as gateway and both domain controllers as DNS servers. Because REPO01 cannot register itself in DNS, the DNS owner adds its A record (and PTR, if the reverse zone exists) from AdminJumpBox01. Install only from that verified media until SBD-REPO01 serves its own snapshot; if the image lacks a package this lesson needs, such as chrony or the web server, bring it signature-checked with the first crossing (lesson 3) and install it from that copy."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Apply course 1.3's chrony file before the first package update, and turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization for the VM, so the domain controllers are its only clock: /etc/chrony.conf names only the two controllers, server SBD-DC01.sbd.test iburst and server SBD-DC02.sbd.test iburst, with maxdistance 16 and no pool, other server, sourcedir or allow line, and reaches both controllers over the time row added in lesson 1. Check that chronyc sources shows one controller selected (^*) and the other usable, with a small, steady offset; the maxdistance line is what admits controllers that serve their own clock, as course 1.3 explains."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create a named local account for each administrator in one admin group with sudo, lock root's password, and copy each administrator's SSH public key from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01. In a drop-in that sorts before the distribution's own (for example /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10-sbd.conf, because the first value read wins), set PermitRootLogin no, PasswordAuthentication no and AllowGroups to that group. Over your first SSH session, add the course 1.6 root certificate to the system trust store, because a server outside the domain gets it from no Group Policy: export it from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's Trusted Root store, check its SHA-256 against the hash course 1.6 recorded, copy it across, place it in /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors and run update-ca-trust, ready for course 1.10's syslog over TLS."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Install Apache (httpd) and give it one site: Listen 10.0.70.20:80, DocumentRoot /srv/repo, Options FollowSymLinks with no Indexes, AllowOverride None, only GET and HEAD allowed, everything outside /srv/repo denied, ServerTokens Prod, ServerSignature Off and TraceEnable Off. Remove the welcome page, autoindex and user directories; with SELinux enforcing, label /srv/repo httpd_sys_content_t with semanage fcontext so restorecon keeps it. The repository files stay owned by root and read-only to the web server."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Make the host firewall the register's copy. With firewalld, create a zone for 10.0.60.20 that allows only ssh and a zone for SBD-SPLUNK01, SBD-NESSUS01 and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.50.10, 10.0.50.20, 10.0.40.40) that allows only http, with no services left in the default zone. Adding those consumers now, before they exist, is safe because F19 already approves them."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "hostnamectl; ip -4 addr show\ngrep -E '^(pool|server|sourcedir|allow|maxdistance)' /etc/chrony.conf   # both controllers and maxdistance 16, nothing else\nchronyc sources -v; chronyc tracking   # ^* one controller, ^+ or ^- the other; System time offset small and steady\nsshd -T | grep -Ei '^(permitrootlogin|passwordauthentication|allowgroups) '\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: export the course 1.6 root and compare its hash with course 1.6's record, then copy it over\nGet-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\Root | Where-Object Subject -like '*<root CA name>*' | Export-Certificate -FilePath C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\sbd-root.cer\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\sbd-root.cer\nscp C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\sbd-root.cer <your-account>@SBD-REPO01.sbd.test:/tmp/\n# On SBD-REPO01: the same fingerprint, then the system trust store\nopenssl x509 -inform der -in /tmp/sbd-root.cer -noout -fingerprint -sha256\nsudo cp /tmp/sbd-root.cer /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ && sudo update-ca-trust\ntrust list --filter=ca-anchors | grep -i '<root CA name>'   # expected: the SBD root\napachectl -S        # expected: one site on 10.0.70.20:80\nss -tlnp            # expected: 22 and 80, plus loopback-only listeners, if any\nfirewall-cmd --get-active-zones\nfirewall-cmd --list-all --zone=public   # expected: no services\ncurl -sI http://SBD-REPO01.sbd.test/ | head -1   # expected: 403, no directory listing"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-REPO01 resolves to 10.0.70.20 and, with course 1.3's chrony file and the hypervisor's time synchronization off, syncs from a domain controller with a small, stable offset; sshd refuses root and passwords and admits only the admin group; the system trust store holds the course 1.6 root, its hash matching course 1.6's record; only TCP 22 and 80 listen; the firewall admits SSH from 10.0.60.20 alone and HTTP from the three registered consumers alone; and the web root returns 403 instead of a listing."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the install media hash, the chrony file with its sources and tracking output, the root certificate's hash compared with course 1.6's record, the effective sshd settings, the Apache site and listener output, the firewall zones, and the administrator account list (names only, no keys or passwords)."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Red Hat · Using shared system certificates\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/securing_networks/using-shared-system-certificates_securing-networks\n\nApache HTTP Server 2.4 · Security tips\nhttps://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/misc/security_tips.html\n\nOpenSSH · sshd_config(5)\nhttps://man.openbsd.org/sshd_config\n\nchrony · chrony.conf(5)\nhttps://chrony-project.org/doc/latest/chrony.conf.html\n\nfirewalld · documentation\nhttps://firewalld.org/documentation/\n\nMicrosoft · Add-DnsServerResourceRecordA\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dnsserver/add-dnsserverresourcerecorda\n\nMicrosoft · Export-Certificate\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/pki/export-certificate\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does chrony on SBD-REPO01 point at both domain controllers?", answers: ["chrony needs exactly two servers", "Only SBD-DC02 answers Linux clients", "They carry the domain's time; two sources keep REPO01 on time if one controller is down, so its log timestamps line up with every other system's"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "REPO01 is outside the domain but not outside the time design. Its log timestamps are evidence, and evidence must line up.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why is plain HTTP on TCP 80 acceptable for this repository?", ["Integrity comes from the GPG signatures every client checks, the packages are public, and F19 registers exactly this path", "HTTPS cannot be configured on Linux", "Nothing else on the network uses HTTP"], 0, "A tampered package fails its signature check whatever the transport. HTTP also keeps patching independent of the PKI's certificate lifecycle."), QuizQuestion("The firewall allows SSH only from 10.0.60.20. Why that one address?", ["It is the core router", "It is SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, the Tier 1 seat rule A02 names for REPO01's administration", "Every VLAN 60 host needs SSH everywhere"], 1, "The tier model names the seat that administers each target. The host firewall enforces the same rule, so a path the router would allow still ends at REPO01.")], recap: ["A minimal install: static address, DNS record, and time from both controllers through course 1.3's chrony file.", "Named accounts, keys only, SSH from one seat, and the SBD root trusted.", "One web site, one directory, and a firewall that matches the register."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 fills it with the first mirrored, verified, dated snapshot.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository#repo-build", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:linux-repository:repo", title: "Mirror, verify, and publish a snapshot", minutes: 35, summary: "The repository's content crosses the boundary like everything else: mirrored on the connected side, hashed, carried across, and checked twice on arrival, once against the hash manifest and once against the vendor's signatures. Only then is it published as a dated snapshot and entered in the catalog.", objectives: ["Mirror the approved repositories with their signatures checked on the connected side.", "Carry the set across and verify both its hashes and its signatures before publishing.", "Publish a dated snapshot and record it in the snapshot catalog."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.8: the domain with DNS and course 1.3's time design with its chrony file, the course 1.4 media procedure and DISA tooling, course 1.6's root certificate and the hash it recorded, and the tiered administration plane, with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as the Tier 1 seat.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-REPO01 on VLAN 70 (10.0.70.20) with a separate data disk for /srv (it holds /srv/repo, /srv/incoming and /srv/repo-records), and install media for the one Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release your Linux servers will use, with the vendor's published checksum.\n\nHave an approved connected system that can reach the distribution's repositories (for Red Hat Enterprise Linux, a registered system of the same release and architecture) and the removable-media procedure for carrying the mirror across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-REPO01 is the Linux side's one road for operating-system packages, built before any Linux server exists so that SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) patches from it from its first day. It also stages the Linux assessment set, SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark, that every Linux server is scanned with. Every Linux server in the lab runs a Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family distribution, so the commands use dnf, rpm, firewalld and update-ca-trust; choose one release for every Linux server and follow it throughout."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On the approved connected system, mirror into a dated directory. From a registered system of the same release, run dnf reposync with --download-metadata, which keeps the vendor's own repository metadata (including groups and modules) so the copy is usable as a repository as it stands, and --gpgcheck, which deletes any package whose signature fails and exits non-zero."), LessonSection("Step 2", "If you trim the set instead of mirroring it whole, the vendor metadata no longer matches the files: rebuild it with createrepo_c (and add module metadata back with modifyrepo_c) and rely on gpgcheck on every client, because your rebuilt metadata carries no vendor signature. Then write a SHA-256 manifest of every file, hash the manifest itself, and carry both across under the course 1.4 media procedure."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On SBD-REPO01, copy the set and its manifest into /srv/incoming, outside the web root, and check the set against the manifest. Then verify signatures independently of the connected side: rpm -K on every package, against the keys verified in lesson 1 (rpm --import those key files first if rpm -q gpg-pubkey does not list them). One failure stops publication and becomes a finding."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Publish: move the set to /srv/repo/snapshots/<date> (a rename on the same file system), restore its SELinux labels, and switch the current link to it with an atomic rename so clients never see a half-copied mix. The previous snapshot stays in place for rollback."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Add the snapshot to the catalog in /srv/repo-records, outside the web root: date, repositories, upstream key fingerprints, manifest hash, transfer record, who published it, and its status (current, previous or retired). Copy the catalog and manifest to C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and hash them."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Connected side: keep vendor metadata, delete anything whose signature fails\ndnf reposync --repoid=<baseos-repo-id> --repoid=<appstream-repo-id> --download-metadata --gpgcheck -p /srv/stage/2026-10-13\ncd /srv/stage/2026-10-13 && find . -type f -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum > ../SHA256SUMS-2026-10-13.txt\n# On SBD-REPO01, after the crossing:\ncd /srv/incoming/2026-10-13 && sha256sum -c --quiet ../SHA256SUMS-2026-10-13.txt   # expected: no output\nfind . -name '*.rpm' -print0 | xargs -0 rpm -K | grep -v 'digests signatures OK'   # expected: no output\nmv /srv/incoming/2026-10-13 /srv/repo/snapshots/ && restorecon -R /srv/repo/snapshots/2026-10-13\nln -sfn snapshots/2026-10-13 /srv/repo/current.new && mv -T /srv/repo/current.new /srv/repo/current"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The manifest check and the signature check both pass with no exceptions, /srv/repo/current points at the new dated snapshot, the previous snapshot (if any) is untouched, and the catalog entry carries the fingerprints and manifest hash."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the manifest and its hash, the transfer record, the clean signature-check output, the catalog entry, and the hashes of the copies filed on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DNF plugins · reposync\nhttps://dnf-plugins-core.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reposync.html\n\ncreaterepo_c · repository metadata tools\nhttps://github.com/rpm-software-management/createrepo_c\n\nRed Hat · Product signing keys\nhttps://access.redhat.com/security/team/key\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "What is the repository's version, for evidence purposes?", answers: ["The hashed snapshot date of its mirrored content", "The server's uptime", "The newest package's name"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "A mirror is a moment in time. The dated, hashed snapshot is what lets anyone say exactly which content the lab could install.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The manifest check passes on SBD-REPO01, but rpm -K reports one package with a bad signature. What does that mean?", ["The media was damaged in transit", "Nothing; the manifest already proved the content", "The package was already bad before it crossed: the manifest proves the copy is identical, not that the original is genuine, so the snapshot is not published and the package is a finding"], 2, "Hashes and signatures answer different questions. The manifest proves nothing changed in transit; the signature proves the vendor produced it. Both have to pass."), QuizQuestion("Why switch the current snapshot with a link and an atomic rename instead of copying over the old files?", ["It saves disk space", "Clients see either the old snapshot or the new one, never a half-copied mix, and the previous snapshot stays whole for rollback", "Apache can only serve symbolic links"], 1, "Publishing is a change, and a change should be all or nothing and reversible. The rename gives you both.")], recap: ["Mirror with signature checks on, then hash the whole set.", "On arrival, check the hashes and the signatures; either failure stops publication.", "A dated snapshot goes live by atomic switch and enters the catalog."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 writes the client configuration every Linux server will use.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository#repo", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:linux-repository:repo-consumers", title: "Configure the Linux clients", minutes: 30, summary: "Write the one client configuration every Linux server will use: a single source definition pointing at SBD-REPO01, the verified signing keys, and no other source. No consumer exists yet, so prove it on SBD-REPO01's own package manager and hand the files to courses 1.12 and 1.18.", objectives: ["Write the dnf source definition and signature settings for the chosen release.", "Trust only the verified keys and remove every other package source.", "Prove the configuration on SBD-REPO01 and package it for the later Linux servers."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.8: the domain with DNS and course 1.3's time design with its chrony file, the course 1.4 media procedure and DISA tooling, course 1.6's root certificate and the hash it recorded, and the tiered administration plane, with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as the Tier 1 seat.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-REPO01 on VLAN 70 (10.0.70.20) with a separate data disk for /srv (it holds /srv/repo, /srv/incoming and /srv/repo-records), and install media for the one Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release your Linux servers will use, with the vendor's published checksum.\n\nHave an approved connected system that can reach the distribution's repositories (for Red Hat Enterprise Linux, a registered system of the same release and architecture) and the removable-media procedure for carrying the mirror across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-REPO01 is the Linux side's one road for operating-system packages, built before any Linux server exists so that SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) patches from it from its first day. It also stages the Linux assessment set, SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark, that every Linux server is scanned with. Every Linux server in the lab runs a Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family distribution, so the commands use dnf, rpm, firewalld and update-ca-trust; choose one release for every Linux server and follow it throughout."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write one source definition that names SBD-REPO01.sbd.test, so DNS carries it (F01): /etc/yum.repos.d/sbd-repo01.repo has one section per repository with baseurl=http://SBD-REPO01.sbd.test/current/<repo-id>/, gpgcheck=1 and gpgkey=file:///etc/pki/rpm-gpg/<vendor key>."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Trust only the keys verified in lesson 1, installed from local files and never fetched from the repository at install time: whoever could change the repository could then change the key too. List the trusted keys (rpm -q gpg-pubkey) and remove any key nothing needs with rpm -e."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Remove or disable every other source: set enabled=0 in every other .repo file, including any install-media entry, and where subscription-manager is installed, stop it managing repositories (subscription-manager config --rhsm.manage_repos=0), so it cannot write its own definitions back."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prove it on SBD-REPO01 itself, which follows the same rule: apply the files, refresh the metadata with no errors or warnings, and confirm that a package you will need in course 1.10, rsyslog-gnutls, resolves from the SBD-REPO01 source. A Failed to fetch line or a missing package is a gap in the mirror to fix before any server depends on it."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Package the source file, the key files and a short apply-and-verify sheet in /srv/repo-records/client, hash them, and file them as the configuration every Linux server applies, courses 1.12 and 1.18 when they build SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01: after course 1.3's chrony file and before the server's first package update."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "dnf repolist --all                 # expected: only the sbd-repo01 repositories enabled\nrpm -q gpg-pubkey --qf '%{NAME}-%{VERSION}-%{RELEASE}\\t%{SUMMARY}\\n'\ndnf makecache && dnf info rsyslog-gnutls | grep -Ei '^(repository|from repo)'   # expected: from the sbd-repo01 repositories\n# Hash the client package for the later courses\nsha256sum /srv/repo-records/client/*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "On SBD-REPO01 the package manager lists SBD-REPO01 as its only source, trusts only the verified keys, refreshes without errors, and resolves rsyslog-gnutls from the snapshot; the client package is hashed and filed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the source listing, the trusted key list, the refresh output, the package's source line, and the client package with its hashes."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Red Hat · Managing software with the DNF tool\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_software_with_the_dnf_tool/index\n\nRed Hat · Product signing keys\nhttps://access.redhat.com/security/team/key\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "How is the one-source rule enforced on a Linux consumer?", answers: ["By asking administrators nicely", "External source definitions are removed or disabled — the configuration itself allows only the internal repository", "By firewall rules alone"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Both layers help, but the consumer's own configuration is the primary control — and the thing the scan or config review verifies.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why install the signing key from a local, verified file rather than from SBD-REPO01?", ["Anyone who could change the repository could then change the key as well, and the signature check would prove nothing", "HTTP cannot carry key files", "Keys expire when downloaded"], 0, "The key is the trust anchor, so it must come through a channel the repository cannot influence. That is why lesson 1 verified it separately."), QuizQuestion("What does gpgcheck=1 do at install time?", ["Encrypts the download", "Makes metadata refresh faster", "Refuses any package whose signature does not verify against a trusted key"], 2, "dnf checks each package's signature before installing it. Your job is to make sure the key they check against is the right one, and the only one.")], recap: ["One source definition, by name, with signature checks on.", "Only verified keys are trusted, and no other source remains.", "Proven on SBD-REPO01, packaged for courses 1.12 and 1.18."], nextStep: "The final lesson keeps the repository current, rehearses rollback, and baselines SBD-REPO01.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository#repo-consumers", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:linux-repository:repo-prove", title: "Keep it current and prove it", minutes: 40, summary: "A repository is only as good as its last snapshot. Set the cadence that keeps it current, rehearse promoting a new snapshot and rolling back, stage SCC for Linux for every Linux server and take SBD-REPO01's first SCAP baseline with it, and close the course with a record an assessor can check.", objectives: ["Set the update cadence for snapshots across the air gap.", "Promote a new snapshot and roll back to the previous one, on record.", "Stage SCC for Linux and the STIG benchmark for every Linux server, baseline SBD-REPO01 with them, and hand off to course 1.10."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.8: the domain with DNS and course 1.3's time design with its chrony file, the course 1.4 media procedure and DISA tooling, course 1.6's root certificate and the hash it recorded, and the tiered administration plane, with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as the Tier 1 seat.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-REPO01 on VLAN 70 (10.0.70.20) with a separate data disk for /srv (it holds /srv/repo, /srv/incoming and /srv/repo-records), and install media for the one Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release your Linux servers will use, with the vendor's published checksum.\n\nHave an approved connected system that can reach the distribution's repositories (for Red Hat Enterprise Linux, a registered system of the same release and architecture) and the removable-media procedure for carrying the mirror across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "SBD-REPO01 is the Linux side's one road for operating-system packages, built before any Linux server exists so that SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) patches from it from its first day. It also stages the Linux assessment set, SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark, that every Linux server is scanned with. Every Linux server in the lab runs a Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family distribution, so the commands use dnf, rpm, firewalld and update-ca-trust; choose one release for every Linux server and follow it throughout."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the cadence: a new snapshot crosses on a fixed schedule (monthly is a common sample value), plus an expedited crossing when the vendor publishes a critical security fix. Keep the current and previous snapshots, retire older ones in the catalog, and name who approves each crossing. Course 1.14 later carries Windows updates on the same monthly run."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Rehearse change control with the next snapshot: publish it as in lesson 3, refresh SBD-REPO01's own client and confirm it sees the new content, then switch current back to the previous snapshot and confirm the client follows. Rolling back stops a bad snapshot from spreading, but it does not uninstall anything; a server that already updated needs dnf history undo or dnf downgrade there. Record both switches in the catalog."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Stage the Linux assessment set once, for every Linux server: SCC for Linux for your distribution and architecture, the DISA STIG SCAP benchmark for the distribution, and, for the manual items, the distribution's STIG and the Apache Server 2.4 UNIX STIGs. Check SCC against DISA's published checksum on the connected side, hash every file into connected-side-hashes.csv, carry the set under the course 1.4 media procedure, and check it again into C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: the Tier 1 copy that courses 1.12 and 1.18 scan their Linux servers with."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Scan every Linux server the same way, starting with SBD-REPO01: copy SCC and the benchmark over your Tier 1 SSH session, check their hashes there, install SCC as its readme directs, and run it as root with the benchmark and the profile course 1.4 recorded. Copy the session back to C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\baseline\\SBD-REPO01, answer the manual items, Apache's included, in a STIG Viewer checklist for SBD-REPO01 on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and classify every open item as in course 1.4: fix now, deviation candidate or POA&M candidate. Plain HTTP on F19 is a deviation candidate, justified by signed content."), LessonSection("Step 5", "File the assessable record in C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository with hashes: the design, the snapshot catalog, the client package, the baseline results and checklist, and the administration record of who changed what. Write the handoff to course 1.10: SBD-REPO01 is running, rsyslog-gnutls is in its snapshot, the SBD root is in its trust store, and its syslog over TLS (F07) is waiting for the collector."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-REPO01: promote the next snapshot, then rehearse rollback\nreadlink /srv/repo/current\nln -sfn snapshots/2026-11-10 /srv/repo/current.new && mv -T /srv/repo/current.new /srv/repo/current\nln -sfn snapshots/2026-10-13 /srv/repo/current.new && mv -T /srv/repo/current.new /srv/repo/current   # rollback\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: no output means every staged file is on the connected-side list\n(Get-ChildItem C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux\\* -File -Exclude *.csv | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256).Hash | Where-Object { \$_ -notin (Import-Csv C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash }\nscp C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux\\<SCC for Linux package> C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux\\<STIG benchmark> <your-account>@SBD-REPO01.sbd.test:/tmp/\n# On SBD-REPO01: the same hashes, then SCC's command-line scanner as root (path and options from the SCC readme)\nsha256sum /tmp/<SCC for Linux package> /tmp/<STIG benchmark>\nsudo <SCC install directory>/cscc\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: collect the session and the record, then hash everything\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\baseline | Out-Null\nscp -r <your-account>@SBD-REPO01.sbd.test:<SCC session folder> C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\baseline\\SBD-REPO01\nscp -r <your-account>@SBD-REPO01.sbd.test:/srv/repo-records/* C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository\\\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\linux-repository-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The cadence is written with an approver, the promotion and the rollback both show in the catalog and in the client's view, the Linux assessment set sits checked in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, SBD-REPO01 has a first baseline with every open item classified, and the record and handoff are filed with hashes."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the cadence, the catalog showing promotion and rollback, the Linux assessment set's hash check, the SCC results and STIG Viewer checklist, the classified findings, the administration record, the hash list, and the handoff note to course 1.10."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · STIGs document library\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "You roll the repository back to the previous snapshot. What happens to servers that already installed the newer packages?", answers: ["They keep them until you undo or downgrade on each server; rollback stops the spread, it does not reverse installs", "They revert automatically on the next refresh", "They stop working until they are rebuilt"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The repository decides what can be installed next, not what is installed now. Undoing an installed update is a separate, recorded change on each server.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How is SBD-REPO01 baselined?", ["It is exempt because it only serves files", "With the Windows Server benchmark", "With SCC and the DISA STIG benchmark for its distribution, plus the manual checklist items such as the Apache web server STIGs"], 2, "A Linux server is held to its own DISA STIG on the same cadence as every other server. The web server's items are manual, so they are answered in STIG Viewer."), QuizQuestion("A STIG item expects the web server to use TLS, but F19 is plain HTTP. How do you record it?", ["Mark it not a finding", "As a deviation candidate with its justification — signed content on a registered path — decided in the exception record", "Turn off the web server"], 1, "A deliberate design choice that differs from a STIG value is neither hidden nor fixed by default. It is written down, justified, and decided by the right person.")], recap: ["Snapshots cross on a written cadence, with an expedited path for critical fixes.", "Promotion and rollback are rehearsed, and rollback's limits are known.", "SCC for Linux is staged for every Linux server, SBD-REPO01 has its first baseline, and course 1.10 inherits a running source."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.9. Course 1.10 builds the collector, SBD-SYSLOG01, and enrolls SBD-REPO01 as its first Linux syslog-over-TLS source.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/linux-repository#repo-prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "logging-pipeline", number: "1.10", title: "Collect the record on SBD-SYSLOG01", subtitle: "Design the logging pipeline and build its collector", category: "Network build", description: "The first of three logging courses: design the whole collect → protect → analyze pipeline, then build its first stage — advanced audit policy on the DCs and admin hosts, source-initiated Windows Event Forwarding from every existing Windows system and a syslog intake on SBD-SYSLOG01, health checks with a named owner, and proof that real events arrive with the right names and times.", icon: "monitoring", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-guide", title: "Collect the record on SBD-SYSLOG01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Design the logging pipeline and build its collector", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "logging-pipeline-package", title: "Collect the record on SBD-SYSLOG01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline/SecureByDesign-logging-pipeline-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:design", title: "Design collect, protect, analyze", minutes: 25, summary: "Three systems, three distinct jobs: SBD-SYSLOG01 gathers, SBD-AUDIT01 preserves, SBD-SPLUNK01 answers questions. This course builds the first; courses 1.11 and 1.12 build the other two — so decide now, for all three, what is collected, how long it lives, who may touch it, and where each stream is picked up.", objectives: ["Assign the three roles, the course that builds each, and the first collection scope.", "Decide retention, readers, and the no-delete rule per stage, recording the period chosen for your environment.", "Name the streams and register every pipeline path."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: every Windows system they built — both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats — becomes a source; the tiered admin plane (course 1.8) means the collector is administered only from VLAN 60 (SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1); and SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, course 1.9) is running and becomes this course's first Linux source.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server joined to sbd.test in the Servers OU, so course 1.5's member-server STIG policies apply from first boot, with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the logs. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization and check that w32tm /query /source names a domain controller (course 1.3).\n\nHave the network's service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, and an approved syslog receiver for Windows (staged across the air gap and hashed, as in course 1.4) ready: Windows has no built-in syslog listener."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the first of three logging courses. Course 1.10 designs the whole collect → protect → analyze pipeline and builds the collect stage on SBD-SYSLOG01; course 1.11 protects the record on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes it in SBD-SPLUNK01. Collection is configuration you can show: a subscription, a listener, and events with the right source names and times."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the role sentence for each host and the course that builds it: SBD-SYSLOG01 (course 1.10) receives Windows events and syslog; SBD-AUDIT01 (course 1.11) holds the protected copy for the full retention period; SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) indexes a working copy for search and alerting. One copy to keep, one copy to question."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Scope the first collection to what already produces evidence: the Security log and the Windows Time events course 1.3 keeps from every Windows system that exists — both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats — plus the core router's deny log and SBD-REPO01's syslog. The fleet joins in course 1.13, and every later server in the course that builds it."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Decide the AU answers per stage. How long each stage keeps its records is dependent on your environment and your records policy, so write down the period you chose for each and why: the collector keeps a local buffer long enough for the downstream pickups to catch up after an outage, administered by Tier 1 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01; the repository keeps the protected record for the whole period your policy requires, written only by a write-only archival account, read by a named auditors group, and deleted by nobody outside the custodian's logged retention process, with independent approval (rule A04); analysis keeps as much searchable history as investigations need, read by analysts."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Name the two streams and where they are picked up: Windows events land in the ForwardedEvents log (file on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents) and syslog in daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog. Course 1.11's archival job and course 1.12's forwarder both read from these two places; nothing else writes there."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Add the register rows with owners: Windows sources to the collector (F17, WinRM 5985, source-initiated), Linux syslog over TLS (F07, TCP 6514), the core router's deny log (rule F24, UDP 514, owned by the network owner: the router already sends it to the collector's address as part of the network's existing configuration, and this course only receives it), the archive path (F11, SMB 3 with encryption) and the forwarder path (F10, TCP 9997 with TLS). The last two stay unused until courses 1.11 and 1.12."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): what the DCs audit today.\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01, SBD-DC02 { auditpol /get /category:* /r } | Out-File C:\\Evidence\\logging-pipeline\\DCs-auditpol-before.csv\n# From SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (Tier 1): no collector runs yet.\nInvoke-Command SBD-SYSLOG01 { Get-Service Wecsvc | Select-Object Status, StartType }"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The design names each stage's job, building course, scope, chosen retention period with its reason, and readers; the no-delete rule is written; the two streams and their pickup folders are named; and the register carries the five pipeline paths with owners, F24 for the router's deny log included."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the pipeline design sheet, the retention and access table, the stream and pickup definitions, and the new register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why separate the repository from the analysis system?", answers: ["The protected record and the working copy have different jobs — tampering with searches must never mean tampering with evidence", "Splunk cannot store data", "Two systems are cheaper than one"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Analysis needs flexibility; the record needs integrity. Splitting them means a compromised search tier still leaves the evidence intact.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What is in the first collection scope?", ["Every system, including the fleet that does not exist yet", "The sources that already produce evidence: every existing Windows system's Security log and time events, the router's deny log, and SBD-REPO01", "Only the collector's own log"], 1, "Collect what exists and matters most. The fleet widens the scope in course 1.13 — into a pipeline already proven."), QuizQuestion("Who may delete from SBD-AUDIT01?", ["Any Domain Admin", "The analysis system automatically", "Nobody outside the written retention process — that restriction is the protection control"], 2, "AU-9 in one sentence: the record survives its administrators. Deletion happens only by the documented retention process, which course 1.11 builds.")], recap: ["Collector gathers (1.10), repository preserves (1.11), analysis answers (1.12).", "Retention, readers, and the no-delete rule are written for every stage.", "Two named streams, their pickup folders, and five registered paths."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 makes sure the right events exist before anything forwards them.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:auditing", title: "Turn on the right auditing", minutes: 25, summary: "Forwarding garbage faster is not monitoring. Set the advanced audit policy so the events that tell the network's story — sign-ins, account changes, directory changes, policy changes — actually get written, at or above the STIG values course 1.4 measured.", objectives: ["Apply the advanced audit policy subcategories the design needs.", "Reconcile audit settings with the course 1.5 STIG Group Policy objects.", "Verify effective auditing on the DCs and admin hosts."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: every Windows system they built — both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats — becomes a source; the tiered admin plane (course 1.8) means the collector is administered only from VLAN 60 (SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1); and SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, course 1.9) is running and becomes this course's first Linux source.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server joined to sbd.test in the Servers OU, so course 1.5's member-server STIG policies apply from first boot, with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the logs. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization and check that w32tm /query /source names a domain controller (course 1.3).\n\nHave the network's service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, and an approved syslog receiver for Windows (staged across the air gap and hashed, as in course 1.4) ready: Windows has no built-in syslog listener."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the first of three logging courses. Course 1.10 designs the whole collect → protect → analyze pipeline and builds the collect stage on SBD-SYSLOG01; course 1.11 protects the record on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes it in SBD-SPLUNK01. Collection is configuration you can show: a subscription, a listener, and events with the right source names and times."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Review what the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Group Policy objects (GPOs) linked in course 1.5 already audit — most of the advanced audit policy arrived with the STIGs, including the setting that makes subcategories override the old category policy. This lesson reconciles and fills gaps rather than starting over."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm the subcategories the design depends on are at least at the STIG values on the DCs: Credential Validation, Logon, Special Logon, User Account Management, Security Group Management, Directory Service Changes, Audit Policy Change, and Security State Change, which writes event 4616 for every clock change course 1.3 records — success and failure where the design says so. Directory Service Changes writes event 5136 only for objects whose auditing entries ask for it, so check the domain's auditing entries too."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Extend the same policy to the Admin OU's computer sub-OUs from course 1.8, so privileged sessions on the admin hosts write their sign-in trail, and confirm that SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, and SBD-DHCP01 already carry the member-server STIG values."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Make every addition a separate, documented GPO linked above the STIG GPO, never an auditpol /set on one server. Advanced audit settings merge across GPOs per subcategory, and only a conflicting subcategory is decided by precedence — record each addition in the course 1.5 exception record, kept on AdminJumpBox01."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): effective audit policy and the policy result on a DC.\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01 { auditpol /get /category:* /r } | Out-File C:\\Evidence\\logging-pipeline\\SBD-DC01-auditpol.csv\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01 { auditpol /get /subcategory:\"Directory Service Changes,Security State Change\" }\ngpresult /s SBD-DC01 /scope computer /h C:\\Evidence\\logging-pipeline\\SBD-DC01-gpresult.html"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Effective audit policy on the DCs and admin hosts covers the designed subcategories at or above the STIG values, applied through GPO, with every addition documented."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the auditpol output per host class, the GPO report, and the documented additions."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Auditpol\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/auditpol\n\nMicrosoft · Advanced security auditing FAQ\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/advanced-security-auditing-faq\n\nMicrosoft · Advanced audit policy configuration\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/advanced-audit-policy-configuration")], question: "Why start from the course 1.5 STIG GPOs instead of a fresh audit policy?", answers: ["Fresh policies are forbidden", "The STIGs already set most of it — reconciling avoids conflicts and double maintenance", "auditpol cannot show existing settings"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The hardening baseline was the first word on auditing. This lesson's job is alignment and gaps, not a rival policy.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Where do audit-policy additions get made?", ["In the GPO structure, documented — never as local tweaks", "Directly in each server's local policy", "In the collector's configuration"], 0, "Local tweaks drift and hide, and Group Policy can overwrite them. Policy through GPO keeps the audit posture visible, scanned, and reproducible."), QuizQuestion("Which event families does the design treat as the core story?", ["Print spooler events", "Disk defragmentation logs", "Sign-ins, account management, directory changes, and policy changes"], 2, "Who authenticated, who changed an account, who touched the directory, who altered policy — that is the identity network's narrative.")], recap: ["Audit policy is reconciled with the STIG baseline, not reinvented.", "DCs and admin hosts write the designed event families.", "Additions are GPO-visible and documented."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the collector those events will flow to.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#auditing", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:collector", title: "Build the collector", minutes: 35, summary: "SBD-SYSLOG01 becomes the funnel: Windows Event Forwarding brings in the events of every Windows system that exists by source-initiated subscription, and a syslog receiver takes the core router's denies and, from lesson 5, the Linux servers over TLS — one host where the network's record first lands.", objectives: ["Configure the Windows Event Collector service and a source-initiated subscription.", "Enroll the sources by GPO with read access to their Security logs.", "Stand up the syslog intake for UDP 514 and TLS on TCP 6514."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: every Windows system they built — both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats — becomes a source; the tiered admin plane (course 1.8) means the collector is administered only from VLAN 60 (SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1); and SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, course 1.9) is running and becomes this course's first Linux source.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server joined to sbd.test in the Servers OU, so course 1.5's member-server STIG policies apply from first boot, with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the logs. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization and check that w32tm /query /source names a domain controller (course 1.3).\n\nHave the network's service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, and an approved syslog receiver for Windows (staged across the air gap and hashed, as in course 1.4) ready: Windows has no built-in syslog listener."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the first of three logging courses. Course 1.10 designs the whole collect → protect → analyze pipeline and builds the collect stage on SBD-SYSLOG01; course 1.11 protects the record on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes it in SBD-SPLUNK01. Collection is configuration you can show: a subscription, a listener, and events with the right source names and times."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-SYSLOG01, run wecutil qc /q, move the ForwardedEvents log file to D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, and create the subscription SBD-ForwardedSecurity from an XML file: SourceInitiated, MinLatency delivery (push, 30-second batches), into ForwardedEvents, with two queries — the whole Security log (event 4616 included), and the Microsoft-Windows-Time-Service events 12, 35, 36, 37, 129, and 142 from the System log that course 1.3 keeps. Allow one computer group, SBD Event Sources, created from AdminJumpBox01 and holding every Windows system that exists now: both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats. The default allows Domain Computers, which does not include domain controllers."), LessonSection("Step 2", "From AdminJumpBox01, enroll the sources with one GPO linked to the Domain Controllers, Tier 0 Servers, Servers, and Admin sub-OUs and security-filtered to SBD Event Sources (Authenticated Users keep read), so the collector and later servers stay out until a course adds them: the subscription manager Server=http://SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test:5985/wsman/SubscriptionManager/WEC,Refresh=60, the WinRM service set to automatic, and NETWORK SERVICE in Event Log Readers — on the DCs through the domain's built-in group, on the other sources as a local group preference. Restart each source so the group changes apply; the sources push, the collector reads nothing."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Allow WinRM 5985 inbound on the collector only from the enrolled sources: VLANs 10 and 60, SBD-SUBCA01 and SBD-PKI-MGMT01 (10.0.30.20 and 10.0.30.30), and SBD-DHCP01 (10.0.20.10), which shares VLAN 20, so only this host firewall scopes it. Rule F17 covers every Windows member; each later course widens this scope when it enrolls new sources. Kerberos authenticates both ends and encrypts the forwarded events, even over HTTP."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Install the approved syslog receiver as a service: UDP 514 for the core router, which already sends its deny log to this address (rule F24), and TCP 6514 with TLS using an SBD Server Authentication certificate for SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test, requested from the course 1.6 issuing CA through its process after adding SBD-SYSLOG01 to the SBD TLS Servers group. Write daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog, and allow each port only from its registered senders."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Confirm the register rows are the only paths in use: WinRM from the enrolled sources (F17), syslog from the core (F24), and nothing listening that the design didn't ask for."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01): the subscription, the first events, and the listeners.\nwecutil es\nwecutil gs SBD-ForwardedSecurity\nGet-WinEvent -LogName ForwardedEvents -MaxEvents 20 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, MachineName\nGet-NetUDPEndpoint -LocalPort 514\nGet-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 6514,5985 -State Listen\n# On each source, from its tier's seat: Network Service is in Event Log Readers, and the forwarder connected (events 104 and 100).\nnet localgroup \"Event Log Readers\"\nGet-WinEvent -LogName 'Microsoft-Windows-Forwarding/Operational' -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, Message"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The subscription is enabled, events from every member of SBD Event Sources — Security and Time-Service — appear in ForwardedEvents with correct source machine names, router deny entries arrive in the syslog files, and only the registered ports listen."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the subscription XML and wecutil gs output, the enrolling GPO report, the first forwarded event per source, a received router deny line, and the listener list."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Setting up a source initiated subscription\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wec/setting-up-a-source-initiated-subscription\n\nMicrosoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nRFC 5425 · Transport Layer Security (TLS) Transport Mapping for Syslog\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5425")], question: "Why source-initiated subscriptions rather than the collector pulling?", answers: ["Pulling is impossible on Windows", "Sources push to one known collector, so no account with read-everything rights roams the network", "Push is the only mode GPO supports"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Collector-initiated collection needs a credential that can read every source's logs — exactly the kind of credential tiering just eliminated.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The admin seats forward, but neither DC appears in the subscription. What is the likely cause?", ["The subscription still allows only Domain Computers, which does not include domain controllers", "Domain controllers cannot forward events", "The DCs need UDP 514 open"], 0, "Domain controllers belong to the Domain Controllers group, not Domain Computers. Naming a group that holds every intended source fixes it and keeps the allowed list explicit."), QuizQuestion("How do you know a forwarded event really came from DC02?", ["All events look identical", "The collector renames events randomly", "The MachineName on the event record in ForwardedEvents"], 2, "Forwarded events keep their source identity — which is what makes the central copy usable as a record at all.")], recap: ["Sources push by subscription; the collector reaches into nothing.", "Windows sources and router records land in two named streams.", "Only registered paths carry the pipeline."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 keeps the collector trustworthy once it is running.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#collector", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:collector-health", title: "Keep the collector trustworthy", minutes: 25, summary: "A collector that silently stops is worse than none, because everyone assumes it works. Watch the subscription's runtime status, the sources that went quiet, the log's size and overwrite behavior, the disk, and the syslog intake — and decide who is told when collection fails.", objectives: ["Read subscription runtime status and find silent sources.", "Size the ForwardedEvents log and data volume for the design's buffer.", "Define what a collection failure is and who responds."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: every Windows system they built — both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats — becomes a source; the tiered admin plane (course 1.8) means the collector is administered only from VLAN 60 (SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1); and SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, course 1.9) is running and becomes this course's first Linux source.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server joined to sbd.test in the Servers OU, so course 1.5's member-server STIG policies apply from first boot, with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the logs. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization and check that w32tm /query /source names a domain controller (course 1.3).\n\nHave the network's service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, and an approved syslog receiver for Windows (staged across the air gap and hashed, as in course 1.4) ready: Windows has no built-in syslog listener."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the first of three logging courses. Course 1.10 designs the whole collect → protect → analyze pipeline and builds the collect stage on SBD-SYSLOG01; course 1.11 protects the record on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes it in SBD-SPLUNK01. Collection is configuration you can show: a subscription, a listener, and events with the right source names and times."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Read wecutil gr SBD-ForwardedSecurity: each source shows a runtime status and its last heartbeat. A source that is inactive, or whose last heartbeat is older than the subscription's heartbeat interval (wecutil gs shows it), has gone silent — check its Microsoft-Windows-Forwarding/Operational log first."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Set the ForwardedEvents log's size and behavior with wevtutil sl: overwrite the oldest events (/rt:false) so new events are never refused, sized so the oldest event is always older than the buffer period you chose in lesson 1. Overwriting is safe only while courses 1.11 and 1.12 pick events up well inside that window; a full log in retain mode would drop new events instead."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Check the data volume and the syslog intake: free space on D:, both listeners up, and today's file growing for every registered sender. A sender with no file today is a silent source too."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the failure response (AU-5): a stopped Windows Event Collector service, an inactive source, the oldest event younger than the buffer, a data volume past the fill level you set (dependent on your environment; record the value you chose), or a silent syslog sender. The logging owner is told the same day, restores collection, and records the gap window. Until course 1.12 turns these into alerts, run the check daily and keep each result."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "wecutil gr SBD-ForwardedSecurity\nwevtutil sl ForwardedEvents /ms:4294967296 /rt:false   # 4 GB in this sample: size it to hold your chosen buffer\nwevtutil gl ForwardedEvents\nGet-WinEvent -LogName ForwardedEvents -Oldest -MaxEvents 1 | Select-Object TimeCreated\nGet-Volume -DriveLetter D | Select-Object SizeRemaining, Size\nGet-ChildItem D:\\Logs\\Syslog -Recurse -File | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 5 FullName, LastWriteTime"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every enrolled source is active with a recent heartbeat, ForwardedEvents overwrites oldest-first and holds more than the chosen buffer period, the data volume has headroom, every registered syslog sender wrote today, and the failure response names its owner."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the runtime status output, the log configuration, the oldest-event time, the volume figures, and the written failure response with its first daily check."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nMicrosoft · Wevtutil\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wevtutil\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "A DC stopped forwarding last night. Where does it first show on the collector?", answers: ["Nowhere — WEF has no status", "In the subscription's runtime status: the source is inactive or its last heartbeat is old", "As an error on the DC's desktop"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The collector keeps each source's heartbeat and bookmark. wecutil gr is the first place a silent source becomes visible.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why overwrite the oldest events instead of retaining them when the log fills?", ["Retain mode refuses new events once the log is full, so the newest record — usually the one you need — would be lost", "Overwrite mode encrypts the log", "Retain mode deletes the whole log"], 0, "The collector is a buffer, not the archive. Size it larger than the pickup window so overwriting only ever removes events already copied downstream."), QuizQuestion("What makes a collection failure a control rather than a surprise?", ["A larger disk", "Restarting the collector weekly", "A defined failure condition, a named owner who is told, and a recorded gap window"], 2, "AU-5 asks for an alert to named people and a defined response. The daily check is that response until course 1.12 automates it.")], recap: ["Runtime status and heartbeats show silent sources.", "The log is sized past the buffer and overwrites oldest-first.", "Collection failures have a definition, an owner, and a record."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 proves collection with real events and closes the course.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#collector-health", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:logging-pipeline:collector-prove", title: "Prove collection and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "Prove the collector with events you cause on purpose: a failed sign-in, a denied probe, and a test message from SBD-REPO01, the first Linux source. Each must land with the right source name and time — then the cadence scans close the course.", objectives: ["Trace a failed sign-in and a router deny to the collector.", "Enroll SBD-REPO01 as the first syslog-over-TLS source.", "Baseline SBD-SYSLOG01, rescan the DCs, and write the handoff."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.9: every Windows system they built — both DCs, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01, SBD-DHCP01, and the three admin seats — becomes a source; the tiered admin plane (course 1.8) means the collector is administered only from VLAN 60 (SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, Tier 1); and SBD-REPO01 (10.0.70.20, course 1.9) is running and becomes this course's first Linux source.\n\nProvision SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) as Windows Server joined to sbd.test in the Servers OU, so course 1.5's member-server STIG policies apply from first boot, with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the logs. Turn off the hypervisor's time synchronization and check that w32tm /query /source names a domain controller (course 1.3).\n\nHave the network's service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, and an approved syslog receiver for Windows (staged across the air gap and hashed, as in course 1.4) ready: Windows has no built-in syslog listener."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the first of three logging courses. Course 1.10 designs the whole collect → protect → analyze pipeline and builds the collect stage on SBD-SYSLOG01; course 1.11 protects the record on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes it in SBD-SPLUNK01. Collection is configuration you can show: a subscription, a listener, and events with the right source names and times."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Generate one failed sign-in on SBD-DC01 with a wrong password for a tier admin account. Find event 4625 in the DC's Security log, then in ForwardedEvents with MachineName SBD-DC01 and the same time; compare the delay with the 30-second delivery setting."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Probe one unregistered port across the core — from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, TCP 23 on SBD-DHCP01 — and find the router's deny line (rule F24) in the syslog files with the right source, destination, port, and time. Attempt and deny now form an evidence pair."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Enroll SBD-REPO01 (rule F07): install rsyslog's TLS driver, rsyslog-gnutls, with dnf from REPO01's own repository; in a file under /etc/rsyslog.d, point the driver's CA file at the system trust bundle, which holds the course 1.6 root that course 1.9 placed there, and forward to SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test on TCP 6514 with x509/name authentication, so the collector's certificate name is checked; then check the configuration and restart rsyslog. Add REPO01 to the 6514 sender list, send a test message with logger, and find it in REPO01's daily file."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Run the cadence: SBD-SYSLOG01's first SCAP baseline with the course 1.4 benchmark and tool versions, run locally in a Tier 1 session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 with SCC copied from that seat's course 1.8 copy over PowerShell remoting and its hash checked, and a rescan of both DCs from AdminJumpBox01, attributing the deltas to lesson 2's audit policy."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the handoff to course 1.11: the two streams, their pickup folders, the buffer period you chose, which the archive job must stay inside, the gap windows the daily check records (those days need re-exporting once a source replays its backlog), and the F11 row waiting for SBD-AUDIT01."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the failed sign-in, and the router's deny of the probe to SBD-DHCP01.\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName\nSelect-String -Path D:\\Logs\\Syslog\\*\\*.log -Pattern '10\\.0\\.20\\.10' | Select-Object -Last 3\n# On SBD-REPO01: the TLS driver, then /etc/rsyslog.d/sbd-forward.conf:\nsudo dnf install rsyslog-gnutls\n#   global(DefaultNetstreamDriverCAFile=\"/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt\")\n#   *.* action(type=\"omfwd\" target=\"SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test\" port=\"6514\" protocol=\"tcp\"\n#          StreamDriver=\"gtls\" StreamDriverMode=\"1\" StreamDriverAuthMode=\"x509/name\"\n#          StreamDriverPermittedPeers=\"SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test\")\n# Check the configuration, restart, confirm the TLS session, then send the test message.\nsudo rsyslogd -N1\nsudo systemctl restart rsyslog\nss -tn 'dport = :6514'\nlogger -t sbd-collector-proof 'SBD-REPO01 syslog over TLS test'\n# Back on SBD-SYSLOG01: the test message in REPO01's daily file.\nSelect-String -Path D:\\Logs\\Syslog\\*\\*.log -Pattern 'sbd-collector-proof' | Select-Object -First 3\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: this course's evidence, hashed.\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\logging-pipeline\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The failed sign-in, the router deny, and REPO01's test message each appear on the collector with the right source and a time that matches the source; SBD-SYSLOG01 has its first baseline; and the DC rescan deltas are attributed to the audit policy."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the three traces with their timestamps, REPO01's forwarding configuration, the scan set with hashes, and the handoff note to course 1.11."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nRed Hat · Configuring a remote logging solution\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/security_hardening/assembly_configuring-a-remote-logging-solution_security-hardening\n\nRFC 5425 · Transport Layer Security (TLS) Transport Mapping for Syslog\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5425\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What does a deliberately caused event prove that configuration checks don't?", answers: ["That event IDs exist", "That the collector has a certificate", "That a real record travels from source to collector with its source name and time intact"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Every setting can look right while nothing arrives. One known event, found where it should be, is the integration test.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-REPO01 use syslog over TLS on TCP 6514 rather than UDP 514?", ["TLS protects the messages in transit and lets REPO01 check it is talking to the real collector; UDP 514 has neither encryption nor delivery guarantees", "UDP 514 does not work on Linux", "TCP 6514 is faster"], 0, "RFC 5425 maps syslog onto TLS. The router's UDP 514 stream stays inside the register as rule F24, a known, recorded exception."), QuizQuestion("Why scan the DCs again in a logging course?", ["Scans are required weekly", "Lesson 2 changed their audit policy, and a change triggers a rescan whose deltas are attributed to it", "The collector cannot work without a scan"], 1, "Change-triggered rescans are the course 1.5 cadence. The deltas should match lesson 2's documented additions — nothing more.")], recap: ["A failed sign-in, a deny, and a Linux message all landed with the right names and times.", "SBD-REPO01 is the first syslog-over-TLS source.", "Baselines, rescans, and the handoff to course 1.11 close the course."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.10. Course 1.11 gives the record its protected home on SBD-AUDIT01, picking up the two streams this course collects.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/logging-pipeline#collector-prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "audit-repository", number: "1.11", title: "Protect the record on SBD-AUDIT01", subtitle: "Keep the audit record intact, even from its administrators", category: "Network build", description: "The second of three logging courses: build SBD-AUDIT01 as the protected home for the collector's record — daily archives over encrypted SMB from a write-only managed account, SHA-256 hashes cataloged on arrival and verified every day, access auditing that leaves the box, retention that runs only as an approved and logged process, and proof that an administrator's delete is refused and a tampered copy is caught.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "audit-repository-guide", title: "Protect the record on SBD-AUDIT01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Keep the audit record intact, even from its administrators", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "audit-repository-package", title: "Protect the record on SBD-AUDIT01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository/SecureByDesign-audit-repository-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:audit-repository:repository-design", title: "Design the repository and build SBD-AUDIT01", minutes: 30, summary: "The repository has one job: keep the collector's record intact for its whole retention period, even from its own administrators. Size it, fix the one way in, name every identity and what it may do, and build SBD-AUDIT01 where the model puts it — before a single archive arrives.", objectives: ["Size the repository for the retention period you record and the volume the collector actually receives.", "Define the single write path and the identities that may write, read, and delete.", "Build SBD-AUDIT01 in its own OU, administered only by the audit custodian."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.10: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects Windows events into the ForwardedEvents log (on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, overwriting the oldest) and syslog into daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog, keeps the buffer period course 1.10 recorded, and the register carries row F11 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40, VLAN 50) as Windows Server with a separate data volume (D: in this sample), ready to join sbd.test, with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off; lesson 1 sizes the volume.\n\nHave course 1.8's administration seats in place — the audit custodian's separate accounts in SBD Audit Custodians work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A04), and directory changes are Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01 — plus the Tier 1 SCC copy course 1.8 installed on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, at course 1.4's tool and benchmark versions, for the first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the second of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, this course gives it a protected home on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01. Protection is something you can show: one write path, permissions that refuse even administrators, a hash for every archive, and retention that runs only as an approved, logged process. How long each stage keeps the record depends on your environment and policy: the course says what each stage keeps and why, and you record the periods you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the repository's rules from course 1.10's design (AU-11): the collector buffers long enough to cover a missed archive night and a source replaying its backlog; the repository keeps the protected copy for the whole retention period your records policy requires; and analysis keeps a shorter searchable window for investigations. Each period depends on your environment and policy — record the values you chose and who approved them. The repository holds the protected copy and nothing else — it is not a search tool, and nothing is removed from it except by the custodian's approved, logged retention process."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Size it (AU-4): on SBD-SYSLOG01, export the last 24 hours of ForwardedEvents to a temporary file and add up the syslog files written in the same day. Multiply a typical day by the days in your recorded retention period, add headroom for growth (the fleet joins in course 1.13, so revisit the figure then), and record the D: size and a disk-space warning threshold set the way the collector's is — a value that depends on your environment, recorded with the reason."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Fix the one way in (rule F11): SBD-SYSLOG01 writes to the LogArchive share over SMB 3 with encryption as SBD-Archive\$, a group managed service account (gMSA) whose password no person knows. Nothing else connects to the share; people work on SBD-AUDIT01 itself, in sessions opened from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 under rule A04."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Name every other identity and its one job: SBD-Catalog\$ hashes arrivals, SBD-Retention\$ is the only account the design lets delete, SBD Auditors read the record in remote sessions from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and SBD Audit Custodians (course 1.8) — the custodian's separate admin accounts — run the server but can't delete records either. In a Tier 0 session from AdminJumpBox01, create SBD Auditors under the Groups OU, add it to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's seat GPO beside SBD Tier 1 Admins and SBD Audit Custodians (Allow log on locally, a change to course 1.8's STIG value that its exception record notes), and run Get-KdsRootKey on SBD-DC01. If it returns nothing, create the forest's one KDS root key with Add-KdsRootKey -EffectiveImmediately now: domain controllers wait up to ten hours before gMSAs can be created and used, so lesson 2 creates the accounts."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Build SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40, VLAN 50) as Windows Server with its data volume, DNS on both domain controllers, and time from the domain hierarchy (course 1.3): hypervisor time synchronization off, and w32tm /query /source naming a domain controller. Join it into a new Audit Servers OU (OU=Audit Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test, created from AdminJumpBox01 with deletion protection) that already carries course 1.5's member-server STIG GPOs and course 1.8's two deny GPOs (the tier logon GPO and the network-logon deny GPO, never its Servers administrators GPO), so nothing granted to ordinary server administrators through the Servers OU reaches it. By GPO on that OU, make SBD Audit Custodians the local Administrators in place of Domain Admins, as the STIG's member-server rule requires, put SBD Auditors in Remote Management Users, and scope Remote Desktop and Windows Remote Management to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (10.0.60.20). Its Windows updates come through SBD-WSUS01 once course 1.14 builds it, in a group of their own: downloaded only, and installed when the custodian starts them, never by ordinary Tier 1 operators."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): one day of each stream, for sizing.\nwevtutil epl ForwardedEvents \$env:TEMP\\sizing.evtx \"/q:*[System[TimeCreated[timediff(@SystemTime) <= 86400000]]]\" /ow:true\n(Get-Item \$env:TEMP\\sizing.evtx).Length / 1MB\n(Get-ChildItem D:\\Logs\\Syslog -Recurse -File | Where-Object LastWriteTime -gt (Get-Date).AddDays(-1) | Measure-Object Length -Sum).Sum / 1MB\n# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the KDS root key's effective time, and where SBD-AUDIT01 landed.\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01 { Get-KdsRootKey | Select-Object KeyId, EffectiveTime }\nGet-ADGroup 'SBD Auditors' | Select-Object DistinguishedName\nGet-ADComputer SBD-AUDIT01 | Select-Object DistinguishedName\n# From SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (custodian account): who administers SBD-AUDIT01.\nInvoke-Command SBD-AUDIT01 { Get-LocalGroupMember Administrators; w32tm /query /source }"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The rules with their recorded retention periods, the sizing, and the identity table are written; the KDS root key exists with its effective time recorded; and SBD-AUDIT01 sits in the Audit Servers OU with SBD Audit Custodians in place of Domain Admins and management allowed from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 alone, and its time comes from a domain controller."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the repository rules and sizing sheet, the identity table (who writes, reads, deletes, and administers), the KDS root key's ID and effective time, and SBD-AUDIT01's OU placement and local Administrators list."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nMicrosoft · Create a Key Distribution Service (KDS) root key\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/create-the-key-distribution-services-kds-root-key\n\nMicrosoft · Wevtutil\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wevtutil\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does SBD-AUDIT01 get its own OU instead of joining the Servers OU?", answers: ["Windows Server can't join an OU named Servers", "So nothing granted to ordinary server administrators through the Servers OU — such as local administrator rights — reaches the repository", "OUs make the archive share faster"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Policy follows links. A separate OU, with the STIG and tier policies linked to it, keeps the repository's administration with the custodian alone (rule A04).", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why create the KDS root key at the start of this lesson?", ["Domain controllers wait up to ten hours after it is created before gMSAs can be created and used", "It encrypts the archive share", "SBD-AUDIT01 can't join the domain without it"], 0, "The wait lets the key replicate to every domain controller first. A forest needs only one KDS root key; later courses reuse it."), QuizQuestion("Which identity may delete records from the repository?", ["Any member of SBD Audit Custodians", "SBD-Archive\$, because it wrote them", "Only SBD-Retention\$, running the approved, logged retention process"], 2, "Deletion is a process, not a person's privilege. The writer, the readers, and even the custodians' admin accounts hold no delete right on the record.")], recap: ["The repository keeps the record for the whole recorded retention period; the collector and analysis keep shorter working copies.", "One write path (F11), one writer, and one identity that can delete.", "SBD-AUDIT01 is built in its own OU and run only by the custodian, who also installs its updates."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds the archive path: the managed accounts, the permissions, the encrypted share, and the tasks that write and catalog each day's record.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository#repository-design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:audit-repository:protect", title: "Archive, hash, and protect the record", minutes: 40, summary: "The repository is where the record outlives mistakes and attackers alike: daily archives from the collector over an encrypted share, hashed before they leave and again on arrival, readable by few, and deletable by process only.", objectives: ["Archive the collector's two streams to the repository every day.", "Hash and catalog every archive on arrival.", "Enforce create-without-delete, read-few, and delete-by-process-only permissions."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.10: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects Windows events into the ForwardedEvents log (on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, overwriting the oldest) and syslog into daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog, keeps the buffer period course 1.10 recorded, and the register carries row F11 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40, VLAN 50) as Windows Server with a separate data volume (D: in this sample), ready to join sbd.test, with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off; lesson 1 sizes the volume.\n\nHave course 1.8's administration seats in place — the audit custodian's separate accounts in SBD Audit Custodians work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A04), and directory changes are Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01 — plus the Tier 1 SCC copy course 1.8 installed on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, at course 1.4's tool and benchmark versions, for the first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the second of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, this course gives it a protected home on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01. Protection is something you can show: one write path, permissions that refuse even administrators, a hash for every archive, and retention that runs only as an approved, logged process. How long each stage keeps the record depends on your environment and policy: the course says what each stage keeps and why, and you record the periods you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0), create the three gMSAs now that the KDS root key is in effect, each usable by one computer only: New-ADServiceAccount SBD-Archive -DNSHostName SBD-Archive.sbd.test -PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword 'SBD-SYSLOG01\$', then SBD-Catalog and SBD-Retention the same way for 'SBD-AUDIT01\$'. That allowed-computer list is each account's real access list: an administrator of that computer can act as the account, so it names one host."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On SBD-AUDIT01, create D:\\LogArchive with ForwardedEvents, Syslog, Catalog, and Retention folders, remove its inherited permissions (icacls D:\\LogArchive /inheritance:r), and grant, inheritable to everything below: SYSTEM full control; read and execute (RX) to SBD Audit Custodians, SBD Auditors, SBD-Catalog\$, and SBD-Retention\$; and OWNER RIGHTS read permissions only on files, '*S-1-3-4:(OI)(IO)(RC)', which takes away a file owner's implicit right to rewrite its permissions. On each stream folder, give SBD-Archive\$ create files on that folder only — icacls D:\\LogArchive\\ForwardedEvents /grant 'SBD\\SBD-Archive\$:(WD,RA,S)' — and SBD-Retention\$ delete on its files, 'SBD\\SBD-Retention\$:(OI)(IO)(DE)'. A file's creator gets the access it asks for while creating it, then only what the file inherits, so the writer can add archives but never read, replace, or delete them."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Share it: New-SmbShare -Name LogArchive -Path D:\\LogArchive -EncryptData \$true -ChangeAccess 'SBD\\SBD-Archive\$'. Share permissions are only the outer gate for network access — effective access is the stricter of share and NTFS — so the share names its one writer, and Get-SmbShareAccess must show nobody else, not even Everyone. Leave RejectUnencryptedAccess at its default (True) with SMB 1 off, so only encrypted SMB 3 sessions reach the share, and scope every enabled inbound rule for TCP 445 to 10.0.20.20: VLAN 50 will also hold SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) and the other security servers Inside the Network places there, whose traffic to this server never crosses the core, so only this host firewall stops them."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 work), register the archive task to run daily at 01:00 as SBD-Archive\$: Register-ScheduledTask with New-ScheduledTaskPrincipal -UserId 'SBD\\SBD-Archive\$' -LogonType Password — no password is typed; Windows retrieves the managed one. Keep the script in a folder only administrators can change, because whoever can edit it acts as the writer. Through a GPO filtered to SBD-SYSLOG01 and linked above the STIG GPO, give SBD-Archive\$ Log on as a batch job and add it to Event Log Readers with a Group Policy preference that adds a member rather than replacing the list (course 1.12 adds the Splunk forwarder's account to the same group the same way) — user rights don't merge across GPOs, so the list repeats any entries the STIG sets for that right — then grant it read on D:\\Logs\\Syslog and a staging folder of its own, and send the grants to the owner of the course 1.5 exception record on AdminJumpBox01."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Each run, the script exports every completed UTC day not yet archived from ForwardedEvents into its staging folder — wevtutil epl with a TimeCreated filter for that day — and zips that day's syslog files, so a missed night is caught up while the collector's buffer still holds the events. It writes each file's SHA-256 (Get-FileHash) into a .sha256 file, copies the archive and then its .sha256 to the share, and keeps its own list of what it sent, because the writer can't list the share. When course 1.10's health check records a gap window, re-export the affected days once the source has replayed its backlog, under a new name such as ForwardedEvents-2026-10-12-r1.evtx: forwarded events keep their source's times, so the late ones land in the right day, and the first archive stays as it was."), LessonSection("Step 6", "On SBD-AUDIT01, register the catalog task the same way, hourly as SBD-Catalog\$, with Log on as a batch job through the Audit Servers OU's policy and write, but not delete, on the Catalog folder and the files in it. For each archive whose .sha256 has arrived, it computes the SHA-256, appends a line to catalog.csv (columns Arrived, File, Size, SentSHA256, SHA256, Result), and writes the same line as an event from the source SBD-Repository (registered once with New-EventLog): 1000 when the hashes match, 1001 when they don't or a .sha256 is still missing after the wait you set and recorded — a finding, not a file. Then fill in the AU table: where each stage's data lives, its retention clock, and who can touch it — the kind of artifact course 2.2 shows joining the Body of Evidence for the audit controls."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-AUDIT01 (custodian session): the share, its encryption, its one writer, and a stream folder's permissions.\nGet-SmbShare -Name LogArchive | Select-Object Name, Path, EncryptData\nGet-SmbShareAccess -Name LogArchive\nGet-SmbServerConfiguration | Select-Object RejectUnencryptedAccess, EnableSMB1Protocol\nicacls D:\\LogArchive\\ForwardedEvents\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the task's identity and its last result (0 = success).\n(Get-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'SBD Archive to AUDIT01').Principal | Select-Object UserId, LogonType\nGet-ScheduledTaskInfo -TaskName 'SBD Archive to AUDIT01' | Select-Object LastRunTime, LastTaskResult\n# Back on SBD-AUDIT01: who wrote the newest archives, and their catalog lines.\nGet-ChildItem D:\\LogArchive\\ForwardedEvents | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 2 Name, Length, @{n='Owner';e={(Get-Acl \$_.FullName).Owner}}\nImport-Csv D:\\LogArchive\\Catalog\\catalog.csv | Select-Object -Last 4"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Daily archives and their .sha256 files arrive owned by SBD-Archive\$ on a share that requires encryption and refuses unencrypted access, every catalog line shows matching hashes, Get-SmbShareAccess lists the writer alone, and icacls shows delete rights for SYSTEM and SBD-Retention\$ only."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the share, encryption, and permission listings, the two task definitions with their gMSA principals, the first catalog lines, and the completed AU table."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nMicrosoft · New-ScheduledTaskPrincipal\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/scheduledtasks/new-scheduledtaskprincipal\n\nMicrosoft · New-SmbShare\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/smbshare/new-smbshare\n\nMicrosoft · SMB security enhancements (SMB encryption)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-security\n\nMicrosoft · Icacls\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/icacls\n\nMicrosoft · Special identity groups (Owner Rights)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/understand-special-identities-groups\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nMicrosoft · Wevtutil\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wevtutil\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What does the hash catalog actually protect against?", answers: ["Disk space exhaustion", "Silent modification — an archive that no longer matches its recorded hash announces itself as tampered or corrupted", "Slow searches"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Integrity is only provable if you recorded what intact looked like. The catalog is that record.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How does anything ever get deleted from the repository?", ["Any administrator's right-click", "The analysis system prunes it", "Only the documented, logged retention process"], 2, "The point of the repository is surviving its administrators. Deletion exists — as a process with a log, never a privilege."), QuizQuestion("Where does the AU table go after this course?", ["Into the Body of Evidence for the audit controls — course 2.2 shows how artifacts like it are organized", "Back into course 1.1, retroactively", "Nowhere — it is decorative"], 0, "Where the records live, how long, and who reads them is precisely what the audit control family asks an assessor to verify.")], recap: ["Archives arrive daily over one encrypted path, with two hashes that must agree.", "Readers are named; deletion is a process, not a right.", "The AU table is written for the authorization path."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 verifies the catalog every day and watches who touches the repository.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository#protect", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:audit-repository:integrity", title: "Verify the catalog and watch the repository", minutes: 30, summary: "A catalog nobody checks is a list of hopes. Re-hash every archive on a schedule, treat a mismatch as a finding, audit who reads or tries to change the repository, and keep the readers as few as the day they were approved.", objectives: ["Verify every archive against the catalog on a schedule and report the result.", "Handle a mismatch as a finding, not a file to fix.", "Audit access to the repository and review who may read and write it."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.10: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects Windows events into the ForwardedEvents log (on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, overwriting the oldest) and syslog into daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog, keeps the buffer period course 1.10 recorded, and the register carries row F11 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40, VLAN 50) as Windows Server with a separate data volume (D: in this sample), ready to join sbd.test, with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off; lesson 1 sizes the volume.\n\nHave course 1.8's administration seats in place — the audit custodian's separate accounts in SBD Audit Custodians work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A04), and directory changes are Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01 — plus the Tier 1 SCC copy course 1.8 installed on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, at course 1.4's tool and benchmark versions, for the first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the second of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, this course gives it a protected home on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01. Protection is something you can show: one write path, permissions that refuse even administrators, a hash for every archive, and retention that runs only as an approved, logged process. How long each stage keeps the record depends on your environment and policy: the course says what each stage keeps and why, and you record the periods you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Add a daily verification task on SBD-AUDIT01, running as SBD-Catalog\$ like the catalog task: it re-hashes every archive and writes D:\\LogArchive\\Catalog\\verify-<date>.csv with one row per file and its result — OK, MISMATCH, MISSING (cataloged but absent, or a day with no archive), or UNKNOWN (present but never cataloged). It then writes one event from SBD-Repository: 1100 when every row is OK, 1101 when any isn't."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Treat event 1101 as a finding: don't re-hash the file until it passes, edit its catalog line, or delete it. Record what failed and when it last verified, and read the repository's access events for that window. Re-export that day under a new name through the normal path while the collector's buffer still holds it — or, once course 1.19 has made them, recover an offline copy — keep the damaged file as evidence, and have the custodian report an incident."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Audit the repository itself: in a separate GPO linked to the Audit Servers OU, recorded as a documented addition with the exception record's owner, turn on Audit File System (success and failure) and Audit Detailed File Share (failure). On D:\\LogArchive, add auditing entries for failed access of any kind by Everyone; successful delete, change permissions, and take ownership by Everyone; and successful reads by SBD Auditors and SBD Audit Custodians. File System auditing writes events only where those entries match, so the service accounts' routine work stays quiet."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Send that trail off the box: from AdminJumpBox01, add SBD-AUDIT01 to SBD Event Sources, link course 1.10's enrolling GPO to the Audit Servers OU, and restart SBD-AUDIT01 so both apply. On the collector, widen the WinRM 5985 scope to 10.0.50.40 (rule F17): its Security log, with events 4656, 4660, 4663, 4670, and 5145, now arrives through the subscription's Security query, so add only a query for its SBD-Repository events in the Application log, filtered to that computer. Someone who can alter SBD-AUDIT01's logs or its catalog still can't reach the copies on the collector, which other administrators run."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Review the people each quarter and after any change: compare SBD Auditors, SBD Audit Custodians, and SBD-AUDIT01's local Administrators with the approved list, and check that only SBD-SYSLOG01 may retrieve SBD-Archive\$'s password — any computer added there could write as the archive job. Record the review with its date and reviewer."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-AUDIT01 (custodian session): the latest verification's exceptions, and the result events.\n\$report = Get-ChildItem D:\\LogArchive\\Catalog\\verify-*.csv | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 1\nImport-Csv \$report.FullName | Where-Object Result -ne 'OK'\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Application'; ProviderName='SBD-Repository'; Id=1100,1101} -MaxEvents 3 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id\n# The audit policy and the auditing entries on the repository.\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"File System\"\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"Detailed File Share\"\n(Get-Acl D:\\LogArchive -Audit).Audit | Format-Table IdentityReference, FileSystemRights, AuditFlags\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the repository's events arrive with their source name.\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; Id=4656,4663,1100,1101; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddDays(-1)} -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, MachineName\n# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the readers, and the computers allowed to act as the writer.\nGet-ADGroupMember 'SBD Auditors' | Select-Object Name, SamAccountName\n(Get-ADServiceAccount SBD-Archive -Properties PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword).PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Today's report covers every archive and its event is 1100 (or an open 1101 is being handled as a finding), the audit policy and auditing entries are in place, the repository's events reach ForwardedEvents on the collector with MachineName SBD-AUDIT01, and the readers review is recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep a verification report with its event, the audit policy and auditing-entry listings, one forwarded repository event, and the dated readers review."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nMicrosoft · Advanced audit policy configuration (File System, Detailed File Share)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/advanced-audit-policy-configuration\n\nMicrosoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "The daily verification reports a MISMATCH. What is the right first move?", answers: ["Re-hash the file until the result is OK", "Update the catalog line to the new hash", "Keep the file as evidence, record the finding, and read the access events since it last verified"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "A mismatch is a finding, not a file. The catalog line records what intact looked like; changing it would erase the evidence that something happened.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why are the repository's own access events forwarded to the collector?", ["So someone who can alter SBD-AUDIT01's logs still can't erase the copy that left the box", "Forwarding makes the events smaller", "SBD-AUDIT01 can't keep a Security log"], 0, "Audit information must be protected from the people it describes. The forwarded copy lives on another system with other administrators."), QuizQuestion("Why must only SBD-SYSLOG01 be allowed to retrieve SBD-Archive\$'s password?", ["Otherwise the gMSA password stops rotating", "Any computer allowed there could write archives as the archive job", "SBD-AUDIT01 needs the password to read archives"], 1, "The allowed-computer list is the gMSA's real access list. Reviewing it is part of reviewing who can write to the record.")], recap: ["Every archive is re-hashed daily, and the result is an event.", "A mismatch is preserved and investigated, never re-hashed away.", "Access to the repository is audited, forwarded, and reviewed."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 runs retention as a process and proves an old archive can still be read.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository#integrity", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:audit-repository:retention", title: "Run retention and prove retrieval", minutes: 30, summary: "Retention cuts both ways: records must last the full period, and they must go when it ends — by a process with a log, never by a person's right-click. Run retention the way the design says, then prove the other half: an archive from the past can be found, verified, and read.", objectives: ["Run retention through an approved, logged process under its own identity.", "Show that nothing younger than the retention period can be removed.", "Retrieve a past archive, verify its hash, and read its events."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.10: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects Windows events into the ForwardedEvents log (on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, overwriting the oldest) and syslog into daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog, keeps the buffer period course 1.10 recorded, and the register carries row F11 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40, VLAN 50) as Windows Server with a separate data volume (D: in this sample), ready to join sbd.test, with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off; lesson 1 sizes the volume.\n\nHave course 1.8's administration seats in place — the audit custodian's separate accounts in SBD Audit Custodians work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A04), and directory changes are Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01 — plus the Tier 1 SCC copy course 1.8 installed on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, at course 1.4's tool and benchmark versions, for the first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the second of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, this course gives it a protected home on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01. Protection is something you can show: one write path, permissions that refuse even administrators, a hash for every archive, and retention that runs only as an approved, logged process. How long each stage keeps the record depends on your environment and policy: the course says what each stage keeps and why, and you record the periods you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build the retention task on SBD-AUDIT01 to run as SBD-Retention\$ (batch logon, like SBD-Catalog\$) with no trigger, so it runs only when the custodian starts it. It reads the newest approved list in D:\\LogArchive\\Retention, re-verifies each listed archive against the catalog, refuses any file younger than the recorded retention period by its catalog arrival date — whatever the list says — and deletes the rest with their .sha256 files. It appends one line per file to retention-log.csv (date, file, catalog hash, list hash, result), which the daily verification now reads, so a retired archive reports RETIRED rather than MISSING. SBD-Retention\$ gets write on the Retention folder and the custodians create files there only, so a list can be added but not edited."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Run it as a two-person process, the independent approval rule A04 asks for (AU-9(5), dual authorization): the custodian prepares the list of archives past retention, an approver who is not a custodian approves it and records the list's SHA-256, and the custodian starts the task with Start-ScheduledTask. The log carries the same list hash, tying the run to the approval, and every deletion also writes events 4663 and 4660 naming SBD-Retention\$, which reach the collector."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Prove the guard now: nothing in a new lab is past its retention period, so put one archive from lesson 2 on an approved list marked as a test. The run must refuse it, log the refusal, and leave the file and its catalog line untouched."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prove retrieval: pick the oldest archive in the catalog, confirm its SHA-256 still matches its catalog line, and read it — Get-WinEvent -Path for a ForwardedEvents archive, or expand a syslog ZIP into D:\\Retrieval, a working folder outside the archive, and search it. Security events render because SBD-AUDIT01 has the same Windows publishers; the event data itself is in the file either way."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Record the retrieval — the archive, both hashes, the events you read, who, and when — and repeat it each quarter with an older archive, so over time you prove retrieval across the whole retention period. Course 1.19 retrieves the offline copies the same way."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-AUDIT01 (custodian session): no trigger, so only a person starts a run.\n(Get-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'SBD Repository Retention').Triggers.Count   # expected 0\n(Get-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'SBD Repository Retention').Principal.UserId   # expected: the SBD-Retention\$ account\nGet-FileHash D:\\LogArchive\\Retention\\approved-test-list.txt   # the hash the approver recorded\nStart-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'SBD Repository Retention'\nImport-Csv D:\\LogArchive\\Retention\\retention-log.csv | Select-Object -Last 5\n# Retrieval: the oldest archive's hash against its catalog line, then the events inside.\n\$file = Get-ChildItem D:\\LogArchive\\ForwardedEvents\\*.evtx | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -First 1\n(Import-Csv D:\\LogArchive\\Catalog\\catalog.csv | Where-Object File -eq \$file.Name).SHA256 -eq (Get-FileHash \$file.FullName).Hash\nGet-WinEvent -Path \$file.FullName -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, MachineName"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The retention task has no trigger and runs as SBD-Retention\$, the approved test run refused the young archive and logged the refusal with the list's hash, and the retrieved archive's hash matches its catalog line and its events read correctly."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the retention task definition, the approved test list with its hash and approval, the retention log, and the retrieval record with both hashes and a sample of the events read."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · New-ScheduledTaskPrincipal\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/scheduledtasks/new-scheduledtaskprincipal\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WinEvent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does the retention task have no schedule?", answers: ["Scheduled tasks can't delete files", "So deletion happens only when the custodian starts an approved run, never silently on a timer", "Triggers would slow the server"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A timer deletes whether or not anyone looked. An approved, started, logged run makes every deletion a decision two people can account for.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("An approved list includes an archive younger than the retention period. What happens?", ["The task refuses it, logs the refusal, and leaves the file and its catalog line in place", "The task deletes it, because the list was approved", "The catalog line is removed but the file stays"], 0, "The retention period is enforced by the process itself, not by the care of whoever wrote the list. An approval can't shorten the record's life."), QuizQuestion("What does a successful retrieval prove that the daily verification doesn't?", ["That the file still exists", "That the hash algorithm is SHA-256", "That someone can actually find, verify, and read the record when it is needed"], 2, "Verification proves the bytes are unchanged; retrieval proves the record is usable for an investigation, which is what retention is for.")], recap: ["Retention runs only on an approved list, under its own identity, with a log.", "The task refuses anything younger than the retention period.", "A past archive was found, verified, and read — and will be again each quarter."], nextStep: "The final lesson tries to break the record on purpose, takes the first baseline, and hands off to analysis.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository#retention", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:audit-repository:repository-prove", title: "Prove the record survives and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "Test the protection the way an attacker or a careless administrator would: try to delete an archive and tamper with a copy, and check that each attempt is refused or caught, and recorded. Then take SBD-AUDIT01's first baseline and hand the record to analysis.", objectives: ["Show that an administrator's delete attempt is refused and logged on and off the repository.", "Show that a tampered copy fails verification against the catalog.", "Baseline SBD-AUDIT01 and write the handoff to course 1.12."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete course 1.10: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects Windows events into the ForwardedEvents log (on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, overwriting the oldest) and syslog into daily files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog, keeps the buffer period course 1.10 recorded, and the register carries row F11 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision SBD-AUDIT01 (10.0.50.40, VLAN 50) as Windows Server with a separate data volume (D: in this sample), ready to join sbd.test, with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off; lesson 1 sizes the volume.\n\nHave course 1.8's administration seats in place — the audit custodian's separate accounts in SBD Audit Custodians work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A04), and directory changes are Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01 — plus the Tier 1 SCC copy course 1.8 installed on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, at course 1.4's tool and benchmark versions, for the first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the second of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, this course gives it a protected home on SBD-AUDIT01, and course 1.12 analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01. Protection is something you can show: one write path, permissions that refuse even administrators, a hash for every archive, and retention that runs only as an approved, logged process. How long each stage keeps the record depends on your environment and policy: the course says what each stage keeps and why, and you record the periods you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "In a custodian session on SBD-AUDIT01 — an administrator of this server — try to delete the newest archive with Remove-Item. It must fail with access denied: administrators hold read only, and only SBD-Retention\$ can delete. An administrator can still force their way in — by taking ownership, using backup and restore privileges, or running a process as SYSTEM — which is why only the custodians administer this server and those steps are audited too. Use the newest archive, so if the delete wrongly succeeds that day can be re-exported from the collector's buffer — and you have a finding to fix first."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Find the record of the attempt: event 4656, an audit failure naming your account, the archive, and DELETE, in SBD-AUDIT01's Security log — then the same event in ForwardedEvents on SBD-SYSLOG01 with MachineName SBD-AUDIT01. The attempt and its refusal now exist on two systems with different administrators."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Tamper with a copy, never the record: copy the newest archive to D:\\Retrieval\\tamper-test, append one byte to the copy, and compare its SHA-256 with the catalog line. The copy must fail while the record still matches — the same check that would catch an altered offline copy in course 1.19."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Take SBD-AUDIT01's first SCAP baseline with the course 1.4 SCC and benchmark versions, run locally in the custodian's session: this server refuses SMB from VLAN 60, so a remote scan can't reach it, and the installer arrives from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's course 1.8 copy over PowerShell remoting (Copy-Item -ToSession) with its course 1.4 hash checked. Store the results under C:\\Evidence\\audit-repository with hashes, and classify open items as course 1.4 taught — fix by GPO, deviation candidate, or POA&M candidate — noting this course's documented additions: batch-logon rights and object-access auditing."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the handoff to course 1.12: the repository keeps the protected copy and stays out of the analysis flow, which takes its working copy from the collector over F10; a day's events reach the repository in the next morning's archive, which is where an end-to-end trace finds them; the repository's events — 1001 and 1101 findings, refused access, and retention deletions — are ready to become alerts; and course 1.19 sets up the backup of the archive and its catalog, which the audit custodian carries to the backup cabinet."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Custodian session on SBD-AUDIT01: try to delete the newest archive (expected: access denied).\n\$newest = Get-ChildItem D:\\LogArchive\\ForwardedEvents\\*.evtx | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 1\nRemove-Item \$newest.FullName\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4656; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddMinutes(-15)} | Where-Object KeywordsDisplayNames -contains 'Audit Failure' | Select-Object -First 3 TimeCreated, Id\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the same refusal, forwarded.\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; Id=4656; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName\n# Back on SBD-AUDIT01: a tampered copy fails the catalog check while the record passes.\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory D:\\Retrieval\\tamper-test -Force | Out-Null\nCopy-Item \$newest.FullName D:\\Retrieval\\tamper-test\\\nAdd-Content \"D:\\Retrieval\\tamper-test\\\$(\$newest.Name)\" -Value 'x' -NoNewline\n\$expected = (Import-Csv D:\\LogArchive\\Catalog\\catalog.csv | Where-Object File -eq \$newest.Name).SHA256\n(Get-FileHash \"D:\\Retrieval\\tamper-test\\\$(\$newest.Name)\").Hash -eq \$expected   # expected False\n(Get-FileHash \$newest.FullName).Hash -eq \$expected   # expected True\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\audit-repository\\*"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The delete failed and appears as event 4656 on SBD-AUDIT01 and in ForwardedEvents on the collector, the tampered copy fails verification while the record passes, SBD-AUDIT01 has its first baseline, and the handoff names what course 1.12 inherits."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the refused delete with both events, the tamper test's hash comparison, the scan set with hashes, and the handoff note to course 1.12."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nMicrosoft · Advanced audit policy configuration (File System, Detailed File Share)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/advanced-audit-policy-configuration\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WinEvent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "The custodian administers SBD-AUDIT01. Why can't their admin account delete an archive?", answers: ["Administrators hold only read on the record; delete belongs to SBD-Retention\$ alone, through the approved process", "Windows never lets administrators delete files", "The archive is open in another program"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Running the server and owning the record are separate rights. The permissions give the custodians read, and the attempt itself is recorded on two systems.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why tamper with a copy instead of the record?", ["Copies are faster to hash", "It proves detection without damaging evidence — the catalog catches a changed copy exactly as it would a changed record", "The record can't be read"], 1, "The check compares bytes with the catalog wherever they sit. Testing on a copy proves the check and leaves the record intact."), QuizQuestion("Where does analysis get its data after this course?", ["From SBD-AUDIT01's archives", "From the offline backup cabinet", "From the collector over the forwarder path (F10); the repository stays out of the analysis flow"], 2, "One copy to keep, one copy to question. The protected record never depends on the search tier.")], recap: ["An administrator's delete was refused and recorded on two systems.", "A tampered copy failed verification; the record still passes.", "SBD-AUDIT01 is baselined, and course 1.12 inherits a protected record."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.11. Course 1.12 builds analysis in SBD-SPLUNK01 on a working copy from the collector and turns the repository's findings into alerts.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/audit-repository#repository-prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "splunk-analysis", number: "1.12", title: "Analyze the record in SBD-SPLUNK01", subtitle: "Search a working copy, own the alerts, prove the chain", category: "Network build", description: "The last of three logging courses: build SBD-SPLUNK01 as a domain-joined Linux server patched only from SBD-REPO01, install Splunk Enterprise from hashed media, forward the collector's two streams over TLS, build the searches that answer this network's first questions, turn them and the pipeline's own failures into alerts someone owns, and trace one event from its source through the collector and the repository to the analyst's screen.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "splunk-analysis-guide", title: "Analyze the record in SBD-SPLUNK01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Search a working copy, own the alerts, prove the chain", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "splunk-analysis-package", title: "Analyze the record in SBD-SPLUNK01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis/SecureByDesign-splunk-analysis-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:splunk-analysis:analysis-plan", title: "Plan analysis and build SBD-SPLUNK01", minutes: 35, summary: "Analysis works on a copy, not on the record: SBD-AUDIT01 keeps the evidence, and SBD-SPLUNK01 keeps a searchable working copy, for a period your environment sets, for the people who question them. Decide the indexes, retention, roles, and console paths first, then build the domain-joined Linux host that runs it — patched only from SBD-REPO01 and on domain time from its first boot.", objectives: ["Define what analysis is for, its indexes and the retention you choose, and who may do what from where.", "Stage the Splunk media across the air gap with verified hashes.", "Build SBD-SPLUNK01 as a domain-joined Linux server on domain time, patched only from SBD-REPO01."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.10 and 1.11: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects the two streams — the ForwardedEvents log on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, sized for the buffer course 1.10 recorded, and daily syslog files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog — SBD-AUDIT01 archives them every night, and the register carries row F10 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-SPLUNK01 on VLAN 50 (10.0.50.10) with a separate data volume for the indexes (/srv/splunk in this sample), and have course 1.9's client package for SBD-REPO01 and its Linux assessment set (SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01), course 1.3's chrony file, course 1.6's root certificate hash and SBD Server Authentication template, and course 1.8's administration seats ready.\n\nStage across the air gap, hashed as in course 1.4: the Splunk Enterprise package for Linux (.tgz), the universal forwarder installer for Windows (.msi), the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, an Enterprise license file, and DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG for lesson 5's checklist (the Linux scan reuses course 1.9's set)."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the last of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, course 1.11 protects it on SBD-AUDIT01, and this course analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01, then traces one event through all three. Analysis is a search someone actually runs and an alert someone owns. Linux commands are for the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose. Retention periods, alert thresholds and alert expirations depend on your environment and policy: the course says what each one is for, and you record the value you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write analysis's job and its limits: SBD-SPLUNK01 indexes a working copy of the collector's two streams (rule F10) for searches, dashboards, and alerts, while the protected record stays on SBD-AUDIT01 — when an investigation needs evidence, it retrieves the archive. Plan one index per stream, sbd_wineventlog for the forwarded Windows events and sbd_syslog for the syslog files, each searchable for as long as investigations in your environment need to look back and then deleted, because the repository already keeps the record for its own retention period. The period depends on your environment and policy; record the value you chose and why. Size each from a typical day times that period plus headroom, starting from course 1.11's daily figures until lesson 2 measures what Splunk actually stores, and cap its size above that, so age rather than size retires the data."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Decide who does what: analysts (sbd_analyst) search and review alerts but change nothing, administrators (sbd_admin) manage inputs, indexes, roles, and alerts, and no role used by people gets delete_by_keyword, so nothing in the working copy can be quietly removed. Everyone signs in with a named Splunk account, so no domain password is typed into the console, and the admin account made at install is sealed like course 1.8's break-glass account. Splunk Web and SSH answer VLAN 60 only: analysts and the host's Tier 1 administrators work from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A02), and the Tier 0 operators who own the alerts on the domain controllers, the model's DC-facing scope, from AdminJumpBox01 (rule A01). Splunk's roles can't fence one administrator's alerts from another's, and root on the host can change any Splunk file, so keep both groups small and every change on record."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Stage the media as course 1.4 taught: the Splunk Enterprise package for Linux (.tgz) and the universal forwarder installer for Windows (.msi), each checked on the connected side against the SHA-512 that Splunk publishes for it, plus the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, your Enterprise license file, and DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG. Hash the set for the transfer record and check it again on the lab side before anything is opened. Don't plan on the 60-day trial license: Splunk Free has no users, roles, or alerts, and an Enterprise license installs from its file with no connection to Splunk."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Build SBD-SPLUNK01 from hashed install media of the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with the VM's hypervisor time synchronization turned off so the domain controllers are its only clock: the name SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test, the static address 10.0.50.10/24 with the core's VLAN 50 interface as gateway (10.0.50.1 in this sample), both domain controllers as DNS servers, and the data volume mounted at /srv/splunk. Before its first update, apply course 1.9's client package, checked against its hashes, so SBD-REPO01 is its only package source (rule F19) and no other source remains; then update from the current snapshot."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Install course 1.3's chrony file, both domain controllers with maxdistance 16, and confirm a controller is selected with a small, steady offset before anything else: Kerberos refuses a clock that is too far from the domain's. Then join sbd.test with realm join --computer-ou='OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' and a Tier 1 account, which course 1.7 allowed to create computers there (rules F01, F02, and F03), permit sign-in only to SBD Tier 1 Admins with realm permit -g, and give that group sudo through a drop-in file; SSSD then registers the host's address in DNS."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Make the host firewall the register's copy, with firewalld: SSH from 10.0.60.20, Splunk Web on TCP 8000 from 10.0.60.10 and 10.0.60.20, and TCP 9997 from SBD-SYSLOG01 (10.0.20.20) alone. Then send the host's own syslog, sudo use included, to the collector over TLS (rule F07) as course 1.10 enrolled SBD-REPO01: rsyslog-gnutls from the snapshot, the course 1.6 root placed in the system trust store by hand as course 1.9 placed it on SBD-REPO01 (exported on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and its SHA-256 checked against course 1.6's record), the collector's certificate name checked, and SBD-SPLUNK01 added to the collector's TCP 6514 sender list."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SPLUNK01 (Tier 1 SSH session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01)\ncd /srv/media && sha256sum -c --quiet SHA256SUMS-splunk.txt   # expected: no output\ndnf repolist --all              # expected: only the sbd-repo01 repositories enabled\nchronyc sources -v; chronyc tracking   # ^* one controller, ^+ or ^- the other\nrealm list                      # sbd.test, kerberos-member, one permitted group\nopenssl x509 -inform der -in /tmp/sbd-root.cer -noout -fingerprint -sha256   # matches course 1.6's record\nfirewall-cmd --get-active-zones\nlogger -t sbd-splunk-proof 'SBD-SPLUNK01 syslog over TLS test'\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the test message in SBD-SPLUNK01's daily file\nSelect-String -Path D:\\Logs\\Syslog\\*\\*.log -Pattern 'sbd-splunk-proof' | Select-Object -First 1"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The design names analysis's job, both indexes with their retention and sizing, the roles and who holds them, and the console paths; the media matches its published and transfer hashes; and SBD-SPLUNK01 resolves to 10.0.50.10, lists SBD-REPO01 as its only package source, has a controller selected with a small, stable offset, is joined with one permitted group, trusts the course 1.6 root whose hash matches that course's record, admits only the registered paths, and its test message arrives on the collector."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the analysis design (job, indexes, the retention period you chose and why, sizing, roles, console paths), the media hashes with the transfer record, and SBD-SPLUNK01's build record: package sources, chrony output, realm list, the root certificate's hash compared with course 1.6's record, firewall zones, and its first syslog line on the collector."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Splunk · product documentation\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation\n\nSplunk · Set a retirement and archiving policy\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Indexer/Setaretirementandarchivingpolicy\n\nSplunk · Define roles on the Splunk platform with capabilities\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Security/Rolesandcapabilities\n\nRed Hat · Integrating RHEL systems directly with Windows Active Directory\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/integrating_rhel_systems_directly_with_windows_active_directory/index\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What is SBD-SPLUNK01's copy of the record for?", answers: ["Searches, dashboards, and alerts on a working copy — the protected record stays on SBD-AUDIT01", "Replacing the repository's archive once Splunk's retention period has passed", "Long-term evidence an assessor reads instead of the archive"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "One copy to keep, one copy to question. When evidence matters, it comes from the repository's hashed archive; Splunk's copy exists to be searched freely.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Which role may run Splunk's delete command on indexed events?", ["sbd_admin, because it inherits the admin role", "Any analyst who owns the search", "None that people use — no role they hold carries delete_by_keyword, so nothing in the working copy can be quietly removed"], 2, "Splunk's admin role doesn't include delete_by_keyword by default. Keeping it off every role people use means no search can hide what happened."), QuizQuestion("Why install an Enterprise license file rather than switching to Splunk Free when the 60-day trial ends?", ["Splunk Free can't read Windows events", "Splunk Free has no users, roles, or alerts, so the analyst role and every alert in this course would stop working", "Free licenses need an internet connection"], 1, "The license decides which features exist. Named accounts, roles, and alerts are the controls this course builds, and the Enterprise license file installs offline.")], recap: ["Analysis keeps a working copy for the period you chose; the record stays on SBD-AUDIT01.", "Named accounts in two roles, no delete, consoles on VLAN 60 only.", "SBD-SPLUNK01 is joined, on domain time, and patched only from SBD-REPO01."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 installs Splunk and brings in the collector's two streams over rule F10.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis#analysis-plan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:splunk-analysis:ingest", title: "Install Splunk and bring in the streams", minutes: 40, summary: "Install Splunk Enterprise on SBD-SPLUNK01 with the indexes, roles, and TLS receiving port lesson 1 designed, then put a universal forwarder on SBD-SYSLOG01 that reads the collector's two streams and sends them over rule F10. Finish by proving the copy is faithful: Splunk's counts and times must match the collector's.", objectives: ["Install Splunk Enterprise with HTTPS, the designed indexes and roles, and a TLS receiving port.", "Forward both of the collector's streams from SBD-SYSLOG01 over rule F10 with TLS.", "Show that Splunk's event counts and timestamps match the collector's."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.10 and 1.11: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects the two streams — the ForwardedEvents log on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, sized for the buffer course 1.10 recorded, and daily syslog files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog — SBD-AUDIT01 archives them every night, and the register carries row F10 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-SPLUNK01 on VLAN 50 (10.0.50.10) with a separate data volume for the indexes (/srv/splunk in this sample), and have course 1.9's client package for SBD-REPO01 and its Linux assessment set (SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01), course 1.3's chrony file, course 1.6's root certificate hash and SBD Server Authentication template, and course 1.8's administration seats ready.\n\nStage across the air gap, hashed as in course 1.4: the Splunk Enterprise package for Linux (.tgz), the universal forwarder installer for Windows (.msi), the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, an Enterprise license file, and DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG for lesson 5's checklist (the Linux scan reuses course 1.9's set)."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the last of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, course 1.11 protects it on SBD-AUDIT01, and this course analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01, then traces one event through all three. Analysis is a search someone actually runs and an alert someone owns. Linux commands are for the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose. Retention periods, alert thresholds and alert expirations depend on your environment and policy: the course says what each one is for, and you record the value you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "As root on SBD-SPLUNK01, create the splunk account (the package doesn't), extract the package with tar -xzf into /opt, and give splunk ownership of /opt/splunk and /srv/splunk. Before the first start, add SPLUNK_DB=/srv/splunk to /opt/splunk/etc/splunk-launch.conf and, where your rules require FIPS mode, SPLUNK_FIPS=1: Splunk can enter FIPS mode only at its first start (it turns it on by itself if the Linux kernel already runs in FIPS mode), and an instance started without it can't gain it later, even by upgrading. Start it as splunk with --accept-license, create the admin account when prompted and seal its password, install the license file, and enable boot-start under systemd for the splunk user."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Request one SBD Server Authentication certificate for SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test through the course 1.6 process, with the key and request made on the host with openssl and the name in both subject and subject alternative name, and keep the key readable by splunk alone. Turn on HTTPS for Splunk Web in web.conf (enableSplunkWebSSL, serverCert, privKeyPath), install the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, and, in a small app of your own, create both indexes with frozenTimePeriodInSecs set to lesson 1's retention period in seconds and the sized maxTotalDataSizeMB, and the two roles: sbd_analyst inheriting the built-in user role, sbd_admin inheriting admin. Create each person's named account with one role, then sign out of the admin account."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Open the receiving port in inputs.conf: a [splunktcp-ssl:9997] stanza and an [SSL] stanza whose serverCert is one PEM file holding the certificate, its key, and the SBD chain, with sslVersions = tls1.2. The forwarder checks the server's certificate, and requireClientCert stays false: as on the collector's syslog path in course 1.10, the sender is held to its address, by the host firewall's rule that admits 10.0.20.20 alone."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On SBD-SYSLOG01, in a Tier 1 session, install the forwarder with msiexec (AGREETOLICENSE=Yes LAUNCHSPLUNK=0 /quiet); by default it runs as a low-privilege virtual account, NT SERVICE\\SplunkForwarder. The STIG's user-rights policy replaces any rights the installer grants, so give the account what it needs explicitly: Event Log Readers membership, added beside SBD-Archive\$ in the course 1.11 GPO as a second add-member preference item so neither account replaces the other, and read on D:\\Logs\\Syslog, both recorded in the course 1.5 exception record. Copy the Windows add-on into the forwarder's apps folder and enable two inputs in its local inputs.conf: [WinEventLog://ForwardedEvents] with renderXml = true and index = sbd_wineventlog, and [monitor://D:\\Logs\\Syslog] with sourcetype = syslog, index = sbd_syslog, and host_segment = 3, so each line's host is its sender, not the collector."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Point it at rule F10 in outputs.conf: server = SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test:9997, useSSL = true, sslVerifyServerCert = true, sslAltNameToCheck = SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test, and useACK = true, so the forwarder keeps each block until the indexer confirms it. In server.conf, set sslRootCAPath to the SBD chain and turn off the forwarder's own management port ([httpServer] disableDefaultPort = true): nothing manages it remotely, and course 1.10 allows no listener the design didn't ask for. Start the service; on its first run the forwarder reads ForwardedEvents from the oldest event, so the whole buffer arrives, and its size on disk, divided by the days it covers, replaces lesson 1's sizing estimate."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Prove the copy is faithful. Pick one closed hour, count it on the collector with Get-WinEvent and in Splunk over the same window, and compare one event on both sides: the counts must match, and the event's time in Splunk must be when its source wrote it, not when it arrived. Do the same for yesterday's syslog file from each sender, check that host names each source system rather than SBD-SYSLOG01, and confirm in the indexer's own metrics that the forwarder's connection is TLS (connectionType cookedSSL)."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SPLUNK01 (Tier 1 SSH session): the first-start decisions, an index, and the TLS port\ngrep -E '^SPLUNK_(DB|FIPS)' /opt/splunk/etc/splunk-launch.conf\nsudo -u splunk /opt/splunk/bin/splunk btool indexes list sbd_wineventlog | grep -E 'frozenTimePeriodInSecs|maxTotalDataSizeMB'\nsudo -u splunk /opt/splunk/bin/splunk btool inputs list splunktcp-ssl:9997 --debug\nopenssl s_client -connect SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test:9997 -CAfile /opt/splunk/etc/auth/sbd/sbd-chain.pem </dev/null 2>/dev/null | grep -E 'Verify return code|Protocol'\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the forwarder's account and connection, and one closed hour\nGet-CimInstance Win32_Service -Filter \"Name='SplunkForwarder'\" | Select-Object StartName, State\nSelect-String 'C:\\Program Files\\SplunkUniversalForwarder\\var\\log\\splunk\\splunkd.log' -Pattern 'Connected to idx' | Select-Object -Last 1\n\$start = (Get-Date).Date.AddHours((Get-Date).Hour - 2); \$end = \$start.AddHours(1)\n(Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; StartTime=\$start; EndTime=\$end}).Count\n# In Splunk Web (Search), as sbd_admin: the same hour, the forwarder's connection, and the stored size\nindex=sbd_wineventlog earliest=-2h@h latest=-1h@h | stats count\nindex=_internal source=*metrics.log group=tcpin_connections | stats latest(connectionType) AS type, latest(version) AS version BY hostname, sourceIp\n| dbinspect index=sbd_wineventlog index=sbd_syslog | stats sum(sizeOnDiskMB) AS MB BY index"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Splunk runs as splunk under systemd with the designed indexes and retention, Splunk Web answers over HTTPS, TCP 9997 presents the SBD-SPLUNK01 certificate over TLS 1.2, the forwarder runs as NT SERVICE\\SplunkForwarder with a TLS connection to the indexer, and a closed hour's count and one event's time match the collector for both streams."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the first-start settings and the FIPS decision, the btool listings for the indexes and the receiving port, the TLS handshake result, the forwarder's inputs and outputs files and its service account, the measured daily size, and the count-and-time comparison for both streams."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Splunk · product documentation\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation\n\nSplunk · Secure Splunk Enterprise with FIPS\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.1.0/Security/SecuringSplunkEnterprisewithFIPS\n\nSplunk · Configure Splunk indexing and forwarding to use TLS certificates\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Security/ConfigureSplunkforwardingtousesignedcertificates\n\nSplunk · Configure Splunk Web to use TLS certificates\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Security/SecureSplunkWebusingasignedcertificate\n\nSplunk · Set a retirement and archiving policy\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Indexer/Setaretirementandarchivingpolicy\n\nSplunk · Configure the Splunk Add-on for Windows (forwarded events)\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/WindowsAddOn/latest/User/Configuration\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WinEvent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent")], question: "Why do SPLUNK_DB and the FIPS decision go into splunk-launch.conf before the first start?", answers: ["Splunk ignores splunk-launch.conf after the first start", "FIPS mode can only be turned on at the first start, and the index location is simplest to set before any index exists", "The license file overwrites them"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "An instance first started without FIPS mode can't gain it, even by upgrading, and moving every index later is a project of its own. Two lines, decided once.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The forwarder connects, but every Windows event shows host SBD-SYSLOG01. What is missing?", ["The Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows on the forwarder and on SBD-SPLUNK01, which reports each forwarded event's source computer in host", "A forwarder on each domain controller", "UDP 514 on the indexer"], 0, "Forwarded events are read on the collector, so without the add-on they look as if the collector wrote them. With it, host names the system each event came from — which every search relies on."), QuizQuestion("How do you show that Splunk's copy is faithful to the collector?", ["Splunk Web shows a green status", "The forwarder's log shows no errors", "Count a closed hour on both sides and compare one event's time — both must match"], 2, "A copy that loses events or shifts their times would mislead every search built on it. A closed window, counted twice, is the simplest proof that nothing was dropped or retimed.")], recap: ["Splunk runs as its own account, with HTTPS and indexes that keep the period you chose.", "Both streams cross rule F10 over TLS, checked by name.", "Counts and times in Splunk match the collector's."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 asks the network its first questions.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis#ingest", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:splunk-analysis:analyze", title: "Build the working searches", minutes: 30, summary: "Analysis earns its place with three working searches: failed privileged sign-ins, the boundary's denies, and the health of time — questions this network can already answer about itself. Build them as an analyst, prove each against an event you caused, and save them for the alerts in lesson 4.", objectives: ["Search as an analyst and read the shape of the indexed data.", "Build the first three searches against real events.", "Save each with its owner and a known test event."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.10 and 1.11: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects the two streams — the ForwardedEvents log on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, sized for the buffer course 1.10 recorded, and daily syslog files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog — SBD-AUDIT01 archives them every night, and the register carries row F10 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-SPLUNK01 on VLAN 50 (10.0.50.10) with a separate data volume for the indexes (/srv/splunk in this sample), and have course 1.9's client package for SBD-REPO01 and its Linux assessment set (SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01), course 1.3's chrony file, course 1.6's root certificate hash and SBD Server Authentication template, and course 1.8's administration seats ready.\n\nStage across the air gap, hashed as in course 1.4: the Splunk Enterprise package for Linux (.tgz), the universal forwarder installer for Windows (.msi), the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, an Enterprise license file, and DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG for lesson 5's checklist (the Linux scan reuses course 1.9's set)."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the last of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, course 1.11 protects it on SBD-AUDIT01, and this course analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01, then traces one event through all three. Analysis is a search someone actually runs and an alert someone owns. Linux commands are for the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose. Retention periods, alert thresholds and alert expirations depend on your environment and policy: the course says what each one is for, and you record the value you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Sign in from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 with your named sbd_analyst account and check what the role allows: | rest /services/authentication/current-context lists your roles and capabilities, and delete_by_keyword is not among them. Then read the shape of the data with | tstats count where index=sbd_* by index, host, sourcetype: every source system should appear, the Windows events with their source computer in host and their event ID in EventCode, and the syslog lines with their sender in host."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Build search one: failed sign-ins (event 4625) involving the tier admin accounts, grouped by source host — the tiering course's denials now have a dashboard. Take the accounts from course 1.8's tier groups (SBD Tier 0 Admins, SBD Tier 1 Admins, and SBD Tier 2 Admins), not from a naming pattern: from AdminJumpBox01, a Tier 0 operator exports the groups' members to sbd_tier_admins.csv (TargetUserName and tier), uploads it as a lookup the analysts can read, and refreshes it after every membership change."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Build search two: router denies by source VLAN and destination port — the network boundary, now trending. Extract src_ip, dest_ip, and dest_port from the core's deny lines (its deny log reaches the collector as syslog on UDP 514 under rule F24, part of the network's existing configuration) once with the field extractor, and derive each VLAN from an address's third octet, since every segment is 10.0.<VLAN>.0/24."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Build search three: time-service events, so clock drift surfaces before Kerberos complains. Course 1.10's SBD-ForwardedSecurity already carries Security event 4616 and the System log's Microsoft-Windows-Time-Service events course 1.3 keeps (12, 35, 36, 37, 129, and 142); event 12 on SBD-DC01 is expected, because the PDC emulator serves its own clock. The Linux servers report through chronyd in the syslog stream: which source they selected, or that none was selectable."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Save each as a report with its question, owner, the events it depends on, and a known event it must find, and have an sbd_admin share it with the analysts read-only: a wrong password for your Tier 1 account at SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's sign-in screen for search one, a probe of an unregistered port for search two, and a chronyd restart on SBD-SPLUNK01 for search three. Cause each, run each search, and keep the result: lesson 4 turns them into alerts."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# In Splunk Web (Search), signed in as an analyst from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01\n| rest /services/authentication/current-context splunk_server=local | table username roles capabilities\n| tstats count where index=sbd_* by index, host, sourcetype\nindex=sbd_wineventlog EventCode=4625 [| inputlookup sbd_tier_admins.csv | fields TargetUserName] | stats count, values(IpAddress) AS sources, latest(_time) AS last BY host, TargetUserName | convert ctime(last)\nindex=sbd_syslog host=<core> src_ip=* | eval src_vlan=\"VLAN \".mvindex(split(src_ip, \".\"), 2) | stats count BY src_vlan, dest_port\n(index=sbd_wineventlog \"Microsoft-Windows-Time-Service\") OR (index=sbd_syslog chronyd) | fillnull value=\"-\" EventCode | stats count, latest(_time) AS last BY host, EventCode | convert ctime(last)\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): course 1.10's subscription carries the Time-Service query\nwecutil gs SBD-ForwardedSecurity /f:xml | Select-String 'Time-Service'"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The analyst role searches both indexes and carries no delete capability, every source system appears with the right host and source type, the subscription carries the Time-Service query, and each saved search finds its known test event."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record your role listing, the data-shape table, each saved search with its question, owner, and a result showing its test event, the lookup's source and date, and the subscription's Time-Service query."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Splunk · Define roles on the Splunk platform with capabilities\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Security/Rolesandcapabilities\n\nSplunk · Configure the Splunk Add-on for Windows (forwarded events)\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/WindowsAddOn/latest/User/Configuration\n\nMicrosoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does the repository stay out of the analysis flow?", answers: ["The protected copy's integrity never depends on the search tier — analysis reads the working stream, not the record", "Splunk cannot read archives", "Repositories are read-only hardware"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The separation from lesson 1, built into lesson 2's plumbing: question the working copy freely; the record stays untouched.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does search one take the tier accounts from a lookup of course 1.8's groups?", ["Lookups make every search faster", "The groups decide who is privileged, so a refreshed lookup follows their membership — a naming pattern misses any account that doesn't follow it", "Splunk can't read account names from Windows events"], 1, "The tier groups are the source of truth for privilege. Exporting them keeps the search honest, and refreshing the lookup after each membership change keeps it current."), QuizQuestion("Why is a time-health search in the first three?", ["Clocks are easy to chart", "Dashboards need a third panel", "Drift breaks Kerberos quietly — course 1.3 set up the clock, this search watches it"], 2, "The pipeline watches the dependencies the network actually has. Time is one failure that looks like everything else until you chart it.")], recap: ["Analysts question the working copy; the protected record stays untouched.", "Three searches answer real questions from day one.", "Each search has an owner and a test event it must find."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 turns these searches, and the pipeline's own failures, into alerts someone owns.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis#analyze", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:splunk-analysis:alerts", title: "Own the alerts", minutes: 35, summary: "An alert nobody owns is a log entry with extra steps. Turn the working searches and the pipeline's own failure conditions into scheduled alerts, each with a threshold set for your environment, a named responder, and a written first response; test each one by causing it, tune the noise, and set the review cadence AU-6 asks for.", objectives: ["Write each alert's threshold, schedule, named responder, and first response, recording the values you chose.", "Alert on the pipeline's own failures and the repository's findings.", "Test and tune every alert, and review them on a set cadence."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.10 and 1.11: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects the two streams — the ForwardedEvents log on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, sized for the buffer course 1.10 recorded, and daily syslog files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog — SBD-AUDIT01 archives them every night, and the register carries row F10 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-SPLUNK01 on VLAN 50 (10.0.50.10) with a separate data volume for the indexes (/srv/splunk in this sample), and have course 1.9's client package for SBD-REPO01 and its Linux assessment set (SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01), course 1.3's chrony file, course 1.6's root certificate hash and SBD Server Authentication template, and course 1.8's administration seats ready.\n\nStage across the air gap, hashed as in course 1.4: the Splunk Enterprise package for Linux (.tgz), the universal forwarder installer for Windows (.msi), the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, an Enterprise license file, and DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG for lesson 5's checklist (the Linux scan reuses course 1.9's set)."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the last of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, course 1.11 protects it on SBD-AUDIT01, and this course analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01, then traces one event through all three. Analysis is a search someone actually runs and an alert someone owns. Linux commands are for the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose. Retention periods, alert thresholds and alert expirations depend on your environment and policy: the course says what each one is for, and you record the value you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the alert register before building anything: for each alert, its search, threshold, schedule, severity, named responder with a deputy, and written first response. The threshold, schedule, throttle and expiration of each alert depend on your environment and policy, so record the value you chose and why. Start from lesson 3's searches: any failed sign-in for a tier account, since privileged sign-ins are few and each failure deserves a look; any deny from VLAN 80 toward VLAN 60 or from VLAN 60 itself, whose seats use only registered paths, or a burst of denies from one source above the count and window you record; and Time-Service events 36, 129, or 142, or chronyd finding no selectable source (never event 12, which SBD-DC01 writes by design). Add any use of a sealed account: course 1.8's break-glass account signing in anywhere, as that course promised, and Splunk's own admin account signing in to Splunk."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Add the conditions course 1.10's daily check has watched by hand (AU-5): no Windows events at all for longer than the silence threshold you record, which means the collector, its forwarder, or rule F10 has stopped, and a known source quiet past its own limit, taken from a lookup of expected sources (each host as Splunk shows it, with the longest quiet it may have). Add course 1.11's repository findings: SBD-Repository events 1001 and 1101 from SBD-AUDIT01, refused access to its archive (event 4656 with an audit failure), and any deletion (event 4660), which the responder matches to an approved retention run. Splunk sees missing data, not disks: the collector's free space and its buffer's oldest event stay in the daily check."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Build each as a scheduled alert owned by sbd_admin, the ones on the domain controllers from AdminJumpBox01: a cron schedule and a search window by index time, the window longer than the interval between runs (earliest=-24h _index_earliest=-<window>), so events that arrive late after an outage are still checked, a trigger when the number of results is greater than zero (so a missing-data search, such as the silence alert, is written to return a row only when its count is zero), throttling on the field that names one problem (the account, the source) for the period you record, a severity, and the Add to Triggered Alerts action. This network has no mail server, so the Triggered Alerts list is the responder's queue, and it keeps each entry for 24 hours by default: set each alert's expiration to cover the longest gap between reviews in your environment, so a weekend can't empty it, and record the value. Name every alert SBD - <condition>, so the register, the queue, and the scheduler's log use the same names."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Test each alert by causing its condition where that is safe, and record when you caused it, when it fired, and who saw it: one wrong password for your Tier 1 account at SBD-ADMIN-SRV01's sign-in screen, a probe of an unregistered port from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 across the core, and the SplunkForwarder service stopped on SBD-SYSLOG01 for longer than the silence threshold, which also shows the backlog arriving without a gap once it restarts. Where a condition can't be caused safely, such as a repository mismatch or a break-glass sign-in, run the alert's search over a window that holds a real or test example instead."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Tune before you trust: run each alert's search over the last seven days and count how often it would have fired. If that is more than the responder can act on, raise the threshold, widen the throttle, or exclude a known benign source, never just lower the severity, and record each change and its reason in the register."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Set the review cadence (AU-6; sample values): the responder works the queue at the start of each day and records every alert's outcome; once a week an analyst reviews each saved search's results and signs the review; and each month the register's thresholds, noise, and owners are reviewed, with both lookups refreshed after any change to the tier groups or the sources."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# In Splunk Web (Search), as sbd_admin: the alerts as built\n| rest /servicesNS/-/-/saved/searches splunk_server=local | search title=\"SBD - *\" is_scheduled=1 | table title cron_schedule dispatch.earliest_time alert.severity alert.suppress.period alert.expires actions\n# Each alert's runs and what it triggered, from the scheduler's own log\nindex=_internal sourcetype=scheduler savedsearch_name=\"SBD - *\" | stats count, sum(result_count) AS results, values(alert_actions) AS actions BY savedsearch_name\n# The silent-source alert's search: each expected source's last event against its limit\n| inputlookup sbd_expected_sources.csv | join type=left host [| tstats max(_time) AS last where index=sbd_* earliest=-7d by host] | where isnull(last) OR last < relative_time(now(), \"-\" . max_quiet_minutes . \"m\")\n# The pipeline test, on SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): stop the forwarder past the silence threshold\nStop-Service SplunkForwarder; Start-Sleep -Seconds <silence threshold plus one run interval, in seconds>; Start-Service SplunkForwarder"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every alert in the register has a recorded threshold, schedule, expiration, severity, responder with deputy, and written first response; each safe condition fired its alert in a test, with the cause and the alert's time recorded; the forwarder stop fired the pipeline alert and its backlog arrived without a gap; and the first daily and weekly reviews are on record."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the alert register, the listing of the alerts as built, each test's cause-and-fire record, the seven-day tuning counts with any changes, and the first review records."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Splunk · Define roles on the Splunk platform with capabilities\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Security/Rolesandcapabilities\n\nSplunk · Getting started with alerts\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Alert/Aboutalerts\n\nSplunk · Monitor triggered alerts\nhttps://docs.splunk.com/Documentation/Splunk/9.4.0/Alert/Triggeredalertaction\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What makes an alert real rather than decorative?", answers: ["A red icon", "A named responder and a written first step", "Firing at least hourly"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Detection without ownership is noise. The responder and the first step are what turn a search into a control.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("No alert has fired for a whole day. Why doesn't that prove the pipeline works?", ["Splunk holds alerts back on quiet days", "Alerts only fire on weekdays", "A stopped collector or forwarder looks exactly like a quiet network — only an alert on missing data tells them apart"], 2, "Silence is ambiguous. The pipeline and silent-source alerts turn missing data into an event someone has to answer, which is what AU-5 asks for."), QuizQuestion("An alert fires forty times a day and the responder has started ignoring it. What is the right fix?", ["Measure it against the last week, then raise the threshold, throttle it, or exclude a known benign source — and record the change", "Lower its severity to informational", "Turn it off until things calm down"], 0, "Tuning keeps the alert meaningful; hiding or disabling it removes the control. The register shows why each threshold is what it is.")], recap: ["Every alert has a recorded threshold, an owner, and a written first response.", "The pipeline's own failures and the repository's findings now raise alerts.", "Each alert was caused, tuned, and put on a review cadence."], nextStep: "The final lesson traces one event through all three logging systems and closes the course.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis#alerts", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:splunk-analysis:prove", title: "Prove the chain and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "One deliberate event, followed all the way: a failed privileged sign-in should appear on the source, the collector, the analyst's screen, and the repository's next-morning archive. That trace closes all three logging courses; SBD-SPLUNK01's first baseline and the handoff to the fleet close this one.", objectives: ["Trace a test event through all three systems, from source to analyst.", "Verify the router-deny stream (rule F24) and its alert on the analysis side.", "Baseline SBD-SPLUNK01 and file the evidence of all three logging courses."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.10 and 1.11: SBD-SYSLOG01 collects the two streams — the ForwardedEvents log on D:\\Logs\\ForwardedEvents, sized for the buffer course 1.10 recorded, and daily syslog files per sender under D:\\Logs\\Syslog — SBD-AUDIT01 archives them every night, and the register carries row F10 waiting for this course.\n\nProvision a virtual machine for SBD-SPLUNK01 on VLAN 50 (10.0.50.10) with a separate data volume for the indexes (/srv/splunk in this sample), and have course 1.9's client package for SBD-REPO01 and its Linux assessment set (SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01), course 1.3's chrony file, course 1.6's root certificate hash and SBD Server Authentication template, and course 1.8's administration seats ready.\n\nStage across the air gap, hashed as in course 1.4: the Splunk Enterprise package for Linux (.tgz), the universal forwarder installer for Windows (.msi), the Splunk Add-on for Microsoft Windows, an Enterprise license file, and DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG for lesson 5's checklist (the Linux scan reuses course 1.9's set)."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This is the last of three logging courses: course 1.10 collects the record on SBD-SYSLOG01, course 1.11 protects it on SBD-AUDIT01, and this course analyzes a working copy in SBD-SPLUNK01, then traces one event through all three. Analysis is a search someone actually runs and an alert someone owns. Linux commands are for the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose. Retention periods, alert thresholds and alert expirations depend on your environment and policy: the course says what each one is for, and you record the value you chose."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Generate the test event: one failed sign-in on SBD-DC01 with a wrong password for a tier admin account. From AdminJumpBox01, record its time, account, and event ID in the DC's Security log, then find the same event in ForwardedEvents on SBD-SYSLOG01 with MachineName SBD-DC01 and the same time, about 30 seconds after it happened."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Watch analysis catch it: the event appears in sbd_wineventlog with host SBD-DC01 and the same time, and the privileged sign-in alert fires on its next run. The responder sees it in the Triggered Alerts list and records the first response as written — the alert is part of the trace, not an extra."), LessonSection("Step 3", "The next morning, after course 1.11's 01:00 run has archived the event's completed UTC day, open that day's ForwardedEvents archive on SBD-AUDIT01 in an auditor's or the custodian's session, confirm its catalog line shows matching hashes, and read the event with Get-WinEvent -Path. Four places, one event, and the same source time in each: that is the chain working."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Repeat with the boundary on the analysis side: probe one unregistered port from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as course 1.10 did, which already traced the deny to the collector over rule F24, and find it in search two with the boundary alert firing for a deny from VLAN 60. Attempt, deny, and alert now form one evidence set."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Run the cadence: take SBD-SPLUNK01's first SCAP baseline the way course 1.9 scans every Linux server. Over your Tier 1 SSH session, copy SCC for Linux and the distribution's STIG benchmark from C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux, check their hashes on the host, install SCC as its readme directs, and run it as root with the benchmark and the profile course 1.4 recorded. Answer DISA's Splunk Enterprise STIG for the application in a STIG Viewer checklist on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, classify every open item as course 1.4 taught, adapted for Linux as in course 1.9 — fix now (no GPO applies to Linux), deviation candidate, or POA&M candidate — and store the session under C:\\Evidence\\splunk-analysis\\baseline\\SBD-SPLUNK01 with hashes."), LessonSection("Step 6", "File the end-to-end trace as the signature evidence of all three logging courses and write the handoff: the fleet course inherits a pipeline that will see its first domain join happen. When course 1.13 enrolls the workstations, their events arrive in sbd_wineventlog with no change in Splunk; add the ones that must never go quiet to the expected-sources lookup, and recheck the index sizing and the license's daily volume."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the test event on its source\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01 { Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, MachineName }\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the same event on the collector\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; Id=4625; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName\n# In Splunk Web (Search), as an analyst: the same event; its alert is in Triggered Alerts\nindex=sbd_wineventlog host=SBD-DC01* EventCode=4625 earliest=-1h | table _time host TargetUserName\n# Next morning, in an auditor's or the custodian's session on SBD-AUDIT01: that UTC day's archive\nGet-WinEvent -Path D:\\LogArchive\\ForwardedEvents\\ForwardedEvents-<UTC date>.evtx -FilterXPath '*[System[EventID=4625]]' | Select-Object TimeCreated, MachineName\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: copy course 1.9's Linux assessment set to the host\nscp C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux\\<SCC for Linux package> C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux\\<STIG benchmark> <your-account>@SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test:/tmp/\n# On SBD-SPLUNK01: the same hashes, then SCC's command-line scanner as root (path and options from the SCC readme)\nsha256sum /tmp/<SCC for Linux package> /tmp/<STIG benchmark>\nsudo <SCC install directory>/cscc\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: collect the session, then hash the course's evidence\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\\Evidence\\splunk-analysis\\baseline | Out-Null\nscp -r <your-account>@SBD-SPLUNK01.sbd.test:<SCC session folder> C:\\Evidence\\splunk-analysis\\baseline\\SBD-SPLUNK01\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\splunk-analysis -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The test event appears on SBD-DC01, in ForwardedEvents, in Splunk with its alert fired, and in the next morning's archive with matching catalog hashes, all with the same source time; the probe's deny appears in search two and fired its alert; and SBD-SPLUNK01 has its first baseline and Splunk STIG checklist with every open item classified."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the four-place trace of the test event with its timestamps and the alert's record, the deny with its alert, SBD-SPLUNK01's scan set and checklist with hashes, and the handoff note to course 1.13."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wecutil (Windows Event Collector)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wecutil\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WinEvent\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent\n\nDISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · STIGs document library (operating system and Splunk Enterprise STIGs)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/downloads\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (AU family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What does the single traced event prove that stage-by-stage checks don't?", answers: ["That events have timestamps", "That the stages connect — one real event surviving source to analyst is the pipeline working as a system", "That 4625 exists"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Components can each pass while the chain fails. The traced event is the integration test.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("How is SBD-SPLUNK01, a domain-joined Linux server, scanned in this lab?", ["It is exempt from evidence", "With the Windows Server benchmark anyway", "With the DISA STIG benchmark for its Linux distribution, on the same cadence as the Windows servers"], 2, "Linux distributions have their own DISA STIGs and Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) content, and Splunk Enterprise has a STIG of its own. A domain-joined Linux server is held to the same scan cadence as every other server."), QuizQuestion("Why insist the pipeline exists before the fleet course?", ["So fifty domain joins happen in view of the pipeline — observed from the first boot instead of retrofitted later", "Fleets cannot join without Splunk", "DHCP requires a SIEM"], 0, "The ordering argument of this whole path: build the watcher, then the watched. Course 1.13 arrives to a network already paying attention.")], recap: ["One event, four places, one source time — the chain is real.", "Boundary denies assemble their own evidence, alert included.", "SBD-SPLUNK01's baseline and the handoff to course 1.13 close the logging courses."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.12 and the three logging courses. Course 1.13 brings in the fifty-workstation fleet — watched, addressed, and policy-managed from first boot.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/splunk-analysis#prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "workstation-fleet", number: "1.13", title: "Bring in the fleet", subtitle: "50 Windows endpoints, managed from first boot", category: "Network build", description: "Join the fifty-workstation fleet to a network that is ready for it: DHCP addressing, OU landing with STIG policy at first boot, Windows LAPS for every local administrator, certificate autoenrollment at scale, 802.1X port admission through NPS on SBD-DHCP01, and a sampled proof with the whole rollout observed by the logging pipeline.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "7 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 7,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-guide", title: "Bring in the fleet · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "50 Windows endpoints, managed from first boot", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "workstation-fleet-package", title: "Bring in the fleet · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet/SecureByDesign-workstation-fleet-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:prepare", title: "Prepare the fleet rollout", minutes: 30, summary: "Fifty of anything needs a factory, not fifty craft projects. Settle the image, the names, the join account, and the policy links so every endpoint is born identical — then prove it on paper before proving it on metal.", objectives: ["Finalize the reference image and naming plan for fifty endpoints.", "Create a least-privilege join account scoped to the Workstations OU.", "Link the Windows client STIG policies and the local Administrators, firewall and DNS registration policies to the Workstations OU before any join, below course 1.8's tier logon GPO."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Finalize the Windows client reference image: current patches via the approved media process, no preinstalled extras, one local administrator account for Windows LAPS to manage (the Windows client STIG disables the built-in Administrator), and a generalized state so each clone takes its own identity. Record the image version and hash — it is the fleet's common ancestor and belongs in evidence."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Write the naming and rollout plan: SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50, addressed by the course 1.7 DHCP scope, joined in waves (pilot of three, then batches) rather than all fifty at once."), LessonSection("Step 3", "From AdminJumpBox01, create a dedicated domain-join account delegated only Create Computer objects on the Workstations OU — no broader privilege rides along with fifty join operations — and record it beside the same delegation course 1.8 gave SBD Tier 2 Admins for its test client. The course 1.5 redirect already guarantees machines land in that OU. Add a GPO, linked only to the Workstations OU, that makes SBD Tier 2 Admins the managing group in each PC's local Administrators (Group Policy Preferences, Local Users and Groups), as course 1.8 did by hand on its test client; course 1.8's tier logon GPO already denies sign-ins there to SBD Tier 0 Admins, SBD Tier 1 Admins and SBD Audit Custodians."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Link the DISA Windows client policies that course 1.5 imported and left unlinked to the Workstations OU now — the computer policy, the user policy, and course 1.5's loopback (Merge) GPO, as course 1.8 did for Admin Workstations — so the very first pilot boots into the hardened state, and note that the Windows client's own secure defaults will already satisfy some checks, just as Windows Server's did. Link each of them below course 1.8's tier logon GPO, which keeps link order 1 on the Workstations OU: user rights don't merge across GPOs, so a STIG GPO linked above it would win and drop the denies for Tier 0, Tier 1 and SBD Audit Custodians. Every GPO this course links to the OU goes below it."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Create the workstation firewall GPO (SBD Workstation Firewall in this sample) on the Workstations OU: Windows Defender Firewall on for every profile with inbound traffic blocked by default, and inbound rules that allow Remote Desktop (TCP 3389) and remote management (TCP 5985–5986) only from SBD-ADMIN-PC01 at 10.0.60.30 — the ports its register row, A03, carries. No rule allows SMB or Remote Desktop between workstations: traffic inside VLAN 80 never reaches the router, so this policy, not the core router's rules, enforces the register's peer denies. Then turn off the PCs' own DNS registration in a separate GPO on the same OU (SBD Workstation DNS in this sample): Computer Configuration > Policies > Administrative Templates > Network > DNS Client > Dynamic update, Disabled. Record the reason with the GPO: SBD-DHCP01 already registers each PC's A and PTR records under svc-dhcp-dns (course 1.7), and a client that registers its own record can take it over, so the DHCP server's account can no longer update it — one owner per record."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Staging\\Windows-client-reference.vhdx\n# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the join account's delegation (svc-fleet-join in this sample) and the OU's links\n(Get-Acl 'AD:\\OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test').Access | Where-Object IdentityReference -match 'svc-fleet-join|SBD Tier 2 Admins' | Select-Object IdentityReference, ActiveDirectoryRights, ObjectType\n(Get-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test').GpoLinks | Select-Object Order, DisplayName   # course 1.8's tier logon GPO stays at order 1\nGet-GPOReport -Name 'SBD Workstation Firewall' -ReportType Html -Path C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\workstation-firewall.html\nGet-GPOReport -Name 'SBD Workstation DNS' -ReportType Html -Path C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\workstation-dns.html"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The image is hashed and recorded, the join account holds only Create Computer objects on the Workstations OU, and the client STIG, loopback, local Administrators, firewall and DNS registration GPO links show on that OU below course 1.8's tier logon GPO, still at link order 1, before any machine exists in it."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the image record with its hash, the naming and wave plan, the delegation output for the join account and SBD Tier 2 Admins, the OU's GPO links in order, and the firewall and DNS registration GPO reports with the recorded reason."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Configure Windows Firewall rules with Group Policy\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/configure\n\nMicrosoft · Policy CSP – ADMX_DnsClient (Dynamic update)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-dnsclient\n\nMicrosoft · DNS dynamic update\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/dynamic-update\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does the join account get only create-computer rights on one OU?", answers: ["Join operations fail with more rights", "Fifty joins should carry the least privilege possible — a stolen join credential then creates computer objects, nothing more", "Delegation is faster than Domain Admin"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Scale multiplies exposure. Scoping the account bounds what the rollout's most-used credential is worth to an attacker.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why link the STIG policies before the first machine joins?", ["So every endpoint's first boot is already the hardened state — no window where an unmanaged machine exists", "GPO links expire if unused", "The OU cannot be created otherwise"], 0, "The redirect lands them in the OU, and the pre-linked policy means compliance from minute one — the course 1.5 design, now paying off at scale."), QuizQuestion("What makes the reference image part of the evidence?", ["Images are always evidence", "It contains the domain password", "Its recorded version and hash are what make fifty 'identical' endpoints a claim you can verify"], 2, "A configuration record for the fleet starts with the common ancestor. The hash is how an assessor — or future you — confirms descent."), QuizQuestion("What stops one workstation from opening SMB or Remote Desktop to another?", ["The router's access control lists", "Each PC's own firewall, set by the workstation firewall GPO — traffic inside VLAN 80 never reaches the router", "SBD-DHCP01, which refuses leases to a PC that tries it"], 1, "Two PCs in VLAN 80 talk through the access switch, so the router never sees their traffic. Each PC's Windows Defender Firewall enforces the register's peer denies, which Inside the Network shows as rules D01 and D02; DHCP only hands out addresses."), QuizQuestion("Why does the Workstations OU turn off the PCs' own DNS registration?", ["Workstations don't need DNS records", "SBD-DHCP01 registers each PC's records under svc-dhcp-dns; a PC that registers its own can take a record over so the DHCP server can no longer update it", "Dynamic registration is blocked by the router"], 1, "One owner per record. Course 1.7 made SBD-DHCP01 the registrar for every lease, so the PCs leave registration to it, and the reason is recorded with the GPO.")], recap: ["One hashed image, one naming plan, waves instead of a big bang.", "The join credential can create computer objects and nothing else.", "The Workstations OU is armed with policy, and its managers named, before it holds a machine — with course 1.8's tier denies still first.", "Peer SMB and Remote Desktop are blocked by each PC's own firewall, set by policy before the first join."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 joins the pilot and inspects everything a first boot should bring.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#prepare", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:pilot", title: "Join the pilot and inspect first boot", minutes: 30, summary: "Three machines go first, and everything the path has built shows up at once: a DHCP lease through the relay, an OU landing, STIG policy, a DNS record — and the join itself appearing on the analyst's screen.", objectives: ["Join a three-machine pilot with the scoped account.", "Verify lease, OU placement, policy, and DNS registration per machine.", "Confirm the joins appear in the logging pipeline."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Deploy SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC03 from the image into VLAN 80 — where the PCs are virtual machines, with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off, as course 1.3 requires — let each take its DHCP lease, and join sbd.test with the delegated account; nothing about the join should require a privileged credential."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Per machine, verify the inheritance chain: the computer object sits in the Workstations OU (the course 1.5 redirect working), gpresult shows the STIG policies applied, w32tm /query /source names a domain controller, and the A and PTR records SBD-DHCP01 registered for it under svc-dhcp-dns (course 1.7) answer from both DCs — with the PC's own registration off, as lesson 1's DNS GPO set."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Confirm the deny side holds: a pilot machine cannot reach another pilot machine's SMB or RDP — the lesson 1 firewall policy refuses it on the destination PC, because traffic inside VLAN 80 never reaches the router — and cannot open anything toward VLAN 60, which the core router's rules deny at the boundary."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Find the three joins in the logging pipeline: the domain controllers' computer-account creation events (4741) should already be on the course 1.10 collector and searchable in SBD-SPLUNK01 (course 1.12) — the first machines this network has watched arrive. If the course 1.10 subscription doesn't carry 4741, add it and record the change."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name, DNSHostName\ngpresult /r /scope:computer\nw32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, never Local CMOS Clock or the hypervisor\nResolve-DnsName SBD-PC01.sbd.test -Server SBD-DC02.sbd.test\nGet-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient' | Select-Object RegistrationEnabled   # 0: the PC leaves registration to SBD-DHCP01\nTest-NetConnection SBD-PC02.sbd.test -Port 445   # expected: TcpTestSucceeded False"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "All three pilots sit in the Workstations OU with STIG policy applied, time from a domain controller and DNS registered by SBD-DHCP01 on both DCs with the PC's own registration off, peer SMB/RDP fails as designed, and the join events are visible in analysis."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep each pilot's placement, gpresult, time source and DNS proof; the peer-deny output; and the analysis search showing the three computer-account creations."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nMicrosoft · Configure Windows Firewall rules with Group Policy\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/configure")], question: "How does a brand-new machine end up hardened without anyone touching it?", answers: ["Windows hardens itself completely", "Redirect lands it in the Workstations OU, where the pre-linked STIG policies apply at first boot", "The DHCP server pushes the STIG"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Three courses cooperate in that one moment: the course 1.5 redirect, the client policies course 1.5 imported from course 1.4's package, and this course's pre-linked OU.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why test pilot-to-pilot SMB and expect failure?", ["SMB is removed from Windows", "The pilots are in different domains", "Each PC's firewall policy denies peer SMB and Remote Desktop — lateral movement inside VLAN 80 is cut at the destination host"], 2, "Peer traffic is switched inside VLAN 80 and never reaches the router, so the register's peer denies (D01 and D02) are enforced by each PC's own firewall — the policy lesson 1 linked before any join."), QuizQuestion("What does seeing the joins in analysis demonstrate?", ["The ordering worked — the pipeline built in courses 1.10–1.12 observed the fleet's very first machines arriving", "That Splunk licenses cover 50 hosts", "Nothing; joins are not logged"], 0, "Build the watcher, then the watched. The pilot's appearance on the analyst's screen is the sequencing argument made visible.")], recap: ["Three pilots: leased, landed, hardened, registered — untouched by hand.", "Peer traffic fails by design; the denies are the fleet's first evidence.", "The pipeline watched the fleet being born."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 gives every endpoint a managed local administrator.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#pilot", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:laps", title: "Manage local administrators with Windows LAPS", minutes: 25, summary: "Fifty identical machines must not share one local administrator password. Windows Local Administrator Password Solution (Windows LAPS) gives each endpoint its own rotating secret, stored in the directory, readable by the people the tier model says may have it.", objectives: ["Prepare the directory schema and permissions for Windows LAPS.", "Apply the LAPS policy to the Workstations OU.", "Retrieve and rotate a managed password under the tier rules."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "From AdminJumpBox01, extend the directory schema for Windows LAPS (Update-LapsADSchema, which needs Schema Admins) and grant the Workstations OU's computers the right to update their own password attributes (Set-LapsADComputerSelfPermission) — a one-time Tier 0 action, recorded like any schema change."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Grant read access on the managed passwords to SBD Tier 2 Admins (Set-LapsADReadPasswordPermission) — exactly the people SBD-ADMIN-PC01 exists for. Domain Admins can read them by default; no other group gets the right, and Tier 0 accounts don't sign in on workstations to use one. The tier model decides who may know an endpoint's local secret."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create the LAPS GPO on the Workstations OU: back up to Active Directory, manage the local administrator account the image carries (named in the policy, because Windows LAPS manages the built-in Administrator unless told otherwise and doesn't create accounts), encrypt the password with SBD Tier 2 Admins as its authorized decryptor (otherwise only Domain Admins can decrypt it), the complexity, length and age the plan sets, and post-authentication actions that reset the password and sign the account out after a short grace period, so a used password does not live on."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On a pilot, confirm the policy applied, retrieve the password from SBD-ADMIN-PC01, use it once, and watch rotation after the grace period — one cycle, fully observed, is the proof the other forty-nine inherit."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): who holds the LAPS rights on the Workstations OU\nFind-LapsADExtendedRights -Identity 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test'\n# Inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (Tier 2)\n# The LAPS cmdlets read the directory over LDAP; the AD module's web service (TCP 9389) is open only to AdminJumpBox01\nGet-LapsADPassword -Identity SBD-PC01 -AsPlainText | Select-Object ComputerName, Account, ExpirationTimestamp\n# On the pilot\ngpresult /r /scope:computer"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The schema holds the LAPS attributes, only SBD Tier 2 Admins (beside the default Domain Admins) can read and decrypt passwords, the policy shows on the pilots, and one retrieve-use-rotate cycle is recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the schema-change record, the permission delegation, the LAPS policy report, and the observed rotation cycle."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows LAPS overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-overview\n\nMicrosoft · Get started with Windows LAPS and Windows Server Active Directory\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/laps/laps-scenarios-windows-server-active-directory")], question: "What problem does LAPS remove from a fifty-machine fleet?", answers: ["Slow sign-ins", "One shared local administrator password — where a single capture would open every endpoint", "Password complexity rules"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Identical images tempt identical secrets. Per-machine rotating passwords make one compromised endpoint worth exactly one endpoint.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Who may read a managed endpoint password?", ["Any authenticated user", "The machine's primary user", "SBD Tier 2 Admins, from SBD-ADMIN-PC01 — the tier model decides"], 2, "LAPS storage is directory data; the reading right is a tiering decision, which course 1.8 already made."), QuizQuestion("Why does post-authentication reset matter?", ["A password that was just used stops being valid — whoever saw it holds an expired secret", "It makes passwords longer", "It disables the account"], 0, "The rotation on use closes the window between 'retrieved for support' and 'floating around in a notes file.'")], recap: ["Every endpoint owns its own rotating local secret.", "Reading and decrypting rights follow the tier model, from the Tier 2 seat.", "One full cycle is observed and on record."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 hands the fleet its certificates.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#laps", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:certificates", title: "Autoenroll the fleet's certificates", minutes: 25, summary: "Course 1.6 proved autoenrollment on both domain controllers and its pilot, SBD-PKI-MGMT01; this lesson turns it loose. Widen the template permission to a fleet computer group, let the autoenrollment GPO already linked at the domain root do the enrolling, and verify a machine certificate chains and checks revocation across the boundary.", objectives: ["Widen the computer template's autoenrollment to a fleet computer group.", "Confirm hands-free enrollment at Group Policy refresh.", "Verify chain and live revocation from an endpoint."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Through the course 1.6 change process (its lesson 8), grant a new computer group — SBD Fleet Computers in this sample, created from AdminJumpBox01 — Read, Enroll and Autoenroll on the SBD Computer Authentication template, from SBD-PKI-MGMT01. Template permissions name groups, never an OU; the change record names the widening and its approver."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm with gpresult that course 1.6's SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO, linked at the domain root, already reaches the Workstations OU, then add the pilots to SBD Fleet Computers and restart them so the membership reaches their Kerberos tickets. Watch certificates arrive with no human in the loop: the requests reach SBD-SUBCA01 over the enrollment row course 1.6 confirmed (F23 in this sample)."), LessonSection("Step 3", "On an enrolled pilot, export its certificate, clear the URL cache, and run the full verification: the chain must reach the approved root, and the AIA and CDP fetches must answer live from pki.sbd.test through the register's publication row, F18."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Record the issuing CA's view: the issued-certificate log now shows the pilots enrolling — volume the CA was designed for, beginning to arrive, and itself a record worth sampling into evidence."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-PC01, after the restart\ngpupdate /force\ncertutil -pulse\nGet-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Select-Object Subject, NotAfter, Thumbprint\nGet-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Where-Object { \$_.EnhancedKeyUsageList.FriendlyName -contains 'Client Authentication' } | Select-Object -First 1 | Export-Certificate -FilePath C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\SBD-PC01.cer\ncertutil -urlcache * delete\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\SBD-PC01.cer   # carry the output to SBD-ADMIN-PC01's record"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Pilot endpoints enroll hands-free once they are in SBD Fleet Computers, verification passes with live publication retrieval and nothing cached, and the CA's issued log shows their SBD Computer Authentication certificates."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the template change record, the SBD Fleet Computers membership, an endpoint's certificate details, the urlfetch output, and a sample of the CA's issued log."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What governs widening the template from pilot to fleet?", answers: ["The course 1.6 change process — requester, reviewer, versioning, record", "A quick edit on the CA console", "Nothing; templates are self-service"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The pilot-then-widen plan was written into the template's own record. Scaling it is a change like any other — documented, not improvised.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does the endpoint's urlfetch verification exercise, end to end?", ["Only the local certificate store", "Template, CA, DNS alias, publication site, and the registered HTTP path, all in one command", "The DHCP relay"], 1, "One passing verification on a fleet machine is the integration test for everything the PKI path built: course 1.6's hierarchy and publication site, and course 1.2's pki.sbd.test alias."), QuizQuestion("Why sample the CA's issued-certificate log into evidence?", ["Logs expire otherwise", "The CA deletes it weekly", "What the CA actually signed — and under which template version — is the PKI's ground-truth record"], 2, "Enrollment at scale is where template mistakes would surface. The issued log is where you would see it, so it joins the record.")], recap: ["The widening went through the change process, not around it.", "Enrollment is hands-free for the fleet group and verified live across the boundary.", "The CA's issued log joins the evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 puts those certificates to work at the switch port, starting in monitor mode.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#certificates", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:nac", title: "Admit only enrolled devices with 802.1X", minutes: 30, summary: "Until now, anything plugged into a VLAN 80 port got an address. With 802.1X, the switch asks each machine to prove who it is with the certificate it enrolled. This lesson turns that check on in monitor mode — every result is logged, nothing is blocked yet — because 47 PCs have no certificate until they join the domain; lesson 6 enforces it once the whole fleet has enrolled. No new server: the NPS role joins DHCP on SBD-DHCP01.", objectives: ["Add the Network Policy Server (NPS) role to SBD-DHCP01 for port authentication.", "Configure Extensible Authentication Protocol–Transport Layer Security (EAP-TLS) with machine certificates on the VLAN 80 access ports, in monitor mode.", "Prove the pilots authenticate and that NPS logs every result."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Add the Network Policy Server role to SBD-DHCP01 in a Tier 1 session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and register it in Active Directory from AdminJumpBox01 (a Tier 0 action: it adds the server to RAS and IAS Servers). Give it an SBD Server Authentication certificate (course 1.6) for SBD-DHCP01.sbd.test, adding SBD-DHCP01 to the SBD TLS Servers group before it requests. It handles port admission only; router logins stay named local accounts, so the server stays in the member-server tier."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Add the VLAN 80 access switch as a RADIUS client with a long, unique shared secret, then create the network policy: wired 802.1X, EAP-TLS only, machine certificates that chain to the approved root and carry the Client Authentication purpose, from the Domain Computers group — any domain-joined computer, servers included where one sits on an 802.1X port. Don't narrow it to SBD Fleet Computers: that group decides who enrolls the fleet's certificate, not who may connect."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create quarantine VLAN 90 on the access switch with no gateway and no routes (the core gets no VLAN 90 interface) and set it as the ports' failure and guest VLAN, but enable 802.1X on the VLAN 80 access ports in monitor (audit) mode: the switch runs every authentication and reports the result, yet keeps forwarding on VLAN 80 whatever the outcome (vendors name this mode differently). The other 47 PCs have no machine certificate until they join the domain in lesson 6, so enforcing now would strand them in VLAN 90, with no route to a domain controller. Push the wired 802.1X client settings to the Workstations OU by Group Policy (Wired AutoConfig, EAP-TLS, the approved root as the trusted CA)."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Turn on Audit Network Policy Server (success and failure) for SBD-DHCP01 in a separate GPO linked above the STIG GPO, recorded as course 1.10 records audit additions — the STIG's audit policy leaves it off. Then prove monitor mode with the pilots: each authenticates with EAP-TLS and NPS logs a grant (event 6272), while a failed attempt is logged — as event 6273 when the request reaches NPS — but still forwards on VLAN 80. Add register row F20 with its owner, as Inside the Network records it, and confirm the NPS events reach the course 1.10 collector, adding 6272 and 6273 to SBD-ForwardedSecurity's query if it doesn't carry them yet."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On an enrolled pilot: the wired interface authenticated with EAP-TLS.\nnetsh lan show interfaces\n# On SBD-DHCP01 (Tier 1): NPS auditing is on, then recent grants (6272) and denials (6273).\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"Network Policy Server\"\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=6272,6273} -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The network policy admits Domain Computers with EAP-TLS; NPS auditing records success and failure; every pilot authenticates with EAP-TLS and NPS logs the grant (6272); a failed authentication is logged but, in monitor mode, the port still forwards on VLAN 80; the ports name VLAN 90 as their failure VLAN; and the NPS events appear in the collector."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the network policy export, the RADIUS client list (without the secret), the audit GPO report, the monitor-mode port configuration, the pilots' grant events and the failed test, and the F20 register row."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Network Policy Server overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/nps/nps-top\n\nMicrosoft · Configure RADIUS clients\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/nps/nps-radius-clients-configure\n\nMicrosoft · Certificate requirements for EAP-TLS\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/certificate-requirements-eap-tls-peap")], question: "Once 802.1X is enforced, what lets a workstation onto a VLAN 80 port?", answers: ["Knowing a shared password", "Its machine certificate, checked by NPS through EAP-TLS", "Being plugged in before the switch boots"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "EAP-TLS proves the device holds the private key for a certificate the approved CA issued. No certificate, no access.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Once enforcement is on in lesson 6, where does an unknown device end up?", ["Quarantine VLAN 90, which has no routes", "VLAN 80, with a warning", "The domain controller VLAN, so it can enroll"], 0, "The failure VLAN gives an unknown device a place to be that leads nowhere: VLAN 90 has no gateway, so nothing it sends is routed."), QuizQuestion("Why does NPS on SBD-DHCP01 handle port admission only, not router logins?", ["NPS cannot authenticate people", "Router logins need a separate license", "If it authenticated the router's administrators, it could control Tier 0 and would have to be managed as Tier 0"], 2, "Anything that can control a Tier 0 system is Tier 0. Keeping NPS to port admission keeps SBD-DHCP01 in the member-server tier."), QuizQuestion("Why does this lesson start 802.1X in monitor mode instead of enforcing it?", ["Monitor mode is faster", "The other 47 PCs have no machine certificate until they join the domain; enforcing now would strand them in VLAN 90, where they could never reach a domain controller", "NPS cannot enforce on wired ports"], 1, "A PC enrolls its certificate only after it joins, and it can join only if the port lets it reach a domain controller. Monitor mode proves the configuration with the pilots without locking the rest of the fleet out.")], recap: ["802.1X runs on every VLAN 80 port in monitor mode: every result is logged, nothing is blocked yet.", "The pilots authenticate with machine certificates; enforcement waits until the whole fleet has enrolled.", "No new server: NPS runs on SBD-DHCP01, and its decisions are logged with everything else."], nextStep: "Lesson 6 scales from three machines to fifty, then switches the ports to enforcement.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#nac", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:scale", title: "Scale to fifty, enforce 802.1X, and prove by sample", minutes: 30, summary: "Roll the remaining waves while the ports are still in monitor mode, confirm every PC enrolled its machine certificate and authenticates, then switch the ports to enforcement — and prove the fleet the only honest way at scale: a defined sample, the same checks as the pilot, and a written rule for what a sample failure means.", objectives: ["Roll out the remaining endpoints in planned waves.", "Confirm fleet-wide enrollment, then enforce 802.1X and prove an unenrolled device is quarantined.", "Verify a defined sample against the pilot's full check set and enrol the fleet in event collection."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Deploy the remaining machines in the planned waves while the VLAN 80 ports are still in monitor mode, adding each wave to SBD Fleet Computers from AdminJumpBox01 and restarting it so it enrolls, and watch the DHCP scope's utilization and the join events arriving in analysis as each wave lands — the pipeline is now the rollout's progress bar."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Confirm every PC enrolled its machine certificate and authenticates: compare the issuing CA's issued certificates and the NPS grant events (6272) against the fifty computer objects, and fix any machine that is missing before going further. Then retire course 1.7's temporary test client, SBD-TEST80, whose Tier 2 test role the pilots now carry: remove it from the domain, delete its computer object, lease and DNS records, and record the retirement."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Switch the VLAN 80 access ports from monitor mode to enforcement, and prove it both ways: an enrolled PC still authenticates and gets its normal lease, and an unenrolled test laptop lands in quarantine VLAN 90 and reaches nothing. Record the switch's port status and the NPS events for both."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Define the sample before checking it: for example one machine per wave plus any machine that logged an error — then run the pilot's full set on each sampled host (OU, policy, DNS, LAPS, certificate, 802.1X admission, peer-deny)."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the sample rule down: one failed check in the sample widens the sample; a repeated failure stops the rollout and becomes a finding. A sample without a failure rule is a formality."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Enrol the fleet in course 1.10's collection: from AdminJumpBox01, add SBD Fleet Computers to SBD Event Sources and link course 1.10's enrolling GPO to the Workstations OU, below course 1.8's tier logon GPO; from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, widen the collector's WinRM 5985 scope to VLAN 80 (rule F17); restart the PCs so both apply — and confirm fleet sign-ins now appear in the analyst's searches."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the fleet's computer objects and its group\n(Get-ADComputer -SearchBase 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter *).Count   # 50, once SBD-TEST80 is retired\n(Get-ADGroupMember 'SBD Fleet Computers').Count   # 50\n# On SBD-DHCP01: authentication results in the last hour (6272 granted, 6273 denied).\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=6272,6273; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Group-Object Id | Select-Object Name, Count\nGet-DhcpServerv4ScopeStatistics -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 | Select-Object PercentageInUse, AddressesInUse\n# On SBD-SYSLOG01: the fleet's events arriving\nGet-WinEvent -LogName ForwardedEvents -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object MachineName, Id"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Fifty computer objects sit in the Workstations OU and in SBD Fleet Computers, SBD-TEST80 is retired, every PC enrolled and authenticates, the ports enforce 802.1X with the unenrolled laptop quarantined in VLAN 90, scope utilization matches the rollout, every sampled host passes the full check set, and fleet events flow through the pipeline."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the wave log, the enrollment reconciliation, the SBD-TEST80 retirement record, the enforcement change with both admission results and their records, the sample definition with its failure rule, the per-sample check results, and the enrolment proof from the collector."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Configure certificate autoenrollment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/core-network-guide/cncg/server-certs/configure-server-certificate-autoenrollment\n\nMicrosoft · Use Windows Event Forwarding to help with intrusion detection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/threat-protection/use-windows-event-forwarding-to-assist-in-intrusion-detection\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · Network Policy Server overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/nps/nps-top")], question: "Why prove the fleet by sample instead of checking all fifty by hand?", answers: ["A defined sample with a failure rule is verifiable and honest at scale — and identical provenance is what the image hash bought you", "Checking is impossible on Windows", "Only pilots can be checked"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The factory model earns the sampling model. The written failure rule is what keeps the sample from being theater.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What turns a sampled failure into more than a shrug?", ["Deleting the failed machine", "The written rule: failures widen the sample, repeats stop the rollout and become findings", "Rebooting twice"], 1, "The rule was written before the checks ran — which is exactly what makes acting on it defensible."), QuizQuestion("What is the pipeline's role during the rollout itself?", ["It pauses to save disk", "It only matters after completion", "Live progress and anomaly surface — each wave's joins and errors appear as they happen"], 2, "The investment of courses 1.10–1.12 turns deployment into something observed, not something reconstructed afterward."), QuizQuestion("When is it safe to switch the VLAN 80 ports from monitor mode to enforcement?", ["As soon as the first pilot authenticates", "Once every PC has enrolled its machine certificate and authenticates in monitor mode", "Never; monitor mode is enough"], 1, "Enforce only after the whole fleet has proven it authenticates. Then a missing certificate cannot strand a machine in VLAN 90, and every unknown device that appears later is quarantined.")], recap: ["Fifty machines, rolled in waves, watched as they landed.", "Every PC enrolled before enforcement; an unenrolled device now lands in VLAN 90.", "A defined sample with a failure rule carried the proof, and the fleet reports into the pipeline."], nextStep: "The final lesson baselines the fleet and hands off to courses 1.14 and 1.15.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#scale", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:workstation-fleet:scan", title: "Delta scan the fleet and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "The cadence meets its first population: the sampled endpoints get Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) baselines with the Windows client benchmark from SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC) copy, installed in course 1.8, the infrastructure this course changed is rescanned, and the fleet inventory is handed to course 1.15.", objectives: ["Baseline the sampled endpoints with the Windows client STIG benchmark from SBD-ADMIN-PC01's existing SCC copy.", "Rescan the infrastructure this course touched.", "Hand the fleet to courses 1.14 and 1.15."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.6–1.12: the course 1.6 PKI with its SBD Computer Authentication template and the SBD Certificate Autoenrollment GPO at the domain root, SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7), whose relay course 1.7 verified, the three administration seats (course 1.8), with SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy and first baseline, and the logging pipeline (courses 1.10–1.12), on the network's routed, segmented VLANs.\n\nPrepare a patched Windows client reference image (or template) for the fifty endpoints, obtained through the approved offline media process.\n\nConfirm the Workstations OU and the computer redirect from course 1.5, and the Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) Group Policy objects (GPOs) for your Windows client release, imported in course 1.5 from the package course 1.4 staged and still unlinked there. Lesson 7 reuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01's own SCC copy, which course 1.8 installed with the Windows client benchmark course 1.4 staged, and the checked installer and connected-side-hashes.csv course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA there.\n\nConfirm the VLAN 80 access switch supports 802.1X port authentication, including a monitor (audit) mode that logs each result without blocking traffic. No new server is needed: the Network Policy Server (NPS) role goes on SBD-DHCP01."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Fifty endpoints join a network that is finally ready for them: addressed by DHCP, landed by OU redirect, hardened by policy at first boot, certified by autoenrollment, and watched by the pipeline from their first sign-in. Tier 0 steps run from AdminJumpBox01, Tier 1 steps on SBD-DHCP01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and Tier 2 steps inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01; each seat keeps its part of the record under C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet. The sample names run SBD-PC01 through SBD-PC50; match them to your own plan."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Baseline the lesson 6 sample with the Windows client benchmark from SBD-ADMIN-PC01, reusing the SCC copy course 1.8 installed there and baselined the seat with — this course scans only the fleet sample. First check the installer course 1.8 kept in C:\\Staging\\DISA against connected-side-hashes.csv. Rule A03 carries remote management, not the file-sharing and RPC ports a remote SCC scan uses, so copy SCC to each sampled PC over PowerShell remoting, check its hash there, scan locally in a Tier 2 session, bring the results back to C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\baseline, and remove SCC from the PC. Note how much the pre-linked policies and the OS defaults already satisfy, and classify open items the course 1.4 way."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Rescan what changed and attribute each delta to its lesson: the DCs (schema and LAPS attributes) and SBD-SUBCA01 (fleet enrollment volume) from AdminJumpBox01, and SBD-DHCP01 (the NPS role, its audit GPO and a scope in daily use) locally in a Tier 1 session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01. Send any new deviation to the exception record's owner on AdminJumpBox01."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Assemble the fleet inventory — names, image ancestry, certificate thumbprints, sample status — as the course's closing artifact; course 1.15 makes it a source of SBD-MGMT01's living inventory."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the handoff: the fleet exists, watched and hardened, but takes no updates yet — course 1.14 builds SBD-WSUS01, with SBD-PC01–SBD-PC03 as its pilot ring and the other 47 PCs as Ring 2, and course 1.15 keeps the inventory current."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Inside SBD-ADMIN-PC01 (Tier 2): the installer course 1.8 kept still matches the record (expected: True)\n\$scc = Get-Item 'C:\\Staging\\DISA\\<SCC installer>'\n(Get-FileHash \$scc.FullName -Algorithm SHA256).Hash -in (Import-Csv C:\\Staging\\DISA\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash\n# Per sampled PC: carry the installer over remote management (A03), install and scan there, bring the results back\n\$s = New-PSSession SBD-PC07.sbd.test\nCopy-Item \$scc.FullName -ToSession \$s -Destination C:\\Windows\\Temp\\\nCopy-Item '<SCC results folder on the PC>' -FromSession \$s -Destination C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet\\baseline\\SBD-PC07 -Recurse\n# After the scans: this seat's record, hashed\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\workstation-fleet-manifest.csv -NoTypeInformation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The installer course 1.8 kept on SBD-ADMIN-PC01 still matches its recorded hash, the sampled endpoints hold dated Windows client baselines with SCC removed afterwards, the infrastructure rescans carry attributed deltas, and the fleet inventory is complete and filed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: the installer's hash check, the endpoint baselines, infrastructure rescans, the fleet inventory, the hashed manifest, and the handoff note to courses 1.14 and 1.15."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "DISA · STIG Group Policy Objects package\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/gpo\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nDISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/")], question: "Which benchmark do the endpoints get?", answers: ["The Windows Server benchmark, close enough", "The router's config export", "The Windows client STIG benchmark — the fleet's own standard from the same DISA package"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Course 1.4 staged the Windows client STIG and its SCAP benchmark beside the server content; the GPO package carries the client GPOs, which course 1.5 imported. The fleet is scanned against its own content.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does the issuing CA get a rescan now?", ["Fifty enrollments changed its operational posture — change-triggered means this too", "CAs are rescanned weekly by rule", "It was missed earlier"], 0, "The cadence follows change wherever it lands. Enrollment at fleet scale is a change worth measuring."), QuizQuestion("What does the fleet still lack as this course closes?", ["Certificates and policy", "Updates — SBD-WSUS01 arrives in course 1.14 — and an inventory kept current by course 1.15", "Network addresses"], 1, "Honest handoffs name the gap. Fifty hardened machines that never patch would drift out of the state just proven — which is why course 1.14 is next.")], recap: ["The endpoints are baselined by sample from SBD-ADMIN-PC01's existing SCC copy.", "Infrastructure deltas are attributed, and the fleet inventory is the closing artifact.", "The gap — updates and a living inventory — is named for courses 1.14 and 1.15."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.13. Course 1.14 builds SBD-WSUS01 and delivers the fleet's first Windows updates, ring by ring, starting with SBD-PC01–SBD-PC03.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/workstation-fleet#scan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "patch-protect", number: "1.14", title: "Deliver Windows updates from SBD-WSUS01", subtitle: "Air-gapped WSUS: offline import, four rings & Group Policy targeting", category: "Network build", description: "Give every Windows system one road for updates: build SBD-WSUS01 as a disconnected WSUS server, feed it each month through the export and import path Microsoft describes for air-gapped networks, target four rings by Group Policy — pilot, workstations, member servers, then the domain controllers and the rest of Tier 0, installed by hand — and prove the cycle with WSUS reports, spot checks and a first STIG baseline.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-guide", title: "Deliver Windows updates from SBD-WSUS01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Air-gapped WSUS: offline import, four rings & Group Policy targeting", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "patch-protect-package", title: "Deliver Windows updates from SBD-WSUS01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect/SecureByDesign-patch-protect-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:design", title: "Design the rings and the Windows update rule", minutes: 25, summary: "Decide how Windows updates reach the network before any arrive: one road through SBD-WSUS01, four rings that try every update on what the network can lose before what it can't, and a monthly crossing on the media procedure. Then confirm the register already carries the road.", objectives: ["Write the Windows update rule and its documented exceptions.", "Place every Windows system in one of four rings, in order.", "Set the timing and the crossing cadence, and confirm the register rows."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.13: the domain with its DNS and time design, the STIG baselines and hardening policies, the PKI, DHCP, the tiered administration seats, SBD-REPO01, the logging pipeline with SBD-AUDIT01, run by its audit custodian (course 1.11), and the fleet of SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50 with its three pilot machines.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30, VLAN 20) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) sized for the update files.\n\nOutside the boundary, have an approved connected system that can run a second WSUS server with access to Microsoft Update, and the course 1.4 removable-media procedure for carrying its output across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course gives every Windows system one road for updates. SBD-WSUS01 never touches the internet: each month a WSUS server on the connected side synchronizes the same scope, and its metadata and update files cross on hashed media to be imported inside. Microsoft deprecated WSUS in September 2024 — it gets no new features — but it still ships in Windows Server and is supported for production use, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle. Ring timings and dates are sample values for your patch owner to set. Linux packages already have their road, SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9); endpoint protection and credentialed scanning are coming soon, as courses 1.16 and 1.17, and nothing here depends on them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the Windows update rule — the network's one-source-per-type rule, applied to Windows: every Windows system takes updates only from SBD-WSUS01, and anything else is a documented exception. SBD-ROOTCA01 is the standing one: it has no network, so any update it takes is carried by hand at its next ceremony and recorded. Linux packages already have their one source, SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9); protection and scanner content are outside this course."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Place every Windows system in one of four rings, in the order rule F06 names. Ring 1, the pilot: the three course 1.13 pilot PCs (SBD-PC01–SBD-PC03) and SBD-WSUS01 itself, the first server to take each server update and one whose loss stops only patching. Ring 2: the other 47 workstations. Ring 3: the member servers and the Tier 1 and Tier 2 seats — SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and its SBD-ADMIN-PC01 virtual machine — so the seats that would fix a broken workstation update still run the proven one. SBD-AUDIT01 takes Ring 3's updates at Ring 3's time but in a computer group of its own, Ring 3 - Audit archive: they download only, and its audit custodian installs them, Ring 4-style, because the custodian alone administers that server (course 1.11) — never ordinary Tier 1 operators."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Ring 4 holds the domain controllers and the rest of Tier 0: SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-PKI-MGMT01 and AdminJumpBox01. It goes last and is Tier 0 work, approved and installed from AdminJumpBox01 with Tier 0 accounts, one system at a time. SBD-WSUS01 itself stays a Tier 1 member server run by the patch operators: Tier 0's hold on Ring 4 is a written procedure (lesson 2), and moving SBD-WSUS01 to Tier 0 is recorded as the target."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the timing as sample values your patch owner approves: a ring advances only after its machines have installed and reported cleanly for a set soak time — three days after Ring 1 and five after Rings 2 and 3 in this sample. A failed or harmful update stops promotion until it is understood, and nothing skips a ring."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Set the crossing cadence: one crossing a month, after Microsoft's monthly security release on the second Tuesday, through the course 1.4 media procedure and on the same run as SBD-REPO01's monthly snapshot (course 1.9) — one custody record, one approval. An urgent out-of-band fix gets an expedited crossing on the same procedure, with shorter soak times the owner approves in writing."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Confirm the register: F06 already lets every Windows system reach SBD-WSUS01 — metadata over HTTPS 8531, signed content over HTTP 8530 — with the patch owner as its owner, and A01 and A02 carry the two consoles. Nothing leads out of the network and no row is added for one: the content arrives on media."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: every Windows computer object and its OU, the input for the ring table\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect | Out-Null\n([adsisearcher]'(&(objectCategory=computer)(operatingSystem=Windows*))').FindAll() | ForEach-Object { \$_.Properties.distinguishedname[0] } | Sort-Object | Set-Content C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect\\windows-computers.txt\n(Get-Content C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect\\windows-computers.txt).Count\n# On any member: no update policy yet, so no WSUS server is named (expected: no output)\nGet-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The rule names its one source and its exceptions, the ring table places every Windows computer object in exactly one ring with the reason for its place, SBD-AUDIT01 in its custodian-installed group, the timing and crossing calendar are written with an approver, and F06, A01 and A02 are confirmed with owners."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the Windows update rule, the computer list and the ring table built from it, the timing and crossing calendar, and the confirmed register rows."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Server Update Services (WSUS) overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/get-started/windows-server-update-services-wsus\n\nMicrosoft · Plan your WSUS deployment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/plan/plan-your-wsus-deployment\n\nMicrosoft · Deploy Windows client updates using WSUS (rings and client-side targeting)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows/deployment/update/waas-manage-updates-wsus\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why do domain controllers sit in the last ring?", answers: ["They update fastest", "A bad update should burn a pilot machine, not the identity fabric — criticality decides order", "DCs cannot be updated"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Rings are a blast-radius design: change proves itself on what the network can lose before touching what it cannot.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does one-approved-source-per-type actually buy?", ["Cheaper licensing", "Faster downloads", "'Unauthorized software' becomes checkable — anything not from the named source is a finding by definition"], 2, "A rule you can test is a control; a preference you cannot is a hope. One source per type draws the testable line."), QuizQuestion("An urgent out-of-band security fix is released mid-month. What does the rule say?", ["An expedited crossing on the same media procedure, through every ring, with soak times the owner shortens in writing", "Copy the fix to each server from a USB stick", "Approve it for all computers at once to save time"], 0, "Urgency changes the timing, not the road: the same custody and the same ring order, compressed by a recorded decision.")], recap: ["One road: Windows updates only from SBD-WSUS01, exceptions written down.", "Four rings, pilot to Tier 0 — updates earn their way up.", "One monthly crossing beside SBD-REPO01's, with an expedited path."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds SBD-WSUS01 as a disconnected update server.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:wsus", title: "Build SBD-WSUS01 as a disconnected server", minutes: 40, summary: "Build the server the network will take its updates from: a hardened member server on VLAN 20 that never synchronizes, serves metadata over TLS and signed files over HTTP, and is administered only from the two seats the tier model allows. Its scope is decided here, because what crosses each month is whatever that scope holds.", objectives: ["Install WSUS on a hardened member server configured never to synchronize.", "Decide and record the update scope the monthly crossing will carry.", "Protect its metadata with TLS and its administration with the tier model."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.13: the domain with its DNS and time design, the STIG baselines and hardening policies, the PKI, DHCP, the tiered administration seats, SBD-REPO01, the logging pipeline with SBD-AUDIT01, run by its audit custodian (course 1.11), and the fleet of SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50 with its three pilot machines.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30, VLAN 20) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) sized for the update files.\n\nOutside the boundary, have an approved connected system that can run a second WSUS server with access to Microsoft Update, and the course 1.4 removable-media procedure for carrying its output across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course gives every Windows system one road for updates. SBD-WSUS01 never touches the internet: each month a WSUS server on the connected side synchronizes the same scope, and its metadata and update files cross on hashed media to be imported inside. Microsoft deprecated WSUS in September 2024 — it gets no new features — but it still ships in Windows Server and is supported for production use, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle. Ring timings and dates are sample values for your patch owner to set. Linux packages already have their road, SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9); endpoint protection and credentialed scanning are coming soon, as courses 1.16 and 1.17, and nothing here depends on them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-WSUS01 as a Windows Server virtual machine at 10.0.20.30 on VLAN 20, with a separate data volume for the update files (D: in this sample), both domain controllers for DNS, and the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off, so it takes time from the domain hierarchy (course 1.3). Join it straight into the Servers OU, so course 1.5's member-server STIG policies, course 1.8's tier logon policies and course 1.10's enrolling GPO, linked to the Servers OU when course 1.10 enrolled SBD-DHCP01, apply from its first boot; confirm with w32tm /query /source that it takes time from a domain controller."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Install the role with its default database, the Windows Internal Database (WID), and run the post-installation step with the content folder on D:. Microsoft announced in September 2024 that WSUS is deprecated: it gets no new features, but it still ships in Windows Server and is supported for production use, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle. WID is deprecated too — Microsoft suggests SQL Server for the roles that use it — so record the database choice as a decision to revisit before either is removed."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Configure it as a disconnected server: cancel the configuration wizard, which needs Microsoft Update to list products, then in Options store update files locally, enable only the languages your systems use, leave express installation files off (the default), and keep synchronization manual — and never run it. SBD-WSUS01 has no route out; its updates arrive only by import (lesson 3)."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Decide the scope and record it: the Windows Server and Windows client products the network runs, security and critical updates and — if Microsoft Defender Antivirus protects the endpoints — its definition updates; no upgrades and no drivers, which are planned changes of their own. The connected-side server in lesson 3 synchronizes exactly this scope, because whatever it holds is what crosses."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Protect the metadata: add SBD-WSUS01 to the SBD TLS Servers group, then request an SBD Server Authentication certificate (course 1.6) for SBD-WSUS01.sbd.test, bind it to the WSUS Administration site's HTTPS port 8531, require TLS on the five web services Microsoft lists (APIRemoting30, ClientWebService, DSSAuthWebService, ServerSyncWebService and SimpleAuthWebService), and run wsusutil configuressl. Update files stay on HTTP 8530, as F06 says: each one is signed, and its hash reaches the client inside the TLS-protected metadata, so a changed file is refused."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Hold the administration to the tier model: scope the 8530 and 8531 inbound rules to the VLANs that hold F06's Windows systems, install the WSUS console on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and AdminJumpBox01, and keep WSUS Administrators to the named patch operators and the Tier 0 task accounts that run Ring 4. From AdminJumpBox01, add SBD-WSUS01 to SBD Tier 0 Console Hosts (course 1.8's recorded network-logon exception for this console) and to SBD Event Sources (course 1.10), and restart it once the collector's WinRM scope includes 10.0.20.30 (F17). WSUS can't limit an administrator to some computer groups, so Ring 4's Tier 0 rule — like the audit custodian's separation for SBD-AUDIT01 — is a written procedure, not a technical boundary. Write the Ring 4 procedure — Ring 4 is approved and installed only by Tier 0 accounts from AdminJumpBox01, and every Ring 4 approval is checked by name before a controller installs (lesson 5) — and record in the exception record the residual risk that a Tier 1 operator could still approve for Ring 4, with the target that closes it: moving SBD-WSUS01 to Tier 0, administered only from AdminJumpBox01. Until then SBD-WSUS01 stays Tier 1."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "w32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, never the hypervisor's time provider\nInstall-WindowsFeature -Name UpdateServices -IncludeManagementTools\n& \"\$env:ProgramFiles\\Update Services\\Tools\\wsusutil.exe\" postinstall CONTENT_DIR=D:\\WSUS\n\$wsus = Get-WsusServer\n\$wsus.GetConfiguration() | Select-Object HostBinariesOnMicrosoftUpdate, DownloadExpressPackages, AllUpdateLanguagesEnabled   # expected after Options: False, False, False\n\$wsus.GetSubscription() | Select-Object SynchronizeAutomatically   # expected: False\n# TLS for metadata: the certificate, the 8531 binding, the five services, then wsusutil\n\$cert = (Get-Certificate -Template SBDServerAuthentication -SubjectName 'CN=SBD-WSUS01.sbd.test' -DnsName SBD-WSUS01.sbd.test -CertStoreLocation Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My).Certificate\n(Get-WebBinding -Name 'WSUS Administration' -Protocol https).AddSslCertificate(\$cert.Thumbprint, 'My')\n'APIRemoting30', 'ClientWebService', 'DSSAuthWebService', 'ServerSyncWebService', 'SimpleAuthWebService' | ForEach-Object { Set-WebConfigurationProperty -PSPath 'IIS:\\' -Location \"WSUS Administration/\$_\" -Filter system.webServer/security/access -Name sslFlags -Value Ssl }\n& \"\$env:ProgramFiles\\Update Services\\Tools\\wsusutil.exe\" configuressl SBD-WSUS01.sbd.test\nGet-WebBinding -Name 'WSUS Administration' | Select-Object protocol, bindingInformation   # http *:8530, https *:8531\nGet-LocalGroupMember 'WSUS Administrators'"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-WSUS01 sits in the Servers OU, takes time from a domain controller, and runs WSUS on WID, stores files locally, never synchronizes, and answers metadata over TLS on 8531 with a certificate that chains to the SBD root; WSUS Administrators holds only the named operators and Tier 0 task accounts; the server is in SBD Tier 0 Console Hosts and forwards its events; and the scope, the database decision, the written Ring 4 procedure, and its residual risk with the Tier 0 target are recorded."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the installation and configuration output, the scope and database decisions, the certificate and binding details, the WSUS Administrators list, the group memberships, the written Ring 4 procedure, and the exception-record entry for the residual risk and its Tier 0 target."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Windows Server Update Services (WSUS) overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/get-started/windows-server-update-services-wsus\n\nMicrosoft · Features removed or no longer developed in Windows Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/removed-deprecated-features-windows-server\n\nMicrosoft · Plan your WSUS deployment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/plan/plan-your-wsus-deployment\n\nMicrosoft · Step 1: Install the WSUS server role\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/deploy/1-install-the-wsus-server-role\n\nMicrosoft · Step 2: Configure WSUS (TLS, computer groups, air-gapped networks)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/deploy/2-configure-wsus\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "How does update content reach this WSUS server?", answers: ["Direct from Microsoft Update over the internet", "By the approved export and import process — hashed media across the air gap", "It generates updates locally"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The air gap holds for updates too. SBD-WSUS01 never synchronizes: Microsoft's design for air-gapped networks pairs an outside WSUS server that exports with an inside one that imports.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What gates an update's arrival at the domain controllers?", ["The calendar date alone", "The DC's own download speed", "Survival in the earlier rings first — approval promotes ring by ring"], 2, "Ring 4 installs only what Rings 1 to 3 have proven, and only when Tier 0 starts it — which is why its approvals are Tier 0 work and are checked by name in lesson 5."), QuizQuestion("Why scope products and classifications tightly?", ["Unused content is imported, stored, and reviewed for nothing — and every artifact crossing the boundary should earn its trip", "WSUS breaks above ten products", "Licensing forbids more"], 0, "In an air-gapped lab, every megabyte is carried by hand. Scope is both hygiene and respect for the boundary process.")], recap: ["A hardened member server that never synchronizes.", "Metadata over TLS on 8531; signed files over HTTP on 8530.", "Two consoles, named administrators, and a written Ring 4 procedure for the limit WSUS can't enforce, with Tier 0 as the recorded target."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 carries the first month's updates across the air gap.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#wsus", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:wsus-offline", title: "Carry updates across the air gap", minutes: 35, summary: "SBD-WSUS01 never talks to Microsoft. Each month a second WSUS server on the connected side synchronizes the recorded scope and downloads its files; its metadata export and content folder cross on approved media, hashed on both sides, and SBD-WSUS01 imports them — the two-server path Microsoft describes for air-gapped networks.", objectives: ["Run the connected-side export server with SBD-WSUS01's recorded scope.", "Export the metadata and the files, and carry both under the media procedure.", "Verify the crossing on SBD-WSUS01 before importing, and file its record."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.13: the domain with its DNS and time design, the STIG baselines and hardening policies, the PKI, DHCP, the tiered administration seats, SBD-REPO01, the logging pipeline with SBD-AUDIT01, run by its audit custodian (course 1.11), and the fleet of SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50 with its three pilot machines.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30, VLAN 20) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) sized for the update files.\n\nOutside the boundary, have an approved connected system that can run a second WSUS server with access to Microsoft Update, and the course 1.4 removable-media procedure for carrying its output across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course gives every Windows system one road for updates. SBD-WSUS01 never touches the internet: each month a WSUS server on the connected side synchronizes the same scope, and its metadata and update files cross on hashed media to be imported inside. Microsoft deprecated WSUS in September 2024 — it gets no new features — but it still ships in Windows Server and is supported for production use, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle. Ring timings and dates are sample values for your patch owner to set. Linux packages already have their road, SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9); endpoint protection and credentialed scanning are coming soon, as courses 1.16 and 1.17, and nothing here depends on them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On the approved connected system, run the export server: WSUS on the same Windows Server release as SBD-WSUS01, synchronizing from Microsoft Update with exactly the products, classifications and languages lesson 2 recorded, and storing files locally. It sits outside the boundary and never connects to the lab; its only output is media."), LessonSection("Step 2", "After each month's synchronization, approve the updates you need on the export server so it downloads their files, and wait until nothing is still downloading — those approvals only switch the downloads on, and they stay outside. Then, as a local administrator on the export server, run wsusutil export for the metadata package and copy the WsusContent folder: the package carries the update metadata, and the files travel beside it (after the first crossing, only those added since the last one)."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Hash the package and every file into connected-side-hashes.csv, which travels with the media, and carry the set under the course 1.4 media procedure with its custody record, on the run that also carries SBD-REPO01's snapshot. Declining an update doesn't stop it from being exported, so the export server's scope is the real limit on what crosses."), LessonSection("Step 4", "On SBD-WSUS01, hash the media's contents into lab-side-hashes.csv and compare the two lists before opening anything: one mismatch stops the import and becomes a finding. Copy the files into D:\\WSUS\\WsusContent first, then run wsusutil import locally as a local administrator — Microsoft warns never to import an export from a source you don't trust, and the custody record and matching hashes are how this network earns that trust. A large import can run for hours."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Check the import log, confirm the month's updates now appear in the console, and file the crossing in C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect\\transfer: dates, carrier, file count, the package hash, both hash lists and the import log. If WSUS later shows an approved update as still waiting for its files, wsusutil reset makes it re-check its content folder against the database."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Connected side, on the export server, once every needed file has downloaded\n& \"\$env:ProgramFiles\\Update Services\\Tools\\wsusutil.exe\" export E:\\2026-10\\wsus-export.xml.gz E:\\2026-10\\export.log\nrobocopy D:\\WSUS\\WsusContent E:\\2026-10\\WsusContent /E   # the first crossing carries the whole folder\nGet-ChildItem E:\\2026-10 -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv E:\\connected-side-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation\n# Inside, on SBD-WSUS01, with the media as E: (no output from Compare-Object means every hash matches)\n\$t = 'C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect\\transfer\\2026-10'; New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force \$t | Out-Null\nGet-ChildItem E:\\2026-10 -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv \"\$t\\lab-side-hashes.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv E:\\connected-side-hashes.csv).Hash (Import-Csv \"\$t\\lab-side-hashes.csv\").Hash\nrobocopy E:\\2026-10\\WsusContent D:\\WSUS\\WsusContent /E\n& \"\$env:ProgramFiles\\Update Services\\Tools\\wsusutil.exe\" import E:\\2026-10\\wsus-export.xml.gz \"\$t\\import.log\"\nCopy-Item E:\\connected-side-hashes.csv, E:\\2026-10\\export.log -Destination \$t"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The export server's scope matches lesson 2's record, every hash matches on both sides, the import log reports success, the month's updates appear on SBD-WSUS01, and the crossing record is complete."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the export server's scope settings, both hash lists and their comparison, the custody record, the export and import logs, and the crossing record with the package hash and file count."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Plan your WSUS deployment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/plan/plan-your-wsus-deployment\n\nMicrosoft · Step 2: Configure WSUS (TLS, computer groups, air-gapped networks)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/deploy/2-configure-wsus\n\nMicrosoft · Synchronize software updates from a disconnected software update point (WSUSUtil export and import)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/intune/configmgr/sum/get-started/synchronize-software-updates-disconnected\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why must the export server synchronize exactly the recorded scope?", answers: ["Because everything it holds is exported, and that is what crosses the boundary", "Because Microsoft Update charges by product", "Because SBD-WSUS01 synchronizes from it over the network"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Declining an update doesn't stop it from being exported: the export server's database is the shipment. A tight scope there keeps every crossing small and reviewable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The lab-side hash of one file doesn't match the connected-side list. What happens?", ["Import anyway — clients check signatures", "Copy that one file again from the media and carry on", "The import stops and the mismatch becomes a finding; the set is carried again"], 2, "A mismatch means the set isn't what left the connected side. The clients' signature checks are a second line of defence, not a reason to skip the first."), QuizQuestion("What does the metadata package carry?", ["Only the approvals made on the export server", "The update metadata — the files travel beside it as the WsusContent folder", "The update files and every approval"], 1, "wsusutil export writes the update metadata from the export server's database, and the files come from its content folder. Both cross, both are hashed, and approvals are made inside, ring by ring.")], recap: ["Two WSUS servers: one outside fills the media, one inside serves the network.", "Package and files cross hashed, under custody.", "Compare first, copy the files, then import — or stop."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 points every Windows system at its ring.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#wsus-offline", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:wsus-targeting", title: "Target the rings with computer groups and Group Policy", minutes: 40, summary: "Rings become mechanics: a computer group per ring on SBD-WSUS01, plus one for SBD-AUDIT01, client-side targeting so each computer files itself into its ring, and one Group Policy object per ring that names the server, the ring and its install behaviour. The domain controllers get a ring of their own: they download on their own but install only when Tier 0 starts it, and SBD-AUDIT01 waits for its audit custodian the same way.", objectives: ["Create the ring groups and switch WSUS to client-side targeting.", "Build and link one Group Policy object per ring, with the pilot set by security filtering.", "Set install behaviour and deadlines, and prove every computer reports to its ring."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.13: the domain with its DNS and time design, the STIG baselines and hardening policies, the PKI, DHCP, the tiered administration seats, SBD-REPO01, the logging pipeline with SBD-AUDIT01, run by its audit custodian (course 1.11), and the fleet of SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50 with its three pilot machines.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30, VLAN 20) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) sized for the update files.\n\nOutside the boundary, have an approved connected system that can run a second WSUS server with access to Microsoft Update, and the course 1.4 removable-media procedure for carrying its output across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course gives every Windows system one road for updates. SBD-WSUS01 never touches the internet: each month a WSUS server on the connected side synchronizes the same scope, and its metadata and update files cross on hashed media to be imported inside. Microsoft deprecated WSUS in September 2024 — it gets no new features — but it still ships in Windows Server and is supported for production use, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle. Ring timings and dates are sample values for your patch owner to set. Linux packages already have their road, SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9); endpoint protection and credentialed scanning are coming soon, as courses 1.16 and 1.17, and nothing here depends on them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-WSUS01, create the four ring groups — Ring 1 - Pilot, Ring 2 - Workstations, Ring 3 - Member servers and Ring 4 - Tier 0 — and Ring 3 - Audit archive for SBD-AUDIT01 alone, and set the Computers option to Use Group Policy or registry settings on computers. The groups must exist first: a computer whose policy names a group WSUS doesn't have waits in Unassigned Computers until the group is created."), LessonSection("Step 2", "From AdminJumpBox01, create one GPO per ring, and one for Ring 3 - Audit archive, around the same core: Specify intranet Microsoft update service location, with https://SBD-WSUS01.sbd.test:8531 for both detection and statistics; Enable client-side targeting, with the ring's group name; and Do not connect to any Windows Update Internet locations, so no system keeps trying the public service. Leave Allow signed updates from an intranet Microsoft update service location disabled, so only Microsoft-signed updates install."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Set each ring's install behaviour in Configure Automatic Updates: option 4, download and schedule the install, at a time that suits the ring's work for Rings 1 to 3, and option 7 — download, then notify to install and to restart, a Windows Server option — for Ring 4, so a domain controller installs only when a Tier 0 operator starts it; Ring 3 - Audit archive uses option 7 too, so SBD-AUDIT01 installs only when its audit custodian starts it in a custodian session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01. Deadlines go on the approvals (two days for Ring 1 and five for Rings 2 and 3 in this sample): at a deadline the update installs whatever the client's own schedule says. Ring 4's approvals carry none, and neither do Ring 3 - Audit archive's."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Link the GPOs where the rings live: Ring 2 at the Workstations OU; Ring 3 at the Servers and Admin Workstations OUs and at Admin Servers, filtered to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01; Ring 3 - Audit archive at the Audit Servers OU; Ring 4 at the Domain Controllers and Tier 0 Servers OUs and at Admin Servers, filtered to AdminJumpBox01. Link Ring 1 above the ring GPO on the Workstations and Servers OUs (link order 1), security-filtered to a new SBD WSUS Pilot group holding SBD-PC01–SBD-PC03 and SBD-WSUS01, and restart those four so the membership reaches their Kerberos tickets."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Prove the targeting on one member of each ring: run gpupdate, read the policy values, and start a detection with the Windows Update Agent's DetectNow call; then confirm on SBD-WSUS01 that each computer sits in its ring's group and that Unassigned Computers is empty. Check Ring 4 from AdminJumpBox01, and record every ring's member list against lesson 1's table."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-WSUS01: the four ring groups and client-side targeting\n\$wsus = Get-WsusServer\n'Ring 1 - Pilot', 'Ring 2 - Workstations', 'Ring 3 - Member servers', 'Ring 3 - Audit archive', 'Ring 4 - Tier 0' | ForEach-Object { [void]\$wsus.CreateComputerTargetGroup(\$_) }\n\$config = \$wsus.GetConfiguration(); \$config.TargetingMode = 'Client'; \$config.Save()\n# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the links in order, and the pilot GPO's security filter\n(Get-GPInheritance -Target 'OU=Workstations,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test').GpoLinks | Select-Object Order, DisplayName\nGet-GPPermission -Name 'SBD WSUS Ring 1 - Pilot' -All | Select-Object @{ n = 'Trustee'; e = { \$_.Trustee.Name } }, Permission\n# On one member of each ring, after gpupdate /force: its server, its ring, and a detection now\nGet-ItemProperty HKLM:\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate | Select-Object WUServer, TargetGroup, DoNotConnectToWindowsUpdateInternetLocations\n(New-Object -ComObject Microsoft.Update.AutoUpdate).DetectNow()\n# Back on SBD-WSUS01: each ring's members, and nobody unassigned\nGet-WsusComputer -ComputerTargetGroups 'Ring 1 - Pilot' | Select-Object FullDomainName, LastSyncTime\nGet-WsusComputer -ComputerTargetGroups 'Unassigned Computers'   # expected: no computers"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The four ring groups and Ring 3 - Audit archive exist and WSUS uses client-side targeting; each ring's GPO is linked where its computers live, with the pilot filter and link order in place; one member of each ring shows SBD-WSUS01 and its ring in its policy values; every Windows computer sits in its ring's group; and Unassigned Computers is empty."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the GPO reports with their links and security filtering, the pilot group's members, each ring's member list from SBD-WSUS01, the policy values from one member per ring, and the install-time and deadline table, with SBD-AUDIT01's custodian-started installs."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Step 2: Configure WSUS (TLS, computer groups, air-gapped networks)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/deploy/2-configure-wsus\n\nMicrosoft · Step 4: Configure Group Policy settings for automatic updates\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/deploy/4-configure-group-policy-settings-for-automatic-updates\n\nMicrosoft · Managing WSUS client computers and WSUS computer groups\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/manage/managing-wsus-client-computers-and-wsus-computer-groups\n\nMicrosoft · Deploy Windows client updates using WSUS (rings and client-side targeting)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows/deployment/update/waas-manage-updates-wsus\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WsusComputer\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/updateservices/get-wsuscomputer\n\nMicrosoft · Group Policy scope\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-policy/group-policy-scope\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "A pilot PC receives both the Ring 1 and the Ring 2 GPO. Why does it still report to Ring 1?", answers: ["The Ring 1 link has the higher precedence on that OU (link order 1), so its target group wins the conflict", "WSUS always picks the lowest ring number", "A computer can receive only one GPO"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Both GPOs apply; where they set the same value, the link with the higher precedence wins. Security filtering keeps the Ring 1 GPO off every PC that isn't in the pilot group.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why do the domain controllers use option 7 instead of a scheduled install?", ["Option 7 makes downloads faster", "A Tier 0 operator decides when each controller installs and restarts, so they never go down together and each is checked before the next", "Domain controllers can't run scheduled installs"], 1, "Downloads still happen on their own; only the install and the restart wait for a person — one controller at a time, with replication checked in between."), QuizQuestion("A new server's policy names Ring 3, but the group was mistyped on SBD-WSUS01. Where does the server land?", ["In Ring 4, as the safest ring", "Nowhere — it stops checking for updates", "In Unassigned Computers, until a group with that exact name exists"], 2, "Client-side targeting only files a computer into a group WSUS already has. An empty Unassigned Computers list is part of the proof.")], recap: ["A group per ring, plus SBD-AUDIT01's own, and computers file themselves in by Group Policy.", "Rings 1–3 install on schedule and by deadline; Ring 4 waits for Tier 0, and SBD-AUDIT01 for its custodian.", "An empty Unassigned Computers list: every machine has a ring."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 runs the first monthly cycle through the rings and proves it.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#wsus-targeting", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:patch-protect:wsus-prove", title: "Run the first cycle and prove it", minutes: 40, summary: "The design meets a real month: approve the imported updates for the pilot, promote them ring by ring on the written soak times, and install the domain controllers by hand. Then prove the result with WSUS's own reports, spot checks on the systems themselves, and the first SCAP baseline of SBD-WSUS01.", objectives: ["Promote one monthly cycle through all four rings on the written timing.", "Report compliance per ring and spot-check it on the systems themselves.", "Baseline SBD-WSUS01 and hand the course's records to course 1.15."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.13: the domain with its DNS and time design, the STIG baselines and hardening policies, the PKI, DHCP, the tiered administration seats, SBD-REPO01, the logging pipeline with SBD-AUDIT01, run by its audit custodian (course 1.11), and the fleet of SBD-PC01–SBD-PC50 with its three pilot machines.\n\nProvision SBD-WSUS01 (10.0.20.30, VLAN 20) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) sized for the update files.\n\nOutside the boundary, have an approved connected system that can run a second WSUS server with access to Microsoft Update, and the course 1.4 removable-media procedure for carrying its output across."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "This course gives every Windows system one road for updates. SBD-WSUS01 never touches the internet: each month a WSUS server on the connected side synchronizes the same scope, and its metadata and update files cross on hashed media to be imported inside. Microsoft deprecated WSUS in September 2024 — it gets no new features — but it still ships in Windows Server and is supported for production use, with security and quality updates under the product lifecycle. Ring timings and dates are sample values for your patch owner to set. Linux packages already have their road, SBD-REPO01 (course 1.9); endpoint protection and credentialed scanning are coming soon, as courses 1.16 and 1.17, and nothing here depends on them."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Approve the month's updates for Ring 1 only, with its deadline, and watch the pilots install and report. Before anything moves, check that the pilot PCs still sign in and apply policy and that SBD-WSUS01 still serves its clients; then approve Ring 2 and Ring 3 on the written soak times — Ring 3 - Audit archive with Ring 3, without a deadline — recording for each promotion the numbers that justified it. SBD-AUDIT01's audit custodian then starts its install in a custodian session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and records it; ordinary Tier 1 operators don't install there."), LessonSection("Step 2", "If an update fails or does harm, stop: leave it unapproved for the later rings and, where the update supports removal, approve it for removal in the rings that installed it. Windows client quality updates delivered through the Unified Update Platform can't be removed through WSUS, so the pilot is where they have to prove themselves."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Ring 4 is Tier 0 work from AdminJumpBox01. First read the approval history and confirm that every Ring 4 approval was made by a Tier 0 account — WSUS records who approved what, for which group. Then install on SBD-DC02, restart it, and check it with dcdiag and repadmin /replsummary before touching SBD-DC01; the two Tier 0 servers follow, and AdminJumpBox01 goes last."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Report compliance per ring with the UpdateServices cmdlets (the console's built-in reports need Microsoft's Report Viewer runtime, one more package to carry across): computers that still need or failed an approved update, and computers silent for a week. Spot-check one system per ring on the machine itself against WSUS's view of it. WSUS reports what clients tell it, so the spot checks are the independent view."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Close the cycle once every ring has installed: Invoke-WsusServerCleanup declines superseded updates that nothing still needs and expired ones, and removes unneeded files and computers silent for 30 days. Then take SBD-WSUS01's first SCAP baseline with course 1.4's SCC and benchmark versions, run locally in a Tier 1 session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, answer the IIS server and site STIG items for the WSUS web site in a STIG Viewer checklist (staged across the air gap as in course 1.4), and classify every open item as course 1.4 taught — update files on HTTP 8530 are a deviation candidate, justified by signed files whose hashes travel in TLS-protected metadata."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Hand off to course 1.15: the ring table, WSUS's computer list and this cycle's compliance report become inventory sources, the crossing calendar sets when its reconciliation runs, and SBD-MGMT01 joins Ring 3 simply by landing in the Servers OU. Collect the course's records into C:\\Evidence\\patch-protect on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and hash them into a manifest."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-WSUS01 (Tier 1): approve for the pilot; deadlines are set in the console's Approve Updates dialog\nGet-WsusUpdate -Classification All -Approval Unapproved -Status FailedOrNeeded | Approve-WsusUpdate -Action Install -TargetGroupName 'Ring 1 - Pilot'\nGet-WsusComputer -ComputerTargetGroups 'Ring 1 - Pilot' -ComputerUpdateStatus FailedOrNeeded   # expected: none once the ring is clean\nGet-WsusComputer -ToLastReportedStatusTime (Get-Date).AddDays(-7)   # computers silent for a week\n# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): who approved each Ring 4 update, then each controller in turn\n\$wsus = Get-WsusServer -Name SBD-WSUS01.sbd.test -PortNumber 8531 -UseSsl\n\$wsus.GetUpdateApprovals((New-Object Microsoft.UpdateServices.Administration.UpdateScope)) | Where-Object { \$_.GetComputerTargetGroup().Name -eq 'Ring 4 - Tier 0' } | Select-Object CreationDate, AdministratorName, Action\ndcdiag /s:SBD-DC02 /q; repadmin /replsummary   # after SBD-DC02 restarts, before SBD-DC01\n# Spot check from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: what a system itself says it installed\nInvoke-Command SBD-DHCP01 { Get-HotFix | Sort-Object InstalledOn -Descending | Select-Object -First 3 HotFixID, InstalledOn }\n# Back on SBD-WSUS01, once every ring has installed\nInvoke-WsusServerCleanup -DeclineSupersededUpdates -DeclineExpiredUpdates -CleanupObsoleteUpdates -CleanupUnneededContentFiles -CleanupObsoleteComputers"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Each ring installed the month's updates in the written order with its promotion recorded, SBD-AUDIT01's custodian installed its updates, every Ring 4 approval names a Tier 0 account, both controllers were healthy between installs, no computer is failing or silent without an explanation, the spot checks agree with WSUS, SBD-WSUS01 has its first baseline with every open item classified, and the course's records are hashed."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the per-ring approval and install timeline with the custodian's install record for SBD-AUDIT01, the Ring 4 approval history and install record, the compliance and silent-computer reports, the spot checks, the cleanup output, the SCC results and STIG Viewer checklist with classified findings, the manifest, and the handoff note to course 1.15."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Plan your WSUS deployment\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/plan/plan-your-wsus-deployment\n\nMicrosoft · WSUS updates operations (approvals, deadlines, removal)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/manage/updates-operations\n\nMicrosoft · The WSUS Server Cleanup Wizard\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/manage/the-server-cleanup-wizard\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WsusComputer\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/updateservices/get-wsuscomputer\n\nMicrosoft · Dcdiag\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/dcdiag\n\nDISA · SCAP content and the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "The pilot installs cleanly, but one pilot PC can no longer apply Group Policy. What happens to the promotion?", answers: ["It stops until the cause is understood: the update stays unapproved for later rings, and is removed where it supports removal", "It continues — the install itself succeeded", "Approve it for every ring to see whether the problem spreads"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "An install that succeeds but breaks work is a failed update. The pilot exists to catch exactly this before the systems that matter feel it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why check Ring 4's approval history before a controller installs?", ["Approvals expire after a day", "WSUS can't stop a non-Tier 0 administrator from approving for Ring 4, so the record shows whether the written rule held", "Domain controllers can't read approvals"], 1, "Where a tool can't enforce a boundary, the evidence has to show it held. WSUS records the administrator behind every approval, so the check takes a minute."), QuizQuestion("WSUS reports every computer compliant. What does that prove?", ["That the network has no vulnerabilities", "Nothing at all", "That every computer reported its approved updates installed — the clients' own report, which the spot checks test"], 2, "WSUS's view is the clients' own report: useful and automatic, but not independent. Spot checks on the machines themselves are what keep it honest.")], recap: ["The month moves pilot → workstations → servers → Tier 0, on record.", "Ring 4: approvals checked by name, controllers one at a time.", "Reports, spot checks and a first baseline prove it."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.14. Course 1.15 builds SBD-MGMT01 and the living inventory, with this course's WSUS records among its sources.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/patch-protect#wsus-prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "management-server", number: "1.15", title: "Keep the living inventory on SBD-MGMT01", subtitle: "Component inventory, discovery sources & monthly reconciliation", category: "Network build", description: "Turn course 1.13's fleet snapshot into a living component inventory for the whole network: build SBD-MGMT01 on VLAN 70 as a keeper that reaches into nothing, record every component once with an accountable owner, check the record against what the directory, DHCP, WSUS and SBD-REPO01 already know, and reconcile it every month so drift becomes a finding.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "management-server-guide", title: "Keep the living inventory on SBD-MGMT01 · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Component inventory, discovery sources & monthly reconciliation", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "management-server-package", title: "Keep the living inventory on SBD-MGMT01 · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server/SecureByDesign-management-server-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:management-server:mgmt-design", title: "Design the inventory and its keeper", minutes: 25, summary: "Decide what the component inventory must hold before SBD-MGMT01 exists: every component inside the boundary counted once, and the columns that make each one accountable. Then name the records that can contradict it, and give it a keeper that holds the record without reaching into anything else.", objectives: ["Draw the inventory's boundary and list every component in it.", "Choose the columns that make each component accountable.", "Name the discovery sources and place SBD-MGMT01 under the tier model."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.14: every system those courses built is running, course 1.13's fleet inventory is filed, and SBD-WSUS01 serves all four update rings.\n\nProvision SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10, VLAN 70) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the record.\n\nKeep the records earlier courses produced within reach: the service-path register, course 1.14's ring table and WSUS computer list, course 1.9's snapshot catalog, each course's baseline sessions, and the exception record."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Course 1.13 handed over a snapshot of the fleet; this course makes the whole network's component inventory a living record on SBD-MGMT01 — NIST SP 800-53 CM-8 in practice. The server keeps the record and reaches into nothing: the directory, the DHCP leases, the WSUS computer list, SBD-REPO01's records and the baseline sessions it is checked against arrive through the administrators' existing sessions. Column names and the monthly cadence are sample values; the system owner decides."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Draw the boundary as CM-8 asks: every component of this network, each counted once, and nothing from outside it. That is every server and controller (SBD-ROOTCA01 included, offline in the vault), the three administration seats, each of the 50 PCs, the core and the VLAN 80 access switch, and the seven hypervisor hosts (SBD-HV01–SBD-HV07) on which Inside the Network places the virtual machines. The connected-side systems that fill the media stay out; the crossing records name them."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Choose the columns, one per attribute: name, type, role, operating system and version, domain membership, address and VLAN, OU, tier, location (host and rack), update source (a WSUS ring, an SBD-REPO01 snapshot, or by hand at a ceremony), latest baseline, the course that built it, an accountable owner by role (CM-8(4)), and status — planned, active or retired. Record the systems the design names but no course has built yet, SBD-EPO01, SBD-NESSUS01 and the four VLAN 40 servers, as planned, so a reconciliation never mistakes them for missing."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Name the records that can contradict it and what each one proves: the directory shows what joined the domain, the DHCP leases what is on VLAN 80 now, WSUS's computer list what reports for updates and in which ring, SBD-REPO01's catalog and access log which Linux servers take packages, and the baseline sessions which hosts have a measured state. Any disagreement is a finding, and lesson 5 makes the comparison a monthly check."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Place the keeper as Inside the Network does: SBD-MGMT01, Windows Server at 10.0.70.10 on VLAN 70 beside SBD-REPO01, a Tier 1 member server administered only from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (rule A02). The model leaves its listeners to define: define them as management only, and give it no collection path of its own — source data arrives through the administrators' existing sessions, so the server that holds the record can't reach, or be used to reach, anything else. Its domain rows (F01–F03, F06 in Ring 3, F09, F17 and F18) already list it."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: start the course record with the inventory's columns\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\\Evidence\\management-server | Out-Null\n'Name,Type,Role,OS,Domain,Address,VLAN,OU,Tier,Location,UpdateSource,Baseline,BuiltIn,Owner,Status' | Set-Content C:\\Evidence\\management-server\\inventory-columns.csv\n# SBD-MGMT01 isn't registered yet; its neighbour on VLAN 70 is\nResolve-DnsName SBD-MGMT01.sbd.test -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue   # expected: no answer\nResolve-DnsName SBD-REPO01.sbd.test | Select-Object Name, IPAddress   # 10.0.70.20"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The boundary lists every component once, with the planned systems marked; the columns cover identity, place, tier, update source, baseline and an accountable owner; each discovery source is named with what it proves; and SBD-MGMT01's placement, administration and lack of any collection path are written down."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the boundary list, the column definitions, the source table with what each source proves, and SBD-MGMT01's placement and administration decision."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CM-8 System Component Inventory)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why record the hypervisor hosts when every server is a virtual machine?", answers: ["They are optional decoration", "They run every virtual machine, so whatever controls them controls the servers — they belong inside the boundary", "The core requires it"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "An inventory that lists the guests but not their hosts misses the components with the most reach. Inside the Network gives every server's host and rack for that reason.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("SBD-EPO01 is designed but not built. How does the inventory show it?", ["It leaves it out until it exists", "It lists it as active so the record looks complete", "As planned, so the record matches the design and a reconciliation doesn't flag it as missing"], 2, "Status carries the difference between designed and installed. When the system is built, its row turns active as part of that build."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-MGMT01 get no collection path of its own?", ["A server that can reach every source becomes a path to all of them; letting data arrive through existing administration sessions keeps it a keeper, not a hub", "Collection paths are slower", "Windows Server can't read other servers"], 0, "Least privilege applies to servers too. Every path the register doesn't need is one an attacker can't use.")], recap: ["Every component inside the boundary, counted once — hosts and network devices included.", "Columns that make each component accountable, owner included.", "Sources that can contradict the record, and a keeper that reaches into nothing."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds SBD-MGMT01 and locks it down.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server#mgmt-design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:management-server:mgmt-build", title: "Build and harden SBD-MGMT01", minutes: 35, summary: "Build the inventory's keeper as a small, quiet member server, joined into the Servers OU so the hardening, tier and update policies apply from its first boot. It answers only the Tier 1 seat, and the record sits on its own volume behind permissions that admit administrators alone.", objectives: ["Build SBD-MGMT01 in its place with the network's policies applied from first boot.", "Limit its administration to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 and prove the limit.", "Prepare the record's folders, permissions and version history."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.14: every system those courses built is running, course 1.13's fleet inventory is filed, and SBD-WSUS01 serves all four update rings.\n\nProvision SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10, VLAN 70) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the record.\n\nKeep the records earlier courses produced within reach: the service-path register, course 1.14's ring table and WSUS computer list, course 1.9's snapshot catalog, each course's baseline sessions, and the exception record."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Course 1.13 handed over a snapshot of the fleet; this course makes the whole network's component inventory a living record on SBD-MGMT01 — NIST SP 800-53 CM-8 in practice. The server keeps the record and reaches into nothing: the directory, the DHCP leases, the WSUS computer list, SBD-REPO01's records and the baseline sessions it is checked against arrive through the administrators' existing sessions. Column names and the monthly cadence are sample values; the system owner decides."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-MGMT01 as a Windows Server virtual machine — on SBD-HV07 beside SBD-REPO01, where Inside the Network places it — with the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off, so the domain is its only clock, a separate data volume for the record (D: in this sample), the static address 10.0.70.10/24 with the core's VLAN 70 interface as gateway (10.0.70.1 in this sample), and both domain controllers for DNS. Join it straight into the Servers OU, so course 1.5's STIG policies, course 1.8's tier logon policies, course 1.10's event-forwarding GPO (linked to the Servers OU in course 1.10) and course 1.14's Ring 3 update policy apply from its first boot."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Check that it took its place: time from a domain controller, as course 1.3 set up, and an update policy that names SBD-WSUS01 and Ring 3, with SBD-WSUS01 listing it there. Add it to SBD Event Sources from AdminJumpBox01, widen the collector's WinRM scope to 10.0.70.10 (F17) from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, and restart it so its security events reach SBD-SYSLOG01."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Close it to everything but its administration: Windows Defender Firewall on in every profile, and the existing Remote Desktop and Windows Remote Management rules scoped to SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (10.0.60.20), because allow rules add up and a new, narrower rule would not restrict the broad ones. Install no other role and no agent; course 1.8's Servers OU policy already denies Tier 0 accounts here."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prepare the record on D:: an Inventory folder with inheritance removed and full control for SYSTEM and Administrators only — the volume's inherited defaults let ordinary users read and create files — holding current, history, sources and findings folders. Every published version is copied into history with its SHA-256 in a hash list, and every change gets a line in a change log: who, what, why, and the change record it answers."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Prove the closure: a remote session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 opens, the same attempt from SBD-ADMIN-PC01 fails at the core because rule A02 names only the Tier 1 seat, and the firewall's remote-address lists show only 10.0.60.20 for management."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# In a session on SBD-MGMT01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01\n([adsisearcher]\"(&(objectCategory=computer)(name=\$env:COMPUTERNAME))\").FindOne().Path   # ...OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test\nw32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, never the local clock\nGet-ItemProperty HKLM:\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate | Select-Object WUServer, TargetGroup   # Ring 3 - Member servers\nGet-NetFirewallRule -DisplayGroup 'Remote Desktop', 'Windows Remote Management' -Enabled True | Get-NetFirewallAddressFilter | Select-Object InstanceID, RemoteAddress   # 10.0.60.20 only\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force D:\\Inventory | Out-Null\nicacls D:\\Inventory /inheritance:r /grant:r 'SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F' 'Administrators:(OI)(CI)F'\n'current', 'history', 'sources', 'findings' | ForEach-Object { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force \"D:\\Inventory\\\$_\" | Out-Null }\nicacls D:\\Inventory\n# From SBD-ADMIN-PC01: the same management path must fail\nTest-NetConnection SBD-MGMT01.sbd.test -Port 5985   # expected: TcpTestSucceeded False"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-MGMT01 sits in the Servers OU with the hypervisor's time synchronization off and time from a domain controller, reports to Ring 3 on SBD-WSUS01, forwards its security events, accepts management only from 10.0.60.20 and refuses SBD-ADMIN-PC01, runs no extra role or agent, and holds an Inventory folder that only SYSTEM and Administrators can open."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the OU placement, the time source, the update policy values and WSUS listing, the firewall scope, the refused connection from SBD-ADMIN-PC01, and the Inventory folder's permissions."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CM-8 System Component Inventory)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · Configure Windows Firewall rules with Group Policy\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/configure\n\nMicrosoft · Icacls\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/icacls")], question: "Why scope the existing Remote Desktop and remote management rules instead of adding a narrow new rule?", answers: ["New rules are slower", "Allow rules add up: a narrow new rule leaves the broad existing one letting everyone in", "Windows allows only one rule per port"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Windows Defender Firewall admits a connection if any enabled allow rule matches. Narrow the rules that exist, then prove it from a seat that must be refused.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("SBD-ADMIN-PC01 shares VLAN 60 with SBD-ADMIN-SRV01. Why is its attempt on SBD-MGMT01 refused?", ["Rule A02 names only SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as the source for member-server management, so the core denies the Tier 2 seat — and the host firewall would too", "Virtual machines can't use the network", "SBD-MGMT01 is offline"], 0, "Sharing a VLAN grants nothing. The register names the seat, the core enforces it between VLANs, and the host firewall enforces it again."), QuizQuestion("Why remove inheritance on the Inventory folder?", ["To make backups faster", "Inheritance can't be used on data volumes", "A new volume's inherited permissions let ordinary users read and create files; the record should open to administrators alone"], 2, "Defaults are someone else's decision. The record's permissions are part of its integrity, alongside its version history and hashes.")], recap: ["In the Servers OU: hardened, tiered and patched from first boot.", "Management from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 only — proven by a refused attempt.", "A record folder for administrators alone, with history and hashes."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 fills it with the first complete inventory.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server#mgmt-build", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:management-server:mgmt", title: "Build the living inventory", minutes: 30, summary: "Course 1.13's fleet inventory was a snapshot of fifty PCs; SBD-MGMT01 makes the whole network's inventory a living record. Every component gets its place, ring, update source, baseline and owner, kept current by the procedures that change it.", objectives: ["Load every component into the inventory from the records the path already keeps.", "Define how the inventory stays current and who owns it.", "Publish the first version with its hash."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.14: every system those courses built is running, course 1.13's fleet inventory is filed, and SBD-WSUS01 serves all four update rings.\n\nProvision SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10, VLAN 70) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the record.\n\nKeep the records earlier courses produced within reach: the service-path register, course 1.14's ring table and WSUS computer list, course 1.9's snapshot catalog, each course's baseline sessions, and the exception record."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Course 1.13 handed over a snapshot of the fleet; this course makes the whole network's component inventory a living record on SBD-MGMT01 — NIST SP 800-53 CM-8 in practice. The server keeps the record and reaches into nothing: the directory, the DHCP leases, the WSUS computer list, SBD-REPO01's records and the baseline sessions it is checked against arrive through the administrators' existing sessions. Column names and the monthly cadence are sample values; the system owner decides."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Start from the records the path already keeps rather than from memory: course 1.13's fleet inventory for the 50 PCs (names, image ancestry, certificate thumbprints, sample status), the service-path register and Inside the Network for names, addresses and locations, course 1.14's ring table for the update rings, course 1.9's snapshot catalog for SBD-REPO01, and each course's baseline set for the latest baseline date."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Load the inventory with every component the boundary lists — the fleet, the servers (SBD-REPO01 included, with its current snapshot as its update source), the admin plane, the core, the access switch and the hypervisor hosts — each with every column filled: the CM-8 component inventory, in one place. Add the planned systems as planned."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Define currency: every build, move and retirement updates the inventory as part of its own procedure (CM-8(1)), so each later course's build lesson adds its rows, and a monthly reconciliation compares the inventory against the directory, the DHCP leases and the WSUS computer list — drift between them is a finding."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Name the owner — a role, with a named deputy — who approves each change, and publish the first version: copy it into history with its hash and log the change. Course 2.2 shows how a record like this one joins the Body of Evidence for the configuration-management controls."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# In a session on SBD-MGMT01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, once the record is filled in\n\$inv = Import-Csv D:\\Inventory\\current\\inventory.csv\n\$inv | Group-Object Status | Select-Object Name, Count\n\$inv | Group-Object Name | Where-Object Count -gt 1 | Select-Object Name   # expected: nothing counted twice\n\$inv | Where-Object { \$_.Status -eq 'active' -and (-not \$_.Owner -or -not \$_.UpdateSource -or -not \$_.Location) } | Select-Object Name   # expected: nothing\n\$stamp = Get-Date -Format yyyy-MM-dd\nCopy-Item D:\\Inventory\\current\\inventory.csv \"D:\\Inventory\\history\\inventory-\$stamp.csv\"\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 \"D:\\Inventory\\history\\inventory-\$stamp.csv\" | Export-Csv D:\\Inventory\\history\\hashes.csv -Append -NoTypeInformation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The inventory lists every component in the boundary exactly once with every column filled, planned systems are marked planned, the update-on-change rule and the monthly reconciliation are written with an owner and deputy, and the first version is in history with its hash."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the populated inventory export, the status counts and completeness checks, the currency rule with its owner and cadence, and the first version's hash."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CM-8 System Component Inventory)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "What makes an inventory 'living' rather than a stale spreadsheet?", answers: ["Pretty formatting", "Update-on-change plus a scheduled reconciliation against sources the record doesn't control — the directory and the leases", "Monthly printing"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Processes keep it current; reconciliation proves it stayed current. Both are written down, so both are checkable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A machine appears in DHCP leases but not the inventory. What is that?", ["Normal churn to ignore", "A reason to restart DHCP", "A finding — either an undocumented system or a broken process, and both matter"], 2, "The reconciliation exists to surface exactly this. Unknown hardware on the network is the classic first question of any assessment."), QuizQuestion("Which control family consumes this record most directly?", ["Configuration management — CM-8's component inventory is literally this artifact", "Physical security", "Awareness training"], 0, "The authorization path's CM controls ask 'what do you have and how do you know' — this lesson is the answer being built.")], recap: ["One record of everything, with place, ring, update source, baseline and owner.", "Every change updates it; a monthly reconciliation keeps it honest.", "Owned, versioned, and ready to join the Body of Evidence."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 gathers what the network already knows, to check the record against.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server#mgmt", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:management-server:inventory-sources", title: "Feed it from what the network already knows", minutes: 35, summary: "The inventory says what should exist; the network's own records say what does. Export the directory's computer objects, the DHCP leases, the WSUS computer list, SBD-REPO01's records and the baseline sessions through the sessions administrators already use, and file them on SBD-MGMT01 with hashes, in one shape lesson 5 can compare.", objectives: ["Export each discovery source through an existing administration path.", "Know what each source can and can't prove.", "File the exports on SBD-MGMT01 with hashes, in one comparable shape."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.14: every system those courses built is running, course 1.13's fleet inventory is filed, and SBD-WSUS01 serves all four update rings.\n\nProvision SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10, VLAN 70) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the record.\n\nKeep the records earlier courses produced within reach: the service-path register, course 1.14's ring table and WSUS computer list, course 1.9's snapshot catalog, each course's baseline sessions, and the exception record."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Course 1.13 handed over a snapshot of the fleet; this course makes the whole network's component inventory a living record on SBD-MGMT01 — NIST SP 800-53 CM-8 in practice. The server keeps the record and reaches into nothing: the directory, the DHCP leases, the WSUS computer list, SBD-REPO01's records and the baseline sessions it is checked against arrive through the administrators' existing sessions. Column names and the monthly cadence are sample values; the system owner decides."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Read the directory over LDAP from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: every computer object's name, operating system, distinguished name (which gives its OU) and lastLogonTimestamp. Use an LDAP search rather than the Active Directory module, whose Web Services port the controllers open only to AdminJumpBox01, and read lastLogonTimestamp as a sign of staleness, not of last use: it is updated only when the stored value is 9 to 14 days old."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Export the leases on SBD-DHCP01 in a session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: every lease in the VLAN 80 scope with its address, client ID (the MAC address), host name, state and expiry. A lease shows what is plugged in now, joined or not; course 1.7's DHCP audit log shows what came and went between exports."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Export SBD-WSUS01's computer list the same way: each computer's name, address, last contact, last status report and requested ring. WSUS's cleanup removes computers silent for 30 days (course 1.14), so a machine missing there has been quiet for a month or more."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Copy SBD-REPO01's records over SSH as course 1.9 does, and count its web server's access log by client address to see which Linux servers actually take packages. List the baseline sessions in the evidence folders you hold, by host and date; Tier 0 reports its hosts' dates through the change record, because its evidence stays on AdminJumpBox01."), LessonSection("Step 5", "File the set: hash every export into a dated list, copy both into D:\\Inventory\\sources on SBD-MGMT01 through a remoting session, and reduce every name to one key — the short host name, so a lease's SBD-PC07.sbd.test and the directory's SBD-PC07 meet. Take all the sources on the same day; sources taken weeks apart disagree about machines that simply moved."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (Tier 1): one dated folder for this month's sources\n\$d = \"C:\\Evidence\\management-server\\sources\\\$(Get-Date -Format yyyy-MM-dd)\"; New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force \"\$d\\repo01\" | Out-Null\n\$s = [adsisearcher]'(objectCategory=computer)'; \$s.PageSize = 500\n\$s.FindAll() | ForEach-Object { \$p = \$_.Properties; [pscustomobject]@{ Name = \"\$(\$p.name)\"; OS = \"\$(\$p.operatingsystem)\"; DN = \"\$(\$p.distinguishedname)\"; LastLogon = if (\"\$(\$p.lastlogontimestamp)\") { [datetime]::FromFileTime([long]\"\$(\$p.lastlogontimestamp)\") } } } | Export-Csv \"\$d\\directory.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nInvoke-Command SBD-DHCP01 { Get-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 10.0.80.0 -AllLeases | Select-Object IPAddress, ClientId, HostName, AddressState, LeaseExpiryTime } | Export-Csv \"\$d\\dhcp-leases.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nInvoke-Command SBD-WSUS01 { Get-WsusComputer -All | Select-Object FullDomainName, IPAddress, LastSyncTime, LastReportedStatusTime, RequestedTargetGroupName } | Export-Csv \"\$d\\wsus-computers.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nscp -r <your-account>@SBD-REPO01.sbd.test:/srv/repo-records/* \"\$d\\repo01\\\"\n# On SBD-REPO01: who fetched packages\nsudo awk '{print \$1}' /var/log/httpd/access_log | sort | uniq -c\n# Back on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: hash the set and file it on SBD-MGMT01\nGet-ChildItem \$d -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv \"\$d-hashes.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\n\$m = New-PSSession SBD-MGMT01; Copy-Item \$d, \"\$d-hashes.csv\" -Destination D:\\Inventory\\sources\\ -ToSession \$m -Recurse"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Each source export exists for the same date with the columns lesson 5 needs, the hash list matches the files copied to SBD-MGMT01, every name reduces to one key, and a note says what each source can and can't prove."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the dated source exports, their hash list, the REPO01 access-log summary, the baseline-session list, and the note on what each source proves."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CM-8 System Component Inventory)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · DirectorySearcher class (LDAP searches of Active Directory)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.directoryservices.directorysearcher\n\nMicrosoft · Last-Logon-Timestamp attribute\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/adschema/a-lastlogontimestamp\n\nMicrosoft · The lastLogonTimestamp attribute may not be accurate\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/mem/configmgr/discovery/lastlogontimestamp-not-accurate\n\nMicrosoft · Get-DhcpServerv4Lease\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/dhcpserver/get-dhcpserverv4lease\n\nMicrosoft · Get-WsusComputer\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/updateservices/get-wsuscomputer\n\nMicrosoft · The WSUS Server Cleanup Wizard\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/windows-server/administration/windows-server-update-services/manage/the-server-cleanup-wizard\n\nMicrosoft · Copy-Item (copying to and from a remote session)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.management/copy-item\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "Why read the directory over LDAP instead of with Get-ADComputer from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01?", answers: ["LDAP is faster for large domains", "The Active Directory module needs the controllers' Web Services port, which only AdminJumpBox01 may reach; LDAP is a path every member already has (F03)", "Get-ADComputer can't read computer objects"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The register decides which tools work from which seat. Reading what every member may read keeps the inventory owner in Tier 1.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("A PC has no lease today. What does that prove?", ["That it was stolen", "That it was retired", "Little on its own — it may simply be switched off; check the audit log and the other sources before calling it missing"], 2, "Each source proves something narrow. Absence from one is a question for the others, not a verdict."), QuizQuestion("Why reduce every name to one key before comparing?", ["So SBD-PC07.sbd.test in the leases and SBD-PC07 in the directory count as one machine, not two findings", "To save disk space", "Because WSUS requires capital letters"], 0, "Most false findings come from formatting, not drift. Normalize once, in one place, and the comparison means something.")], recap: ["Five sources, gathered through paths administrators already use.", "Each proves something narrow; together they test the record.", "Same day, one name key, hashed and filed on SBD-MGMT01."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 reconciles the record with the sources and proves the checks work.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server#inventory-sources", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:management-server:mgmt-prove", title: "Reconcile the inventory and prove it", minutes: 35, summary: "Compare the record with what the network reports, classify every difference, and prove the comparison catches drift before trusting a clean result. Then take SBD-MGMT01's first SCAP baseline and hand a reconciled inventory — and the monthly habit — to the courses that follow.", objectives: ["Reconcile recorded and discovered components and classify every difference.", "Prove the reconciliation catches drift with a deliberate test.", "Baseline SBD-MGMT01 and hand off the reconciled record."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.14: every system those courses built is running, course 1.13's fleet inventory is filed, and SBD-WSUS01 serves all four update rings.\n\nProvision SBD-MGMT01 (10.0.70.10, VLAN 70) as a Windows Server virtual machine with a separate data volume (D: in this sample) for the record.\n\nKeep the records earlier courses produced within reach: the service-path register, course 1.14's ring table and WSUS computer list, course 1.9's snapshot catalog, each course's baseline sessions, and the exception record."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "Course 1.13 handed over a snapshot of the fleet; this course makes the whole network's component inventory a living record on SBD-MGMT01 — NIST SP 800-53 CM-8 in practice. The server keeps the record and reaches into nothing: the directory, the DHCP leases, the WSUS computer list, SBD-REPO01's records and the baseline sessions it is checked against arrive through the administrators' existing sessions. Column names and the monthly cadence are sample values; the system owner decides."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-MGMT01, compare the inventory's active rows with each source by the name key: the domain-joined rows against the directory, the VLAN 80 rows against the leases, and the Windows rows against WSUS's computer list and its rings. In a source but not the record is an unrecorded component; recorded but absent where it should appear is a gap; present in both with a different value — a ring, an OU, an address — is drift."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Prove the comparison works before trusting a clean result: run it against a test copy of the inventory with one PC's row removed — the PC must appear as found in the directory but not in the record — and against another copy with one ring changed, which the ring comparison must report. Test copies are never published."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Classify every real difference and close it: correct the record through the change log, bring the component under management or remove it, or record the explanation — a PC switched off for a week has no lease and isn't missing. An unrecorded device is reported to the system owner the day it is found (CM-8(3)), and drift that keeps coming from one procedure is fixed in that procedure."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Take SBD-MGMT01's first SCAP baseline with course 1.4's SCC and benchmark versions, run locally in a Tier 1 session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, classify its open items as course 1.4 taught, and enter the date in its own inventory row."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Hand off: publish the reconciled version with its hash, set the monthly reconciliation in the week after course 1.14's cycle closes so the WSUS list is fresh, and note what later courses add — course 1.18 turns the VLAN 40 servers' planned rows active as it builds them, and course 1.19 carries D:\\Inventory into the recovery set."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-MGMT01 (session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01): the newest sources against the active record\n\$src = (Get-ChildItem D:\\Inventory\\sources -Directory | Sort-Object Name | Select-Object -Last 1).FullName\n\$short = { \$args[0] -replace '\\..*\$' }\n\$active = Import-Csv D:\\Inventory\\current\\inventory.csv | Where-Object Status -eq 'active'\n\$directory = (Import-Csv \"\$src\\directory.csv\").Name\n\$leases = Import-Csv \"\$src\\dhcp-leases.csv\" | Where-Object HostName | ForEach-Object { & \$short \$_.HostName }\n\$rings = Import-Csv \"\$src\\wsus-computers.csv\" | ForEach-Object { '{0}|{1}' -f (& \$short \$_.FullDomainName), \$_.RequestedTargetGroupName }\nCompare-Object (\$active | Where-Object Domain -eq 'sbd.test').Name \$directory   # <= recorded only, => directory only\nCompare-Object (\$active | Where-Object VLAN -eq '80').Name \$leases\nCompare-Object (\$active | Where-Object UpdateSource -like 'Ring*' | ForEach-Object { '{0}|{1}' -f \$_.Name, \$_.UpdateSource }) \$rings\n# Prove it: with SBD-PC07's row left out of a test copy, SBD-PC07 must show as directory only\nCompare-Object (\$active | Where-Object { \$_.Domain -eq 'sbd.test' -and \$_.Name -ne 'SBD-PC07' }).Name \$directory"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every difference between the record and the sources is listed and classified, the deliberate tests were caught, unrecorded components were reported to the system owner, SBD-MGMT01 has its first baseline, and the reconciled version is published with its hash and the next reconciliation date."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the comparison output, the classified findings with their resolutions, the tests that proved detection, the SCC results with classified findings, the reconciled version's hash, and the handoff note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CM-8 System Component Inventory)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash\n\nDISA · SCAP content and the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools")], question: "Why run the comparison against a deliberately broken copy first?", answers: ["A clean result proves nothing until you have seen the same check catch a known difference", "To make the report longer", "Because the real record might be deleted"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "A check that can't fail isn't a check. One planted difference, caught, turns 'no findings' into evidence.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The directory holds SBD-PC51, which no record mentions, and it has a lease. What happens?", ["Delete it from the directory and move on", "It is reported to the system owner the day it is found and investigated as an unrecorded component, and the procedure that let it in is fixed", "Add it to the inventory so the counts match"], 1, "Unknown hardware is the classic first question of an assessment. Making the record agree without asking why would hide the very thing CM-8 exists to catch."), QuizQuestion("Why schedule the reconciliation after course 1.14's monthly cycle closes?", ["Because WSUS is offline the rest of the month", "Because the directory changes only once a month", "Because the WSUS computer list and rings are freshest then, so a difference is drift, not timing"], 2, "Sources taken at the right moment produce findings worth acting on. Timing is part of the method.")], recap: ["Recorded against discovered: unrecorded, gap or drift — each classified.", "A planted difference, caught, makes a clean result mean something.", "Reconciled, baselined and handed on — every month from now on."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.15. Courses 1.16 and 1.17, endpoint protection and credentialed scanning, are coming soon; course 1.18 stands up the business services and turns their planned rows in this inventory active.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/management-server#mgmt-prove", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "business-services", number: "1.18", title: "Stand up the business services", subtitle: "Files, database, licensing & web — by documented paths", category: "Network build", description: "Give the users what the network exists for: SBD-FS01's role-based shares over verified SMB security, SBD-WEB01 serving internal HTTPS from a hardened, domain-joined Linux Apache, SBD-APPS01's licensing service and application API, and SBD-SQL01 running as a managed identity behind exactly one caller — every hop a documented, proven register row.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "5 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 5,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-guide", title: "Stand up the business services · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Files, database, licensing & web — by documented paths", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "business-services-package", title: "Stand up the business services · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services/SecureByDesign-business-services-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:design", title: "Design the service paths and identities", minutes: 30, summary: "Four services, and before any of them exist: who talks to what over which port, which identity each service runs as, and which groups own access — the register and the directory answer all three before a role installs.", objectives: ["Write the register rows for every business service path.", "Choose managed service identities instead of password accounts.", "Define the access groups that will own each service's data."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.15: the domain with its DNS, time and hardened OUs, the course 1.6 PKI and its autoenrollment GPO, DHCP and the admin seats, SBD-REPO01 and its Linux client package, the logging pipeline with course 1.11's KDS root key, SBD-SPLUNK01 as the Linux server pattern, the fleet, the update rings, and the SBD-MGMT01 inventory.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts as virtual machines on SBD-HV05: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20) and SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30) on Windows Server, and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40) on the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with Apache, to be joined to sbd.test with SSSD as SBD-SPLUNK01 was in course 1.12.\n\nHave the service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, course 1.9's client package and Linux assessment set, and course 1.4's SCC media within reach — every service here gets a documented path, a real certificate and a first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the service-path rows into the register, with the IDs Inside the Network gives them and an owner each: the fleet to SBD-FS01 on SMB 445 (F05), to SBD-APPS01 on its licensing port, recorded per application (F12), and to SBD-WEB01 on HTTPS 443 (F13); SBD-WEB01 to SBD-APPS01 for the application API on HTTPS 443 (F21); and SBD-APPS01 — and only SBD-APPS01 — to SBD-SQL01 on TCP 1433 with TLS (F22). Add the explicit deny from SBD-WEB01 to SBD-SQL01 (F14): the web tier has no database dependency. All four servers share VLAN 40, so the router never sees their traffic to each other — each server's host firewall enforces those rows. Everything else toward VLAN 40 stays denied, including endpoint-to-SQL directly."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Add the rows every new server needs: administration from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (A02), and for the three Windows servers the domain rows (F01–F03, F09), Ring 3 updates (F06), event forwarding (F17), certificate publication (F18) and enrollment to SBD-SUBCA01 on the row course 1.6 confirmed. SBD-WEB01's Linux rows already name it: packages from SBD-REPO01 only (F19, HTTP with GPG-signed packages, course 1.9), syslog over TLS to the collector, SBD-SYSLOG01 (F07, TCP 6514), and time from both domain controllers (F09) — the same paths SBD-SPLUNK01 has used since course 1.12."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Choose the service identities: group managed service accounts (gMSAs) for the SQL and application services, so passwords rotate themselves. They rely on the forest's one KDS root key, which course 1.11 created: confirm it with Get-KdsRootKey and never create another. Each account is created from AdminJumpBox01 once its host has joined, allowed to that one computer, as course 1.11 did (lesson 3). Note delegated managed service accounts, introduced in the newest Windows Server release, as the migration path for any legacy service account this lab later inherits."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Create the access groups under the course 1.5 Groups OU from AdminJumpBox01 — per-share and per-application role groups — because permissions will be granted to roles, never to named humans."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Plan the server certificates: one SBD Server Authentication certificate (course 1.6) each for SQL01, APPS01 (for its application API), and WEB01, with the host's DNS name in the subject alternative name. Each host requests its own in its build lesson through the course 1.6 process, so the private key never leaves it — the template allows exportable keys only where course 1.19's recovery plan says so. Course 1.6's autoenrollment GPO, linked at the domain root, already reaches every new member, so no other enrollment policy is created."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): the course 1.11 KDS root key, the existing gMSAs, and the role groups\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01 { Get-KdsRootKey | Select-Object KeyId, EffectiveTime }   # one key, from course 1.11\nGet-ADServiceAccount -Filter * | Select-Object Name, Enabled   # course 1.11's three; this course's arrive with their hosts\nGet-ADGroup -SearchBase 'OU=Groups,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test' -Filter * | Select-Object Name"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The register carries every VLAN 40 service row with its ID and owner — the SQL path restricted to APPS01 alone (F22), the WEB01-to-SQL01 deny (F14), and WEB01's repository, syslog and time rows — the course 1.11 KDS root key is the forest's only one, the role groups exist, and each server certificate is planned for the host that will request it."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the service-path rows, the identity and group design, the KDS root key check, and the certificate plan."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nMicrosoft · Delegated Managed Service Accounts overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/delegated-managed-service-accounts/delegated-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nMicrosoft · Create a Key Distribution Service (KDS) root key\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/create-the-key-distribution-services-kds-root-key")], question: "Who may reach SQL01 on 1433?", answers: ["Any domain member", "WEB01 and APPS01", "Only APPS01 — the application tier is the database's whole audience"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Browsers talk to the web tier, the web tier calls the application API, and only the application tier queries the database. The register encodes that chain — including an explicit deny from WEB01 to SQL01 — and denies shortcuts around it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why gMSAs instead of a user account named svc-sql?", ["The directory manages and rotates the password itself — no human knows it, nothing expires unnoticed", "gMSAs run queries faster", "Password accounts cannot start services"], 0, "Service account passwords are a classic standing weakness. Managed accounts remove the password from human hands entirely."), QuizQuestion("Why grant access to role groups rather than people?", ["Groups are alphabetical", "Access follows the job: membership changes are one record, and permissions never accumulate on individuals", "Permissions cannot hold user names"], 1, "Role-based access is what makes 'who can touch this data' answerable from group membership instead of archaeology.")], recap: ["Every path is a row; the database hears only SBD-APPS01.", "Service identities are managed accounts, not passwords.", "Access belongs to roles; each certificate is planned for its own host."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 builds the file service the fleet will actually use.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#design", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:fileserver", title: "Build SBD-FS01 with least-privilege shares", minutes: 30, summary: "The file server is where access control meets daily life. Build the shares against the role groups, require SMB signing on the server instead of trusting client defaults, and make 'who can read this' a one-lookup question.", objectives: ["Build SBD-FS01 in its place and create the shares with role-group permissions and no individual grants.", "Confirm the SMB signing and encryption posture end to end.", "Verify access and denial per role from the fleet."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.15: the domain with its DNS, time and hardened OUs, the course 1.6 PKI and its autoenrollment GPO, DHCP and the admin seats, SBD-REPO01 and its Linux client package, the logging pipeline with course 1.11's KDS root key, SBD-SPLUNK01 as the Linux server pattern, the fleet, the update rings, and the SBD-MGMT01 inventory.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts as virtual machines on SBD-HV05: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20) and SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30) on Windows Server, and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40) on the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with Apache, to be joined to sbd.test with SSSD as SBD-SPLUNK01 was in course 1.12.\n\nHave the service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, course 1.9's client package and Linux assessment set, and course 1.4's SCC media within reach — every service here gets a documented path, a real certificate and a first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-FS01 as a Windows Server virtual machine at 10.0.40.10/24, with the core's VLAN 40 interface as gateway (10.0.40.1 in this sample), both domain controllers for DNS, a separate data volume for the shares, and the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off. Join it straight into the Servers OU, so course 1.5's STIG policies, course 1.8's tier policies, course 1.10's event-forwarding GPO and course 1.14's Ring 3 update policy apply from its first boot, and check that it takes its time from a domain controller. Lessons 3 and 4 build SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 the same way."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Create the planned shares, granting NTFS permissions to the lesson-1 role groups only — share permissions stay simple, the filesystem carries the detail, and no named human appears in an access control list (ACL)."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Enable access-based enumeration so users see only what their roles permit — the quiet UX of least privilege."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Confirm the SMB posture. The newest Windows client release (Pro, Enterprise, and Education) requires SMB signing for both inbound and outbound connections, but the newest Windows Server release requires it only for its own outbound connections — so require signing on SBD-FS01 as well (RequireSecuritySignature in its SMB server configuration), and opt the sensitive share into SMB encryption. Verify both on a live connection from a fleet machine rather than assuming the defaults."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Test as members of two different roles from fleet machines: each sees and opens exactly its shares, and the other's are invisible and, opened by path, denied. Turn on Audit Detailed File Share (failure) for SBD-FS01 in a GPO security-filtered to it, recorded as a documented addition as course 1.11 did for SBD-AUDIT01, so each refusal is event 5145 in its Security log; lesson 5 sends that event to the collector."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# In a Tier 1 session on SBD-FS01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01\n([adsisearcher]\"(&(objectCategory=computer)(name=\$env:COMPUTERNAME))\").FindOne().Path   # ...OU=Servers,OU=SBD,DC=sbd,DC=test\nw32tm /query /source   # a domain controller, never the local clock\nGet-SmbShare | Select-Object Name, Path, FolderEnumerationMode, EncryptData\nGet-SmbServerConfiguration | Select-Object RequireSecuritySignature   # expected True\nicacls D:\\Shares\\Projects | Select-String 'SBD'\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"Detailed File Share\"   # Failure\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=5145} -MaxEvents 5 | Select-Object TimeCreated, Message\n# On the fleet machine, during the test session\nGet-SmbConnection | Where-Object ServerName -like 'SBD-FS01*' | Select-Object ShareName, Dialect, Signed, Encrypted"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "SBD-FS01 sits in the Servers OU with time from a domain controller, shares grant to role groups only, FS01 requires signing, a live connection from the fleet shows Signed True — or, on the encrypted share, Encrypted True, where Signed reads False because encryption, which also protects integrity, takes the place of signing — and the two-role test proves both the access and the invisible-denied behavior, with each refusal recorded as event 5145."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the OU placement and time source, the share and NTFS permission reports, FS01's signing setting and the live connection's signing/encryption output, and the two-role test record with its 5145 events."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · SMB security enhancements\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-security\n\nMicrosoft · SMB signing\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing\n\nMicrosoft · Get-SmbConnection\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/smbshare/get-smbconnection\n\nMicrosoft · MSFT_SmbConnection class (Signed and Encrypted)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/desktop/smb/msft-smbconnection\n\nMicrosoft · What's new in Windows Server (newest release)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/whats-new-windows-server-2025\n\nMicrosoft · Advanced audit policy configuration (File System, Detailed File Share)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/plan/security-best-practices/advanced-audit-policy-configuration")], question: "Where does the fine-grained permission detail live?", answers: ["In the share permissions", "On the NTFS filesystem, granted to role groups — shares stay simple on top", "In a spreadsheet"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The classic split: simple share grants, precise filesystem ACLs, and roles everywhere — auditable at a glance.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What does the live Get-SmbConnection check prove that settings review doesn't?", ["That the actual connection negotiated signing or encryption — posture in effect, not posture on paper", "That the share has files", "Nothing extra"], 0, "Defaults differ by edition and direction — the newest Windows Server release requires signing only for its own outbound connections — so the evidence standard of this whole path is observed behavior: inspect the connection itself."), QuizQuestion("A user can't see a share their colleague uses. What is that?", ["A replication failure", "A help desk ticket to grant access immediately", "Access-based enumeration doing its job — visibility follows role membership"], 2, "Invisible-unless-permitted is the designed behavior; the role group, not an exception, is the path to access.")], recap: ["Roles own the ACLs; humans own role memberships.", "Signing and encryption are verified on a live connection.", "Both the access and the denial are on record."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 builds the database the applications will depend on.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#fileserver", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:database", title: "Build SBD-SQL01 behind its applications", minutes: 30, summary: "The database runs as a managed identity, encrypts its connections with a real certificate, and answers exactly one caller, SBD-APPS01 — the narrowest audience on the network, by design.", objectives: ["Build SBD-SQL01 and run the database service under its gMSA.", "Bind the SBD Server Authentication certificate and force encrypted connections.", "Prove the one-caller rule: SBD-APPS01 connects; a VLAN 40 neighbour and the fleet are refused."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.15: the domain with its DNS, time and hardened OUs, the course 1.6 PKI and its autoenrollment GPO, DHCP and the admin seats, SBD-REPO01 and its Linux client package, the logging pipeline with course 1.11's KDS root key, SBD-SPLUNK01 as the Linux server pattern, the fleet, the update rings, and the SBD-MGMT01 inventory.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts as virtual machines on SBD-HV05: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20) and SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30) on Windows Server, and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40) on the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with Apache, to be joined to sbd.test with SSSD as SBD-SPLUNK01 was in course 1.12.\n\nHave the service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, course 1.9's client package and Linux assessment set, and course 1.4's SCC media within reach — every service here gets a documented path, a real certificate and a first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Build SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20) and the host of its one caller, SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30), as SBD-FS01 was built in lesson 2. Then, from AdminJumpBox01, create one gMSA per service, each allowed to one computer as course 1.11 did: SBD-SQL\$ for SBD-SQL01\$ and SBD-Apps\$ for SBD-APPS01\$ (sample names). Install the database engine with SBD\\SBD-SQL\$ as its service account and no password — the install itself demonstrates the managed account working where a password account used to live."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Add SBD-SQL01 to the SBD TLS Servers group, then on SBD-SQL01 request its SBD Server Authentication certificate through the course 1.6 process, with SBD-SQL01.sbd.test in the subject alternative name. Give the gMSA read on the certificate's private key — without it the service won't start with the certificate — select the certificate in SQL Server Configuration Manager, set Force Encryption to Yes, and restart the service; the chain validates against pki.sbd.test like every other certificate on this network."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Create the application databases with role-based database access for one login only, SBD\\SBD-Apps\$, the identity SBD-APPS01's services run as in lesson 4 — least privilege continues inside the engine, not just at its port. Record the databases and the logins they depend on: course 1.19 backs them up, so this course sets up no backup job."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Prove the audience: allow TCP 1433 in SBD-SQL01's host firewall from 10.0.40.30 alone (F22), and turn on its dropped-packet logging if it is off. A connection from APPS01 succeeds. The same attempt from SBD-FS01 is refused by SBD-SQL01's host firewall — the two share VLAN 40, so the router never sees it, which is exactly how SBD-WEB01 will be refused in lesson 4 — and one from a fleet machine dies at the router with a deny-log entry."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# From AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0): one gMSA per service, each allowed to one computer\nNew-ADServiceAccount SBD-SQL -DNSHostName SBD-SQL.sbd.test -PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword 'SBD-SQL01\$'\nNew-ADServiceAccount SBD-Apps -DNSHostName SBD-Apps.sbd.test -PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword 'SBD-APPS01\$'\n# In a Tier 1 session on SBD-SQL01 from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: identity, encryption, certificate, firewall scope\nGet-CimInstance Win32_Service -Filter \"Name='MSSQLSERVER'\" | Select-Object Name, StartName, State   # StartName SBD\\SBD-SQL\$\nGet-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Microsoft SQL Server\\*\\MSSQLServer\\SuperSocketNetLib' | Select-Object ForceEncryption, Certificate   # 1 and the thumbprint\n\$cer = Get-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My | Where-Object Subject -like '*SBD-SQL01*' | Export-Certificate -FilePath \"\$env:TEMP\\sql01.cer\"\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch \$cer.FullName   # chain and revocation from pki.sbd.test\nGet-NetFirewallPortFilter | Where-Object LocalPort -eq 1433 | Get-NetFirewallRule | Where-Object { \$_.Enabled -eq 'True' -and \$_.Direction -eq 'Inbound' } | Get-NetFirewallAddressFilter | Select-Object RemoteAddress   # 10.0.40.30 only\n# From SBD-APPS01: expected TcpTestSucceeded True. From SBD-FS01 and from a fleet machine: expected False.\nTest-NetConnection SBD-SQL01.sbd.test -Port 1433"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The service runs as the gMSA, Force Encryption is on with the issued certificate bound and its chain validating, the database role admits SBD\\SBD-Apps\$ alone, TCP 1433 is open to 10.0.40.30 only, APPS01 reaches it, and both other attempts fail with a record: SBD-FS01's at SQL01's host firewall, the fleet's at the router."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the service identity proof, the encryption setting, certificate binding and urlfetch output, the database role grants, the firewall scope, and APPS01's successful connection paired with both refusals and their log entries."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Securing SQL Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/securing-sql-server\n\nMicrosoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nMicrosoft · Encrypt connections to SQL Server by importing a certificate\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/database-engine/configure-windows/configure-sql-server-encryption\n\nMicrosoft · Configure Windows Firewall rules with Group Policy\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/windows-firewall/configure")], question: "What demonstrates the gMSA actually working?", answers: ["The service running under it — a production role started by an identity no human holds a password for", "A password in the evidence folder", "The account's description field"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The running service is the proof: managed identity in use, with rotation handled by the directory.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why does the database get a certificate at all?", ["Certificates speed up queries", "Connection encryption with a validating internal chain — the PKI path's work, consumed by a real service", "SQL refuses to install without one"], 1, "This is the payoff moment for course 1.6: a business service encrypting with a certificate the lab issued, published, and can revoke."), QuizQuestion("What is the complete evidence for the one-caller rule?", ["The register row alone", "A diagram of VLAN 40", "APPS01's successful connection plus the refused attempts from a VLAN 40 neighbour and a fleet machine, each with its log entry"], 2, "Same standard as every boundary check: the permit shown working and the deny shown refusing, each with its record."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-WEB01's attempt on SQL01 never reach the router?", ["The router is offline during the test", "WEB01 and SQL01 share VLAN 40, so the traffic is switched inside the VLAN and SQL01's host firewall has to refuse it", "SQL Server only accepts UDP"], 1, "Traffic between two hosts on the same VLAN never crosses the routed boundary. That is why the VLAN 40 rows are enforced by each server's host firewall, and why the WEB01-to-SQL01 deny is written down explicitly.")], recap: ["A managed identity runs the engine; no password exists to leak.", "Connections encrypt under the lab's own validated chain.", "One caller in: SBD-APPS01. Its VLAN 40 neighbours are refused at SQL01's host firewall and the fleet at the router."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 stands up the services users actually touch.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#database", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:apps-web", title: "Build SBD-APPS01 and SBD-WEB01", minutes: 40, summary: "The application server and the internal web server complete the dependency chain: fleet to web, web to application, application to database — each hop a register row, the web tier hardened against its Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA) guidance, and HTTPS served from the lab's own trust.", objectives: ["Stand up SBD-APPS01's licensing service and application API on their registered paths.", "Build the domain-joined Linux Apache server the way courses 1.9 and 1.12 built theirs, hardened per its DISA Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) and serving HTTPS with the internal certificate.", "Prove the full chain: fleet → web → application → database, with the web-to-database shortcut refused."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.15: the domain with its DNS, time and hardened OUs, the course 1.6 PKI and its autoenrollment GPO, DHCP and the admin seats, SBD-REPO01 and its Linux client package, the logging pipeline with course 1.11's KDS root key, SBD-SPLUNK01 as the Linux server pattern, the fleet, the update rings, and the SBD-MGMT01 inventory.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts as virtual machines on SBD-HV05: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20) and SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30) on Windows Server, and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40) on the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with Apache, to be joined to sbd.test with SSSD as SBD-SPLUNK01 was in course 1.12.\n\nHave the service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, course 1.9's client package and Linux assessment set, and course 1.4's SCC media within reach — every service here gets a documented path, a real certificate and a first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "On SBD-APPS01, built in lesson 3, and added to the SBD TLS Servers group, request its SBD Server Authentication certificate through the course 1.6 process and run both of its services as SBD\\SBD-Apps\$, or record an exception where the product can't use a gMSA: the licensing service, verified from the fleet on its registered vendor port (F12), and the application API on HTTPS 443 (F21), with its host firewall admitting the API from SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40) alone. APPS01 is the network's one SQL consumer — and the template for any future service: identity, row, evidence."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Build SBD-WEB01 at 10.0.40.40/24 from hashed install media of the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with both domain controllers for DNS and the hypervisor's time synchronization turned off. Before anything else, install course 1.3's chrony file (both domain controllers, maxdistance 16) and confirm a controller is selected with a small, steady offset; then apply course 1.9's client package, checked against its hashes, so SBD-REPO01 is its only package source (F19), and update from the current snapshot. Place the course 1.6 root in its system trust store by hand (/etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors, then update-ca-trust), its SHA-256 checked against course 1.6's record, as course 1.9 did for SBD-REPO01."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Join sbd.test with realm join into the Servers OU, as SBD-SPLUNK01 was joined in course 1.12, permit sign-in only to SBD Tier 1 Admins with realm permit -g, and give that group sudo through a drop-in file. Make the host firewall the register's copy: HTTPS 443 from the fleet (F13) and SSH from 10.0.60.20 alone. Then send its syslog over TLS to SBD-SYSLOG01 (F07) as course 1.10 enrolled SBD-REPO01: rsyslog-gnutls from the snapshot, the collector's certificate name checked, and SBD-WEB01 added to the collector's TCP 6514 sender list."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Request SBD-WEB01's SBD Server Authentication certificate through the course 1.6 process, with the key and request made on the host with openssl and SBD-WEB01.sbd.test in both subject and subject alternative name, and the key readable by root alone. Serve the internal application over HTTPS with its chain, and harden Apache against the DISA Apache Server 2.4 UNIX STIGs that course 1.9 staged — the 2,048-bit key floor from course 1.6's templates applies here as everywhere. Export the hardened configuration and hash it as the change record (course 1.19 backs up SBD-APPS01's and SBD-WEB01's configuration and certificates); lesson 5 measures the host."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Prove the chain from a fleet machine: the HTTPS page loads with a validating certificate, and its data travels fleet → WEB01 → APPS01 → SQL01, every hop a register row — on SBD-SQL01, sys.dm_exec_connections shows APPS01's session (10.0.40.30) with encrypt_option TRUE. Then prove the shortcuts stay shut: a direct attempt from SBD-WEB01 to SQL01 on 1433 is refused by SQL01's host firewall, and the fleet still cannot touch 1433 at all — three courses of design visible in one browser request."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-WEB01 (Tier 1 SSH session from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01): time, packages, domain, firewall, logging, web\nchronyc sources -v; chronyc tracking   # ^* one controller, ^+ or ^- the other\ndnf repolist --all   # expected: only the sbd-repo01 repositories enabled\nrealm list   # sbd.test, kerberos-member, permitted group SBD Tier 1 Admins\nsudo firewall-cmd --get-active-zones\nopenssl s_client -connect SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test:6514 -verify_hostname SBD-SYSLOG01.sbd.test </dev/null 2>/dev/null | grep 'Verify return code'   # 0 (ok): the SBD root is trusted\nlogger -t sbd-web-proof 'SBD-WEB01 syslog over TLS test'\nsudo apachectl -S   # one HTTPS site on 10.0.40.40:443\ncurl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://SBD-APPS01.sbd.test/   # use your application's health path\ntimeout 5 bash -c '</dev/tcp/SBD-SQL01.sbd.test/1433' && echo open || echo 'refused or timed out'   # expected: refused or timed out\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: the issued certificate's chain and revocation\ncertutil -verify -urlfetch C:\\Evidence\\business-services\\web01.cer\n# From a fleet machine: the page answers, the database does not\nTest-NetConnection SBD-WEB01.sbd.test -Port 443\nTest-NetConnection SBD-SQL01.sbd.test -Port 1433   # expected: False\n# On SBD-SQL01, in a query: APPS01's live session is encrypted\n# SELECT client_net_address, encrypt_option, auth_scheme FROM sys.dm_exec_connections WHERE client_net_address = '10.0.40.30'"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The licensing path and the application API work as registered under the managed identity; SBD-WEB01 takes its time from the controllers and its packages only from SBD-REPO01, trusts the SBD root, is domain-joined with SSSD in the Servers OU, and sends its syslog to the collector over TLS; the Apache host serves HTTPS with a validating internal chain from a hardened, hashed configuration; and the end-to-end request proves fleet → web → application → database, encrypted to the database, with both shortcuts denied."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep APPS01's identity and path proof, WEB01's time, repository, trust, domain-join and syslog checks, its hardened config export with hashes, the certificate validation output, the end-to-end chain test with SQL01's connection record, and the refused WEB01-to-SQL01 attempt."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Securing SQL Server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/securing-sql-server\n\nMicrosoft · Group managed service accounts\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts/group-managed-service-accounts-overview\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nRed Hat · Integrating RHEL systems directly with Windows Active Directory\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/integrating_rhel_systems_directly_with_windows_active_directory/index\n\nchrony · chrony.conf(5)\nhttps://chrony-project.org/doc/latest/chrony.conf.html\n\nRed Hat · Using shared system certificates\nhttps://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/securing_networks/using-shared-system-certificates_securing-networks\n\nfirewalld · documentation\nhttps://firewalld.org/documentation/\n\nApache HTTP Server 2.4 · Security tips\nhttps://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/misc/security_tips.html")], question: "Where do WEB01's packages come from?", answers: ["Any public mirror that responds", "A USB stick from home", "SBD-REPO01 — the one approved source course 1.9 built for Linux software (F19)"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The one-source rule follows software wherever it runs; the web tier installs only what crossed the boundary through the repository, on the row lesson 1 registered for it.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What is the Apache host's assessable record?", ["A screenshot of the home page", "An SCC for Linux baseline with the distribution's STIG benchmark, plus the Apache STIG items answered in a STIG Viewer checklist and the hashed configuration", "Its uptime counter"], 1, "Linux servers get SCAP automation too: course 1.9 staged SCC for Linux and the distribution's benchmark once, so every Linux server is scanned the same way. What a scanner can't read, such as most of the Apache STIG, a person answers with evidence, and the configuration's hash shows later whether it changed."), QuizQuestion("What does one successful page load from the fleet actually demonstrate?", ["The whole designed chain — the registered fleet-to-web path, validating internal HTTPS, the web tier's call to the application API, and the application tier's registered path into the database", "Only that Apache started", "That the fleet can reach the database directly"], 0, "The request traverses three register rows (fleet to WEB01, WEB01 to APPS01, APPS01 to SQL01) and the PKI in one go — which is why it is the lesson's closing test."), QuizQuestion("SBD-WEB01 tries SQL01 directly on 1433. What happens, and why?", ["It connects, because both servers sit in VLAN 40", "SQL01's host firewall refuses it: WEB01 has no database dependency, and APPS01 is the only registered SQL consumer", "The router drops it at the core"], 1, "The web tier reaches data only through the application API. The explicit WEB01-to-SQL01 deny, enforced on SQL01 because both servers share a VLAN, is what keeps a compromised web server from going straight for the database.")], recap: ["APPS01 serves licensing and the application API; WEB01 joins the domain like the other Linux servers.", "The web tier is built the Linux way — chrony, SBD-REPO01, the SBD root by hand — hardened to its guidance and serving the lab's own trust.", "One page load proves fleet → web → application → database; the shortcuts stay shut."], nextStep: "The final lesson enrols the new servers, baselines them, and reconciles the register against reality one last time.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#apps-web", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:business-services:prove-scan", title: "Reconcile, scan, and hand off", minutes: 30, summary: "The business services close the way every build course has: the register reconciled against live behavior, the new systems baselined, the inventory updated — and for the first time, the network is functionally complete.", objectives: ["Enrol the new servers and reconcile the full register against observed permits and denies.", "Baseline the four service hosts with the tool each platform takes.", "Update the living records and write the course's handoff."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.15: the domain with its DNS, time and hardened OUs, the course 1.6 PKI and its autoenrollment GPO, DHCP and the admin seats, SBD-REPO01 and its Linux client package, the logging pipeline with course 1.11's KDS root key, SBD-SPLUNK01 as the Linux server pattern, the fleet, the update rings, and the SBD-MGMT01 inventory.\n\nProvision the VLAN 40 hosts as virtual machines on SBD-HV05: SBD-FS01 (10.0.40.10), SBD-SQL01 (10.0.40.20) and SBD-APPS01 (10.0.40.30) on Windows Server, and SBD-WEB01 (10.0.40.40) on the Red Hat Enterprise Linux–family release course 1.9 chose, with Apache, to be joined to sbd.test with SSSD as SBD-SPLUNK01 was in course 1.12.\n\nHave the service-path register, the course 1.6 SBD Server Authentication template, course 1.9's client package and Linux assessment set, and course 1.4's SCC media within reach — every service here gets a documented path, a real certificate and a first baseline."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The users finally get what the network exists for: files, licensing, and an internal web application with its application tier and database. The model's rule governs the whole course — permit only documented business service paths — so every service arrives with its register rows, its service identity, and its certificate, and nothing reaches it any other way."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Enrol the three Windows servers in the record: from AdminJumpBox01, add SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 to SBD Event Sources (course 1.10). On the collector, in a Tier 1 session, widen the WinRM 5985 scope to 10.0.40.10, 10.0.40.20 and 10.0.40.30 (F17) and add a query to SBD-ForwardedSecurity for SBD-FS01's event 5145, filtered to that computer as course 1.11 did for SBD-AUDIT01. Restart the three servers and confirm their events reach ForwardedEvents; SBD-WEB01 already sends its syslog over TLS (lesson 4)."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Run the full-register reconciliation one more time: every business-service row proven from its real source, every adjacent deny probed and logged, including WEB01 to SQL01 — the VLAN 40 rows join the register's evidence beside the earlier courses' proofs."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Take the first baselines. SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 get course 1.4's SCC and Windows Server benchmark versions, run locally in Tier 1 sessions from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 as course 1.14 did for SBD-WSUS01, plus DISA's SQL Server STIGs for the instance, staged across the air gap as course 1.4 taught and answered in a STIG Viewer checklist. SBD-WEB01 gets SCC for Linux and the distribution benchmark from course 1.9's set in C:\\Staging\\DISA\\linux, scanned the course 1.9 way, with the Apache STIG items in its checklist. Classify every open item as course 1.4 taught — fix by GPO, deviation candidate or POA&M candidate, with fix now (no GPO applies to Linux) for SBD-WEB01. Credentialed vulnerability scanning arrives with course 1.17, coming soon."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Update the living records: the four hosts' planned rows in the SBD-MGMT01 inventory turn active through its change log (course 1.15), with their identities, certificates, rings and baselines; the new rows' owners go into the register; and any service-hardening deviations go to the owner of the exception record, C:\\Evidence\\exception-record.csv on AdminJumpBox01."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Write the handoff: every modeled system that isn't marked coming soon is now built, watched, patched, and evidenced. This course sets up no backups: course 1.19 defines and schedules them for the VLAN 40 servers — SBD-FS01's share data with its NTFS permissions, SBD-SQL01's databases and the logins they depend on, and SBD-APPS01's and SBD-WEB01's configuration and certificates — and proves the network can come back from the dead; then path 2 takes the whole record to authorization."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-SYSLOG01 (Tier 1 session): the new sources report\nwecutil gr SBD-ForwardedSecurity   # SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01: Active\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='ForwardedEvents'; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddHours(-1)} | Group-Object MachineName | Select-Object Name, Count\nSelect-String -Path D:\\Logs\\Syslog\\*\\*.log -Pattern 'sbd-web-proof' | Select-Object -First 1\n# On SBD-ADMIN-SRV01: collect the course's records and hash them\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\business-services -Recurse -File | Select-Object FullName, LastWriteTime\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\business-services -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\business-services-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The three Windows servers forward their events and SBD-WEB01 its syslog, the register covers every VLAN 40 row with paired permit/deny proof, all four hosts carry first baselines with classified findings, and inventory, register, and exception record all reflect the finished services."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: the enrolment proof, the reconciliation record, the baselines and checklists with classified findings, the updated living records, and the handoff note to course 1.19."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "NIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final\n\nDISA · Security Content Automation Protocol (SCC and SCAP benchmarks)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/SCAP/\n\nDISA · SRG/STIG tools (SCC and STIG Viewer)\nhttps://www.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools\n\nMicrosoft · Get-FileHash\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/get-filehash")], question: "Why re-run the whole register reconciliation now?", answers: ["The register deleted itself", "Four services changed what the boundary must permit — the reconciliation proves intent and behavior still match after the biggest change yet", "Reconciliations are decorative"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Each course's additions could silently break the last course's guarantees. The periodic full reconciliation is how the register stays true.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What distinguishes WEB01's closing evidence from FS01's?", ["WEB01 has none", "FS01 is exempt from scans", "Only the instrument: FS01 is scanned with SCC and the Windows Server benchmark, WEB01 with SCC for Linux and its distribution's benchmark plus the Apache checklist — same standard"], 2, "The evidence standard is constant; the tool varies by platform. Course 1.9 staged the Linux set once, so every Linux server is measured the same way."), QuizQuestion("What remains before the authorization path begins?", ["Backing up and proving recovery — course 1.19, which backs up the VLAN 40 servers with everything else — so the Body of Evidence includes the network coming back, not just running", "Nothing at all", "Rebuilding the domain controllers"], 0, "A network that runs but cannot recover is half a story. The last build course makes restoration a demonstrated fact.")], recap: ["Register and reality reconcile across the finished network.", "Every service host carries its platform-appropriate evidence.", "One course remains: proving the network can be brought back."], nextStep: "That completes course 1.18. Course 1.19 closes the Build path by backing up and proving recovery — directory, CA, policy, logs, and the VLAN 40 services built here — before authorization begins.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/business-services#prove-scan", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "prove-recovery", number: "1.19", title: "Prove you can recover", subtitle: "Backups with custody, one rehearsed restore & the closing record", category: "Network build", description: "Close the Build path by proving the network can come back: every backup in the network defined and scheduled in one place — the directory, the CA under ceremony-grade custody, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log archive, and the living records — in one hashed catalog, a timed restore rehearsal in isolation, and the recovery record that completes the Body of Evidence.", icon: "directory", published: true, studyTime: "6 guided lessons · Self-paced", topics: 6,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-guide", title: "Prove you can recover · Interactive guide", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Backups with custody, one rehearsed restore & the closing record", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "prove-recovery-package", title: "Prove you can recover · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word), checklist, and verification commands.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery/SecureByDesign-prove-recovery-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:requirements", title: "Decide what recovery must prove", minutes: 25, summary: "Recovery starts as a list of promises: which systems and records must be restorable, how old a restored copy may be, how long restoration may take, and the one schedule that says how each is backed up — written as commitments the rest of the course exists to keep.", objectives: ["Name the recovery set: what must come back, in what order.", "Set the recovery point and time objectives per item.", "Write the backup schedule: every backup in the network, defined in one place.", "Decide where backups live and who can touch them."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.18 (courses 1.16 and 1.17 are coming soon and aren't needed): the full network, the VLAN 40 business services, their records, the protected log archive, and the living inventory all exist.\n\nProvision the backup storage this course schedules into: a protected backup volume (E: in this sample) on every Windows server in the recovery set — both domain controllers, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 — never one of the volumes being backed up and written only by the backup jobs, and a separate volume mounted at /srv/backup, readable by root alone, on SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01. Earlier courses set up no backup jobs; this course defines and schedules every one. Also provide an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the sealed Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords set in course 1.1 and the course 1.6 ceremony record for SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course is the one place every backup in the network is defined and scheduled — the directory, both CAs, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log record, and the living records — then proves one real restore in isolation and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Write the recovery set in dependency order — the order the path built it: the directory on both controllers first, because everything trusts it; then the certificate authorities (SBD-SUBCA01's database and key, and SBD-ROOTCA01's course 1.6 ceremony backup); Group Policy; SBD-DHCP01 (course 1.7); the boundary's records (the service-path register and the core router's configuration); SBD-REPO01's snapshot catalog and snapshots (course 1.9); SBD-SPLUNK01's configuration (course 1.12); the log archive and its catalog on SBD-AUDIT01 (course 1.11); the inventory on SBD-MGMT01 (course 1.15); the VLAN 40 business services (course 1.18) — SBD-FS01's share data with its NTFS permissions, SBD-SQL01's databases and the logins they depend on, SBD-APPS01's and SBD-WEB01's configuration and certificates; and the exception record."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Set the two numbers per item: how much history may be lost (recovery point) and how long restoration may take (recovery time) — lab-honest values you will actually measure against in lesson 5, not aspirations. A directory backup older than the forest's tombstone lifetime (180 days by default) can't be restored at all, so the directory's recovery point stays well inside it."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Write the backup schedule, the one place every backup in this network is defined: for each item, what is captured, the tool, when it runs, where it lands, and who restores it. No earlier course scheduled a backup; the one build-time copy is SBD-ROOTCA01's, taken inside the course 1.6 ceremony, and the schedule accounts for it rather than repeating it. Lessons 2 and 3 put each row into effect."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Decide backup placement: each protected backup volume is written only by its backup jobs and readable by the recovery role, and custodians of the matching tier carry copies to the offline backup cabinet on media — the air-gapped network's version of an off-site copy. There is no backup server and no network path to the cabinet."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Confirm the standing custody items: the DSRM passwords set in course 1.1 are sealed and findable, and SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup from the course 1.6 ceremony is accounted for — recovery depends on secrets that must exist before the bad day."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On each Windows server in the recovery set: capture the current protection state before this course changes it.\nwbadmin get versions\nGet-Volume -DriveLetter E | Select-Object DriveLetter, FileSystemLabel, SizeRemaining   # the protected backup volume\n# The tombstone lifetime bounds the directory's recovery point (empty means the 180-day default).\n(Get-ADObject \"CN=Directory Service,CN=Windows NT,CN=Services,\$((Get-ADRootDSE).configurationNamingContext)\" -Properties tombstoneLifetime).tombstoneLifetime"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The recovery set is ordered by dependency with point and time objectives per item, the backup schedule defines every backup in one place, the placement and access rules are written, and the DSRM and root-key custody records check out."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the recovery requirements sheet, the objectives table, the backup schedule, the placement and access decisions, and the custody verification notes."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CP family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Why does the directory lead the recovery order?", answers: ["It has the most data", "Everything else authenticates against it — services restored before identity have nothing to trust", "Alphabetical order"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Recovery order mirrors build order for the same reason: the dependency chain starts at identity.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("What makes a recovery time objective honest?", ["Making it very small", "Leaving it unstated", "Setting a value you will actually measure a rehearsal against"], 2, "Lesson 5 times a real restore. An objective nobody measures is marketing, not a commitment."), QuizQuestion("What is the offline media copy for?", ["Surviving a failure that takes the online backup with it — the air-gapped answer to off-site", "Faster restores", "Decoration for the evidence binder"], 0, "Replication and online backups share failure modes with what they protect. One custody-tracked copy stands apart."), QuizQuestion("What does this course do about the offline root's backup?", ["Copies it onto the network for convenience", "Ignores it", "Accounts for the ceremony backup in the schedule and verifies its custody — it already exists, offline, and stays that way"], 2, "The root's backup is the one build-time copy, taken inside the course 1.6 ceremony. Its protection is its isolation, so this course checks the promise rather than breaking it with a networked copy.")], recap: ["The recovery set is ordered by dependency, directory first, VLAN 40 included.", "Point and time objectives exist to be measured, not admired.", "One schedule defines every backup in the network.", "Backups get a protected home plus one offline, custody-tracked copy."], nextStep: "Lesson 2 puts the Tier 0 rows of the schedule into effect.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#requirements", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:directory", title: "Back up Tier 0: directory, CA, and policy", minutes: 35, summary: "The Tier 0 rows of the schedule: system state from both controllers, the issuing CA under ceremony-grade protection, and the whole Group Policy estate with its links — plus the quiet feature that handles the most common 'disaster' of all: the Recycle Bin for deleted directory objects.", objectives: ["Schedule system state and critical-volume backups on both domain controllers, and verify and catalog them.", "Back up the issuing CA's database, key, and configuration under ceremony-grade protection.", "Schedule the Group Policy backup with its link export.", "Enable the Active Directory Recycle Bin for object-level recovery."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.18 (courses 1.16 and 1.17 are coming soon and aren't needed): the full network, the VLAN 40 business services, their records, the protected log archive, and the living inventory all exist.\n\nProvision the backup storage this course schedules into: a protected backup volume (E: in this sample) on every Windows server in the recovery set — both domain controllers, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 — never one of the volumes being backed up and written only by the backup jobs, and a separate volume mounted at /srv/backup, readable by root alone, on SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01. Earlier courses set up no backup jobs; this course defines and schedules every one. Also provide an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the sealed Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords set in course 1.1 and the course 1.6 ceremony record for SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course is the one place every backup in the network is defined and scheduled — the directory, both CAs, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log record, and the living records — then proves one real restore in isolation and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "In a Tier 0 session from AdminJumpBox01, schedule Windows Server Backup on SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02, each to its own protected backup volume, with the system state and all critical volumes (wbadmin's -systemState and -allCritical): the system state is what a directory restore in DSRM needs, and the critical volumes let the same backup rebuild a controller whose hardware is gone. Two controllers, two independent backups — replication is availability and was never recovery, as course 1.1 put it."), LessonSection("Step 2", "After the first run, verify each backup's completion, record its version identifier in the backup catalog (C:\\Evidence\\recovery on AdminJumpBox01), and hash the catalog — an unverified backup is a hope with a timestamp."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Back up the issuing CA. Schedule SBD-SUBCA01's own system state and critical-volume backup to its protected volume, so the server can be rebuilt; then take the CA's own backup — database and private key — with certutil to E:\\Backups\\CA, with its registry configuration exported beside it, on the cadence the schedule sets and after every CA key renewal. It prompts for the password that protects the key, so it is a Tier 0 task, never an unattended one. Never write it to C:\\Evidence: the private key is a secret, and secrets never go into evidence. Record custody and put only the hashes in evidence; SBD-ROOTCA01's ceremony backup is verified against its record, never duplicated or brought onto the network."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Schedule the Group Policy backup: a daily task on SBD-DC01, running as SYSTEM from a script folder only Tier 0 administrators can write, that runs Backup-GPO -All into a dated folder on E:\\Backups\\GPO and exports every link beside it — a restored GPO brings back its settings and permissions, not its links. That covers the whole estate, course 1.6's SBD Certificate Autoenrollment and course 1.8's tier logon GPOs included; the GPO reports and link exports courses 1.5 and 1.6 filed stay as their change records."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Enable the Active Directory Recycle Bin from the Tier 0 seat — a one-way, forest-level change run against the domain naming master (SBD-DC01) and recorded as the change it is — so a deleted user or OU comes back intact without touching system state. Objects deleted before it was enabled can come back only from a backup."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Write the restore decision tree: deleted object → Recycle Bin; corrupted DC on its own hardware → system state restore in DSRM (lesson 5's rehearsal); DC whose hardware is gone → full server (bare-metal) recovery from the critical-volumes backup; a damaged GPO → Restore-GPO from the dated set, then its links from the export; whole-forest loss → the Microsoft forest recovery guide, referenced and staged with the evidence rather than improvised on the day."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Tier 0 session from AdminJumpBox01: daily schedules on each controller and the issuing CA (E: is never a volume being backed up)\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01.sbd.test, SBD-DC02.sbd.test, SBD-SUBCA01.sbd.test { wbadmin enable backup -addtarget:E: -schedule:21:00 -systemState -allCritical -quiet }\nInvoke-Command SBD-DC01.sbd.test, SBD-DC02.sbd.test { wbadmin get versions }   # after the first run\n# On SBD-SUBCA01 (Tier 0 session): the CA's own backup, to its protected volume, never to the evidence folder\ncertutil -backup E:\\Backups\\CA   # prompts for the password that protects the exported key\nreg export HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\CertSvc\\Configuration E:\\Backups\\CA\\certsvc-config.reg\nGet-ChildItem E:\\Backups\\CA -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv E:\\Backups\\ca-backup-hashes.csv -NoTypeInformation   # only this list goes to C:\\Evidence\\recovery\n# On SBD-DC01: C:\\Scripts\\Backup-SbdGpo.ps1 (writable by Tier 0 administrators only) runs these lines, then the task runs it daily\n#   \$d = 'E:\\Backups\\GPO\\{0:yyyy-MM-dd}' -f (Get-Date); New-Item -ItemType Directory \$d -Force | Out-Null; Backup-GPO -All -Path \$d\n#   @('DC=sbd,DC=test') + (Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter *).DistinguishedName | ForEach-Object { (Get-GPInheritance -Target \$_).GpoLinks } | Select-Object Target, Order, DisplayName, Enabled, Enforced | Export-Csv \"\$d\\gpo-links.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\nRegister-ScheduledTask 'SBD GPO backup' -Action (New-ScheduledTaskAction -Execute powershell.exe -Argument '-NoProfile -File C:\\Scripts\\Backup-SbdGpo.ps1') -Trigger (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Daily -At 22:00) -User 'NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM' -RunLevel Highest\n# Back on AdminJumpBox01: the Recycle Bin, then the catalog\nEnable-ADOptionalFeature 'Recycle Bin Feature' -Scope ForestOrConfigurationSet -Target sbd.test -Server SBD-DC01.sbd.test\nGet-ADOptionalFeature -Filter {Name -like 'Recycle Bin*'} | Select-Object Name, EnabledScopes\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\recovery\\backup-catalog.txt"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Both DCs and SBD-SUBCA01 show scheduled, completed system state and critical-volume versions on their protected volumes; the CA backup and its configuration sit on SBD-SUBCA01's protected volume with key custody recorded and only hashes in evidence; the GPO task has written a dated set with its link export; the Recycle Bin reports an enabled scope; and the decision tree names the tool for each failure class."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the backup schedules and version listings, the CA backup's hash list and custody record (never the backup itself), the GPO task definition and its first set's hashes, the hashed catalog, the Recycle Bin enablement record, and the restore decision tree."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nMicrosoft · Wbadmin (Windows Server Backup)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin\n\nMicrosoft · wbadmin enable backup\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin-enable-backup\n\nMicrosoft · AD forest recovery: perform a full server recovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-perform-full-server-recovery\n\nMicrosoft · Enable-ADOptionalFeature (Active Directory Recycle Bin)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/activedirectory/enable-adoptionalfeature\n\nMicrosoft · Certutil reference (CA backup and restore)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil\n\nMicrosoft · Backup-GPO\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/grouppolicy/backup-gpo\n\nMicrosoft · Register-ScheduledTask\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/scheduledtasks/register-scheduledtask")], question: "Both DCs replicate — why back up both?", answers: ["Replication copies mistakes as faithfully as data; independent backups are the history replication doesn't keep", "Backups run faster in pairs", "Only one actually holds the directory"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The course 1.1 teaching point, finally implemented: availability and recoverability are different capabilities with different tools.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("An administrator deletes an OU by mistake. First tool?", ["Rebuild the forest", "The Recycle Bin — object-level restore with attributes intact, no system state needed", "Ask users to recreate their accounts"], 1, "The decision tree's first branch: match the restore to the loss. Most disasters are small, and the small tool is already enabled."), QuizQuestion("Why does the CA key backup get ceremony-grade handling?", ["A copied issuing key is the power to mint trusted certificates — the backup is as sensitive as the CA itself", "Keys are large files", "certutil requires it"], 0, "Backups inherit the sensitivity of what they contain. The course 1.6 custody discipline applies to every copy of key material, forever."), QuizQuestion("Where does the issuing CA's backup go?", ["Into C:\\Evidence, so assessors can inspect it", "Onto SBD-SUBCA01's protected backup volume, with only its hashes and the custody record in evidence", "Onto pki.sbd.test, beside the CRLs"], 1, "The backup holds the CA's private key, and secrets never go into evidence. Evidence records that the backup exists and is intact: its hashes and its custody, not its contents."), QuizQuestion("What makes a backup more than a hope?", ["A long retention setting", "Its file size", "Verified completion, a recorded version, and a hashed catalog entry — and eventually a rehearsed restore"], 2, "The evidence standard applies to safety nets too: recorded, verifiable, and — in lesson 5 — actually exercised.")], recap: ["Two independent system state schedules, verified and cataloged.", "CA database, key, and configuration: backed up to the protected volume, custody recorded, only hashes in evidence.", "Every GPO is backed up daily with its links beside it.", "The Recycle Bin handles the everyday disaster in seconds; a decision tree matches each failure class to its tool."], nextStep: "Lesson 3 puts the member servers' rows into effect, VLAN 40 included.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#directory", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:trust-state", title: "Back up the member servers and records", minutes: 40, summary: "The rest of the schedule: SBD-DHCP01 and the inventory, SBD-REPO01 and SBD-SPLUNK01, and the business services — SBD-FS01's shares with their permissions, SBD-SQL01's databases with the logins they depend on, SBD-APPS01's and SBD-WEB01's configuration and certificates — then the log archive and the boundary's records, all in one hashed catalog.", objectives: ["Schedule the Tier 1 infrastructure backups — SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 — with the restore notes each needs.", "Schedule the VLAN 40 backups so permissions, logins and certificates come back with the data.", "Fold the log archive, the boundary records and the living records into the same catalog."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.18 (courses 1.16 and 1.17 are coming soon and aren't needed): the full network, the VLAN 40 business services, their records, the protected log archive, and the living inventory all exist.\n\nProvision the backup storage this course schedules into: a protected backup volume (E: in this sample) on every Windows server in the recovery set — both domain controllers, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 — never one of the volumes being backed up and written only by the backup jobs, and a separate volume mounted at /srv/backup, readable by root alone, on SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01. Earlier courses set up no backup jobs; this course defines and schedules every one. Also provide an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the sealed Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords set in course 1.1 and the course 1.6 ceremony record for SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course is the one place every backup in the network is defined and scheduled — the directory, both CAs, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log record, and the living records — then proves one real restore in isolation and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "In Tier 1 sessions from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, schedule SBD-DHCP01: a daily task, running as SYSTEM from a script folder only SBD Tier 1 Admins can write, takes Backup-DhcpServer into the default backup folder, copies it to a dated folder on E:\\Backups\\DHCP with an Export-DhcpServer of the configuration and leases, and hashes both. File course 1.7's restore note with it: after Restore-DhcpServer, set the DNS update credential again with Set-DhcpServerDnsCredential from the sealed record, because the backup doesn't carry it. On SBD-MGMT01, schedule Windows Server Backup of D:\\Inventory (course 1.15) to its E:."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Schedule SBD-FS01: Windows Server Backup of D:\\Shares to its E: every night. It keeps each file's NTFS permissions, and a file recovery restores them by default — never run it with -notRestoreAcl. Beside it, after every access change, save the readable record: icacls /save of the share tree's ACLs and an export of the share definitions (the LanmanServer Shares registry key, which also holds the share permissions), so permissions can be checked or reapplied without restoring data."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Schedule SBD-SQL01 inside the engine: a nightly SQL Server Agent job backs up each application database and the master, model and msdb system databases WITH CHECKSUM to E:\\Backups\\SQL, then checks each file with RESTORE VERIFYONLY. The engine writes the files as its gMSA, so grant SBD\\SBD-SQL\$ modify on that folder alone. master is where the logins live, msdb holds the Agent jobs, and the application databases' users map to the SBD\\SBD-Apps\$ login by its directory SID — so record the logins and each database's user mappings, and write the restore order: the instance and master (or the logins, recreated from the record) before the application databases. Use the simple recovery model unless your lesson 1 recovery point needs log backups, and a rebuilt SBD-SQL01 requests a new certificate through the course 1.6 process."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Schedule SBD-APPS01's Windows Server Backup with system state and critical volumes to its E:, so its services' configuration and its certificate, private key included, come back with the server — which is why the course 1.6 template never needs exportable keys. On SBD-WEB01, a root cron job in /etc/cron.d writes a dated tar archive of /etc/httpd, /etc/pki/tls (the certificate and its root-only key) and /etc/sssd, with SELinux contexts and ACLs, to /srv/backup with its SHA-256; SBD-REPO01 does the same for /srv/repo-records and the current snapshot each time it changes, so the previous one is already on media (course 1.9), and SBD-SPLUNK01 for /opt/splunk/etc (course 1.12 — the indexes are a working copy, and the record itself lives on SBD-AUDIT01). The WEB01 and Splunk archives hold keys, so they stay on protected media."), LessonSection("Step 5", "Account for the log archive: SBD-AUDIT01's hashed archives in D:\\LogArchive and their catalog (D:\\LogArchive\\Catalog\\catalog.csv) are themselves the record, so their backup is the audit custodian's offline copy on the schedule's cadence (lesson 4), and no job runs on that server."), LessonSection("Step 6", "Export the boundary and living records into the set: the service-path register, the exception record (C:\\Evidence\\exception-record.csv), this course's own sheets, and a read-only export of the core router's configuration taken with the router's own export function from AdminJumpBox01 — this course reads the router's configuration and changes nothing on it. Record every first run's hashes in C:\\Evidence\\recovery on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01. A recovered network without its records would pass no assessment."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# Tier 1 sessions from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01. On SBD-DHCP01, the task script (C:\\Scripts, writable by SBD Tier 1 Admins only):\n\$b = 'E:\\Backups\\DHCP\\{0:yyyy-MM-dd}' -f (Get-Date); New-Item -ItemType Directory \$b -Force | Out-Null; \$dir = Join-Path \$env:windir 'System32\\dhcp\\backup'\nBackup-DhcpServer -Path \$dir; Copy-Item \$dir (Join-Path \$b 'database') -Recurse; Export-DhcpServer -File (Join-Path \$b 'SBD-DHCP01.xml') -Leases\n# On SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01 and SBD-APPS01: one daily schedule each, to the local E:\nwbadmin enable backup -addtarget:E: -include:D:\\Inventory -schedule:22:00 -quiet   # SBD-MGMT01\nwbadmin enable backup -addtarget:E: -include:D:\\Shares -schedule:22:00 -quiet   # SBD-FS01; then, after each access change:\nicacls D:\\Shares\\* /save E:\\Backups\\FS01\\shares-acl.txt /t /c; reg export HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanmanServer\\Shares E:\\Backups\\FS01\\shares.reg /y\nwbadmin enable backup -addtarget:E: -schedule:22:30 -systemState -allCritical -quiet   # SBD-APPS01\n# On SBD-SQL01: the engine's identity may write the backup folder; the Agent job's step runs this T-SQL for each database\nicacls E:\\Backups\\SQL /grant 'SBD\\SBD-SQL\$:(OI)(CI)M'\n#   BACKUP DATABASE [master] TO DISK = N'E:\\Backups\\SQL\\master.bak' WITH CHECKSUM, INIT;   -- then model, msdb, each application database\n#   RESTORE VERIFYONLY FROM DISK = N'E:\\Backups\\SQL\\master.bak' WITH CHECKSUM;\nsqlcmd -S SBD-SQL01.sbd.test -E -Q \"SELECT name, type_desc, sid FROM sys.server_principals WHERE type IN ('U','G','S')\"   # the login record\n# On SBD-WEB01 (and the same pattern on SBD-REPO01 and SBD-SPLUNK01): the cron job's work, run once by hand\nsudo tar --selinux --acls --xattrs -czf /srv/backup/web01-\$(date +%F).tar.gz /etc/httpd /etc/pki/tls /etc/sssd\nsudo sha256sum /srv/backup/web01-*.tar.gz"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every member-server row in the schedule has run once and verified: SBD-DHCP01's set with its restore note, D:\\Inventory, SBD-FS01's shares with the ACL record and share definitions, SBD-SQL01's application and system databases verified with RESTORE VERIFYONLY and the login record filed, SBD-APPS01's full-server backup, and the dated archives on SBD-WEB01, SBD-REPO01 and SBD-SPLUNK01 — and the catalog covers them with the log archive, router export, and living records."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the task, job and cron definitions, each first run's hashes, the DHCP and SQL restore notes, the ACL record and share export hashes, the login record, and the complete recovery catalog — never the WEB01 or Splunk archives themselves, which hold keys."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · wbadmin enable backup\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin-enable-backup\n\nMicrosoft · Backup-DhcpServer\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/backup-dhcpserver\n\nMicrosoft · Set-DhcpServerDnsCredential\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/dhcpserver/set-dhcpserverdnscredential\n\nMicrosoft · Register-ScheduledTask\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/scheduledtasks/register-scheduledtask\n\nMicrosoft · wbadmin start recovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin-start-recovery\n\nMicrosoft · icacls\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/icacls\n\nMicrosoft · BACKUP (Transact-SQL)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/backup-transact-sql\n\nMicrosoft · Back up and restore: system databases (SQL Server)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/backup-restore/back-up-and-restore-of-system-databases-sql-server")], question: "SBD-SQL01's application database is backed up nightly, but master isn't. What breaks on a rebuilt instance?", answers: ["Nothing: everything lives in the application database", "The logins live in master, so the restored database's users have no login to map to until master is restored or the logins are recreated from the record", "The database restores read-only"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Microsoft lists master, model and msdb as system databases you must always back up. master records the logins; without it or a login record, the application data comes back unreachable.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("SBD-FS01's Projects folder is restored from Windows Server Backup. What happens to its permissions?", ["They come back with the files, because file recovery restores ACLs unless -notRestoreAcl is used", "They are always reset to the parent folder's", "They have to be granted again by hand"], 0, "Windows Server Backup restores each file's security ACLs by default. The icacls record and share export let you check or reapply them without restoring data."), QuizQuestion("Why do the register, inventory, and exception record join the backup set?", ["They are the largest files", "A restored network without its records has lost its assessable story — the evidence must survive what it evidences", "Backups require ten items minimum"], 1, "This path's real product is a defensible record. Recovery that saves the machines and loses the story saves the wrong half."), QuizQuestion("Why does SBD-WEB01's nightly archive stay on protected media, out of the evidence?", ["Archives are too large for evidence", "It holds the web server's private key in /etc/pki/tls, and secrets never go into evidence", "Linux files can't be hashed"], 1, "Backups inherit the sensitivity of what they contain. Evidence records the archive's hash and custody; the archive itself is handled like the key it carries.")], recap: ["SBD-DHCP01, the inventory, the repository and Splunk's configuration each run on schedule, with their restore notes.", "The business services come back whole: shares with their permissions, databases with their logins, applications with their certificates.", "The log archive, the boundary's records and the living records join one hashed catalog.", "Every backup in the network is now defined and scheduled in this course."], nextStep: "Lesson 4 moves the copies to the offline cabinet, by hand and on record.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#trust-state", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:offline-copies", title: "Carry the copies to the offline cabinet", minutes: 20, summary: "A backup that sits on the server it protects fails with it. Move a copy of each backup to the offline cabinet by hand, under the same custody rules as any media crossing a boundary, and keep the custody record that proves it.", objectives: ["Define which custodian carries which backup, by tier.", "Transfer the copies to offline media with hashes and custody records.", "Prove a copy can be found, verified, and returned for a restore."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.18 (courses 1.16 and 1.17 are coming soon and aren't needed): the full network, the VLAN 40 business services, their records, the protected log archive, and the living inventory all exist.\n\nProvision the backup storage this course schedules into: a protected backup volume (E: in this sample) on every Windows server in the recovery set — both domain controllers, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 — never one of the volumes being backed up and written only by the backup jobs, and a separate volume mounted at /srv/backup, readable by root alone, on SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01. Earlier courses set up no backup jobs; this course defines and schedules every one. Also provide an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the sealed Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords set in course 1.1 and the course 1.6 ceremony record for SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course is the one place every backup in the network is defined and scheduled — the directory, both CAs, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log record, and the living records — then proves one real restore in isolation and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Assign custodians by tier: a Tier 0 custodian handles the controllers' system state, the issuing CA's database and key backup, the Group Policy set, and the router configuration export; the audit custodian (SBD Audit Custodians, course 1.8) handles the log archive and its catalog from SBD-AUDIT01; a Tier 1 server custodian handles the member-server sets — SBD-DHCP01, SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01, SBD-MGMT01, and the VLAN 40 servers SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01, SBD-APPS01 and SBD-WEB01. No one carries a tier they do not hold."), LessonSection("Step 2", "On the cadence the backup schedule sets, for each copy, write the media ID, hash the backup on its protected volume, copy it to encrypted removable media, and verify the hash on the media before it leaves the room. Keep Windows Server Backup's WindowsImageBackup folder layout on the media so wbadmin can read it there."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Carry the media to the offline backup cabinet and record the handoff in the custody register: who, what, when, the hashes, and the storage slot. This is the course 1.4 media procedure used for backups."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Pull one copy back as a test: find it from the register, verify its hash, and confirm it is readable. Lesson 5's rehearsal restores from a copy that has made this round trip."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On SBD-DC02 (Tier 0), with encrypted media MEDIA-0012 attached as F:: hash on the volume, copy, hash on the media\nGet-ChildItem E:\\WindowsImageBackup\\SBD-DC02 -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv F:\\MEDIA-0012-volume.csv -NoTypeInformation\nCopy-Item E:\\WindowsImageBackup -Destination F:\\ -Recurse\nGet-ChildItem F:\\WindowsImageBackup\\SBD-DC02 -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv F:\\MEDIA-0012-media.csv -NoTypeInformation\nCompare-Object (Import-Csv F:\\MEDIA-0012-volume.csv).Hash (Import-Csv F:\\MEDIA-0012-media.csv).Hash   # no output means the copy matches\nwbadmin get versions -backupTarget:F: -machine:SBD-DC02   # the media is readable as a backup"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "Every item in the recovery set has an offline copy with matching hashes on the volume and the media, a custody entry with a named custodian of the right tier, and one copy has been retrieved and verified."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the custodian assignments, the hash pairs (copied into C:\\Evidence\\recovery on the tier's seat), the custody register entries, and the retrieval test record."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Wbadmin (Windows Server Backup)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CP family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "Who carries the domain controllers' system-state backup to the cabinet?", answers: ["Any server operator who is free", "A Tier 0 custodian", "The help desk"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A DC backup holds the directory's secrets. Whoever can handle it can rebuild or read the domain, so it stays with Tier 0.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why is there no network path to the offline backup cabinet?", ["So ransomware or an intruder on the network cannot reach or encrypt the copies", "Because backups are too large to send", "Because NIST forbids network backups"], 0, "An offline copy survives an attack that takes the whole network. The custody record replaces the network path as the proof of what happened."), QuizQuestion("The hash on the media does not match the hash on the volume. What do you do?", ["Store it anyway and note it", "Delete the original", "Do not store it; recopy and investigate, because the copy is not trustworthy"], 2, "A mismatch means the copy is not the backup you verified. Storing it would put an untrusted copy where you will look on the worst day.")], recap: ["Custodians carry only the backups their tier allows.", "Every copy has matching hashes and a custody entry.", "A copy has been retrieved and verified before anyone needs it."], nextStep: "Lesson 5 stops trusting the backups and tests one.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#offline-copies", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:rehearse", title: "Rehearse a real restore, in isolation", minutes: 35, summary: "One restore, actually performed: a domain controller's system state brought back on the isolated segment, timed against the objective, verified against the directory — plus the small restore everyone will someday need, from the Recycle Bin.", objectives: ["Restore a DC's system state in Directory Services Restore Mode on the isolated recovery segment.", "Measure the restore against the time objective and verify directory health.", "Perform and record an object-level Recycle Bin restore."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.18 (courses 1.16 and 1.17 are coming soon and aren't needed): the full network, the VLAN 40 business services, their records, the protected log archive, and the living inventory all exist.\n\nProvision the backup storage this course schedules into: a protected backup volume (E: in this sample) on every Windows server in the recovery set — both domain controllers, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 — never one of the volumes being backed up and written only by the backup jobs, and a separate volume mounted at /srv/backup, readable by root alone, on SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01. Earlier courses set up no backup jobs; this course defines and schedules every one. Also provide an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the sealed Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords set in course 1.1 and the course 1.6 ceremony record for SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course is the one place every backup in the network is defined and scheduled — the directory, both CAs, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log record, and the living records — then proves one real restore in isolation and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Stage the rehearsal: the isolated recovery segment has no path to the production VLANs — a restored controller believing it is authoritative must never meet the live directory; that isolation rule is written before the restore starts. Turn the hypervisor's time synchronization off on the rehearsal copy, as for every virtual machine in the path."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Restore SBD-DC02 from the offline copy retrieved in lesson 4, timing the work from decision to directory services running against the lesson 1 objective. A system state restore needs the same server instance, so use a copy of SBD-DC02's virtual machine attached only to the isolated segment, start it in DSRM (bcdedit /set safeboot dsrepair, restart, sign in with the DSRM password), and run wbadmin start systemstaterecovery with -authsysvol: the copy is the only controller in its isolated world, so its SYSVOL must be authoritative or SYSVOL and NETLOGON never come up. Restoring to different hardware needs a full server (bare-metal) recovery from the critical-volumes backup instead."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Remove the DSRM boot setting (bcdedit /deletevalue safeboot), restart normally, and verify the restored directory the course 1.1 way — services, the SYSVOL and NETLOGON shares, a directory query. Then check the clock: the copy comes back with the time it was saved with, and with no SBD-DC01 to follow nothing corrects it, so compare it with the reference and record the offset; a restored SBD-DC01 in production is checked against the reference before any member follows it (course 1.3). Record what the rehearsal taught — surprises, the measured time, decision-tree updates — then tear the rehearsal down completely."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Back in production, from AdminJumpBox01, run the everyday restore: delete a disposable test object, bring it back from the Recycle Bin with attributes intact, and file both rehearsals — the big one and the small one — as the tested-recovery evidence."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On the isolated copy of SBD-DC02: start in DSRM, then sign in with the DSRM password.\nbcdedit /set safeboot dsrepair\nshutdown /r /t 0\nwbadmin get versions -backupTarget:F: -machine:SBD-DC02   # F: = the retrieved media\nwbadmin start systemstaterecovery -version:<version-id> -backupTarget:F: -machine:SBD-DC02 -authsysvol\nbcdedit /deletevalue safeboot   # then restart normally\n# After the restart, on the restored copy\nGet-Service NTDS, DNS, Netlogon, DFSR | Select-Object Name, Status\nGet-SmbShare -Name SYSVOL, NETLOGON\nGet-ADDomain -Server localhost | Select-Object DNSRoot, PDCEmulator\nw32tm /query /status   # then compare Get-Date with the reference clock and record the offset\n# Back in production, from AdminJumpBox01: the Recycle Bin rehearsal\nGet-ADObject -Filter \"Name -eq '<test object name>'\" -IncludeDeletedObjects | Restore-ADObject"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The isolated restore ran in DSRM and reached a healthy directory within (or honestly against) the time objective, its clock offset is recorded, the DSRM boot setting is removed, the teardown is confirmed, and the production object restore returned the test object intact."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Keep the timed rehearsal log with its verification output and clock reading, the lessons-learned and decision-tree updates, the teardown confirmation, and the object-restore record."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nMicrosoft · wbadmin start systemstaterecovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/wbadmin-start-systemstaterecovery\n\nMicrosoft · AD forest recovery: perform a nonauthoritative restore\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-perform-nonauthoritative-restore\n\nMicrosoft · Restore a virtual domain controller\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/virtual-dc/restore-virtualized-domain-controller\n\nMicrosoft · AD forest recovery: perform a full server recovery\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-perform-full-server-recovery\n\nMicrosoft · Windows Time service tools and settings\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/windows-time-service/windows-time-service-tools-and-settings")], question: "Why must the rehearsal segment be isolated from production?", answers: ["To make the restore faster", "A restored controller carries old directory state — meeting the live forest would inject the past into the present", "Isolation is optional for labs"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The rehearsal's first rule protects production from the rehearsal. Old authoritative state on a live network is its own disaster.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("The restore took longer than the objective. What now?", ["Hide the timing", "Declare the objective met anyway", "Record the honest number and either fix the process or revise the objective — both are legitimate; silence is not"], 2, "The rehearsal exists to replace belief with measurement. Either outcome — better process or truer objective — improves the real bad day."), QuizQuestion("Why rehearse the Recycle Bin restore too?", ["The most likely recovery event deserves the same tested-and-recorded treatment as the rare one", "It is required before system state restores", "Deleted objects expire daily"], 0, "Recovery evidence should match recovery reality: object-level loss is the common case, so its tool gets proven, not presumed."), QuizQuestion("Your recovery host is new hardware. Which restore applies?", ["A system state restore, exactly as on the original server", "A full server (bare-metal) recovery from the critical-volumes backup — system state restores are for the same server instance", "Copying NTDS.dit across by hand"], 1, "Microsoft's forest recovery guidance calls for a full server recovery when you restore to different hardware or a different operating system instance. A system state restore runs on the original instance, in DSRM.")], recap: ["One real restore, isolated, timed, verified, and torn down.", "The measured number replaced the hoped-for one.", "The everyday restore is proven alongside the dramatic one."], nextStep: "The final lesson files the recovery record and closes the Build path.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#rehearse", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:prove-recovery:record", title: "File the recovery record and close the path", minutes: 20, summary: "The Build path ends the way it worked: owners, schedules, and evidence. The recovery record joins the living documents, the closing SCAP results snapshot the finished network, and the Body of Evidence is ready for the authorization courses to assemble.", objectives: ["Assemble the tested-recovery record with owners and review dates.", "Take the closing SCAP snapshot across the finished network.", "Hand the complete evidence set to the authorization path."], sections: [LessonSection("Before you start", "Complete courses 1.1–1.18 (courses 1.16 and 1.17 are coming soon and aren't needed): the full network, the VLAN 40 business services, their records, the protected log archive, and the living inventory all exist.\n\nProvision the backup storage this course schedules into: a protected backup volume (E: in this sample) on every Windows server in the recovery set — both domain controllers, SBD-SUBCA01, SBD-DHCP01, SBD-MGMT01, SBD-FS01, SBD-SQL01 and SBD-APPS01 — never one of the volumes being backed up and written only by the backup jobs, and a separate volume mounted at /srv/backup, readable by root alone, on SBD-REPO01, SBD-SPLUNK01 and SBD-WEB01. Earlier courses set up no backup jobs; this course defines and schedules every one. Also provide an offline backup cabinet with removable media and a custody register, and an isolated recovery network segment (or host) for the restore rehearsal.\n\nHave the custody records handy: the sealed Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) passwords set in course 1.1 and the course 1.6 ceremony record for SBD-ROOTCA01's protected backup — recovery is where those promises come due."), LessonSection("The lab plan", "The model's final chapter: a network that cannot come back was never really built. This course is the one place every backup in the network is defined and scheduled — the directory, both CAs, Group Policy, DHCP, the repository and analysis configuration, the VLAN 40 business services with their permissions, logins and certificates, the log record, and the living records — then proves one real restore in isolation and files the tested-recovery evidence the authorization path expects."), LessonSection("Step 1", "Assemble the recovery record: the requirements and objectives, the backup schedule and catalog, both rehearsal results with the measured times, the decision tree, the DHCP and SQL restore notes, and a named owner with a scheduled next rehearsal that rotates through the set — an SBD-FS01 folder restored with its permissions, an SBD-SQL01 database with its logins, SBD-DHCP01 with its credential step — recovery is a practice with a calendar, not a binder. Keep its Tier 0 half in C:\\Evidence\\recovery on AdminJumpBox01 and its Tier 1 half on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, each sealed with a manifest."), LessonSection("Step 2", "Take the closing snapshot: current Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) results for every system on the course 1.5 cadence, each tier from its own seat — Tier 0 from AdminJumpBox01, the Windows and Linux member servers from SBD-ADMIN-SRV01, the fleet from SBD-ADMIN-PC01 — with the recorded benchmark versions, triaged as always: the dated 'as-authorized' picture path 2 will reference. Credentialed vulnerability scanning joins it when course 1.17 (coming soon) arrives."), LessonSection("Step 3", "Reconcile the three living records one final time — the inventory on SBD-MGMT01 against the directory, DHCP leases, and WSUS rings (the course 1.15 method), the register against observed behavior, and the exception record against open findings — so the authorization path inherits agreement, not archaeology."), LessonSection("Step 4", "Write the Build path's closing note: what exists, where every record lives, and what is intentionally open — then start course 2.1, where this network stops being a project and becomes a system seeking authorization."), LessonSection("Inspect your configuration", "# On AdminJumpBox01 (Tier 0), then the same on SBD-ADMIN-SRV01 (Tier 1): seal each half of the recovery record\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence -Directory | Select-Object Name\nGet-ChildItem C:\\Evidence\\recovery -Recurse -File | Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 | Export-Csv C:\\Evidence\\recovery-manifest.csv -NoTypeInformation\nGet-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 C:\\Evidence\\recovery-manifest.csv"), LessonSection("What success looks like", "The recovery record is complete with an owner and next-rehearsal date and sealed on both seats, the closing SCAP snapshot is filed with triage, and all three living records reconcile cleanly into the handoff note."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Finish the checklist: the recovery record and its two manifests, the closing SCAP snapshot, the three reconciliations, and the Build path's closing note."), LessonSection("Source documentation", "Microsoft · Active Directory forest recovery guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/manage/forest-recovery-guide/ad-forest-recovery-guide\n\nNIST · SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security controls (CP family)\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final")], question: "What makes recovery 'a practice with a calendar'?", answers: ["A named owner and a scheduled next rehearsal — the test repeats as the network changes", "Laminating the decision tree", "Buying more storage"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "One rehearsal proves today's backups. The schedule is what keeps tomorrow's honest.", moreQuestions: [QuizQuestion("Why take a closing SCAP snapshot at path's end?", ["Scanners need exercise", "It is the dated security picture the authorization decision will actually be about", "To fill the evidence folder"], 1, "Path 2's assessors and the authorizing official evaluate a system at a moment. The snapshot is that moment, captured."), QuizQuestion("What does path 2 inherit from the Build path?", ["Just the virtual machines", "A to-do list", "A reconciled, evidenced system: inventory, register, exceptions, scans, scheduled backups, and tested recovery — the Body of Evidence, already real"], 2, "The authorization path was never going to invent evidence; it organizes what the Build path produced on purpose, course by course.")], recap: ["Recovery has an owner, a date, and a measured track record.", "The closing snapshot freezes the as-authorized picture.", "The Body of Evidence exists — path 2 assembles, it doesn't excavate."], nextStep: "That completes the Build the Network path. Continue with course 2.1 to carry this network — and its evidence — through the Risk Management Framework to an authorization decision.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/prove-recovery#record", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "rmf-boe-ato", number: "2.1", title: "Risk Management Framework", subtitle: "Prepare, Categorize, and Select", category: "Network authorization", description: "Take the network the Build the Network courses (1.1–1.19) create, the system shown in Inside the Network, through the Prepare, Categorize, and Select steps of the Risk Management Framework (RMF), following NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2 with the April 2016 Joint Special Access Program Implementation Guide (JSIG) mapped in. Define and register the system, describe and categorize it in a draft System Security Plan, and select its controls with a draft risk assessment and a monitoring strategy, ready for the Body of Evidence in course 2.2.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "4 modules · 12 topics · 6 study hours", topics: 12,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-guide", title: "Risk Management Framework · Interactive course", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Course 2.1 · Prepare, Categorize, and Select · 12 lessons", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-boe-ato-package", title: "Risk Management Framework · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word) and evidence starters.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/SecureByDesign-rmf-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-boe-ato-evidence", title: "Risk Management Framework · Evidence workspace", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Course 2.1 · six examples with editable Markdown and CSV starters.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#evidence", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:01", title: "01 · RMF roles, steps, and editions", minutes: 60, summary: "Explain who performs, assesses, and approves Risk Management Framework (RMF) work, and how NIST and the Joint Special Access Program Implementation Guide (JSIG) organize its steps and outputs.", sections: [LessonSection("Before the RMF steps", "NIST names roles by function (system owner, control assessor, common control provider, authorizing official); the JSIG uses SAP titles for the same work (ISO, SCA, CCP, AO) alongside the ISSM, ISSO, ISSE, and PSO. In both, only the AO accepts risk."), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• A role map of who performs, assesses, and approves the work, in NIST and JSIG terms (the role assignments NIST covers in Task P-1)\n• A record of the governing documents and editions: SP 800-37 Revision 2, the April 2016 JSIG or the successor your program directs, and the adopted SP 800-53 release\n• The scope record, started with those roles and that governing direction"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG of 11 April 2016 follows SP 800-37 Revision 1’s six steps, starting at Categorize, with SP 800-53 Revision 4 and CNSSI 1253 (March 2014). Prepare is the NIST Revision 2 addition. DoDM 5205.07 refers to the JSIG or its successor."), LessonSection("Start with the mission, then ask about risk.", "The Risk Management Framework (RMF) connects a system’s purpose to the safeguards, evidence, and decisions needed to operate it. Begin with what the organization needs the system to do. Then ask what could happen if its information is exposed, changed incorrectly, or unavailable.\n\nEach step produces outputs the next one builds on, ending in an authorization package. An Authorization to Operate (ATO) is the authorizing official’s decision about the risk that remains; installing tools or finishing a checklist doesn’t make it."), LessonSection("Everyone contributes. The responsibilities differ.", "NIST names roles by function: system owner, control assessor, common control provider, and authorizing official. The Joint Special Access Program (SAP) Implementation Guide (JSIG) uses SAP titles for that work: Information System Owner (ISO), Security Control Assessor (SCA), Common Control Provider (CCP), and Authorizing Official (AO).\n\nAn Information System Security Manager (ISSM) oversees the security program, an Information System Security Officer (ISSO) supports day-to-day implementation, and an Information System Security Engineer (ISSE) designs security in; SAP programs add a Program Security Officer (PSO). Local appointments set the exact split. Only the AO accepts risk."), LessonSection("Know which document, and which edition, you’re following.", "NIST Special Publication (SP) 800-37 Revision 2 (December 2018) has seven steps: Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, and Monitor. Each is a set of numbered tasks, such as P-11 for the authorization boundary, with expected outputs and responsible roles. SP 800-53 supplies the controls.\n\nThe JSIG of 11 April 2016 applies that foundation to SAP systems, with SP 800-53 Revision 4 and Committee on National Security Systems Instruction (CNSSI) 1253 of March 2014. It follows SP 800-37 Revision 1’s six steps, so it has no Prepare step; its tasks run from 1-1 to 6-7, and each step lists its outputs.\n\nThe public JSIG is this 2016 edition. Department of Defense Manual (DoDM) 5205.07 refers to the JSIG or its successor, so confirm what your program directs. This course follows the NIST steps, maps the JSIG tasks into each, and notes where the two differ."), LessonSection("From a claim to a decision", "Claim: “Privileged account changes are logged and reviewed,” as the System Security Plan (SSP) states it for the practice system.\n\nEvidence: The control owners supply the event-forwarding configuration, a sample account change from SBD-DC01, and the analyst’s review record in SBD-SPLUNK01.\n\nAssessment: The assessor (SCA) tests the event and checks that the expected review happened.\n\nDecision: The AO weighs the supported result and any remaining gap. Only the AO can accept the risk."), LessonSection("Build", "Start the scope record. Map who holds each role for the practice system (system owner or ISO, ISSM, ISSO, ISSE, SCA, CCP, AO, and a PSO if assigned) and mark who performs, assesses, and approves. Record the governing documents and editions, the adopted control release, and privacy responsibilities."), LessonSection("Prove", "Given a missing test result and an unresolved risk, identify who supplies the evidence, who assesses it, and who can accept the risk, in NIST and JSIG terms. Then explain why the 2016 JSIG has no Prepare step.")], question: "Your program directs the public JSIG of 11 April 2016. Which statement fits it?", answers: ["It is built on SP 800-53 Revision 5 and begins with Prepare.", "It replaces NIST, so SP 800-37 no longer applies.", "It follows SP 800-37 Revision 1’s six steps, starting at Categorize, with SP 800-53 Revision 4 and CNSSI 1253."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "NIST is the foundation; the JSIG is SAP-specific direction for applying it. The 2016 edition predates the Revision 2 Prepare step and the Revision 5 controls, and your program may direct a successor.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/01", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:02", title: "02 · Define and register the system", minutes: 90, summary: "Establish what is being authorized and why: the mission, stakeholders, assets, boundary, information and its life cycle, early risks, and requirements. Then register the system.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Prepare", "NIST gives the organization-level tasks (P-1 to P-7) to senior leaders and the risk executive function. At the system level, the mission or business owner and the system owner lead, the authorizing official determines the boundary (P-11), and the system owner registers the system (P-18). The 2016 JSIG has no Prepare step, so it assigns no roles here."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "P-1 · Risk management roles\nP-2 · Risk management strategy\nP-3 · Risk assessment—organization\nP-4 · Organizationally-tailored baselines and Cybersecurity Framework Profiles (optional)\nP-5 · Common control identification\nP-6 · Impact-level prioritization (optional)\nP-7 · Continuous monitoring strategy—organization\nP-8 · Mission or business focus\nP-9 · System stakeholders\nP-10 · Asset identification\nP-11 · Authorization boundary\nP-12 · Information types\nP-13 · Information life cycle\nP-14 · Risk assessment—system\nP-15 · Requirements definition\nP-16 · Enterprise architecture\nP-17 · Requirements allocation\nP-18 · System registration"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "The 2016 JSIG has no Prepare step."), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• Mission and stakeholders, recorded in the scope record (P-8, P-9)\n• An asset list and a documented authorization boundary, with dependencies and candidate common controls (P-10, P-11)\n• Information types and their life cycle, including every boundary transfer (P-12, P-13)\n• An early risk picture (P-14), carried into the draft Risk Assessment Report in module 04\n• Requirements defined, placed in the architecture, and allocated to the system or its environment (P-15 to P-17)\n• A registered system, recorded in the registration record (P-18)"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The 2016 JSIG has no Prepare step; it follows SP 800-37 Revision 1. It describes the system and its boundary (Task 1-2) and registers it (Task 1-3) in Categorize, and drafts its first Risk Assessment Report in Select. NIST does this work in Prepare (P-11, P-14, P-18)."), LessonSection("Prepare starts above the system.", "Prepare is the step NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2 added. Its organization-level tasks (P-1 to P-7) assign roles, set the risk strategy and tolerance, identify common controls systems can inherit, and set an organization-wide monitoring strategy. A system team works inside those decisions.\n\nAt the system level, start with the mission (P-8) and the stakeholders (P-9): what work the system supports, who depends on it, who owns its information, and what an interruption or misuse would affect."), LessonSection("Draw the boundary, then test the assumptions.", "Identify the assets to protect (P-10), then the authorization boundary (P-11), which the authorizing official determines. It’s more than a subnet diagram: consider management interfaces, hosting, shared services, information flows, and dependencies.\n\nList the information types the system processes, stores, and transmits (P-12), and follow each from creation to disposal (P-13). A component outside the boundary can still affect risk. Record the relationship and the responsible party; don’t use the boundary to make a hard dependency disappear."), LessonSection("Assess early, allocate the requirements, then register.", "Assess system risk early (P-14): threats, weaknesses, and likely consequences, so the requirements can answer them. Then define the security and privacy requirements (P-15), place the system in the enterprise architecture (P-16), and allocate each requirement to the system or its environment (P-17).\n\nLast, the system owner registers the system with the office the organization designates (P-18). The 2016 JSIG has no Prepare step, so it describes the system and its boundary and registers it in Categorize (Tasks 1-2, 1-3), and drafts its first Risk Assessment Report (RAR) in Select."), LessonSection("Resolve a boundary question", "Question: Are the seven hypervisor hosts that run the enclave’s servers covered by this authorization?\n\nRecord: List SBD-HV01 to SBD-HV07, who administers them, and the systems each one runs, including the domain controllers and the issuing CA.\n\nResolve: They can control the domain controllers, so they are Tier 0 assets wherever the line falls. Propose inside or external with the reason, and route it to the authorizing official, who determines the boundary (P-11).\n\nProve: Keep the asset list, boundary diagram, scope record, and registration consistent with the decision."), LessonSection("Build", "Complete the scope record’s Prepare fields for the practice system: mission and stakeholders, assets and boundary, information types and life cycle, the early risk picture, and requirements and allocation. Check each information flow against the connection map in Inside the Network, which shows every permitted, pending, and denied rule for a system. Then fill in the registration record."), LessonSection("Prove", "Explain whether a hypervisor host, the core switch’s management, a shared service, or an offline update transfer is inside the boundary, external, or inherited, with the supporting responsibility evidence. Say what changes in the registration if the boundary moves.")], question: "Your program follows the 2016 JSIG, which has no Prepare step. Where does the boundary and registration work happen?", answers: ["In Categorize: describe the system and its boundary (Task 1-2), then register it (Task 1-3).", "Nowhere. Without a Prepare step, the JSIG skips it.", "In Monitor, once the system is operating."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The JSIG follows SP 800-37 Revision 1, so work that NIST Revision 2 puts in Prepare (P-11, P-18) appears in the JSIG’s Categorize step.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/02", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:03", title: "03 · Describe and categorize the system", minutes: 90, summary: "Describe the system, justify its confidentiality, integrity, and availability impacts in the draft System Security Plan (SSP), and route the categorization for approval. Treat privacy applicability as an explicit decision.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Categorize", "The system owner describes the system (C-1) and categorizes it with the information owner (C-2); the authorizing official or a designated representative approves the categorization (C-3). In the JSIG, the ISO categorizes with the ISSM/ISSO and ISSE (Task 1-1)."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "C-1 · System description\nC-2 · Security categorization\nC-3 · Security categorization review and approval"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "1-1 · Categorize the system and document the results in the SSP\n1-2 · Describe the system, including its boundary, and document it in the SSP\n1-3 · Register the system with the appropriate SAP Service/Agency AO or designee"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• A documented system description in the draft SSP (C-1; JSIG Task 1-2)\n• Impact levels for each information type and security objective, with a written rationale (C-2)\n• The system’s security categorization recorded in the draft SSP (C-2; JSIG Task 1-1)\n• The categorization routed to the authorizing official or a designated representative for approval (C-3)\n• The module 02 registration checked against the JSIG route (Task 1-3)\n\nJSIG step outputs: Draft SSP; System registration"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "NIST registers the system in Prepare (P-18) and describes it here (C-1). The JSIG describes the system and its boundary (Task 1-2) and registers it (Task 1-3) in this step."), LessonSection("Describe the system someone else will assess.", "Categorize opens with a system description (C-1): what the system is for, what it contains, who uses it, what it connects to, and where its boundary lies, drawn from the Prepare records. The JSIG asks for the same in Task 1-2: describe the system, including its boundary, in the System Security Plan (SSP).\n\nWrite it so a reviewer who has never seen the system can follow it. Every later decision, from categorization to control selection, points back to this description."), LessonSection("Evaluate the consequences for each objective.", "Confidentiality concerns inappropriate disclosure, integrity improper change, and availability reliable access when needed. For each information type, judge the impact of a loss on each objective, then set the system’s impact for each objective from those results (C-2), using the method that applies.\n\nIn the JSIG, the ISO categorizes the system with the ISSM or ISSO and the ISSE, and documents the results in the SSP (Task 1-1). Write a rationale someone else can review: the information, what’s affected, the consequence, and why each level fits. An isolated network alone doesn’t decide the result."), LessonSection("Apply the right authority, then get approval.", "Federal systems categorize under Federal Information Processing Standard (FIPS) 199 with the information types in NIST SP 800-60; national security systems follow CNSSI 1253, and a SAP adds its program direction. Treat privacy applicability as an explicit decision.\n\nNIST makes approval a task of its own (C-3): the authorizing official or a designated representative approves the categorization. In the JSIG, the categorization is documented in the draft SSP, and the whole plan is approved in Select (Task 2-4). This JSIG step also registers the system (Task 1-3), which NIST did in Prepare (P-18)."), LessonSection("A new dependency reopens availability", "Initial rationale: Engineering work can wait out a short outage of the internal web application, so the exercise input rates availability Low.\n\nNew information: A deadline-driven engineering process now depends on timely access to the application on SBD-WEB01 and its records in SBD-SQL01.\n\nReview: Reevaluate the availability consequences for those information types under the applicable method (C-2).\n\nUpdate: Record the new rationale in the draft SSP and the scope record’s categorization basis, route it for approval (C-3), and update the registration if the category changes."), LessonSection("Build", "Draft the SSP’s system description and security categorization from the scope record: information types, impact rationale, mission dependencies, privacy considerations, and approval routing. Justify the exercise planning input (Moderate confidentiality, Low integrity, Low availability); don’t copy it. Complete the scope record’s categorization basis and confirm the registration follows JSIG Task 1-3."), LessonSection("Prove", "Defend a changed availability impact after a new mission dependency, and explain why an air gap alone doesn’t establish a low impact level. Then say who approves the categorization in NIST terms and where the JSIG documents it.")], question: "The system owner has categorized the system and wants to start selecting controls. What does NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2 call for first?", answers: ["Nothing more. The system owner’s categorization is final.", "Review and approval of the categorization by the authorizing official or a designated representative (C-3).", "A control assessment by the security control assessor."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The system owner and information owner categorize (C-2); approval is a separate task (C-3). In the JSIG, the categorization is documented in the draft SSP (Task 1-1), and the plan is approved in Select (Task 2-4).", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/03", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:04", title: "04 · Select controls and plan monitoring", minutes: 120, summary: "Turn the approved categorization into a selected, tailored, and allocated control set, with a draft Risk Assessment Report and a continuous monitoring strategy, ready for plan review and approval.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Select", "The system owner and any common control provider select, tailor, and document the controls and the monitoring strategy (S-1, S-2, S-4, S-5); security architects and the system security officer allocate them (S-3); the authorizing official or a designee approves the plan (S-6). In the JSIG, the ISO does Tasks 2-1 to 2-3 with the ISSE and/or ISSM/ISSO; the AO, SCA, or AO designee approves (2-4)."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "S-1 · Control selection\nS-2 · Control tailoring\nS-3 · Control allocation\nS-4 · Documentation of planned control implementations\nS-5 · Continuous monitoring strategy—system\nS-6 · Plan review and approval"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "2-1 · Identify common controls and document them in a security plan\n2-2 · Select the security controls for the information system and document the controls in the security plan\n2-3 · Develop a strategy for continuous monitoring of security control effectiveness\n2-4 · Review and approve the SSP"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• Selected and tailored controls, with parameters and rationale, in the SCTM (S-1, S-2)\n• Controls allocated to the system, its components, or a common control provider (S-3; JSIG Task 2-1)\n• Planned implementations documented in the security plan and SCTM (S-4; JSIG Task 2-2)\n• A draft Risk Assessment Report built from the early risk picture and the planned controls (JSIG Step 2 output)\n• A system continuous monitoring strategy, including reporting and decommissioning (S-5; JSIG Task 2-3)\n• The plan routed for review and approval (S-6; JSIG Task 2-4)\n\nJSIG step outputs: Updated draft SSP/SCTM; Draft Risk Assessment Report (RAR); Documented Continuous Monitoring (ConMon) Plan/Strategy; Approved draft SSP/SCTM"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG produces a draft RAR in this step and updates it in Assess; NIST assesses system risk in Prepare (P-14) and analyzes and determines it in Authorize (R-2)."), LessonSection("A catalog is not the same as a selected control set.", "SP 800-53 is a catalog; a baseline is a starting set under a selection method, and overlays can change it. SP 800-53B holds the federal baselines; a national security system selects under CNSSI 1253 and its overlays, and a SAP adds the JSIG and approved program direction.\n\nSelect the controls (S-1), then tailor them (S-2): record why each is added, adjusted, or not applicable, resolve organization-defined parameters such as roles and frequencies, and note who approves. Difficulty alone doesn’t make a requirement not applicable."), LessonSection("Allocate the work before asking for evidence.", "Allocate each control to the system, its components, or its environment (S-3), and name its owner: system-specific, common from a provider, or hybrid with shared responsibility. The JSIG begins Select here, with Task 2-1: identify the common controls and document them in a security plan.\n\nThen document the planned implementations (S-4). The JSIG’s Task 2-2 selects the controls and documents them in the security plan. A Security Controls Traceability Matrix (SCTM) can record each control’s selection, parameters, allocation, and inheritance; its format follows local requirements."), LessonSection("Plan how you’ll know it still works, then get approval.", "A system continuous monitoring strategy (S-5; JSIG Task 2-3) says which controls are monitored, how often, by whom, and what triggers action, within the organization’s strategy (P-7). The JSIG also drafts a Risk Assessment Report (RAR) here; NIST assessed system risk in Prepare (P-14).\n\nThen the plan is reviewed and approved: by the authorizing official or a designated representative in NIST (S-6), or by the AO, SCA, or AO designee in the JSIG (Task 2-4). The JSIG calls the result the approved draft SSP/SCTM."), LessonSection("Credentialed scanning isn’t built yet", "Proposal: “Mark RA-5 not applicable until a credentialed scanner exists.”\n\nQuestion: Is the control inapplicable, or only hard to meet today? The network still runs software that needs vulnerability monitoring.\n\nDecision: Keep RA-5 selected. Allocate what exists, SCAP Compliance Checker and Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) results from the administration seats, and record credentialed scanning as a gap.\n\nRecord: The SCTM row shows the allocation and the gap, the draft RAR carries the risk, and the monitoring strategy reviews scan coverage."), LessonSection("Build", "Start the Security Controls Traceability Matrix (SCTM) with each selected control’s adopted revision, source, tailoring rationale, parameters, owner, and system-specific, common, or hybrid allocation. Draft the Risk Assessment Report (RAR) from the module 02 risk picture, write the continuous monitoring strategy, and route the plan for approval."), LessonSection("Prove", "Resolve a proposed not-applicable control and an inherited-control claim using evidence and the required approval path. Show which monitoring activity watches each draft RAR risk, and pin the control and assessment-procedure versions.")], question: "A facility office says physical access to the server room is “covered.” What does the SCTM need before the plan goes for approval?", answers: ["Nothing more. Mark PE-3 inherited on the provider’s word.", "Mark PE-3 not applicable, since the system team doesn’t run the building.", "The provider, the parts it performs, its assessment information, and what the system team still does."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "An inherited or hybrid allocation needs an identified provider and applicable evidence. Record the split in the SCTM so the assessor knows what to ask of whom.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf/index.html#catalog/04", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "rmf-body-of-evidence", number: "2.2", title: "Body of Evidence", subtitle: "Implement and Assess: document, prove, and test the network", category: "Network authorization", description: "Develop the Body of Evidence for the same Inside the Network build through the Risk Management Framework (RMF) Implement and Assess steps, following NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2 with the 2016 Joint Special Access Program Implementation Guide (JSIG) mapped in. Record the controls as implemented, prove them with indexed evidence, prepare and perform an independent assessment, and carry its findings into a Security Assessment Report (SAR) and a Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M).", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "5 modules · 15 topics · 9 study hours", topics: 15,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-body-of-evidence-guide", title: "Body of Evidence · Interactive course", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Course 2.2 · Implement and Assess: document, prove, and test the network · 15 lessons", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-body-of-evidence-package", title: "Body of Evidence · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word) and evidence starters.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/SecureByDesign-rmf-body-of-evidence-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-evidence", title: "Body of Evidence examples and starters", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Course 2.2 · seven examples with editable Markdown and CSV starters.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html#evidence", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:05", title: "05 · Implement and document the controls", minutes: 120, summary: "Implement the selected controls and record them as built in the System Security Plan (SSP) and Security Controls Traceability Matrix (SCTM), in statements an assessor can test.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Implement", "NIST gives I-1 and I-2 to the system owner and any common control provider, with engineers and security staff supporting. The Joint Special Access Program (SAP) Implementation Guide (JSIG) gives Tasks 3-1 and 3-2 to the Information System Owner (ISO), working with the Information System Security Engineer (ISSE)."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "I-1 · Control implementation\nI-2 · Update control implementation information"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "3-1 · Implement the security controls specified in the SSP\n3-2 · Document the control implementation in the SCTM portion of the SSP"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• The controls the Build the Network courses put in place, checked against the approved selection and parameter values (NIST I-1; JSIG 3-1)\n• As-implemented SSP statements for each practice control: who, what, where, when, and which records (I-2)\n• SCTM rows updated with implementation status and evidence references (JSIG 3-2, output “Updated SSP/SCTM”)\n• Planned work and differences from the approved plan recorded, not presented as operating\n• Supporting plans referenced by owner, version, and review status\n\nJSIG step outputs: Updated SSP/SCTM"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG records the implementation in the SCTM portion of the SSP. NIST updates the security and privacy plans themselves, with as-implemented detail sufficient for an assessor to use."), LessonSection("Build what the approved plan describes.", "Implement turns the approved plan into a working system. NIST Task I-1 implements the controls in the security and privacy plans; Task 3-1 of the Joint Special Access Program (SAP) Implementation Guide (JSIG) implements those specified in the System Security Plan (SSP).\n\nThe system owner is accountable, and engineers build to the approved selection, parameter values, and control revision. That revision matters: the public JSIG is the 11 April 2016 edition, built on SP 800-53 Revision 4 and CNSSI 1253, and a program may direct a successor or its own guidance."), LessonSection("Record the controls as they were built.", "NIST Task I-2 updates the plans with the as-implemented state, in enough detail for an assessor to use. JSIG Task 3-2 records it in the Security Controls Traceability Matrix (SCTM) portion of the SSP. Both lead to the JSIG output “Updated SSP/SCTM.”\n\nWrite who performs each activity, what happens, where it applies, when it happens, and which records show it. “We comply with policy” leaves an assessor nothing to examine, interview, or test."), LessonSection("Keep planned work and supporting plans honest.", "Describe what operates today, and mark planned work as planned. Where the build could not follow the plan, say so; the assessor will test each statement as written.\n\nConfiguration, access, incident response, contingency, and other supporting plans describe processes the SSP relies on. Reference each by owner, version, and review status instead of copying procedures that drift apart. A plan shows intent; records show the process happens."), LessonSection("Turn a vague statement into an as-implemented one", "Before: “Audit records are reviewed according to policy.” It names no reviewer, sources, schedule, or record.\n\nAs built: Windows systems forward the selected events to SBD-SYSLOG01 by Windows Event Forwarding (F17), the Linux servers send syslog over TLS (F07), and the collector feeds SBD-SPLUNK01 (F10) and SBD-AUDIT01 (F11).\n\nAfter: The assigned analyst reviews those events in SBD-SPLUNK01 on the approved schedule, records each exception and its follow-up, and keeps the review record.\n\nTrace: Put the statement in the SSP and update the AU-6 row of the SCTM with its status and evidence identifiers, ready for the assessor."), LessonSection("Build", "Update the SSP that course 2.1 drafted with as-implemented statements for the practice controls (AU-2, AU-3, AU-6, AU-12): who performs each activity, what happens, where, when, and which records show it. Update the matching SCTM rows with implementation status and evidence references, and mark planned work as planned."), LessonSection("Prove", "Replace a vague “compliant with policy” statement with a specific as-implemented narrative. Reconcile it with the diagrams, inventory, and inheritance record, and show how you would record a difference between the build and the approved plan.")], question: "The engineers built the log path differently from the approved plan. What does the Implement step call for?", answers: ["Leave the plan as approved and let the assessor find the difference.", "Update the SSP and SCTM to the as-implemented state.", "Change the system back, whatever the reason for the difference."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "NIST Task I-2 and JSIG Task 3-2 record the controls as actually implemented. The assessor tests that record, so it has to match the system.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html#catalog/05", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:06", title: "06 · Connect implementation to proof", minutes: 120, summary: "Collect technical and operational evidence of the as-built system, index it, and correct the SSP and SCTM wherever the evidence and the plan disagree.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Implement", "Engineers and administrators produce the records, and common control providers supply evidence for what the system inherits. The system owner (in the JSIG, the ISO with the ISSE) keeps the SSP and SCTM true to that evidence."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "I-1 · Control implementation\nI-2 · Update control implementation information"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "3-1 · Implement the security controls specified in the SSP\n3-2 · Document the control implementation in the SCTM portion of the SSP"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• Configuration evidence with system, scope, version, and collection time: scan results, hardening checklists, exports (I-1)\n• Operational records that show processes run, such as log reviews and a restore test\n• A master evidence index giving each record an identifier, owner, date, scope, and version\n• Coverage gaps and limitations recorded, including credentialed vulnerability scanning not yet available\n• SSP and SCTM corrected to the as-built state (I-2; JSIG output “Updated SSP/SCTM”)\n\nJSIG step outputs: Updated SSP/SCTM"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG calls the record an assessment rests on the Body of Evidence (BoE): the SSP, test plans, test procedures, test reports, and exceptions. NIST SP 800-37 treats the same material task by task: updated plans (I-2), assessment plans (A-2), assessment evidence (A-3), and assessment reports (A-4)."), LessonSection("Capture the configuration and its context.", "Evidence shows what Implement actually produced. A configuration export or Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) checklist is useful when a reviewer can identify the system, setting, scope, version, and collection time. Keep the review record alongside it.\n\nA passing automated check has a defined scope. Manual checks, applicability decisions, and technical limits still need attention."), LessonSection("Show that processes run, and how far the evidence reaches.", "Logs, review records, tickets, access reviews, and restore tests support different claims. Connect each item to the question it can answer. Owning a security product does not show that a control is effective; its records might.\n\nCheck that every relevant system was included, the collection worked, and the data is current. Keep collection failures rather than silently excluding them, and say what an item cannot show: a backup job’s success message does not prove recovery."), LessonSection("Index the evidence, then let it correct the plan.", "A master evidence index gives each record a stable identifier, title, scope, owner, collection date, version, and location, so nobody depends on a folder name or someone’s memory. One record can support several objectives, and one objective can need several records.\n\nWhen the evidence shows the system differs from the SSP or SCTM, update them to the as-built state. That is still NIST Task I-2 and JSIG Task 3-2, and it means the assessor tests the real system."), LessonSection("A clean view with a coverage gap", "Result: A Splunk search by host shows recent events from the domain controllers and member servers.\n\nInspection: The audit source inventory lists SBD-MGMT01, but none of its events reached SBD-SYSLOG01 in the collection period.\n\nConclusion: The evidence does not support complete source coverage, so the SSP’s AU-12 statement cannot claim it.\n\nFollow-up: Record the gap in the evidence index and the SSP, and hand it to the assessor. Do not drop SBD-MGMT01 from the inventory to make the view look complete."), LessonSection("Build", "Collect configuration exports, reviewed STIG checklists, SCAP Compliance Checker results with their coverage, log review records, and the course 1.19 restore record, or equivalent supplied training records. Start the master evidence index, and correct the SSP and SCTM wherever the evidence shows the system differs from the plan."), LessonSection("Prove", "Explain what each item proves and leaves untested. Identify a scan that missed an in-scope host, an unreviewed alert, and a configuration screenshot that lacks system or time context. Record credentialed vulnerability scanning (RA-5) as a limitation: the practice network does not run it yet.")], question: "A configuration scan reports no open findings, but one in-scope server is missing from its results. What does the scan support?", answers: ["A clean result for every in-scope server.", "Nothing, so it should be deleted.", "A clean result for the servers it covered; the missing server is a coverage gap to record and close."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "A result supports only the scope it covered. Keep it, record the gap, and collect the missing evidence before anyone relies on the result.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html#catalog/06", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:07", title: "07 · Prepare the assessment", minutes: 90, summary: "Select an independent assessor, hand over current evidence, and get a Security Assessment Plan approved before any testing starts.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Assess", "The Authorizing Official (AO) or a designated representative selects the assessor (A-1) and, in NIST, approves the plan the assessor develops (A-2). In the JSIG, the ISO develops the plan with the Information System Security Manager (ISSM), Information System Security Officer (ISSO), or ISSE, and the Security Control Assessor (SCA) reviews and approves it (4-1)."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "A-1 · Assessor selection\nA-2 · Assessment plan"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "4-1 · Develop, review, and approve a plan to assess the security controls"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• An assessor or team selected by the AO or designated representative, with the independence required (NIST A-1)\n• A record of the evidence handed over: SSP, SCTM, evidence index, and known exceptions, with versions\n• A Security Assessment Plan with scope, objectives, methods, objects, procedures, sampling, schedule, and limits (A-2; JSIG 4-1)\n• The plan’s review and approval, recorded before testing (JSIG output “Security Assessment Plan”)\n\nJSIG step outputs: Security Assessment Plan"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "Assessor selection is a separate task only in NIST (A-1). NIST has the assessor develop the plan for the AO’s approval; the JSIG has the ISO’s team develop it and the SCA approve it, and defines it as the SSP, the SCTM, and the Security Control Assessment Procedures."), LessonSection("Choose an assessor who can be trusted with the answer.", "NIST Task A-1 opens Assess: the Authorizing Official (AO), or a designated representative, selects the assessor or team. In the JSIG, Step 4 starts with the plan, and the Security Control Assessor (SCA) is a defined role.\n\nChoose for skill and independence. An impartial assessor does not assess their own work or advocate for the team that built it. The AO decides how much independence the system’s categorization and risk call for."), LessonSection("Hand over evidence the assessor can rely on.", "The assessor starts from what Implement produced: the SSP, the SCTM, the evidence index, and the exceptions you already know about. Check that each record is current, and record the versions you hand over so the plan and results refer to the same material.\n\nThe JSIG describes this as the Body of Evidence (BoE): the SSP, test plans, test procedures, test reports, and exceptions. The system team supplies the plan and the exceptions; the assessment adds the rest."), LessonSection("Agree on the plan before anyone tests.", "In NIST Task A-2, the assessor develops the plan from the implementation detail, and the AO or a designated representative approves it. In JSIG Task 4-1, the ISO develops it with the information system security manager, officer, or engineer, and the SCA reviews and approves it.\n\nThe JSIG’s Security Assessment Plan is made up of the SSP, the SCTM, and the Security Control Assessment Procedures. Either way, the plan states scope, objectives, methods, objects, coverage, responsibilities, schedule, and limits, with sampling decisions written down."), LessonSection("Plan the logging assessment", "Assessor: The exercise AO’s designated representative selects a training assessor who did not build or operate the logging pipeline.\n\nEvidence: The assessor receives the SSP and SCTM at their recorded versions, evidence index EI-001, and the known SBD-MGMT01 coverage gap.\n\nProcedures: Examine the event configuration and source records, interview the analyst, test an approved account change on SBD-DC01, and replay the attack drill’s six attempts as negative tests.\n\nApproval: Plan AP-001 is reviewed and approved before any testing: by the SCA in JSIG terms, or by the AO or a designated representative in NIST’s."), LessonSection("Build", "Record who selected the assessor and the independence they provide. List the evidence handed over, with versions and known exceptions. Draft a Security Assessment Plan for the practice logging objectives: scope, objectives, methods, objects, sampling rationale, the attack drill’s six attempts as negative tests, responsibilities, schedule, and limits. Record its review and approval."), LessonSection("Prove", "Check the handover before approval: find stale evidence, a missing record, and a mismatch between the SSP and an exported configuration, and record each gap’s effect on the plan. Explain why your assessor is independent enough for this system.")], question: "The engineer who built the logging pipeline offers to assess it. What is the problem?", answers: ["An assessor should not assess their own work; the AO sets the independence required.", "None, as long as the engineer follows the procedures.", "Engineers cannot take part in an assessment at all."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Independence keeps the result credible. The engineer still takes part, as someone the assessor interviews and observes.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html#catalog/07", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:08", title: "08 · Perform and report the assessment", minutes: 120, summary: "Assess the controls as planned, report supported findings in a Security Assessment Report (SAR), and remediate and reassess what can be fixed now.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Assess", "The control assessor performs the assessment and writes the report; in the JSIG that is the SCA or an AO designee, and the SCA submits the SAR with a recommended authorization decision. The system owner carries out initial remediation, and the assessor reassesses (A-5)."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "A-3 · Control assessments\nA-4 · Assessment reports\nA-5 · Remediation actions"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "4-2 · Assess the security controls per the Security Assessment Plan\n4-3 · Prepare the Security Assessment Report (SAR)\n4-4 · Conduct initial remediation based on the SAR and reassess remediated controls"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• Completed assessments with the observation and evidence for each objective (A-3; JSIG 4-2)\n• A SAR with results, findings, limitations, and recommendations (A-4; JSIG output “SAR”)\n• Initial remediation and reassessment, with original findings kept in the updated SAR (A-5; JSIG 4-4)\n• The SSP updated where an implementation changed (A-5; JSIG output “Updated SSP”)\n• The Risk Assessment Report (RAR) updated with the risk from confirmed weaknesses (JSIG output “Updated RAR”)\n\nJSIG step outputs: SAR; Updated RAR; Updated SSP"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG updates the RAR here and has the SCA submit the SAR with a recommended authorization decision. NIST assesses system risk in Prepare (P-14) and analyzes and determines it in Authorize (R-2); its assessment report carries findings and recommendations for correcting deficiencies."), LessonSection("Run the approved procedures and record what you see.", "NIST Task A-3 and JSIG Task 4-2 assess the controls the way the approved plan describes. Each assessment asks whether a control is implemented correctly, operating as intended, and producing the desired outcome.\n\nExamine records and configurations, interview the people who run the process, and test behavior where the plan calls for it, including what should fail. Record the expected result, the observation, the evidence, and any limitation, using the adopted SP 800-53A procedures."), LessonSection("Report only what the evidence supports.", "NIST Task A-4 and JSIG Task 4-3 produce the Security Assessment Report (SAR): what was assessed, the result for each objective, the findings and limitations, and recommendations for correcting deficiencies. An objective the evidence cannot support is “other than satisfied,” not a pass by default.\n\nIn the JSIG, the SCA submits the SAR with a recommended authorization decision. The recommendation informs the AO; it is not the decision."), LessonSection("Fix what you can, reassess it, and keep the record.", "NIST Task A-5 and JSIG Task 4-4 cover initial remediation. Review each finding’s significance; the system owner corrects those that should be fixed now, and the assessor reassesses the changed controls. The SAR keeps the original finding beside the new result.\n\nThen update what the results changed: the SSP for any implementation that moved and, in the JSIG, the Risk Assessment Report (RAR) drafted in Select. Whatever stays open goes to the Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M)."), LessonSection("Assess the logging objectives", "Test: Make an approved account change on SBD-DC01 and follow it through SBD-SYSLOG01 to SBD-SPLUNK01 and the analyst’s review. Replay the attack drill: all six attempts should be refused.\n\nObserve: The event arrives and is reviewed (EV-003, EV-004), and every drill attempt is refused. SBD-MGMT01 has no verified events in the period, and one review exception has no recorded follow-up.\n\nReport: SAR-001 records the results, FND-001 for SBD-MGMT01 coverage, and FND-002 for the missing follow-up, and cites the traffic matrix for the drill.\n\nRemediate: The analyst records the follow-up, and the reassessment finds the AU-6 objective satisfied; FND-002 stays in the report as remediated. FND-001 needs a scheduled change, so it stays open for the POA&M."), LessonSection("Build", "Run the procedures in your approved plan and prepare a SAR excerpt. Include negative tests: run the six attempts in the Inside the Network attack drill, record each expected and observed result in the Operational verification column of the network traffic matrix, and cite the matrix in your SAR. Remediate one finding, reassess it, and update the SSP and the Risk Assessment Report (RAR) that course 2.1 drafted."), LessonSection("Prove", "Link each conclusion to its objective, evidence, observation, and limitation. Record unsupported objectives as other than satisfied rather than treating missing evidence as a pass, and show a remediated finding still in the report beside its reassessment.")], question: "During the assessment, the team fixes a finding and the reassessment passes. What happens to the original finding in the SAR?", answers: ["It is deleted, since the control now works.", "It stays, with the remediation and the reassessment result beside it.", "It moves to the POA&M as an open item."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Initial remediation (NIST A-5, JSIG 4-4) adds the reassessment to the report; it does not erase what the assessment found. Findings that remain open go to the POA&M.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html#catalog/08", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:09", title: "09 · Build the plan of action and milestones", minutes: 90, summary: "Turn the findings still open into a risk-ranked Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) with accountable owners, resources, dated milestones, and closure criteria.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Assess", "NIST gives A-6 to the system owner and any common control provider. In the JSIG, the ISO initiates and updates the POA&M (5-1), and the AO uses it to monitor progress."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "A-6 · Plan of action and milestones"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "5-1 · Initiate and update the POA&M from the SAR’s findings and recommendations"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• A POA&M entry for each finding still open after initial remediation (A-6; JSIG output “POA&M”)\n• Entries ranked by contextual risk, not technical severity alone\n• An accountable owner, resources, milestones, and completion dates for each entry\n• Closure criteria tied to reassessment, linked to the SAR finding and the RAR\n\nJSIG step outputs: POA&M"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "NIST prepares the POA&M at the end of Assess (A-6); the JSIG initiates it as the first task of Authorize (5-1). Both build it from the assessment report’s findings and recommendations."), LessonSection("Rank what is still open by risk.", "Findings that survive initial remediation go into the Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M). NIST Task A-6 prepares it from the assessment report’s findings and recommendations; JSIG Task 5-1 initiates it the same way, as the first task of Authorize.\n\nRank the entries by risk in the system’s context: threat, exposure, mission consequence, existing safeguards, and remaining uncertainty. Technical severity informs the order, but it is not the complete risk determination."), LessonSection("Write an action someone can manage.", "Each entry names the weakness, affected scope, accountable owner, planned actions, resources, milestones with completion dates, dependencies, and closure criteria. In the JSIG, the ISO keeps it updated with corrective actions, resources, milestone completion dates, and residual findings.\n\n“Fix soon” is not a milestone. A useful milestone describes a verifiable action with a target date and a clearly accountable owner."), LessonSection("Track each entry to verified closure.", "The AO reviews the POA&M and uses it to monitor progress. Close an entry only when a reassessment verifies the fix, and keep the original finding and dates when plans change.\n\nA POA&M is a management record; it does not accept risk or grant permission to operate. Tracking a weakness and accepting its risk are different actions, and only the AO accepts risk."), LessonSection("Turn the open logging finding into a POA&M entry", "Finding: FND-001: SBD-MGMT01 is not in verified audit collection. It remained open after initial remediation because the fix needs a scheduled change.\n\nRisk: Privileged activity on a management server could go unreviewed. Weigh the local records that remain, then set the entry’s contextual risk and rank.\n\nMilestones: Confirm the forwarding configuration, test a selected event end to end, and verify the analyst’s review covers SBD-MGMT01, each with an owner and a date.\n\nClosure: Close PM-001 only after reassessment, then update the SSP, evidence index, RAR, and SAR. FND-002 was fixed during the assessment and needs no entry."), LessonSection("Build", "Rank the findings still open after initial remediation and write a POA&M entry for each: the weakness, affected assets and controls, owner, resources, milestones with target dates, status, and closure criteria tied to reassessment. Link each entry to its SAR finding and the RAR."), LessonSection("Prove", "Prioritize two findings with different exposure and mission impact, and defend the order. Explain why a finding fixed and reassessed during the assessment needs no entry, and why an open entry cannot close without a verified reassessment.")], question: "Does adding a finding to the POA&M mean its risk has been accepted?", answers: ["Yes, automatically.", "Only if the entry has a due date.", "No. Only the AO accepts risk, as part of the authorization decision."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The POA&M plans and tracks corrective action. Accepting the remaining risk is a separate decision that belongs to the AO.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-body-of-evidence/index.html#catalog/09", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
  LearningPath(id: "rmf-authorization-to-operate", number: "2.3", title: "Authorization to Operate", subtitle: "Authorize and Monitor: the package, the decision, and continuous monitoring", category: "Network authorization", description: "Take the Body of Evidence for the Inside the Network system through the Authorize and Monitor steps of the Risk Management Framework (RMF) in NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2, with the matching tasks of the Joint Special Access Program (SAP) Implementation Guide (JSIG), 11 April 2016. Assemble the authorization package, brief the risk for the Authorizing Official (AO), record a fictional decision with its terms, conditions, and duration, and keep the authorization current through change, reporting, and disposal before the package capstone.", icon: "risk", published: true, studyTime: "3 modules · 9 topics · 9 study hours", topics: 9,
    materials: [
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-authorization-to-operate-guide", title: "Authorization to Operate · Interactive course", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Course 2.3 · Authorize and Monitor: the package, the decision, and continuous monitoring · 9 lessons", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/index.html", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-authorization-to-operate-package", title: "Authorization to Operate · Training materials (ZIP)", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Workbook (PDF and Word) and evidence starters.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/SecureByDesign-rmf-authorization-to-operate-Learning-Package.zip", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-capstone", title: "Authorization package capstone", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Assemble, defend, and maintain the practice package.", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/index.html#capstone", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory"),
      LearningResource(id: "rmf-evidence-manifest", title: "Package manifest", publisher: "Secure By Design", description: "Organize the evidence and submission records", url: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/index.html#evidence/manifest", category: "Training materials", icon: "directory")
    ],
    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:10", title: "10 · Assemble the authorization package", minutes: 120, summary: "Assemble an authorization package whose plans, assessment results, and open risks agree, and submit it for the authorizing official’s decision.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Authorize", "The system owner (in the JSIG, the Information System Owner, ISO) assembles and submits the package; a common control provider does the same for the controls it provides. The Authorizing Official (AO) receives it and may direct additional evidence."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "R-1 · Authorization package"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "5-2 · Assemble and submit the security authorization package to the AO"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• An authorization package assembled and submitted to the AO (NIST R-1; JSIG output: Security Authorization Package)\n• An executive summary of the system, the assessment results, and the remaining risk (NIST R-1)\n• A versioned package manifest naming each component, its basis, owner, and review check\n• A consistency review that reconciles scope, inventory, findings, and open actions across the SSP, SCTM, RAR, SAR, and POA&M\n\nJSIG step outputs: Security Authorization Package"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG’s minimum package is the SSP (with the SCTM, continuous monitoring strategy, and RAR), the SAR, and the POA&M, plus any evidence the AO directs. NIST R-1 adds an executive summary and treats privacy plans and privacy assessment reports alongside the security ones."), LessonSection("Assemble what the decision needs.", "In NIST SP 800-37 Revision 2, Task R-1, the system owner assembles the authorization package and submits it to the Authorizing Official (AO). It holds an executive summary, the security and privacy plans, the assessment reports, and the Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M).\n\nThe Joint Special Access Program (SAP) Implementation Guide (JSIG) applies that NIST foundation to SAP systems; its public edition, 11 April 2016, is built on SP 800-53 Revision 4. Its Task 5-2 sets a minimum: the System Security Plan (SSP), with its Security Controls Traceability Matrix (SCTM), continuous monitoring strategy, and Risk Assessment Report (RAR); the Security Assessment Report (SAR); and the POA&M. The AO can direct more."), LessonSection("Check that the documents agree.", "Compare the boundary, inventory, selected controls, implementation statements, findings, and open actions across the SSP, SCTM, RAR, SAR, and POA&M. Resolve identifiers, status, dates, and scope that disagree.\n\nFollow the risk as well: each significant SAR finding should reach the RAR, the POA&M or a proposed acceptance, and the executive summary. A package can be complete as a collection of files and still be unreliable."), LessonSection("Submit through the approved workflow.", "Follow the authority’s system of record, required reviews, signatures, and submission steps. In the Enterprise Mission Assurance Support Service (eMASS) or another tool, the records still need the same substance.\n\nSubmitting hands the package to the AO; it decides nothing. This course uses a generic record workflow, and no live government submission is part of the training."), LessonSection("A package consistency review", "SSP: Describes 21 named systems, two of them planned and not yet built (SBD-NESSUS01, SBD-EPO01), and 50 Windows workstations.\n\nSAR: Reports testing on the network for 18 named systems and the workstation sample.\n\nReconcile: Nothing claims the planned servers’ controls. SBD-ROOTCA01 is never on the network, so it is assessed during its key ceremonies: a stated limitation, not a missing test.\n\nRecord: Note each explanation in the manifest’s review checks and the executive summary before submitting."), LessonSection("Build", "Assemble the practice system’s package: an executive summary, the SSP with its SCTM, continuous monitoring strategy, and RAR, the SAR, the POA&M, and a privacy applicability record. List each in a versioned manifest with its basis (JSIG 5-2 or NIST R-1), owner, and review check."), LessonSection("Prove", "Reconcile control identifiers, asset counts, findings, dates, and status across the SSP, SCTM, RAR, SAR, and POA&M, and explain each difference you keep. Walk the package through a generic eMASS-style submission using training data.")], question: "Your authority follows the JSIG. What must the authorization package contain at a minimum?", answers: ["The executive summary only; the AO requests the rest.", "The SSP and the latest scan report.", "The SSP (with its SCTM, continuous monitoring strategy, and RAR), the SAR, and the POA&M, plus any evidence the AO directs."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "That is the JSIG Task 5-2 minimum. NIST Task R-1 adds an executive summary and puts privacy plans and assessment reports alongside the security ones.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/index.html#catalog/10", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:11", title: "11 · Support the authorization decision", minutes: 90, summary: "Brief the risk the authorizing official must determine, propose a response to each significant risk, and record what an authorization decision contains.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Authorize", "The AO, helped by a designated representative for everything but the decision itself, determines the risk and chooses the response; making and signing the decision stays with the AO. The system owner (JSIG: ISO) and the Security Control Assessor (SCA) brief the risk and answer questions."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "R-2 · Risk analysis and determination\nR-3 · Risk response\nR-4 · Authorization decision\nR-5 · Authorization reporting"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "5-3 · Determine the risk\n5-4 · Determine whether the risk is acceptable (the authorization decision)"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• A risk determination for operating the system, informed by the package and risk assessments (NIST R-2; JSIG 5-3)\n• A response to each significant risk: mitigation tracked in the POA&M or set as conditions, acceptance, or avoidance (NIST R-3)\n• An authorization decision document: ATO, IATT, or DATO, with terms and conditions and a duration (NIST R-4; JSIG 5-4)\n• A report of the decision and any significant control deficiencies to the officials who oversee risk (NIST R-5)\n• An executive risk summary and AO briefing that support the decision without making it\n\nJSIG step outputs: Authorization decision document"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The JSIG’s decisions are ATO, IATT, and DATO; NIST names authorization to operate, authorization to use, common control authorization, or a denial. NIST also makes risk response (R-3) and authorization reporting (R-5) tasks of their own; the JSIG covers the AO’s work in Tasks 5-3 and 5-4."), LessonSection("Support the AO’s risk determination.", "In NIST Task R-2, the AO analyzes the package and determines the risk of operating the system. In JSIG Task 5-3, risk assessments, formal or informal, inform the AO’s final determination.\n\nYour briefing supports that analysis: the mission, the assessed posture, significant findings and their consequences, limitations, and the recommendation the Security Control Assessor (SCA) made with the SAR. A request for more evidence is part of the process, not a setback."), LessonSection("Choose a response for each significant risk.", "In NIST Task R-3, the AO chooses a response to the risk determined. Planned mitigation is tracked in the POA&M, and conditions on operation go into the decision. Accepted deficiencies stay documented in the assessment report and are watched for change. Avoiding a risk can mean limiting the operation.\n\nA POA&M entry tracks work; it doesn’t accept risk. JSIG Task 5-4 puts the explicit acceptance of risk with the AO, who decides whether the risk to organizational operations and assets, individuals, other organizations, or national security is acceptable."), LessonSection("Record the decision so people can act on it.", "The authorization decision document records the decision, its terms and conditions, and its duration. JSIG decisions are an Authorization to Operate (ATO), an Interim Authorization to Test (IATT), for testing rather than operation, or a Denied Authorization to Operate (DATO). NIST Task R-4 names authorization to operate or to use, common control authorization, or a denial.\n\nDuration is time-driven or ongoing, backed by robust continuous monitoring. Only the AO makes and signs the decision. NIST Task R-5 then reports it, with any significant control deficiencies, to the officials who oversee risk."), LessonSection("From an open finding to a decision record", "Determine: FND-001: SBD-MGMT01 is missing from verified audit collection, so privileged activity there could go unseen in Splunk review.\n\nRespond: Mitigate through PM-001, and propose an interim condition: the Information System Security Officer (ISSO) reviews SBD-MGMT01’s local Security log and records each review.\n\nDecide: The reviewer, acting as AO, records an exercise decision: the type, terms and conditions tied to PM-001, and a duration.\n\nReport: Monitoring reports PM-001’s milestones and the interim reviews. A course score is not an ATO."), LessonSection("Build", "Write the executive risk summary and a five-minute AO briefing for the practice system. Then complete a fictional decision record: the decision type, terms and conditions, duration, and what monitoring must report."), LessonSection("Prove", "Defend one unresolved finding and answer a request for more evidence. Propose a response (mitigate, accept, or avoid), and explain why the decision belongs to the AO and why a complete package or a passing score doesn’t produce an ATO.")], question: "The AO will allow operation while an open finding is fixed. What must the decision document record?", answers: ["The decision, the terms and conditions tied to that finding, and the duration.", "Only the POA&M due dates.", "Nothing more: the open POA&M entry already accepts the risk."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The decision document carries the decision, its terms and conditions, and its duration. A POA&M tracks corrective work; accepting the risk is the AO’s decision.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/index.html#catalog/11", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:rmf-boe-ato:12", title: "12 · Monitor and maintain authorization", minutes: 90, summary: "Keep the authorization true as the system changes: analyze each change, assess and report as the strategy requires, support ongoing authorization, and plan disposal.", sections: [LessonSection("The RMF step · Monitor", "The system owner (JSIG: ISO), with the Information System Security Manager (ISSM) and the ISSO, analyzes changes, remediates, updates the package, reports status, and carries out disposal. The control assessor (JSIG: SCA) runs the ongoing assessments. The AO, or in the JSIG a designee, reviews the status and decides whether the risk is still acceptable."), LessonSection("NIST SP 800-37 Rev. 2 tasks", "M-1 · System and environment changes\nM-2 · Ongoing assessments\nM-3 · Ongoing risk response\nM-4 · Authorization package updates\nM-5 · Security and privacy reporting\nM-6 · Ongoing authorization\nM-7 · System disposal"), LessonSection("JSIG (11 April 2016) tasks", "6-1 · Determine the security impact of proposed or actual changes\n6-2 · Assess a selected subset of controls under the AO-approved monitoring strategy\n6-3 · Conduct remediation actions from monitoring, risk assessment, and open POA&M items\n6-4 · Update the SSP, SAR, and POA&M\n6-5 · Report the security status\n6-6 · Review the reported status: is the risk still acceptable?\n6-7 · Implement a decommissioning strategy"), LessonSection("What this module produces", "• A security impact analysis for each proposed or actual change (NIST M-1; JSIG 6-1)\n• Ongoing assessment results under the approved monitoring strategy (NIST M-2; JSIG 6-2)\n• Remediation and an updated SSP, SAR, and POA&M that keep the original information (NIST M-3, M-4; JSIG 6-3, 6-4)\n• Security status reports to the AO, event-driven, time-driven, or both (NIST M-5; JSIG 6-5)\n• An ongoing authorization review, with the authorization updated as appropriate (NIST M-6; JSIG 6-6)\n• A decommissioning strategy carried out when the system is retired (NIST M-7; JSIG 6-7)\n\nJSIG step outputs: Periodic ConMon deliverables; Updated SSP, SAR, POA&M; Updated Security Authorization, as appropriate"), LessonSection("Where the JSIG and NIST differ", "The steps match task for task. The JSIG is more specific: a security impact analysis that notifies the SCA, updates that keep the original information for oversight and audit, and a decommissioning strategy that includes a decommission letter. NIST reports security and privacy posture together."), LessonSection("Analyze a change before it changes the risk.", "NIST Task M-1 watches the system and its environment for changes that affect its security and privacy posture. JSIG Task 6-1 makes that a security impact analysis (SIA): determine the impact of a proposed or actual change, notify the SCA, and revise the package as required.\n\nA new component, information flow, dependency, control provider, or threat can undercut the assessment the decision relied on. The size of a technical change doesn’t tell you the size of its consequence."), LessonSection("Assess, respond, update, and report.", "The continuous monitoring (ConMon) strategy the AO approved sets which controls are assessed, how often, and by whom; JSIG Task 6-2 assesses a selected subset (NIST M-2). Continuous doesn’t mean testing every control constantly.\n\nResults feed remediation and risk response (M-3, 6-3) and updates to the SSP, SAR, and POA&M that keep the original information needed for oversight and audit (M-4, 6-4). The system owner reports status to the AO, event-driven, time-driven, or both (M-5, 6-5)."), LessonSection("Keep the decision current until the system retires.", "In NIST Task M-6 and JSIG Task 6-6, the AO (or, in the JSIG, a designee) reviews the reported status to decide whether the risk is still acceptable. If not, the AO decides what follows: new conditions, reassessment, or a new decision.\n\nDisposal closes the loop (M-7, 6-7). The JSIG’s decommissioning strategy covers media sanitization, a records retention plan the AO approves, inventory updates, notifications, a review of inherited relationships, and a decommission letter."), LessonSection("A change goes through security impact analysis", "Change: A second licensed application is proposed for SBD-APPS01, adding its vendor licensing port to rule F12 for the VLAN 80 workstations.\n\nImpact: New software and a changed flow: configuration (CM-6, a SCAP Compliance Checker rescan), the vendor’s update path (SI-2), its events (AU-2, AU-12), and the course 1.19 recovery set (CP-9).\n\nDecide: Notify the SCA, reassess before go-live, and give the SIA to the AO or designee, who decides whether it fits the current authorization.\n\nUpdate: Update the inventory, SSP, SCTM, rule register, and evidence index, report the change in the next status report, and keep the prior versions."), LessonSection("Build", "Run one cycle of the continuous monitoring strategy course 2.1 drafted: the controls assessed, owners, evidence, reporting dates, and escalation triggers. Write a security impact analysis for one proposed change and a status report for the AO."), LessonSection("Prove", "Process a new licensed application on SBD-APPS01 (rule F12), a missed update in one of SBD-WSUS01’s rings, and a changed common control such as the server room’s badge access: what is reassessed, which records change, what the status report says, and when the AO is engaged. Then list the disposal steps if SBD-WEB01’s service were retired, including what depends on it.")], question: "A proposed change adds a port to a documented information flow. What comes first?", answers: ["Make the change; the current authorization covers future services.", "A security impact analysis: identify the affected controls and records, notify the SCA, and follow the required review.", "Nothing until the next reauthorization."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "NIST Task M-1 and JSIG Task 6-1 evaluate a change against the assessed and authorized scope before it quietly changes the risk.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/rmf-authorization-to-operate/index.html#catalog/12", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ])
];
